local _, ns = ...
local E = ns.DB.entries
E["item:agamand-family-axe"] = {t="item",n="Agamand Family Axe",z="tirisfal",s="The Agamand Family Axe is an ancestral heirloom preserved within the Agamand family crypt in northern Tirisfal Glades. Once a martial symbol of the wealthy mill-owning dynasty, it is sought by Coleman Farthing to strip the undead family of their remaining dignity.",h="The Agamand Family Axe is an ancestral heirloom preserved within the Agamand family crypt in northern...",sec={{t="A Weapon of the Agamands",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand dynasty reigned as prosperous landowners across the northern hills of Tirisfal Glades, maintaining vast mills and a private family crypt. The Agamand Family Axe rested among the ancestral arms entombed alongside deceased patriarchs and warriors, kept intact on underground weapon racks even as the Scourge overran the estate and slaughtered its inhabitants.",sp=0},{t="Spiteful Restitution",b="When the Scourge raised the fallen Agamands as mindless thralls to the Lich King, their former servant Coleman Farthing survived—in undeath—with bitter hatred for his old masters. Farthing blames the family for his own demise and commissions Forsaken wanderers to enter the crypt, strip the weapon racks of the axe, sword, mace, and dagger, and erase the final vestiges of the Agamand heritage.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Agamand Family Axe located?",a="The axe rests upon a weapon rack inside the Agamand Family Crypt, which lies at the northern edge of Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"axe location","where is the axe","agamand axe place"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who wants the Agamand Family Axe?",a="Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken residing in Brill, seeks the weapon alongside the family's dagger, mace, and sword to strip his former masters of their honor.",al={"who needs the axe","coleman farthing quest","who wants agamand heirlooms"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing hate the Agamand family?",a="The Agamands employed Farthing during life and, by his account, directly caused his death during the Scourge invasion. He takes vengeance by plundering their crypt while they wander the estate as undead minions.",al={"why does farthing want heirlooms","coleman farthing backstory","farthing grudge"},it="motive"},{q="Is the Agamand Family Axe a usable weapon?",a="No. The axe is an ancestral relic retrieved purely as a quest objective to prove the desecration of the family's vault.",al={"can i equip the axe","axe stats"},it="power",gp=true},{q="What is the Agamand Family Axe?",a="The Agamand Family Axe is an heirloom weapon belonging to the prominent Agamand dynasty of Tirisfal Glades. Preserved within their ancestral crypt alongside other cherished armaments, it represents the lineage's martial heritage and legacy from the days before the Scourge swept Lordaeron and turned the living family into mindless undead thralls.",al={"agamand axe","what is agamand family axe","agamand heirloom axe"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, looting the Agamand heirlooms is a visceral settling of scores against Lordaeron's former aristocracy, who fell just as easily to the plague.",["class:Warrior"]="The heirloom weapons preserved within the crypt show that the Agamand lineage possessed martial traditions alongside their wealth and grain stores."},kw={"agamand","agamand crypt","agamand family axe","agamand mills","coleman farthing","heirloom","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:1821","npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","item:agamand-family-sword","item:agamand-family-mace","item:agamand-family-dagger","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Agamand Family Axe","Agamand Heirlooms"}}
E["item:agamand-family-dagger"] = {t="item",n="Agamand Family Dagger",z="tirisfal",s="The Agamand Family Dagger is an ancestral heirloom of the fallen Agamand dynasty, resting on weapon racks within their ancestral crypt in northern Tirisfal Glades. Coleman Farthing seeks this blade alongside the rest of their family arsenal to strip his former masters of their remaining legacy and honor.",h="The Agamand Family Dagger is an ancestral heirloom of the fallen Agamand dynasty, resting on weapon racks...",sec={{t="A Legacy in the Crypt",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family maintained vast estates and grain mills across the rolling meadows of Tirisfal Glades. Like the rest of their lineage's armaments, the Agamand Family Dagger survived the devastation wrought by the Scourge, locked within the family crypt north of Agamand Mills. Protected by the restless, undead remnants of the household and mindless minions of the Lich King, the dagger represents the prestige and martial heritage that once elevated the family above their servants and tenants.",sp=0},{t="A Spiteful Requisition",b="For Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken residing at Brill who died in the Agamands' service, the blade represents bitter resentment rather than noble lineage. Holding the family directly responsible for his death, Farthing directs adventurers into the Agamand Family Crypt to recover the dagger along with the family sword, axe, and mace. In stripping these ancestral armaments from their tomb, Farthing aims to rob the now-Scourge patriarchs of their last vestiges of dignity and ensure the Agamand memory is utterly extinguished.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Agamand Family Dagger?",a="It is an ancestral weapon belonging to the wealthy Agamand family of Tirisfal Glades. It was interred alongside other armaments in the family crypt, which has since fallen under Scourge control.",al={"agamand dagger","what is the agamand dagger","family dagger tirisfal"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the Agamand Family Dagger found?",a="It sits upon an Agamand Weapon Rack deep inside the Agamand Family Crypt, situated at the northern end of Agamand Mills in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to find agamand dagger","agamand dagger location","dagger weapon rack location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want this dagger?",a="Coleman Farthing was employed by the Agamands in life and blames them for his demise. He demands the dagger and other heirlooms to strip his former masters of their honor and finish their ruin.",al={"why does farthing want the dagger","farthing agamand quest","why rob agamand crypt"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Who are the other weapons in this heirloom set?",a="Along with the dagger, the heirloom set includes the Agamand Family Sword, the Agamand Family Axe, and the Agamand Family Mace, all scattered among weapon racks in the family crypt.",al={"other agamand heirlooms","agamand weapons list","what else is in the crypt"},it="item"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Agamand heirlooms represent the bitter divide between the surviving Forsaken of Lordaeron and their former masters who succumbed to mindless servitude in the Scourge.",["class:Rogue"]="Slipping past the risen undead in the crypt to pilfer prized family blades from weapon racks exemplifies classic underworld burglary turned into an act of cold vengeance."},kw={"agamand","agamand mills","coleman farthing","crypt","dagger","heirloom","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:1821","npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","item:agamand-family-sword","item:agamand-family-axe","item:agamand-family-mace","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Agamand Family Dagger","Agamand Heirlooms"}}
E["item:agamand-family-mace"] = {t="item",n="Agamand Family Mace",z="tirisfal",s="The Agamand Family Mace is an ancestral heirloom preserved within the Agamand Family Crypt in northern Tirisfal Glades. Once a symbol of pride for a wealthy dynasty of northern Lordaeron, it has become an object of spiteful retribution sought by their former servant Coleman Farthing.",h="The Agamand Family Mace is an ancestral heirloom preserved within the Agamand Family Crypt in northern...",sec={{t="A Legacy in Ruin",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family held extensive farmlands and windmills in the northern reaches of Tirisfal Glades. Like the rest of the regional aristocracy, they fell before the plague of undeath. The Agamands were reanimated as mindless thralls in service to the Lich King, wandering their ruined estate while their ancient family treasures remained locked within the depths of the family crypt. The Agamand Family Mace is stored among these weapons, an emblem of a forgotten lineage now reduced to shambling Scourge.",sp=0},{t="A Servant's Vengeance",b="The mace is one of four martial heirlooms targeted by Coleman Farthing, a former employee who blames the family for his demise during the fall of Lordaeron. Resurrected into the ranks of the Forsaken, Farthing seeks to strip the mindless Agamands of every vestige of dignity and honor. By retrieving the mace alongside the ancestral sword, axe, and dagger, Farthing aims to ensure that the memory and pride of his former masters are permanently wiped from the lands they once ruled.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Agamand Family Mace located?",a="The mace is stored on a weapon rack inside the Agamand Family Crypt, located at the far northern end of Agamand Mills in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is the mace","agamand mace location","find agamand mace"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who wants the Agamand Family Mace?",a="Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken residing at Brill, demands the mace along with the other family heirlooms to settle an old score against his former masters.",al={"who needs the mace","who takes the agamand mace","coleman farthing heirloom"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want the heirlooms stolen?",a="Farthing served the Agamands in life and blames them directly for his death. Taking their prized weapons from their crypt is his way of wiping out the final traces of their family honor.",al={"why steal the heirlooms","coleman farthing motive","why hate the agamands"},it="motive"},{q="What happened to the Agamand family?",a="The Agamand family ruled the prosperous Agamand Mills in northern Lordaeron until the plague fell upon them. Now, their reanimated corpses roam the estate and the crypts as mindless thralls of the Scourge.",al={"who were the agamands","what happened to the agamands","agamand lore"},it="history"},{q="What is the Agamand Family Mace?",a="The Agamand Family Mace is an ancestral heirloom belonging to the once-prosperous Agamand family of Tirisfal Glades. Stored away within the family crypt alongside their other martial treasures, this weapon represents a lingering vestige of the lineage's former standing and honor before the family fell to the Scourge.",al={"agamand mace","what is agamand family mace","agamand mace heirloom"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The desecration of the Agamand crypts illustrates how lingering mortal grievances and resentments often drive Forsaken to settle scores from life."},kw={"agamand family crypt","agamand family mace","agamand heirlooms","agamand mills","coleman farthing","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:1821","npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","item:agamand-family-sword","item:agamand-family-axe","item:agamand-family-dagger","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Agamand Family Mace","Agamand Heirlooms"}}
E["item:agamand-family-sword"] = {t="item",n="Agamand Family Sword",z="tirisfal",s="The Agamand Family Sword is a tarnished ancestral blade kept inside the Agamand Family Crypt in northern Tirisfal Glades. Once a proud symbol of the wealthy mill-owning dynasty, it is sought by the Forsaken Coleman Farthing to erase the final vestiges of his former masters' legacy.",h="The Agamand Family Sword is a tarnished ancestral blade kept inside the Agamand Family Crypt in northern...",sec={{t="A Legacy in the Tomb",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand dynasty held immense wealth and influence across the rolling mills of northern Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, the family met a gruesome end, their bodies raised into mindless thralls of the Lich King. Their ancestral weapons, including the ceremonial family sword, were sealed away in the Agamand Family Crypt at the northern edge of the mills. Stripping these blades from their burial racks serves not merely as looting, but as a deliberate act to break whatever memory and noble standing remains of the fallen household.",sp=0},{t="Vengeance from Beyond the Grave",b="The sword is coveted by Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken residing in Brill who worked for the Agamands in life. Farthing holds a bitter grudge against his former masters, blaming their decisions for his death during the kingdom's fall. By dispatching adventurers to claim the sword alongside the dynasty's ancestral axe, dagger, and mace, Farthing seeks to rob the shambling Agamands of their last shred of honor, ensuring their bloodline's dignity is thoroughly extinguished.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Agamand Family Sword?",a="It is an ancestral weapon belonging to the Agamands, a prominent noble family that operated the massive grain mills in northern Tirisfal Glades prior to the fall of Lordaeron.",al={"agamand sword lore","what is agamand family sword"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the sword located?",a="The sword rests on a weapon rack within the Agamand Family Crypt, located at the northern edge of Agamand Mills in Tirisfal Glades, defended by Scourge thralls and risen members of the Agamand bloodline.",al={"where is agamand sword","sword location agamand"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want this sword?",a="Coleman Farthing was employed by the Agamand family in life and blames them for his untimely death. He seeks the family's heirloom weapons to strip away their remaining honor and erase the memory of their dynasty.",al={"who wants the sword","why does farthing want heirlooms"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What happened to the Agamand family?",a="The Agamands were slaughtered during the Scourge invasion of Lordaeron. Unlike the free-willed Forsaken, the deceased family members remain trapped in mindless servitude to the Lich King, haunting their ancestral estate and crypt.",al={"who are the agamands","agamand mills story"},it="history"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaiming artifacts from Tirisfal's former nobility represents a settling of scores with a living past that failed to protect them.",["class:Warrior"]="Though once a polished emblem of Lordaeron aristocracy, the blade now sits neglected in an unhallowed tomb, an artifact of ruined martial pride."},kw={"agamand","crypt","farthing","heirloom","mills","scourge","sword","tirisfal"},rel={"quest:1821","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","npc:coleman-farthing","item:agamand-family-axe","item:agamand-family-dagger","item:agamand-family-mace","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Agamand Family Sword","Agamand Heirlooms"}}
E["item:blackened-skull"] = {t="item",n="Blackened Skull",z="tirisfal",s="A blackened skull is the charred cranial remnant of a Darkeye Bonecaster animating in the Agamand Mills. Deathguard Dillinger in Brill demands these grisly trophies as proof of thinning the mindless Scourge forces threatening Tirisfal Glades.",h="A blackened skull is the charred cranial remnant of a Darkeye Bonecaster animating in the Agamand Mills.",sec={{t="Trophy of the Bonecasters",b="Charred and saturated with necromantic energies, these skulls are salvaged from the Darkeye Bonecasters haunting the Agamand Mills. These skeletal spellcasters direct necromantic malice against the living and the Forsaken alike, attempting to entrench a forward base for the Lich King's armies in northern Tirisfal. Presenting their ruined skulls to Deathguard Dillinger serves as tangible evidence that the Scourge's mystical vanguard is being crushed.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where do blackened skulls come from?",a="They are collected from the remains of Darkeye Bonecasters, the skeletal spellcasters prowling the fields and structures around the Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"blackened skull source","who drops blackened skulls","where to get blackened skulls"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger want blackened skulls?",a="Deathguard Dillinger tasks Forsaken defenders with gathering blackened skulls and notched ribs as proof of active combat against the Scourge. The Forsaken cannot permit the Lich King's mindless minions to establish a foothold so close to Brill and the Undercity.",al={"dillinger skulls","why collect skulls","mills overrun skulls"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why are the skulls blackened?",a="The charring and discoloration are the result of the volatile dark magic wielded by the Bonecasters, which scorches their own brittle, reanimated bones as they cast spells.",al={"why is it blackened","blackened skull lore"}},{q="What is the Scourge doing at Agamand Mills?",a="The Scourge are attempting to fortify the abandoned Agamand Mills as a forward staging ground, using it to launch deeper attacks and raids across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"agamand mills scourge","why are scourge at the mills"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Blackened Skull?",a="A Blackened Skull is a grim trophy harvested from the Darkeye Bonecasters haunting Agamand Mills. Charred by necrotic sorcery, these dark remains serve as physical proof to the Forsaken Deathguards that another Scourge spellcaster has been put down before it could help establish an enemy base in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what is blackened skull","blackened skull item","blackened skulls lore"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, bringing down Darkeye Bonecasters is a matter of pure survival, preventing their former master from establishing a staging ground on their doorstep.",["class:Mage"]="Studying the singed bone reveals how raw, unstable necromantic casting burns directly through the skeletal caster's own physical frame."},kw={"agamand mills","blackened skull","brill","darkeye bonecaster","deathguard dillinger","notched rib","scourge","the mills overrun"},rel={"quest:426","subzone:agamand-mills","npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:brill","item:notched-rib","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Blackened Skull","The Mills Overrun (Classic)"}}
E["item:candle-of-beckoning"] = {t="item",n="Candle of Beckoning",z="tirisfal",s="The Candle of Beckoning is an enchanted ritual taper kept by the reclusive necromancer Gunther Arcanus on Brightwater Lake. Placing and lighting the candle upon an altar on a nearby islet summons forth a restless spirit known as Lillith Nefara.",h="The Candle of Beckoning is an enchanted ritual taper kept by the reclusive necromancer Gunther Arcanus on...",sec={{t="A Test of Allegiance",b="Found stored in a crate beside the solitary mage Gunther Arcanus on his island in Brightwater Lake, the Candle of Beckoning serves as an instrument in a dark ritual of summoning. Gunther is a fiercely independent undead sorcerer who broke free of the Lich King's command on his own, remaining wary of the Scourge and the Forsaken alike. To prove your loyalty is not to the Lich King, Gunther directs you to take one of these candles southwest to a smaller islet. There, set upon an old, bloodstained table, the candle's flame pierces the veil between worlds to awaken and beckon the dormant shade of Lillith Nefara so she may be laid to rest.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Candle of Beckoning used for?",a="The candle is used to summon the shade Lillith Nefara. By placing and lighting it atop the old, bloodstained ritual table located on the smaller islet southwest of Gunther Arcanus's camp, the dormant spirit is drawn into the physical realm.",al={"what does candle of beckoning do","use of candle of beckoning","how to use candle of beckoning"},it="purpose"},{q="Where do you get the Candle of Beckoning?",a="You obtain the candle from a crate sitting beside Gunther Arcanus on his island in Brightwater Lake, situated in eastern Tirisfal Glades, while undertaking his test of allegiance.",al={"where to find candle of beckoning","how to get candle of beckoning","candle crate location"},gp=true},{q="Who is summoned by the candle?",a="Lighting the candle summons Lillith Nefara, a vengeful ghostly shade tethered to the lake's islets. Defeating her is required to prove that you do not serve the Scourge.",al={"who does it summon","candle summon target","who is lillith nefara"},it="target"},{q="Why does Gunther Arcanus have these candles?",a="Gunther uses the candles and the ritual surrounding them as a trial. Suspicious of outsiders and fiercely guarding his autonomy from the Scourge, he demands that anyone seeking his alliance face and destroy the restless dead he summons.",al={"why does gunther have candles","gunther ritual candles"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Candle of Beckoning?",a="The Candle of Beckoning is an enchanted ritual taper found on the isolated island retreat of the necromancer Gunther Arcanus in Brightwater Lake. Kept within a crate beside the reclusive mage, the candle carries potent summoning magic tailored to pierce the veil of death. When set upon a bloodied ritual table on a nearby islet and ignited, its flame acts as a supernatural lure, calling forth the dormant shade of Lillith Nefara.",al={"what is candle of beckoning","candle of beckoning lore","what is the beckoning candle"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why would anyone want a Candle of Beckoning?",a="Anyone seeking an audience or alliance with Gunther Arcanus must first prove their loyalty lies away from the Lich King. Gunther demands proof of allegiance before sharing his formidable talents with the Forsaken. By retrieving a Candle of Beckoning, you can draw forth and destroy Lillith Nefara, demonstrating your resolve to oppose the Scourge and earning the hermit necromancer's hard-won respect.",al={"why do i need candle of beckoning","why want candle of beckoning","purpose of candle of beckoning"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken emissaries sent by the Undercity, handling Gunther's ritual candle is a necessary step to convince the fiercely independent necromancer that you are not thralls of the Lich King.",["class:Mage"]="Gunther's ability to bind spirits to ritual implements like this candle showcases his mastery over necromantic and conjuration arts outside the Kirin Tor's sanctioned teachings."},kw={"brightwater lake","candle of beckoning","gunther arcanus","lillith nefara","proving allegiance","the dormant shade","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:410","quest:409","quest:431","npc:gunther-arcanus","subzone:brightwater-lake","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Candle of Beckoning","The Dormant Shade"}}
E["item:darkhound-blood"] = {t="item",n="Darkhound Blood",z="tirisfal",s="Darkhound Blood is a foul bodily fluid harvested from the tainted canine beasts roaming the fields and forests of Tirisfal Glades. Gathered at the behest of the Royal Apothecary Society, it serves as an early reagent in their dark experiments to engineer a lethal new plague.",h="Darkhound Blood is a foul bodily fluid harvested from the tainted canine beasts roaming the fields and...",sec={{t="Fuel for the New Plague",b="Under the command of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Royal Apothecary Society strives to develop a virulent contagion capable of wiping out Arthas and his Scourge. Stationed in Brill, Apothecary Johaan theorizes that local beasts, corrupted by dark energies and resilient to the surrounding decay, possess unusual biological properties. By distilling the blood of wild darkhounds, Johaan aims to uncover biological compounds that can resist or propagate virulent necrotic strains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Darkhound Blood used for?",a="It is used by Apothecary Johaan in Brill as a foundational reagent in the Royal Apothecary Society's research to concoct a brand new plague deadlier than anything Azeroth has ever seen.",al={"darkhound blood use","why get darkhound blood","darkhound blood purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Which creatures yield this blood?",a="It is drawn from the various darkhounds wandering Tirisfal Glades, including cursed, ravenous, and decrepit darkhounds.",al={"where to get darkhound blood","who drops darkhound blood","darkhound blood source"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why are darkhounds targeted for plague research?",a="Apothecary Johaan theorizes that the blood of beasts acclimated to the corrupted lands of Tirisfal might hold the biological secrets needed to synthesize an unstoppable contagion.",al={"why darkhounds","apothecary johaan theory","darkhound properties"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What is Darkhound Blood?",a="Darkhound Blood is the tainted vital fluid harvested from the feral darkhounds prowling Tirisfal Glades. Warped by dark magic and lingering undeath across the glades, these wretched canines possess blood that carries unique, resilient properties. While repulsive and hazardous to ordinary living creatures, alchemists and researchers find it an ideal medium for biological experiments, particularly when investigating virulent compounds or resisting necrotic decay.",al={"what is darkhound blood","darkhound blood lore","explain darkhound blood"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does Darkhound Blood come from, and who made it?",a="This blood is naturally harvested from the cursed, ravenous, and decrepit darkhounds that stalk the haunted woodlands and ruined farmsteads of Tirisfal Glades. It is not an artificial concoction, but rather the bodily fluid of beasts that have adapted to and absorbed the blight poisoning the region since the Third War.",al={"source of darkhound blood","where does darkhound blood come from","who created darkhound blood"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Harvesting reagents like Darkhound Blood is essential work for the Forsaken, helping Lady Sylvanas and the Royal Apothecary Society forge the ultimate weapon against the Scourge.",["faction:Horde"]="While dark experiments unfold in Brill, few outside the Forsaken inner circle fully grasp the toxic potential being unlocked by the Royal Apothecary Society."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill","darkhound","darkhound blood","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:367","npc:apothecary-johaan","topic:royal-apothecary-society","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","topic:scourge"},src={"Darkhound Blood","A New Plague"}}
E["item:devlin-s-remains"] = {t="item",n="Devlin's Remains",z="tirisfal",s="Devlin's Remains are the collected skeletal bones of Devlin Agamand, the younger son of the wealthy family who owned the Agamand Mills. Stalking the roads near his ruined ancestral estate as a mindless Scourge thrall, he was struck down to deliver his bones to a vengeful survivor in Brill.",h="Devlin's Remains are the collected skeletal bones of Devlin Agamand, the younger son of the wealthy family...",sec={{t="A Bitter Legacy",b="In life, Devlin Agamand stood in the shadow of his elder brother Thurman. Where Thurman was gentle and tall, Devlin was remembered as physically frail and viciously sharp-tongued. When the Plague of Undeath swept through northern Tirisfal Glades, the Agamand family failed to protect their lands and laborers. Devlin was among the first to fall, reanimated as a ravenous Scourge ghoul whose frantic, mad chattering haunted the roadside leading up to Agamand Mills.",sp=0},{t="Vengeance on the Mantle",b="Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken residing in Brill, holds a deep and abiding grudge against the entire Agamand bloodline. Holding them directly responsible for failing his family when the plague arrived, Farthing tasked travelers with laying Devlin to rest and collecting his remains. Farthing sought to display Devlin's bones upon his mantle, savoring the knowledge that his former masters have been reduced to dust and shattered fragments under his heel.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Devlin Agamand in life?",a="Devlin was the younger son of the Agamand family, the wealthy landowners who built the Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades. Unlike his mild-tempered and broad-shouldered brother Thurman, Devlin was weak, sickly, and notoriously ill-tempered, known for a bitter and sharp tongue.",al={"who is devlin","devlin agamand backstory","about devlin"},it="people"},{q="Where were Devlin's remains gathered from?",a="They were taken from Devlin Agamand's Scourge-controlled corpse, located just south of the Agamand Mills along the road winding through Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is devlin","where to find devlin","devlin agamand location"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want these bones?",a="Coleman Farthing despises the Agamand family, believing they abandoned and failed him and his loved ones during the outbreak of the Plague. He intends to mount Devlin's bones on his mantelpiece in Brill as a trophy of vengeance and satisfaction.",al={"why farthing wants remains","coleman farthing motive","why collect agamand bones"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Is Devlin part of the Forsaken?",a="No. When Devlin succumbed to the Plague, his mind was lost entirely to the Scourge. He wandered the grounds near the mills as a mindless undead ghoul, babbling madness until destroyed.",al={"is devlin forsaken","was devlin scourge"},it="side"},{q="What are Devlin's Remains?",a="Devlin's Remains are the skeletal bones of Devlin Agamand, the younger son of the prominent Agamand dynasty. After falling to the Plague of Undeath, Devlin was reduced to a mindless Scourge wretch lingering along the road to his family's ruined mills until put down and collected.",al={"what are devlins remains","devlin remains item","who is devlins remains"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the Agamands represent the old, privileged nobility of Lordaeron whose failures left common folk to perish and rise as monsters, making the desecration of Devlin's remains an act of class retribution."},kw={"agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","devlin agamand","devlin's remains","the haunted mills","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:362","npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-mills","zone:tirisfal","quest:354","quest:355","quest:408","subzone:brill"},src={"Devlin's Remains","The Haunted Mills (Classic)"}}
E["item:duskbat-wing"] = {t="item",n="Duskbat Wing",z="tirisfal",s="A Duskbat Wing is a raw material harvested from the leather-winged bats flitting through the gloom around Deathknell. In the resource-starved Forsaken settlement, these leathery appendages are salvaged and repurposed by local tailors into sturdy makeshift armor for newly risen soldiers.",h="A Duskbat Wing is a raw material harvested from the leather-winged bats flitting through the gloom around...",sec={{t="Improvised Protection in Deathknell",b="When new undead claw their way from the soil of Deathknell, they emerge bearing little more than rags and brittle bones. Surrounded by the lingering Scourge mindless and hostile wildlife, the fledgling Forsaken must scrape together whatever protective gear they can find. Novice Elreth, assistant to Shadow Priest Sarvis, collects Duskbat Wings alongside wolf paws to piece together and reinforce clothing and armor for freshly raised warriors before they are sent into the wilderness to fight.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Duskbat Wings used for?",a="In Deathknell, Novice Elreth uses duskbat wings alongside scavenger paws as raw materials to craft, mend, and reinforce armor and garments for newly awakened Forsaken recruits.",al={"duskbat wing use","why get duskbat wings","purpose of duskbat wings"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do Duskbat Wings come from?",a="They are harvested from Duskbats and Mangy Duskbats found haunting the groves and hillsides surrounding the village of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to find duskbat wings","duskbat wing source","who drops duskbat wings"},it="origin"},{q="Who requests these wings in Deathknell?",a="Novice Elreth, who tends to the wounded and crafts gear under the direction of Shadow Priest Sarvis inside the Deathknell chapel, asks recruits to gather them.",al={"who needs duskbat wings","turn in duskbat wing","duskbat wing quest giver"}},{q="Why do the Forsaken rely on bat parts for armor?",a="Deathknell has almost no supply lines or traditional manufacturing. The local caretakers must scavenge tough hides, fur, and leathery bat wings from native Tirisfal predators to fashion rudimentary protective clothing for recruits heading into combat.",al={"why bat wings for armor","bat leather armor forsaken"},it="motive"},{q="What is a Duskbat Wing?",a="A Duskbat Wing is the leathery wing harvested from duskbats that flutter through the haunted woods of Tirisfal Glades. In Deathknell, where manufactured goods are scarce, these wings serve as a vital raw material. Tailors and caretakers among the Forsaken stitch and cure the bat hide into durable garments and light armor for newly awakened undead before sending them out to face the hazards of the glades.",al={"what is duskbat wing","duskbat wing item","about duskbat wing"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For freshly awakened Forsaken, gathering these wings is one of the very first survival tasks assigned to ensure you don't fall to pieces in your initial skirmishes.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Novice Elreth demonstrates the rough-and-ready survival tailoring needed in Tirisfal, utilizing tough bat wings as workable leather and fabric substitutes."},kw={"deathknell","duskbat","duskbat wing","novice elreth","scavenger paw","the damned","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:novice-elreth","quest:376","item:scavenger-paw","subzone:deathknell","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","topic:forsaken","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Duskbat Wing","The Damned (Classic)"}}
E["item:embalming-ichor"] = {t="item",n="Embalming Ichor",z="tirisfal",s="Embalming Ichor is an unnatural preserving fluid harvested from the bodies of Rot Hide gnolls in northern Tirisfal Glades. It sustains the gnolls' animated existence and has drawn the close interest of the Forsaken Apothecaries, who seek to analyze its properties to counter the Scourge.",h="Embalming Ichor is an unnatural preserving fluid harvested from the bodies of Rot Hide gnolls in northern...",sec={{t="Unnatural Preservation",b="The Rot Hide gnolls infesting Garren's Haunt and the nearby mass graves are not typical wild beasts; they are rotting, preserved thralls deployed by the Scourge to dig up plague victims. Flowing through their decayed veins is Embalming Ichor, a foul fluid that artificially preserves their flesh and grants them enduring unlife. For Magistrate Sevren in Brill, recovering vials of this ichor serves a dual purpose: it confirms the destruction of these graverobbers and provides Royal Apothecary Society researchers with fresh specimens to dissect, study, and weaponize against the Lich King's minions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Embalming Ichor?",a="It is a preserving alchemical substance found within the animated bodies of Rot Hide gnolls in Tirisfal Glades. The fluid sustains their rotting flesh and keeps them moving in service to the Scourge.",al={"embalming ichor","what is ichor","gnoll fluid"},it="identity"},{q="Who wants this ichor collected?",a="Magistrate Sevren, presiding over the town of Brill, orders Forsaken champions to harvest it from slain Rot Hides so that the Royal Apothecary Society can study its chemical makeup.",al={"who needs embalming ichor","magistrate sevren ichor","who wants ichor"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why are the Rot Hide gnolls digging up graves?",a="The Scourge uses the Rot Hide gnolls to exhume the bodies of plague victims buried in the northern mass graves near Garren's Haunt, seeking raw material to bolster their ranks.",al={"why are gnolls graverobbing","mass graves tirisfal","gnoll scourge connection"},it="motive"},{q="What do the Forsaken plan to do with the fluid?",a="The Apothecaries in the Undercity study the ichor to uncover its secrets, hoping to develop countermeasures or new alchemical formulas against the Scourge.",al={"what does sevren do with ichor","apothecary study ichor","use of embalming ichor"},it="purpose"},{q="Where does Embalming Ichor come from, and who made it?",a="Embalming Ichor is harvested from the Rot Hide gnolls haunting the Mass Grave and Garren's Haunt in Tirisfal Glades. It is the foul unnatural fluid used by the Scourge to animate and preserve these undead gnoll servants, sustaining the pseudo-life that drives them to unearth corpses for their masters' armies.",al={"embalming ichor origin","who made embalming ichor","where does ichor come from"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, denying the Scourge fresh cadavers from the mass graves is vital to protecting Tirisfal and securing raw materials for their own survival.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The preservation fluid inside the Rot Hides showcases dark alchemical methods that intrigue herbalists and chemists studying the plague's reagents."},kw={"apothecaries","brill","embalming ichor","garren's haunt","graverobber","magistrate sevren","mass graves","rot hide"},rel={"quest:358","npc:magistrate-sevren","subzone:garren-s-haunt","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:gnoll","topic:scourge","topic:forsaken"},src={"Embalming Ichor","Graverobbers (Classic)"}}
E["item:encrypted-scroll"] = {t="item",n="Encrypted Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Encrypted Scroll is a discreet parchment delivered to promising newly risen rogues in Deathknell. Bearing the seal of David Trias, it directs the recipient to introduce themselves for formal training among the Forsaken's covert operatives.",h="The Encrypted Scroll is a discreet parchment delivered to promising newly risen rogues in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Covert Summons",b="When a freshly awakened Forsaken shows an aptitude for stealth and subterfuge, they do not remain unnoticed for long. The Encrypted Scroll is handed over in Deathknell once a scout proves capable against the mindless Scourge roaming the nearby graveyard. Bearing the personal seal of the rogue trainer David Trias, the scroll instructs the bearer to seek him out in the local inn. It serves as an initial vetting process, pointing fledgling operatives toward the discipline required to serve the Dark Lady rather than wasting their second chance at life as mere pickpockets.",sp=0},{t="Eyes of the Shadowstalkers",b="The message itself is brief, directing the rogue to establish contact within Deathknell before venturing deeper into Tirisfal Glades. Trias keeps tabs on up-and-coming talent on behalf of the Shadowstalkers, the covert arm of the Forsaken. To survive in Lordaeron, the Forsaken rely heavily on silent blades and cunning scouts to counter the Scarlet Crusade, surviving Alliance enclaves, and leftover Scourge filth. Answering this ciphered summons is the first step toward joining that covert network.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Encrypted Scroll?",a="The Encrypted Scroll is an introductory summons directed to newly risen Forsaken rogues. It instructs them to report to David Trias at the Deathknell inn to begin their professional rogue training.",al={"what does scroll do","why encrypted scroll","scroll purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who sends the Encrypted Scroll?",a="The scroll bears the personal seal of David Trias, the resident rogue trainer stationed in Deathknell who keeps watch for promising new recruits.",al={"who wrote scroll","scroll sender","who is the scroll from"},it="origin"},{q="What are the Shadowstalkers mentioned by David Trias?",a="The Shadowstalkers are a covert group of Forsaken rogues, scouts, and assassins who operate across Lordaeron. Trias evaluates fresh talent to determine who is worthy of eventually joining their ranks.",al={"who are shadowstalkers","shadowstalkers forsaken","shadowstalker order"},it="people"},{q="Where do I take the Encrypted Scroll after reading it?",a="After reading the scroll, bring it directly to David Trias, who can be found lurking inside the inn at Deathknell.",al={"where turn in scroll","who do i bring scroll to","scroll hand in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What is the Encrypted Scroll?",a="The Encrypted Scroll is a discreet, ciphered dispatch delivered to newly awakened rogues in Deathknell. Stamped with the personal seal of the rogue trainer David Trias, it serves as an initial summons and test of confidentiality, directing promising stealth operatives to introduce themselves before taking on deeper reconnaissance for the Forsaken.",al={"what is encrypted scroll","what is this scroll","encrypted scroll info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does David Trias want to contact you with the Encrypted Scroll?",a="David Trias uses the scroll to recruit and assess fresh talent among the newly risen Forsaken. The Shadowstalkers and the fledgling nation need sharp, capable infiltrators rather than clumsy pickpockets. By quietly summoning promising recruits to the Deathknell tavern, he ensures prospective agents receive proper training and stay on the path toward serving the Dark Lady's intelligence network.",al={"why does david trias send the scroll","why is encrypted scroll wanted","why contact me encrypted scroll"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="The scroll marks the beginning of your professional instruction in stealth, lockpicking, and dirty fighting under the watchful eyes of the Forsaken rogue cadre.",["race:Undead"]="For the newly risen in Deathknell, this summons represents an early opportunity to secure a purposeful role within Sylvanas's emerging kingdom."},kw={"david trias","deathknell","encrypted scroll","forsaken rogues","rogue trainer","shadowstalkers","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:3096","npc:david-trias","subzone:deathknell","topic:rogue","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Encrypted Scroll","Encrypted Scroll (quest)"}}
E["item:gloom-weed"] = {t="item",n="Gloom Weed",z="tirisfal",s="Gloom Weed is a sickly plant native to the blighted soil of Tirisfal Glades, commonly growing in the fields between Deathknell and Brill. It is best known as the wrong plant collected by Gordo, a lumbering Forsaken abomination who confuses it with the alchemy reagent doom weed.",h="Gloom Weed is a sickly plant native to the blighted soil of Tirisfal Glades, commonly growing in the fields...",sec={{t="A Mistaken Harvest",b="Sprouting amid the gloom and decay of Tirisfal Glades, Gloom Weed thrives in the fallow earth west of Brill and east of the Solliden Farmstead. Despite its morbid name, the plant is relatively harmless and lacks the potent alchemical kick prized by the Royal Apothecary Society. Its primary notoriety stems from the abomination Gordo, who wanders the roadway attempting to pull weeds with his massive, clumsy hands. Having misunderstood his master's commands, Gordo constantly gathers Gloom Weed and enlists gullible travelers to carry bunches of it back to Master Holland in the Brill graveyard, much to the apothecary's supreme annoyance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Gloom Weed used for?",a="Practically nothing of value to Forsaken alchemists. It is gathered purely because the abomination Gordo mistakes it for the herb doom weed, which his master actually requested.",al={"gloom weed use","what does gloom weed do","who needs gloom weed"},it="purpose"},{q="Where does Gloom Weed grow?",a="It grows scattered across the fields of Tirisfal Glades between Brill and Deathknell, particularly along the road and in the meadows east of the Solliden Farmstead.",al={"where to find gloom weed","gloom weed location","gloom weed spots"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Gordo and why is he picking it?",a="Gordo is an abomination sent out by Master Holland to harvest reagents. Because his stitched hands are too clumsy and his mind is simple, he wanders the roads pulling the wrong plants and begging passersby to assist him.",al={"who is gordo","why is gordo picking weeds","gordo abomination"},it="motive"},{q="What did Master Holland actually want?",a="Master Holland needs doom weed for his experiments in the Brill graveyard. When handed Gloom Weed, he scoffs at the mistake and redirects travelers to gather what he truly required.",al={"master holland weed","what does holland need","doom weed vs gloom weed"}},{q="What is Gloom Weed?",a="Gloom Weed is an ordinary, sickly-looking wild plant that sprouts in the cursed soil of Tirisfal Glades, primarily found along the roadways and open fields between Brill and Deathknell. It holds no magical potency or valuable alchemical properties. In truth, it is little more than a common roadside weed—the Tirisfal equivalent of a daisy—that happens to flourish despite the blighted conditions of the countryside.",al={"what is gloom weed","gloom weed lore","gloom weed plant"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want Gloom Weed?",a="Virtually no one actually wants Gloom Weed. The only reason anyone gathers it is because Gordo, a dim-witted abomination wandering the fields near Brill, completely misunderstood his instructions. His master, Junior Apothecary Holland, sent him out to harvest valuable doom weed for Forsaken experiments, but Gordo's lumbering mind confused the names and sent unwitting travelers to pick worthless gloom weed instead.",al={"who wants gloom weed","why gather gloom weed","why collect gloom weed"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Trained herbalists can sense the plant through Find Herbs, making it much easier to spot against Tirisfal's shadowy undergrowth.",["race:Undead"]="For new Forsaken leaving Deathknell, fetching Gloom Weed is a swift lesson in the shortcomings of abomination labor and the caustic temperaments of Undercity alchemists."},kw={"abomination","brill","doom weed","gloom weed","gordo","master holland","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:5481","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","subzone:deathknell","subzone:solliden-farmstead","topic:horde"},src={"Gloom Weed","Gordo's Task (Classic)"}}
E["item:glyphic-scroll"] = {t="item",n="Glyphic Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Glyphic Scroll is a summons delivered to newly raised Forsaken magi in Deathknell. Bearing the seal of Isabella, the local arcane trainer, it calls fledgling spellcasters to the desecrated church to refine their craft in undeath.",h="The Glyphic Scroll is a summons delivered to newly raised Forsaken magi in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Summons from the Church",b="When a freshly awakened Forsaken mage returns from culling mindless Scourge in the graveyard of Deathknell, this sealed missive awaits them. Written under the mark of Isabella, the settlement's resident mage trainer, the scroll calls the recipient into the ruined parish sanctuary. For newly risen magi, it serves as an initial orientation into the discipline of the arcane under the Forsaken banner—reminding them that while death may have unburdened them of mortal weakness, the corrupting temptations of magical power remain as persistent as ever.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Glyphic Scroll?",a="It is an introductory dispatch given to newly raised Forsaken magi in Deathknell. Bearing the personal seal of the arcane trainer Isabella, it directs nascent spellcasters to report to her inside the town church to begin their magical instruction.",al={"what is this item","glyphic scroll use","what does scroll do"},it="identity"},{q="Who sends the Glyphic Scroll?",a="The scroll is delivered on behalf of Isabella, the mage trainer stationed within the ruined church in Deathknell. She reaches out to any newly raised undead possessing an aptitude for the arcane.",al={"who wrote it","who gave me this","scroll sender"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Isabella want to train new Forsaken mages?",a="Isabella seeks kindred spirits who share the pain of dying and the desire to reclaim their stolen homeland and destinies. She trains them to cultivate enough arcane power to destroy anyone opposing the Forsaken, while cautioning them not to succumb to magical addiction and corruption.",al={"isabella motive","why train mages"},it="why_wanted"}},ang={["class:Mage"]="The scroll is the first formal summons a Forsaken mage receives, emphasizing that undeath does not free a spellcaster from the corrupting addictions of arcane magic.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaimed arcane knowledge is not merely scholarly study—it is an essential weapon to secure their sovereignty against both the Scourge and living enemies."},kw={"arcane","deathknell","glyphic scroll","isabella","mage","scroll","tirisfal"},rel={"quest:3098","subzone:deathknell","topic:mage","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Glyphic Scroll","Glyphic Scroll (quest)"}}
E["item:gregor-s-remains"] = {t="item",n="Gregor's Remains",z="tirisfal",s="Gregor's Remains are the physical bones recovered from the Scourge-enslaved Gregor Agamand. Once part of the wealthiest dynasty in Tirisfal Glades, his remains are sought by a former mill worker eager to settle an old score from beyond the grave.",h="Gregor's Remains are the physical bones recovered from the Scourge-enslaved Gregor Agamand.",sec={{t="A Legacy of Ruin",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family held immense wealth and vast tracts of land across northern Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge invaded Lordaeron, the patriarchs chose pride over flight, fortifying their family mills and persuading their loyal laborers to make a desperate stand against the undead. The defense failed entirely. The estate was overrun, and those who stood their ground were butchered and raised into undeath. Gregor Agamand became another mindless thrall in the service of the Lich King, haunting the very mills his family once ruled.",sp=0},{t="The Price of Vengeance",b="For survivors who broke free from the Lich King's grip to join the Forsaken, the memory of that fateful defense remains a bitter wound. Coleman Farthing, a former mill worker stationed in Brill, blames the hubris of the Agamands for trapping his soul in eternal rot. Collecting Gregor's remains offers Farthing a measure of personal retribution, closing the chapter on a master who led his household straight into damnation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Gregor Agamand?",a="Gregor Agamand was a prominent member of the Agamand dynasty, the wealthiest land-owning family in Tirisfal Glades before the Third War. When the Plague of Undeath arrived, he and his family chose to fortify their estate rather than flee, leading to the slaughter and reanimation of everyone inside.",al={"gregor lore","who is gregor","gregor agamand"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want Gregor's Remains?",a="Coleman Farthing was a mill worker employed by the Agamands in life. He deeply resents the family for convincing their workers to stay and fight the Scourge, a blunder that condemned them all to undeath. Having Gregor's remains brought to him serves as long-delayed vengeance.",al={"why coleman wants remains","coleman farthing grudge","why collect gregor bones"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where are Gregor's Remains found?",a="The remains are recovered from the risen form of Gregor Agamand at the Agamand Mills, located in the northern hills of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to find gregor","gregor location","agamand mills gregor"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Are the Agamands part of the Forsaken?",a="No. Unlike the Forsaken who broke free under Queen Sylvanas, Gregor Agamand and his kin remain mindless servants chained to the will of the Scourge.",al={"is gregor forsaken","are agamands scourge","who controls gregor"},it="side"},{q="What are Gregor's Remains?",a="They are the physical remnants of Gregor Agamand, one of the heirs of the prominent Agamand dynasty. After falling to the Plague of Undeath, Gregor rose as a mindless minion of the Scourge, haunting the ruins of his family's once-prosperous mills until put down permanently.",al={"what are gregors remains","gregors remains item","what is gregors remains"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Gregor's Remains come from?",a="These remains are what is left of Gregor Agamand after he is struck down at the Agamand Mills in western Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, his family fortified their estate and urged their workers to stand and fight, only for all of them to be slaughtered and raised into undeath.",al={"gregors remains origin","who made gregors remains","where did gregors remains come from"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Agamand tragedy mirrors the fate of many Forsaken: ordinary folk betrayed by the pride of nobility and abandoned to the horrors of the Plague.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing the Agamand Mills strikes a direct blow against lingering Scourge pockets threatening the security of Tirisfal Glades."},kw={"agamand","brill","coleman","deaths in the family","farthing","gregor","mills","remains","scourge"},rel={"quest:354","npc:coleman-farthing","item:thurman-s-remains","item:nissa-s-remains","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},src={"Gregor's Remains","Deaths in the Family (Classic)"}}
E["item:gunther-s-spellbook"] = {t="item",n="Gunther's Spellbook",z="tirisfal",s="Gunther's Spellbook is a personal arcane tome belonging to Gunther Arcanus, a former Kirin Tor wizard dwelling in Tirisfal Glades. Imbued with an enchantment by Bethor Iceshard, the book serves as proof to Gunther that other mages have successfully broken free of the Lich King's control.",h="Gunther's Spellbook is a personal arcane tome belonging to Gunther Arcanus, a former Kirin Tor wizard...",sec={{t="A Token of Old Kinship",b="Before the Third War, Gunther Arcanus studied the arcane arts in Dalaran alongside Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw as members of the Kirin Tor. When the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron, Gunther fell and was raised into undeath, yet eventually managed to wrest his own mind free of the Scourge. Believing himself isolated and perpetually hounded by the Lich King's agents, he remained deeply hostile to outsiders. After the tome was recovered, Bethor Iceshard layered an arcane enchantment over its pages—a deliberate magical signature that allows Gunther to recognize Bethor's presence and realize he is not the only mage to possess free will.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Whose spellbook is this?",a="It belongs to Gunther Arcanus, a powerful undead mage residing on an isolated island on Brightwater Lake in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"who owns the book","gunther book owner","spellbook owner"},it="identity"},{q="Why did Bethor enchant the spellbook?",a="Bethor placed a personal spell on the tome to serve as unmistakable proof that he survives in Lordaeron and is free from the Lich King's control, hoping to convince Gunther to ally with the Forsaken.",al={"why enchant the book","bethor spell on book","book magic"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why is Gunther suspicious when the book is returned?",a="Gunther assumes anyone approaching his retreat is an agent of the Scourge sent to re-enslave him, and initially believes the adventurer was merely the thief who took his tome.",al={"why is gunther mad","gunther angry","gunther hostile"}},{q="How did Gunther and Bethor know each other?",a="Long ago, Gunther, Bethor Iceshard, and Thule Ravenclaw studied magic together as friends and Kirin Tor apprentices in the city of Dalaran before the Plague took them.",al={"bethor and gunther past","kirin tor connection","dalaran friends"},it="origin"}},ang={["class:Mage"]="For practitioners of the arcane, the tome represents the shared Dalaran roots connecting the Forsaken's current magical leadership to rogue practitioners in the wilderness.",["race:Undead"]="The enchanted book highlights the Forsaken's ongoing effort to recruit powerful independent undead who broke free of the Lich King on their own."},kw={"arcanus","bethor","dalaran","gunther","iceshard","kirin tor","spellbook","tirisfal"},rel={"quest:366","subzone:gunther-s-retreat","quest:446","subzone:dalaran","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:mage"},src={"Gunther's Spellbook","Return the Book"}}
E["item:hallowed-scroll"] = {t="item",n="Hallowed Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Hallowed Scroll is a formal summons delivered to newly awakened Forsaken priests in Deathknell. Bearing the personal seal of Dark Cleric Duesten, it directs fledglings to the ruined chapel to begin their re-education in faith, willpower, and shadow.",h="The Hallowed Scroll is a formal summons delivered to newly awakened Forsaken priests in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Faithless",b="Newly risen Forsaken priests find that their connection to the world has violently shifted. Delivered amidst the ongoing chaos of Deathknell's reclamation, the Hallowed Scroll directs nascent spellcasters to seek guidance within the settlement's ruined chapel. Marked with the seal of Dark Cleric Duesten, the parchment serves as both an introduction and an order to report for formal instruction, initiating the priest into the pragmatic philosophy that defines Forsaken priesthood.",sp=0},{t="The Path of Self-Reliance",b="Upon answering the scroll's summons, priests are immediately disabused of old dogmas. Duesten makes it clear that traditional crutches—the veneration of the Holy Light, ancestral spirits, or demonic pacts—no longer serve the undead. Instead, the scroll marks the start of an inward discipline rooted strictly in individual willpower. The teachings initiated by this parchment emphasize surviving and ruling through sheer conviction, turning religious devotion into an instrument of self-determination and mastery.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Hallowed Scroll?",a="The Hallowed Scroll is a written message delivered to newly risen Forsaken priests in Deathknell. It instructs the reader to report to Dark Cleric Duesten inside the local chapel to begin their formal priesthood training.",al={"what is this scroll","hallowed scroll purpose","item hallowed scroll"},it="identity"},{q="Who sent the Hallowed Scroll?",a="The scroll bears the personal seal of Dark Cleric Duesten, the priest trainer stationed in the abandoned church of Deathknell.",al={"who wrote hallowed scroll","who sent the scroll","duesten scroll"},it="origin"},{q="What does Dark Cleric Duesten teach when you bring him the scroll?",a="Duesten teaches that undead priests must discard traditional reliance on the Holy Light, ancestral spirits, or otherworldly powers. He preaches that the Forsaken survived through sheer personal willpower alone and instructs young priests to cultivate self-faith to guide and dominate others.",al={"what does duesten say","forsaken priest beliefs","deathknell priest lore"}},{q="Why does Dark Cleric Duesten want you to have the Hallowed Scroll?",a="Dark Cleric Duesten uses the sealed scroll to summon newly risen Forsaken priests to Deathknell's church. Rather than leaving you to cling to the forgotten Light or ancestral spirits, he intends to instruct you in the grim philosophy of the Forsaken priesthood—relying strictly on your own unyielding willpower to wield power and survive in a hostile world.",al={"why hallowed scroll","why duesten hallowed scroll","purpose of hallowed scroll"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="This parchment marks the definitive break from traditional human faith, orienting Forsaken priests toward self-willed conviction and the shadowy arts practiced by the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow.",["race:Undead"]="Receiving this summons reflects the urgent, pragmatic mobilization of Deathknell, ensuring newly awakened undead are quickly trained and integrated into Sylvanas's forces."},kw={"deathknell","duesten","forsaken","hallowed scroll","light","priest","tirisfal"},rel={"quest:3097","subzone:deathknell","topic:priest","zone:tirisfal","topic:light","topic:scourge"},src={"Hallowed Scroll","Hallowed Scroll (quest)"}}
E["item:johaan-s-special-drink"] = {t="item",n="Johaan's Special Drink",z="tirisfal",s="Johaan's Special Drink is a lethal concoction prepared by Apothecary Johaan in the village of Brill. Disguised as a common refreshment, the mixture serves as a field test for the Royal Apothecary Society's developing plague.",h="Johaan's Special Drink is a lethal concoction prepared by Apothecary Johaan in the village of Brill.",sec={{t="A Toxic Brew",b="Brewed in the brewing cauldrons of Brill, Johaan's Special Drink is far from a tavern beverage. Apothecary Johaan concocts the draft by refining various venomous components and local reagents into a single draught, tailored specifically to test virulence against living physiology. Handed over under the guise of slaking a captive's thirst, it represents the Forsaken apothecaries' ongoing ambition to engineer a weapon potent enough to fulfill Sylvanas Windrunner's designs for Azeroth.",sp=0},{t="The Cellar Trial",b="The drink finds its intended recipient in the dingy cellar beneath the Gallows' End Tavern, which the Deathguard uses as a temporary holding cell for stray intruders. When offered to a captured dwarven mountaineer languishing in chains, the thirsty prisoner drinks it eagerly, mistaking it for crude ale. The draft acts instantly, killing the dwarf on the spot and proving to the Royal Apothecary Society that their latest strain can swiftly extinguish living victims.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Johaan's Special Drink?",a="It is a deadly concoction created by Apothecary Johaan in Brill. Though presented as a beverage, it is infused with a strain of the Forsaken's developing plague designed to test its lethal efficacy against living beings.",al={"what is johaans drink","johaan drink","what is this item"},it="identity"},{q="Who is the drink given to?",a="The drink is brought down to the cellar of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill and given to a Captured Mountaineer who was taken prisoner by the local Deathguard.",al={"who drinks it","who do i give the drink to","drink target"},it="target"},{q="What happens when someone drinks it?",a="The prisoner downs the liquid expecting harsh tavern swill to quench his thirst, only to immediately drop dead from the brew's virulent toxins.",al={"does the dwarf die","effect of the drink","what does it do"},sp=1},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan make this drink?",a="Apothecary Johaan is conducting trials for the Royal Apothecary Society's new plague. He uses the captured dwarf as an unwitting live test subject to see how effective the mixture is at snuffing out mortal life.",al={"why did johaan make it","apothecary johaan motive","why test the plague"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where does Johaan's Special Drink come from, and who made it?",a="The concoction is brewed directly by Apothecary Johaan at Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Working on behalf of the Royal Apothecary Society and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, Johaan refines experimental toxins gathered from local creatures to craft this potent, lethal mixture.",al={"who made johaans special drink","where did johaans special drink come from","who brewed the drink"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, delivering Johaan's brew represents practical service to the Royal Apothecary Society and Lady Sylvanas's grand plans against the living.",["race:Dwarf"]="The concoction reveals the grim fate awaiting dwarven scouts who stray too close to Tirisfal Glades, where captives are used as laboratory test subjects."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","captured mountaineer","gallows' end tavern","johaan","johaan's special drink","new plague"},rel={"quest:367","npc:captured-mountaineer","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Johaan's Special Drink","A New Plague (4)"}}
E["item:laced-pumpkin"] = {t="item",n="Laced Pumpkin",z="tirisfal",s="A Laced Pumpkin is an ordinary gourd treated with a lethal concoction developed by Apothecary Johaan in Brill. Handed to a starving, imprisoned Scarlet Crusader under the guise of sustenance, it serves as a live field test for the Forsaken's growing arsenal of alchemical weapons.",h="A Laced Pumpkin is an ordinary gourd treated with a lethal concoction developed by Apothecary Johaan in Brill.",sec={{t="A Bitter Harvest",b="To the untrained eye, the laced pumpkin appears to be ordinary produce salvaged from the blighted soil of Tirisfal Glades. Under the supervision of Apothecary Johaan in Brill, however, the vegetable is treated with experimental toxins designed to realize the full potential of undeath. The Royal Apothecary Society constantly seeks new weapons to repel both the Scourge in the north and living human forces advancing from the south, turning even simple harvest goods into vectors for infection and chemical death.",sp=0},{t="The Cellar Experiment",b="The laced pumpkin's primary purpose is practical testing on living subjects. In the damp cellar of the Gallows' End Tavern, Forsaken captors hold a famished Captured Scarlet Zealot. Desperate for nourishment, the prisoner readily accepts the tainted offering in relief. Upon consuming the pumpkin, the apothecary's concoction triggers a violent transformation, twisting the human zealot into a mindless ghoul who stumbles briefly in agony before collapsing dead on the cold stone floor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Laced Pumpkin?",a="It is a field pumpkin injected with an experimental formula concocted by Apothecary Johaan in Brill, intended to test the effects of Forsaken alchemy on living subjects.",al={"laced pumpkin","poison pumpkin","johaan pumpkin"},it="identity"},{q="Who eats the Laced Pumpkin?",a="A Captured Scarlet Zealot imprisoned in the cellar beneath the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill consumes it after being starved by his captors.",al={"who eats it","pumpkin target","feed the pumpkin"},it="target"},{q="What happens when someone eats the pumpkin?",a="The tainted flesh instantly mutates the living zealot into a ghoul, which wanders erratically for a few moments before perishing entirely.",al={"pumpkin effect","what happens to zealot","zealot ghoul"},sp=1,it="power"},{q="Why did the Forsaken make this?",a="Apothecary Johaan and the Royal Apothecary Society are testing new biological concoctions to combat both the northern Scourge and encroaching living humans, using captured enemies as test subjects.",al={"why make poisoned pumpkin","forsaken plague testing"},it="purpose"},{q="Where does the Laced Pumpkin come from, and who made it?",a="Apothecary Johaan prepared this pumpkin in Brill. Seeking weapons to defend the Forsaken against the Scourge to the north and the living humans to the south, he harvested ordinary produce from the Solliden Farmstead and treated it with his experimental blight concoction.",al={"who made laced pumpkin","where did laced pumpkin come from","creator of laced pumpkin"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want the Laced Pumpkin?",a="Apothecary Johaan needs the pumpkin fed to a living subject to evaluate his latest chemical formula. It is offered to a starving Scarlet Crusade prisoner locked in the cellar beneath the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, serving as a lethal trial for the Royal Apothecary Society's research.",al={"why want laced pumpkin","why use the laced pumpkin","what is the laced pumpkin needed for"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, turning ordinary food into a weapon demonstrates the pragmatism of the Royal Apothecary Society in using any means necessary to secure Tirisfal against zealous living crusaders.",["faction:Alliance"]="The fate of the captive zealot serves as a stark, horrifying reminder of the grotesque fates awaiting any living human captured alive in Forsaken territory."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","captured scarlet zealot","gallows' end","laced pumpkin","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:captured-scarlet-zealot","quest:365","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal","topic:human","topic:light"},src={"Laced Pumpkin","Fields of Grief (2)"}}
E["item:maggot-eye-s-paw"] = {t="item",n="Maggot Eye's Paw",z="tirisfal",s="Maggot Eye's Paw is the gruesome trophy taken from the gnoll chieftain Maggot Eye at Garren's Haunt. Carried back to Brill, it serves as physical proof of the beast's execution for grave-robbing on behalf of the Scourge.",h="Maggot Eye's Paw is the gruesome trophy taken from the gnoll chieftain Maggot Eye at Garren's Haunt.",sec={{t="Proof of Execution",b="Maggot Eye earned the harsh wrath of the Forsaken by raiding graves and pillaging the countryside near Garren's Haunt, providing corpses to bolster the ranks of the Lich King's armies. In response, a formal death sentence and bounty were posted in the town of Brill. Because mere rumors of a death are never enough for military administrators like Executor Zygand, bounty hunters must sever the villain's paw and bring it back to town to collect their reward and close the bounty.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Executor Zygand want Maggot Eye's paw?",a="Executor Zygand demands undeniable proof that the condemned gnoll has truly been put down. A severed paw serves as physical confirmation of the execution before Brill will pay out the bounty.",al={"why paw","maggot eye bounty proof","zygand paw"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What crimes did Maggot Eye commit against the Forsaken?",a="Maggot Eye led pillaging raids across the northern reaches of Tirisfal Glades, specifically stealing buried corpses from farmsteads like Garren's Haunt to supply the armies of the Lich King.",al={"maggot eye crimes","why is maggot eye wanted","what did maggot eye do"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is Maggot Eye found?",a="Maggot Eye resides at Garren's Haunt, a ruined farmstead located northeast of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, usually lurking inside the dilapidated farmhouse.",al={"maggot eye location","where to find maggot eye","garrens haunt gnoll"},it="target",gp=true},{q="Why are gnolls working with the Lich King's forces?",a="While gnolls are opportunistic scavengers by nature, rogue bands in Tirisfal Glades have allied with or fallen under the sway of the Scourge, raiding human and Forsaken graves to deliver bodies to necromancers.",al={"gnolls and scourge","why do gnolls steal bodies","scourge gnolls"},it="allies"},{q="What is Maggot Eye's Paw?",a="It is the severed paw of the notorious gnoll Maggot Eye. Taking it serves as undeniable, physical proof of the beast's execution for the authorities in Brill.",al={"what is maggot eyes paw","maggot eye paw","what is this paw"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does Maggot Eye's Paw come from, and who made it?",a="It is not a crafted artifact, but a trophy carved straight from the corpse of Maggot Eye himself, a treacherous gnoll operating around Garren's Haunt in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where did maggot eye paw come from","maggot eye paw origin","who made maggot eyes paw"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken of Brill, bringing back Maggot Eye's paw is a direct strike against body thieves supplying the hated Lich King with raw material."},kw={"bounty","brill","executor zygand","garren's haunt","gnoll","maggot eye","maggot eye's paw","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:398","subzone:garren-s-haunt","npc:executor-zygand","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:gnoll","topic:lich-king","quest:358"},src={"Maggot Eye's Paw","Wanted: Maggot Eye"}}
E["item:nissa-s-remains"] = {t="item",n="Nissa's Remains",z="tirisfal",s="Nissa's Remains are the recovered earthly remnants of Nissa Agamand, a noblewoman of the once-wealthy Agamand dynasty in northern Tirisfal Glades. Slain and raised as mindless servants of the Scourge during the Third War, she and her family are now hunted down by former laborers seeking vengeance.",h="Nissa's Remains are the recovered earthly remnants of Nissa Agamand, a noblewoman of the once-wealthy Agamand...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Pride",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family stood as the wealthiest and most influential dynasty in Tirisfal Glades, operating prosperous mills across the northern hills. When the Plague of Undeath crept across Lordaeron, the family patriarchs stubbornly refused to evacuate. Instead, they fortified their estates and convinced their retainers and laborers to stand their ground. That arrogance cost everyone their lives. The Agamands fell to the Scourge, rising not with their proud independence intact, but as shambling, mindless thralls bound to the Lich King's will.",sp=0},{t="Bitter Retribution",b="In the town of Brill, survivors who later broke free under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner have not forgotten the family's fatal pride. Coleman Farthing, a former laborer in the Agamand mills, holds a bitter grudge against his former masters for luring their workers into the jaws of undeath. He charges adventurers with putting down the risen Agamands and retrieving their physical remains, such as Nissa's. For Farthing and others among the Forsaken, burning or destroying these relics offers a grim sense of final closure and spiteful revenge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Nissa Agamand?",a="Nissa was a member of the Agamand family, Lordaeron nobles who owned the sprawling Agamand Mills in Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, she perished alongside her kin and was resurrected as an undead minion under the Lich King's control.",al={"who is nissa","nissa lore","nissa agamand"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want Nissa's remains?",a="Coleman Farthing worked in the Agamand mills in life. He bitterly resents the family because their arrogant refusal to flee persuaded loyal workers to stay behind and die. Collecting their remains allows him to take revenge on his former masters.",al={"why coleman want remains","why kill agamands","coleman farthing motive"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where are these remains gathered?",a="Nissa Agamand haunts the ruined Agamand Mills in the northern reaches of Tirisfal Glades, wandering among the decrepit farmhouses alongside her reanimated relatives and mindless Scourge thralls.",al={"where do i find nissa","nissa agamand location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Did Nissa retain her free will like the Forsaken?",a="No. Unlike the Forsaken who broke away from the Lich King, Nissa and her family remained enslaved to the Scourge as mindless undead, lacking the sentience and freedom found in Undercity's citizens.",al={"is nissa forsaken","did nissa have free will","is nissa scourge"}},{q="What are Nissa's Remains?",a="These are the physical remnants of Nissa Agamand, a member of the wealthy Agamand dynasty that once owned the mills in northern Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, the family fortified their estate and convinced their workers to stand fast. Like the rest of her kin, Nissa fell to the Plague and rose as a mindless thrall in the service of the Lich King.",al={"what is nissas remains","nissa remains","who do nissas remains belong to"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Nissa's Remains come from, and who made them?",a="They are recovered directly from the destroyed corpse of Nissa Agamand at the Agamand Mills. Once a proud aristocrat, she succumbed to the Plague of Undeath and lingered on her ancestral lands as an undead creature. The remains are what you salvage after laying her mindless Scourge form to rest.",al={"where do you get nissas remains","how to get nissas remains","who dropped nissas remains"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaiming Tirisfal means purging both their former Scourge oppressors and the wealthy aristocrats who doomed common laborers to undeath through foolish pride.",["faction:Horde"]="Slaying the reanimated Agamands and clearing their mills secures northern Tirisfal Glades from lingering Scourge pockets threatening Brill and the Undercity."},kw={"agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","deaths in the family","nissa agamand","nissa's remains","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:354","npc:coleman-farthing","item:gregor-s-remains","item:thurman-s-remains","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},src={"Nissa's Remains","Deaths in the Family (Classic)"}}
E["item:nondescript-letter"] = {t="item",n="Nondescript Letter",z="tirisfal",s="The Nondescript Letter is an unsealed, unmarked missive entrusted to newly awakened Forsaken leaving Deathknell. Handed over by Calvin Montague under the guise of an errand, it directs the bearer down the road to Innkeeper Renee at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill.",h="The Nondescript Letter is an unsealed, unmarked missive entrusted to newly awakened Forsaken leaving...",sec={{t="A Courier's Errand",b="Newly arisen Forsaken looking to venture beyond Deathknell are approached by Calvin Montague, a shifty rogue lingering near the village perimeter. Claiming to be weary from fighting mindless zombies and arachnids, Calvin asks travelers to carry a plain, unmarked envelope along the eastward road to Brill. Addressed casually to an innkeeper named Renee, the letter serves as an excuse to push travelers out of the valley and toward the Gallows' End Tavern, though Calvin's promises of rich compensation ring hollow from the start.",sp=0},{t="Suspicious Correspondence",b="Upon delivery in Brill, Innkeeper Renee initially attempts an exaggerated, cheerful charade, claiming the letter must be sweet news from her saintly mother in Deathknell. When she realizes the courier sees through her awkward acting, her pleasant demeanor vanishes into sharp irritation. She snatches the letter, refuses to reveal who actually sent it or what it contains, and brusquely orders the bearer away unless they intend to pay for a room.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Nondescript Letter?",a="It is an unadorned, ordinary-looking envelope given to fresh Forsaken adventurers by Calvin Montague in Deathknell, meant for delivery to Innkeeper Renee in Brill.",al={"nondescript letter","what is this letter","calvin's letter"},it="identity"},{q="Who sends the letter, and who receives it?",a="Calvin Montague, a rogue stationed in Deathknell, gives the letter to couriers. It is addressed to Innkeeper Renee at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill.",al={"who sent the letter","who gets the letter","letter sender"},it="origin"},{q="What does the letter actually say?",a="The specific contents of the letter are not revealed to the player. When questioned, Innkeeper Renee drops her cheerful facade and angrily tells you that the letter's origins and contents are none of your business.",al={"what does the letter say","letter contents","can you read the letter"},it="item"},{q="Why does Calvin Montague want it delivered?",a="Calvin claims he is too tied up fighting local zombies and spiders to run the errand himself. In practice, the delivery directs travelers out of Deathknell and introduces them to the amenities and resting spots available in Brill.",al={"why send the letter","calvin's motive","why brill"},it="why_wanted"}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Calvin Montague's shifty posture and Renee's abrupt change in demeanor carry all the hallmarks of low-level rogue intrigue and coded underworld communication.",["race:Undead"]="For newly risen Forsaken, carrying this letter is one of the earliest incentives to leave the confines of Deathknell and explore the broader settlements of Tirisfal Glades."},kw={"a rogue's deal","brill","calvin montague","deathknell","innkeeper renee","nondescript letter","renee"},rel={"quest:8","npc:calvin-montague","subzone:deathknell","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:rogue","topic:forsaken","quest:5481"},src={"Nondescript Letter","A Rogue's Deal"}}
E["item:notched-rib"] = {t="item",n="Notched Rib",z="tirisfal",s="A Notched Rib is a bone trophy harvested from the ranks of mindless undead wandering Tirisfal Glades. Deathguard Dillinger in Brill collects these scarred remains as proof of efforts to break the Scourge's foothold at the Agamand Mills.",h="A Notched Rib is a bone trophy harvested from the ranks of mindless undead wandering Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Grim Proof of Battle",b="The Scourge forces massing across the northern fields of Tirisfal Glades consist of reanimated skeletons clad in ruined armor. Among their ranks, Cracked Skull Soldiers and Rattlecage Soldiers throw themselves against any living or free-willed being they encounter. Slicing through their brittle frames leaves their bone structure pitted and scored. To ensure that Forsaken scouts and volunteers actually cull these mindless intruders rather than merely observing them, the Deathguards demand tangible evidence. A notched, battle-marked rib serves as unmistakable proof that another of the Lich King's thralls has been shattered.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Mills",b="The Agamand Mills represent a critical strategic position northwest of Brill. Should the Scourge firmly entrench themselves among the ruined farmsteads and windmills, they would possess a direct staging ground to launch assaults deep into the heart of Tirisfal. Deathguard Dillinger monitors the roads leading north, dispatching champions to crush the skeleton cohorts before they can consolidate. Piles of notched ribs returned to Brill demonstrate that the Forsaken lines are holding, slowly grinding down the Scourge vanguard bone by bone.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Notched Rib?",a="It is a scarred skeletal bone taken from the animated Scourge soldiers occupying northern Tirisfal Glades. It is brought to Deathguard Dillinger as physical confirmation of destroyed enemies.",al={"notched rib","what is notched rib","notched rib item"},it="identity"},{q="Where do Notched Ribs come from?",a="They are recovered from Cracked Skull Soldiers and Rattlecage Soldiers found patrolling the grounds of the Agamand Mills, northwest of Brill.",al={"where to find notched rib","who drops notched ribs","notched rib source"},it="origin"},{q="Who wants these bones?",a="Deathguard Dillinger stationed along the road just outside Brill collects them, alongside Blackened Skulls, to confirm the Scourge advance has been repelled.",al={"who takes notched ribs","notched rib turn in","who needs notched rib"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why is the Scourge gathering at Agamand Mills?",a="The mindless undead are attempting to establish a staging base at the mills. From that vantage, Scourge forces could easily launch strikes across Tirisfal Glades and threaten Forsaken settlements.",al={"scourge at agamand mills","why are skeletons at the mills","agamand mills scourge threat"},it="threat"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, collecting these shattered bones is part of an ongoing war for survival against the mindless minions of the Lich King who would drag them back into servitude."},kw={"agamand mills","brill","cracked skull soldier","deathguard dillinger","notched rib","rattlecage soldier","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:426","item:blackened-skull","npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:agamand-mills","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Notched Rib","The Mills Overrun (Classic)"}}
E["item:putrid-claw"] = {t="item",n="Putrid Claw",z="tirisfal",s="Putrid Claws are the decaying hands severed from mindless undead prowling the woods west of Brill. Deathguard Dillinger collects them as definitive proof that the encroaching Scourge threat near the old bridge is being held at bay.",h="Putrid Claws are the decaying hands severed from mindless undead prowling the woods west of Brill.",sec={{t="Trophy of the Mindless",b="Even in their own homeland, the Forsaken are not entirely safe from the Lich King's reach. Pockets of mindless Scourge—rotting dead and ravaged corpses—regularly wander across the eastern borders into Tirisfal Glades, clustering along roads and near the old bridge west of Brill. To ensure the safety of travelers and garrison supply lines, the Deathguards place bounties on these wandering horrors. A severed putrid claw serves as unmistakable, physical evidence that one more shambling corpse has been put down for good.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are putrid claws used for?",a="Deathguard Dillinger stationed in Brill collects them as bounties to confirm that local Forsaken recruits are thinning out the Scourge wandering Tirisfal Glades.",al={"putrid claw use","why collect putrid claws","who wants putrid claws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do putrid claws come from?",a="They are harvested from the rotting dead and ravaged corpses roaming the haunted woods west of Brill, particularly near the old river bridge.",al={"where to get putrid claws","putrid claw drops","rotting dead drops"},it="origin"},{q="Why is the Scourge in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Following the devastation of Lordaeron, remnants of the Lich King's mindless Scourge continuously trickle westward across the borders into Tirisfal, menacing roads and settlements unless actively culled.",al={"why are zombies near brill","scourge in tirisfal"},it="threat"},{q="Why don't the Forsaken just let the mindless undead wander?",a="Unlike the Forsaken, who broke free under Queen Sylvanas and regained their will, the mindless Scourge obey only dark instincts or the Lich King's lingering command, attacking any living or free undead on sight.",al={"forsaken vs mindless zombies","why kill other undead"},it="motive"},{q="What is a Putrid Claw?",a="A Putrid Claw is the severed, rotting hand of a mindless zombie belonging to the Scourge. These decaying appendages are taken from the walking corpses haunting Tirisfal Glades as proof of their destruction.",al={"what is putrid claw","putrid claws info","putrid claw lore"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For fresh Forsaken initiates leaving Deathknell, clearing these mindless Scourge is an early reminder that liberation comes with constant defense against the mindless husks they once resembled."},kw={"a putrid task","brill","deathguard dillinger","putrid claw","ravaged corpse","rotting dead","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:404","npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Putrid Claw","A Putrid Task (Classic)"}}
E["item:rattlecage-skull"] = {t="item",n="Rattlecage Skull",z="tirisfal",s="Rattlecage Skulls are the shattered remnants of mindless undead roaming the ruined outskirts of Deathknell. Novice Forsaken warlocks must gather these calcified trophies for Venya Marthand to demonstrate their emerging will and qualify for the dark arts of summoning.",h="Rattlecage Skulls are the shattered remnants of mindless undead roaming the ruined outskirts of Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Neophyte's Test",b="Within the sunless bounds of Deathknell, newly raised Forsaken with an affinity for dark magic are guided by Venya Marthand. To prove that a fledgling practitioner has the fortitude to bend extraplanar creatures to their command, Venya tasks them with subduing the lingering Rattlecage Skeletons near the ruined smithy. Striking down these lesser undead and retrieving their severed skulls serves as both a practical test of discipline and a ritual foundation before learning to breach the void and enslave a first demonic minion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Rattlecage Skulls used for?",a="They are collected by Venya Marthand in Deathknell. Forsaken warlocks must bring her three skulls to prove their focus and resolve before she instructs them in the rites of summoning and binding an imp.",al={"what are rattlecage skulls","rattlecage skull use","who wants rattlecage skulls"},it="purpose"},{q="Where do Rattlecage Skulls come from?",a="They are taken from the broken remains of Rattlecage Skeletons, mindless undead that wander the overgrown roads and crumbling grounds near Deathknell's abandoned smithy.",al={"where to get rattlecage skulls","who drops rattlecage skulls","rattlecage skull location"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Venya Marthand require skeletal skulls for a warlock trial?",a="Venya believes power eludes the ignorant and that mastery requires bending external forces to one's will. Slaying the mindless skeletons and claiming their heads demonstrates that an apprentice possesses the fundamental will to control chaotic energies rather than be consumed by them.",al={"why skulls for warlocks","venya marthand test"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What knowledge is granted after gathering these skulls?",a="Once the skulls are offered, Venya Marthand imparts the secret ritual needed to call forth and bind an imp, granting young warlocks their first true demonic minion.",al={"what do you get from rattlecage skulls","piercing the veil reward"},it="next_step"},{q="What is a Rattlecage Skull?",a="A Rattlecage Skull is the severed head of a Rattlecage Skeleton, one of the mindless undead wandering the blighted grounds near Deathknell's abandoned smithy. For aspiring Forsaken warlocks, these grim trophies serve as proof of basic magical resolve and focus, showing that an initiate can confront and overcome the walking dead.",al={"what is rattlecage skull","rattlecage skull lore"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="For Forsaken warlocks, gathering these skulls represents the initial milestone of their discipline, proving they have the cold will required to enslave their first demon.",["race:Undead"]="The act of shattering lesser, unthinking skeletons underscores the Forsaken belief in asserting dominion through sheer will, differentiating free-willed undead from mindless thralls."},kw={"deathknell","imp summoning","piercing the veil","rattlecage skeleton","rattlecage skull","tirisfal glades","venya marthand"},rel={"quest:1470","topic:warlock","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Rattlecage Skull","Piercing the Veil"}}
E["item:samuel-s-remains"] = {t="item",n="Samuel's Remains",z="tirisfal",s="Samuel's Remains are the recovered earthly remnants of Samuel Fipps, a former resident of Tirisfal Glades who fell to the Scourge. Gathered by adventurers near Deathknell, they are laid to rest beside his wife Marla to fulfill her dying wish.",h="Samuel's Remains are the recovered earthly remnants of Samuel Fipps, a former resident of Tirisfal Glades who...",sec={{t="A Tragic Reunion",b="Before the plague of undeath swept across Lordaeron, Samuel Fipps lived peacefully with his wife, Marla. When the contagion struck, Samuel succumbed and was bound into mindless service to the Scourge. Marla escaped undeath only to be struck down by the very husband she loved. Despite this brutal end, her final wish was not vengeance, but eternity together: she pleaded to be interred beside Samuel once his cursed form was put to rest. Novice Elreth in Deathknell enlists newly awoken Forsaken to track down Samuel's wandering form northeast of the settlement, recover his bones, and bury them in the front row of the local cemetery.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Samuel Fipps?",a="Samuel Fipps was a human living in Tirisfal Glades prior to the Third War. When the Scourge plague invaded, he perished and rose as an undead thrall of the Lich King. Driven by mindless hunger, he eventually slew his own wife, Marla.",al={"samuel fipps","who is samuel","samuel backstory"},it="identity"},{q="Where are Samuel's remains found?",a="They are recovered from Samuel Fipps himself, who roams among the ruined camp along the road northeast of Deathknell. Putting an end to his mindless existence allows his earthly remains to be retrieved.",al={"find samuel remains","where is samuel fipps","samuel location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the Forsaken bury him beside his wife?",a="Marla's dying wish was to rest alongside her beloved husband, despite him being the one who killed her. Novice Elreth honors this request to prove that the newly liberated Forsaken still possess compassion, respect for the dead, and humanity that separates them from the Scourge.",al={"why bury samuel","marla grave","marlas last wish"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do Samuel's Remains come from?",a="These are the mortal physical remains of Samuel Fipps, a former resident of Tirisfal Glades who fell to the plague of undeath and became a mindless pawn of the Scourge. After roaming the wilderness northeast of Deathknell, his undead body is struck down so his remnants can finally be gathered and laid to rest.",al={"samuels remains origin","where do samuels remains come from","who made samuels remains"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Burying Samuel's remains is an early lesson in Forsaken identity: respecting the tragic dead marks the crucial moral divide between free undead and the mindless thralls of the Scourge."},kw={"deathknell","marla fipps","marla's grave","marla's last wish","novice elreth","samuel fipps","samuel's remains"},rel={"quest:6395","npc:novice-elreth","subzone:deathknell","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Samuel's Remains","Marla's Last Wish"}}
E["item:scarlet-armband"] = {t="item",n="Scarlet Armband",z="tirisfal",s="Scarlet Armbands are insignia worn by members of the Scarlet Crusade, marking their fanatic allegiance to the eradication of all undead. In Deathknell, Executor Arren demands them as proof that fledgling Forsaken have struck back against the zealots encroaching on their territory.",h="Scarlet Armbands are insignia worn by members of the Scarlet Crusade, marking their fanatic allegiance to the...",sec={{t="A Badge of Fanaticism",b="Worn on the sleeves of Scarlet Initiates and fresh converts alike, the Scarlet Armband is the unmistakable mark of the Scarlet Crusade. To the newly risen Forsaken emerging from Deathknell, these strips of red cloth represent a lethal threat right on their doorstep. The Crusade makes no distinction between the mindless minions of the Scourge and the free-willed subjects of the Dark Lady; in their eyes, any walking corpse must be put to the torch. Executor Arren uses the collection of these armbands as a grim rite of passage, confirming the deaths of enemy scouts southeast of the settlement and proving that new Forsaken can defend their fledgling nation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Executor Arren want Scarlet Armbands?",a="Executor Arren requires the armbands as proof that the encroaching Scarlet Crusade encampment southeast of Deathknell is being culled. The Crusade poses an immediate existential threat to newly awakened Forsaken, and collecting these tokens ensures that their forward scouts and recruits have been eliminated.",al={"why collect armbands","arren armband quest","scarlet armband purpose"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Who wears these armbands?",a="The armbands are worn by lower-ranking members of the Scarlet Crusade stationed near Deathknell, specifically Scarlet Initiates, Scarlet Converts, and their local leader Meven Korgal.",al={"who drops scarlet armbands","where to find armbands"},it="people"},{q="Why does the Scarlet Crusade hate the Forsaken?",a="Driven by extreme zealotry, the Scarlet Crusade refuses to see any difference between the Lich King's mindless Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken. They view all undead as abominations that must be permanently eradicated.",al={"why scarlet attack undead","crusade vs forsaken","scarlet zealots"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is the camp where these armbands can be found?",a="The camp is situated just southeast of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades, nestled in the hills beyond the settlement's perimeter.",al={"scarlet camp location","deathknell scarlet camp","where are scarlet converts"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is a Scarlet Armband?",a="A Scarlet Armband is a piece of crimson cloth worn by initiates and converts of the Scarlet Crusade. It serves as an identifying insignia for the zealous human order, marking its wearers as pledged crusaders dedicated to eradicating all undead from Lordaeron, regardless of whether those undead serve the Scourge or possess free will like the Forsaken.",al={"what is scarlet armband","scarlet armband meaning","scarlet armband lore"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly raised Forsaken, gathering these armbands is a grim introduction to the living world's hatred, showing that survival demands ruthless defense against the Crusade."},kw={"deathknell","executor arren","scarlet armband","scarlet convert","scarlet crusade","scarlet initiate","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:381","npc:executor-arren","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","quest:382","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king"},src={"Scarlet Armband","The Scarlet Crusade (quest)"}}
E["item:scarlet-crusade-documents"] = {t="item",n="Scarlet Crusade Documents",z="tirisfal",s="These intercepted papers were carried by a Scarlet Crusade messenger dispatched from the Scarlet Monastery to the camps near Deathknell. Containing tactical maps and orders for field commanders across Tirisfal Glades, they provide the Forsaken with the exact military intelligence needed to target the Crusade's regional leadership.",h="These intercepted papers were carried by a Scarlet Crusade messenger dispatched from the Scarlet Monastery to...",sec={{t="Intercepted Orders",b="Dispatched straight from the Scarlet Monastery, the courier Meven Korgal carried these documents toward the Crusade's southern encampment outside Deathknell. While the papers contain ordinary scouting dispatches and standard orders urging zealots to fortify positions near the ruined Capital City, their true value lies in detailed strategic maps. The documents reveal the names, locations, and dispositions of Scarlet field officers stationed across Tirisfal Glades, turning an otherwise routine courier run into a catastrophic intelligence breach for the zealots.",sp=0},{t="Delivery to Brill",b="Executor Arren swiftly recognized that local forces in Deathknell could not exploit the full breadth of the intelligence. Instead, the captured dossier serves as a newly awakened Forsaken's passport out of the starter valley. Carried northeast past Cold Hearth Manor into Brill, the documents are handed directly to Executor Zygand outside the Brill Town Hall. With these maps in hand, the Forsaken military leadership plans swift decapitation strikes against Scarlet commanders entrenched across the Glades.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was carrying these documents?",a="They were carried by Meven Korgal, a Scarlet Crusade courier sent from the Scarlet Monastery down to the Crusade's encampment southeast of Deathknell.",al={"who dropped these","scarlet messenger","meven korgal"},it="target"},{q="What information is inside the documents?",a="The bundle includes scouting reports, orders to fortify positions near Lordaeron's ruined capital, and a detailed map laying out the names and locations of Scarlet Crusade field commanders across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what do the papers say","document contents"},it="power"},{q="Who needs to see these documents?",a="After being recovered for Executor Arren in Deathknell, they are entrusted to you for delivery to Executor Zygand, who oversees regional military efforts outside Brill Town Hall.",al={"where do i take them","who do i give the documents to"},it="next_step"},{q="Why are these documents important to the Forsaken?",a="The captured maps pinpoint Crusade officer deployments throughout Tirisfal Glades, giving the Forsaken the upper hand to eliminate enemy leadership and shatter the zealots' regional morale.",al={"why do forsaken want them","scarlet documents importance"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What are the Scarlet Crusade Documents?",a="They are a sheaf of confidential military papers, battle reports, and orders carried by a courier of the Scarlet Crusade. While much of the bundle consists of ordinary scouting accounts and propaganda about skirmishes with the Forsaken, it also contains detailed operational instructions intended for Crusade officers stationed throughout Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what are the documents","scarlet crusade documents info","scarlet documents item"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the Scarlet Crusade Documents come from?",a="These orders originated from high command within the Scarlet Monastery, the Crusade's primary fortress in eastern Tirisfal Glades. They were entrusted to a specialized courier named Meven Korgal, tasked with delivering them down to the forward encampments threatening Deathknell and the surrounding countryside.",al={"who wrote scarlet crusade documents","scarlet documents source","where did documents come from"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Carrying these intercepted papers marks an awakened Forsaken's graduation from the cemetery trials of Deathknell into the wider war effort directed from Brill."},kw={"brill","deathknell","executor arren","executor zygand","meven korgal","scarlet crusade documents","the red messenger","vital intelligence"},rel={"quest:382","quest:383","npc:executor-arren","npc:executor-zygand","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:deathknell","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Scarlet Crusade Documents","The Red Messenger","Vital Intelligence (Classic)"}}
E["item:scarlet-insignia-ring"] = {t="item",n="Scarlet Insignia Ring",z="tirisfal",s="A Scarlet Insignia Ring is a signet band worn by members of the Scarlet Crusade garrisoned throughout Tirisfal Glades. For the Forsaken defenders of Brill, these rings serve as gruesome proof that another zealot has been eliminated from Lordaeron's soil.",h="A Scarlet Insignia Ring is a signet band worn by members of the Scarlet Crusade garrisoned throughout...",sec={{t="Tokens of Zealotry",b="Every fighting member of the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades—from fresh neophytes and zealous friars to seasoned captains like Melrache and Perrine—carries an insignia ring. Marked with the Crusade's fiery sigil, the rings identify brother and sister within an organization consumed by paranoia. To outsiders and enemies, however, these trinkets serve entirely different purposes: they are unmistakable identifiers of Lordaeron's most fanatical human remnant.",sp=0},{t="Bounty of the Dreadlord",b="Under the direct mandate of Varimathras, Lady Sylvanas's dreadlord lieutenant, Deathguard Burgess in Brill actively collects these rings. The Forsaken consider the Scarlet Crusade an intolerable pestilence crawling across their newly claimed homeland. For Forsaken recruits, bringing stacks of these rings back to Brill is treated as undeniable proof of demise and a direct affirmation of loyalty to the throne of the Undercity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who wears these insignia rings?",a="Nearly any member of the Scarlet Crusade stationed in Tirisfal Glades wears one. This includes rank-and-file soldiers such as neophytes, missionaries, zealots, and vanguards, as well as field officers like Captain Perrine, Captain Melrache, and Captain Vachon.",al={"who drops rings","which crusaders have rings","ring owners"},it="people"},{q="Why does Deathguard Burgess want Scarlet Insignia Rings?",a="Deathguard Burgess collects the rings as physical proof that Scarlet Crusaders are being culled. Acting on decrees originating from Varimathras and the Dark Lady, the Forsaken garrison in Brill uses the rings to measure a recruit's loyalty and effectiveness in clearing humans from Lordaeron.",al={"why collect rings","proof of demise reason","burgess rings"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What does the insignia on the ring represent?",a="The ring bears the mark of the Scarlet Crusade, symbolizing their holy war to eradicate all undeath from Lordaeron. The Crusaders wear it with fierce pride, viewing it as a badge of righteousness, even as their obsession drives them to slaughter anyone not belonging to their order.",al={"scarlet symbol","what is the insignia","ring emblem"}},{q="What role does Varimathras have in collecting these rings?",a="Varimathras serves as Sylvanas Windrunner's chief lieutenant in the Undercity. He oversees strategic military efforts to wipe out hostile living humans in Tirisfal Glades, making the eradication of the Scarlet Crusade a top priority for Forsaken commanders like Executor Zygand and Deathguard Burgess.",al={"varimathras orders","why varimathras want rings","dreadlord involvement"},it="purpose"},{q="What is a Scarlet Insignia Ring?",a="A Scarlet Insignia Ring is an identifying band carried by members of the Scarlet Crusade across Tirisfal Glades. Bearing the militant order's emblem, it marks the wearer's allegiance to their fanatical crusade to eradicate undeath. For the Forsaken defending their new borders, these rings serve as indisputable proof that an enemy soldier or officer has been eliminated.",al={"what is scarlet insignia ring","scarlet insignia ring lore","what are these rings"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, tearing these rings from the hands of dead Crusaders is a direct declaration that Tirisfal belongs to the dead, fulfilling the orders of the Dark Lady and Varimathras.",["faction:Horde"]="Collecting these rings helps the fledgling Horde secure its foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms by weakening the fanatical crusaders surrounding the Undercity."},kw={"brill","deathguard burgess","executor zygand","proof of demise","scarlet crusade","scarlet insignia ring","tirisfal glades","varimathras"},rel={"quest:374","npc:deathguard-burgess","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal","npc:varimathras","npc:executor-zygand","quest:370","subzone:brill"},src={"Scarlet Insignia Ring","Proof of Demise (quest)"}}
E["item:scavenged-goods"] = {t="item",n="Scavenged Goods",z="tirisfal",s="Scavenged Goods are miscellaneous pieces of discarded gear and supplies packed into equipment boxes around Deathknell. Newly risen Forsaken recover them to ensure the endless stream of fresh recruits can be armed and clothed.",h="Scavenged Goods are miscellaneous pieces of discarded gear and supplies packed into equipment boxes around...",sec={{t="Fending for Themselves",b="When new Forsaken pull themselves out of the earth in Deathknell's graveyard, they awaken with little more than rotting burial shrouds. Surrounding settlements and kingdoms offer no aid to the undead, leaving them entirely dependent on what they can salvage. Deathguard Saltain coordinates the collection of discarded weapons, tools, and garments cached in supply crates across the village, distributing them directly to the next batch of risen recruits.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are scavenged goods used for?",a="Deathguard Saltain collects them to outfit fresh Forsaken recruits rising from the cemetery. Without salvaging remnants from nearby crates, newly risen undead would have to stumble out into Tirisfal Glades completely unarmed and unclad.",al={"scavenged goods purpose","why collect scavenged goods","what does saltain do with scavenged goods"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where are these goods found?",a="They are recovered from equipment boxes resting outside cottages and around ruined buildings across Deathknell.",al={"where to find scavenged goods","scavenged goods location","where are equipment boxes"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who asks for the scavenged goods?",a="Deathguard Saltain, an officer stationed in Deathknell, directs newly awakened Forsaken to round up these provisions to teach them self-reliance and prepare for incoming cohorts.",al={"who wants scavenged goods","deathguard saltain quest"},it="wants",gp=true},{q="What are scavenged goods?",a="These are assorted pieces of serviceable equipment, clothing, and gear salvaged from old crates left scattered around Deathknell. Rather than pristine armaments, they are discarded remnants of the town's past life that can still serve a practical purpose for newly awakened Forsaken.",al={"scavenged goods what are they","what is scavenged goods"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the scavenged goods come from?",a="They come from equipment boxes abandoned among the ruined structures of Deathknell. Once belonging to the living inhabitants of Lordaeron before the kingdom fell to the plague, these supplies remained forgotten in the ruins until the Forsaken began reclaiming the town as their staging ground.",al={"scavenged goods origin","who made scavenged goods","where did scavenged goods come from"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Saltain want scavenged goods?",a="The Forsaken are continually awakening newly risen dead from the graveyard, and these fresh recruits enter the world with little to their names. Deathguard Saltain gathers these salvaged items so the emergent undead have basic clothing and equipment rather than stumbling into service unarmed and exposed.",al={"why does saltain want scavenged goods","why collect scavenged goods","why are scavenged goods needed"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, scavenging discarded equipment is a necessity of survival, cementing the harsh truth that the living will grant them no aid or mercy."},kw={"deathguard saltain","deathknell","equipment boxes","forsaken supplies","scavenged goods","scavenging deathknell"},rel={"quest:3902","subzone:deathknell","topic:forsaken","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Scavenged Goods","Scavenging Deathknell"}}
E["item:scavenger-paw"] = {t="item",n="Scavenger Paw",z="tirisfal",s="Scavenger Paws are severed limbs gathered from the young and ragged wolves roaming the hills of Deathknell. Newly awakened Forsaken collect them for local caretakers to stitch together into crude, protective gear.",h="Scavenger Paws are severed limbs gathered from the young and ragged wolves roaming the hills of Deathknell.",sec={{t="Cobbled Armor in Deathknell",b="Resources are scarce for the newly returned in Deathknell. With the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade pressing against Tirisfal, every scrap of hide and bone is put to practical use. Novice Elreth, assistant to Shadow Priest Sarvis, collects paws from the local scavenger wolves alongside bat wings to fashion serviceable garments and armor for disoriented Forsaken stepping fresh from their graves.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Scavenger Paws used for?",a="They are collected by Novice Elreth in Deathknell, who tailors raw animal parts into basic armor and stitched garments for fresh Forsaken initiates.",al={"scavenger paw purpose","use for paws","why collect paws"},it="why_wanted",gp=true},{q="Where do you find them?",a="They are taken from Young Scavengers and Ragged Scavengers, the feral wolves that roam the woodlands and fields directly surrounding Deathknell.",al={"paw drop location","where to get paws","which wolves drop paws"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Who asks for these paws?",a="Novice Elreth, who assists Shadow Priest Sarvis by treating wounded recruits, tailoring equipment, and outfitting those sent into the field.",al={"who wants scavenger paws","paw quest giver","novice elreth paws"},gp=true},{q="What is a Scavenger Paw?",a="A Scavenger Paw is the severed paw of a scavenger wolf roaming the haunted woods of Tirisfal Glades around Deathknell. While seemingly grim trophies, these durable animal parts serve as raw material for the newly awakened Forsaken, who must utilize every scrap of native wildlife to craft necessities and survive.",al={"scavenger paw identity","what are scavenger paws","what is scavenger paw"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Scavenger Paw come from?",a="These paws are harvested from the young and ragged scavenger wolves prowling the perimeter of Deathknell. Stalking the withered glades just beyond the village walls, these feral beasts scavenge the blighted countryside for carrion, making them easy prey for fresh Forsaken recruits looking to prove their strength.",al={"scavenger paw source","where to get scavenger paw","scavenger wolf paws"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want Scavenger Paws?",a="Novice Elreth in Deathknell collects them to tailor armor and sturdy clothing for newly awakened Forsaken soldiers. Resources are desperately scarce for the newborn free undead, and Elreth pieces together pelts, wings, and paws to patch together gear that can protect you from the mindless undead and Scarlet Crusaders threatening the haven.",al={"why collect scavenger paws","who needs scavenger paws","scavenger paws use lore"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Awakening with decaying flesh and little gear, collecting parts from local predators is often the first step in assembling enough armor to endure the hazards of Tirisfal.",["profession:Tailoring"]="The crude assembly of wolf paws and bat wings by Novice Elreth shows how improvised stitchcraft sustains the fledgling Forsaken settlements."},kw={"deathknell","novice elreth","ragged scavenger","scavenger paw","the damned","young scavenger"},rel={"quest:376","item:duskbat-wing","npc:novice-elreth","subzone:deathknell","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},src={"Scavenger Paw","The Damned (Classic)"}}
E["item:thurman-s-remains"] = {t="item",n="Thurman's Remains",z="tirisfal",s="Thurman's Remains are the corporeal remnants of Thurman Agamand, recovered from the family's ruined mills in northern Tirisfal Glades. Once a wealthy mill owner who persuaded his workers to stand against the Scourge, Thurman succumbed to the Plague and wanders his former estate as an undead thrall.",h="Thurman's Remains are the corporeal remnants of Thurman Agamand, recovered from the family's ruined mills in...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Ruin",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family stood as the most prosperous landowners in Tirisfal Glades, operating extensive mills and employing dozens of local workers. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, Thurman Agamand and his kin fortified their estate rather than fleeing. Convinced that their wealth and fortified mills could weather the onslaught, they persuaded their loyal laborers to take up arms beside them. The defense failed utterly. The Agamands and their workers were slaughtered and transformed into mindless servants of the Lich King. Today, Coleman Farthing—a former employee risen as Forsaken—demands the destruction of his former masters, seeking Thurman's remains as proof that the foolish patriarch has paid for dooming his household.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Thurman Agamand?",a="Thurman Agamand was a prominent member of the wealthy Agamand dynasty that owned Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge struck Lordaeron, his pride led him to convince his laborers to stay and fight, a choice that resulted in the slaughter and reanimation of everyone on the estate.",al={"thurman agamand","who is thurman","who was thurman"},it="people"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want Thurman's remains?",a="Coleman Farthing was a mill worker who trusted the Agamands and died defending their estate. Now a member of the Forsaken, Coleman harbors deep bitterness toward his former employers for dooming their workforce to undeath, seeking Thurman's remains as vengeance.",al={"why coleman wants remains","coleman farthing motive","why kill thurman"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Did Thurman have free will when he was destroyed?",a="No. Unlike the Forsaken of the Undercity, Thurman Agamand remained under the mental domination of the Scourge after death, serving as an unthinking minion until destroyed at the mills.",al={"did thurman have free will","was thurman scourge","is thurman forsaken"}},{q="Who else from the Agamand family is being targeted?",a="Alongside Thurman, Coleman Farthing seeks the destruction and recovered remains of Gregor Agamand and Nissa Agamand, ending the bloodline that once ruled the mills.",al={"other agamands","agamand family members","deaths in the family targets"}},{q="What are Thurman's Remains?",a="These are the physical remnants of Thurman Agamand, recovered after putting down his restless undead form at the Agamand Mills. Once part of the prosperous Agamand dynasty that owned the surrounding mills, Thurman fell victim to the Plague of Undeath and lingered as a mindless thrall of the Scourge until finally struck down.",al={"what is thurmans remains","thurman remains item","who is thurman remains"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Thurman's Remains come from?",a="They are taken directly from the destroyed corpse of Thurman Agamand at the Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through the land, Thurman and his kin fortified their estate and persuaded their workers to make a stand. The defense failed, turning the family into Scourge monstrosities until their bodies were broken.",al={"where do you get thurmans remains","thurmans remains origin","how are thurmans remains made"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaiming these remains is a grim settling of accounts against aristocratic masters whose hubris led their laborers directly into the grasp of the Scourge."},kw={"agamand","agamand mills","coleman farthing","deaths in the family","thurman agamand","thurman's remains","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:354","item:gregor-s-remains","item:nissa-s-remains","npc:coleman-farthing","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","subzone:brill"},src={"Thurman's Remains","Deaths in the Family (Classic)"}}
E["item:tirisfal-pumpkin"] = {t="item",n="Tirisfal Pumpkin",z="tirisfal",s="Tirisfal Pumpkins grow in the tilled plots of the Solliden Farmstead, tended by surviving living humans struggling to survive in the blighted glades. The Forsaken routinely raid these fields to supply the Royal Apothecary Society with organic matter for their ongoing chemical concoctions.",h="Tirisfal Pumpkins grow in the tilled plots of the Solliden Farmstead, tended by surviving living humans...",sec={{t="Harvest in a Blighted Land",b="Despite the corruption settling over Lordaeron, crops still somehow sprout in the fields west of Brill. At the Solliden Farmstead, living human farmers cling to their soil, cultivating massive orange squash under the watchful guard of the nearby Scarlet Crusade. For these mortals, the harvest is vital sustenance. For the nearby Forsaken of Brill, however, the pumpkins represent both a spiteful target of sabotage and a bounty of raw organic reagents needed by Apothecary Johaan.",sp=0},{t="Apothecary Reagents",b="Deathguard Simmer directs young Forsaken out into the contested fields to pilfer the pumpkins directly from beneath the farmers' noses. Rather than feeding the dead, these vegetables find their way into the boiling cauldrons of the Royal Apothecary Society. The apothecaries frequently test how natural flora reacts to their plagues and experimental mixtures, turning Lordaeron's remaining agriculture against the living.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can Tirisfal Pumpkins be found?",a="They grow in the patches of the Solliden Farmstead, situated west of Brill and just north of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"pumpkin location","where are the pumpkins","solliden farm pumpkins"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does the Forsaken want these pumpkins?",a="Deathguard Simmer orders them stolen to deny resources to the surviving humans and the Scarlet Crusade, and to deliver them to Apothecary Johaan in Brill as raw organic reagents for apothecary research.",al={"why steal pumpkins","johaan pumpkin","what are pumpkins for"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Can players eat or cook with Tirisfal Pumpkins?",a="No. They are gathered strictly as a quest reagent for the Royal Apothecary Society and serve no culinary purpose for adventurers.",al={"can you eat tirisfal pumpkins","pumpkin cooking ingredient"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who is currently growing them?",a="Living human farmers still till the soil at the Solliden Farmstead, protected loosely by the Scarlet Crusade stationed at the nearby watchtower.",al={"who owns the farm","solliden farmers"},it="origin"},{q="What is a Tirisfal Pumpkin?",a="A Tirisfal Pumpkin is a hardy gourd cultivated by surviving human farmers at the Solliden Farmstead in Tirisfal Glades. In a blighted realm where living vegetation struggles to endure, these gourds represent precious sustenance for the living and vital alchemical reagents for the Forsaken.",al={"what is tirisfal pumpkin","tirisfal pumpkin","what are tirisfal pumpkins"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Raiding the Solliden patch serves a dual purpose: depriving the remaining living mortals of sustenance while providing Apothecary Johaan with test materials for the Forsaken's dark research.",["faction:Alliance"]="The pumpkins grown at the Solliden Farmstead represent the desperate efforts of Lordaeron's surviving populace to feed themselves amid constant Forsaken incursions."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","deathguard simmer","fields of grief","pumpkin","reagent","solliden farmstead","tirisfal pumpkin"},rel={"quest:365","subzone:solliden-farmstead","npc:apothecary-johaan","npc:deathguard-simmer","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:forsaken"},src={"Tirisfal Pumpkin","Fields of Grief (Classic)"}}
E["item:vicious-night-web-spider-venom"] = {t="item",n="Vicious Night Web Spider Venom",z="tirisfal",s="Vicious Night Web Spider Venom is a potent natural toxin gathered from the arachnids haunting eastern Tirisfal Glades. The Royal Apothecary Society prizes it for its peculiar ability to carry and preserve historical disease strains, making it a critical reagent in the development of the Forsaken's New Plague.",h="Vicious Night Web Spider Venom is a potent natural toxin gathered from the arachnids haunting eastern...",sec={{t="A Preserved Contagion",b="According to research conducted by Apothecary Johaan in Brill, ancient texts chronicle a devastating historical plague that wiped out thousands of victims across northern Lordaeron. Curiously, the contagious agent behind that forgotten outbreak managed to endure over centuries by embedding itself within the venom of the local Night Web spiders. By hunting down the vicious breed lurking in Venomweb Vale south of the Scarlet Monastery, the apothecaries extract this preserved virulence to serve as a vital catalyst in their ongoing alchemical experiments.",sp=0},{t="Fueling the New Plague",b="The Forsaken seek a weapon capable of exterminating both the Scourge and the living, and the venom of the vicious night web spider provides a crucial bridge between natural toxins and infectious blight. Once boiled down and incorporated into Johaan's experimental concoction, the venom imparts a virulent vector that stabilizes the mixture, preparing it for preliminary testing on captive subjects.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What makes this venom unique compared to regular spider poison?",a="Scholarly tomes studied by the Royal Apothecary Society record that an ancient plague's pathogen was biologically preserved inside the venom glands of the Night Web spider lineage, turning their poison into a natural reservoir for infectious contagion.",al={"why this venom","night web venom lore","spider venom plague"},it="power"},{q="Where can the spiders carrying this venom be found?",a="The vicious night web spiders reside primarily in Venomweb Vale, a web-choked canyon located in eastern Tirisfal Glades south of the Scarlet Monastery.",al={"where to find spider venom","vicious night web spider location","venomweb vale spiders"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who wants this venom in Brill?",a="Apothecary Johaan, an alchemist stationed on the eastern edge of Brill, requests multiple samples to complete his research on a new infectious agent for the Forsaken.",al={"who needs spider venom","apothecary johaan venom","who uses the venom"},it="why_wanted"},{q="How does the venom fit into the Forsaken's research?",a="It serves as the final stabilizing catalyst in Johaan's recipe for the New Plague, allowing him to combine the darkhound blood and murloc scales already boiled into his concoction with an ancient, self-sustaining biological pathogen.",al={"apothecary research venom","new plague component","forsaken spider venom"},it="purpose"},{q="What is Vicious Night Web Spider Venom?",a="This is a potent, lethal toxin harvested directly from the vicious night web spiders that infest eastern Tirisfal Glades. Beyond ordinary predatory poison, it harbors a rare preserving quality, maintaining the virulence of an ancient deadly contagion within the spiders' fangs.",al={"what is vicious night web spider venom","vicious night web venom","spider venom tirisfal"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does Vicious Night Web Spider Venom come from, and who made it?",a="It is naturally produced by the vicious night web spiders dwelling in Tirisfal Glades, notably throughout Venomweb Vale south of the Scarlet Monastery. According to ancient texts uncovered by the Royal Apothecary Society, the spiders absorbed and preserved a virulent contagion that wiped out thousands in bygone ages, carrying that ancient pestilence in their venom to this day.",al={"who made vicious night web venom","where does vicious night web venom come from","origin of night web venom"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, harvesting this venom is a direct contribution to Lady Sylvanas's supreme mandate: developing a plague powerful enough to bring down Arthas and his Scourge.",["class:Rogue"]="Rogues familiar with handling natural neurotoxins can appreciate how the Royal Apothecary Society repurposes dangerous wild venoms into engineered bio-weapons."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","new plague","spider venom","tirisfal glades","venomweb vale","vicious night web"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","quest:367","subzone:venomweb-vale","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","zone:scarlet"},src={"Vicious Night Web Spider Venom","A New Plague (3)"}}
E["item:vile-fin-scale"] = {t="item",n="Vile Fin Scale",z="tirisfal",s="Vile Fin Scales are harvested from the murloc tribes prowling the waterways and northern coasts of Tirisfal Glades. In Brill, the Royal Apothecary Society utilizes these resilient biological samples in their ongoing research to engineer a devastating new plague.",h="Vile Fin Scales are harvested from the murloc tribes prowling the waterways and northern coasts of Tirisfal...",sec={{t="Apothecary Reagents",b="The Royal Apothecary Society works relentlessly to craft a virulent blight capable of decimating both the living and the Scourge. Simple toxins are easy enough to brew, but creating an infectious disease that spreads effortlessly across populations requires specialized alchemical properties. Apothecary Johaan in Brill studies Vile Fin scales specifically to understand how their natural resilience and mucous coatings can aid in stabilizing and transmitting his experimental contagions.",sp=0},{t="The Coastline Menace",b="The Vile Fin tribe infests the northern shores and western shallows of Tirisfal Glades, threatening travelers and encroaching upon Forsaken territory. Gathering their scales serves a dual purpose for the Undercity: keeping the feral murloc populations in check while providing Johaan's laboratory with fresh organic components for biochemical warfare tests.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want these scales?",a="Apothecary Johaan is developing a new plague for the Royal Apothecary Society. He finds that ordinary poisons lack transmissible power, so he uses the unique biological properties of murloc scales to help his experimental concoction spread effectively.",al={"what does johaan do with scales","why vile fin scales","johaan plague scale"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can Vile Fin murlocs be found?",a="Vile Fin murlocs inhabit the coasts of Tirisfal Glades, primarily found gathered along the northern shoreline and western waters bordering Brightwater Lake and the sea.",al={"where are vile fin","murloc location tirisfal","where to get scales"},gp=true},{q="What makes the New Plague project important to the Forsaken?",a="The Forsaken seek a weapon capable of exterminating their bitter rivals—both the mindless Scourge and the living races who view them as monsters. Johaan's work in Brill represents early grassroots experimentation toward this kingdom-wide ambition.",al={"why new plague","forsaken plague goal","what is apothecary johaan researching"},it="motive"},{q="Which specific creatures carry these scales?",a="They are obtained from members of the Vile Fin tribe in Tirisfal, including Vile Fin Minor Oracles, Muckdwellers, and Puddlejumpers.",al={"who drops vile fin scale","which murlocs drop scales"},gp=true},{q="What is a Vile Fin Scale?",a="A Vile Fin Scale is a tough, aquatic scale harvested from the hides of the Vile Fin murloc tribe dwelling along the shores and waterways of Tirisfal Glades. Naturally slick and resistant to northern coastal waters, these scales make an unusually durable biological material.",al={"what are vile fin scales","vile fin scale lore","murloc scale"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Vile Fin Scales come from?",a="These scales are biological remnants harvested directly from members of the local Vile Fin murloc tribe, such as puddlejumpers, muckdwellers, and minor oracles prowling the northern and western coastlines of Tirisfal Glades. They are natural growths rather than manufactured goods.",al={"who made vile fin scale","where to get vile fin scales","origin of vile fin scale"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every vile fin scale gathered directly aids the Royal Apothecary Society's sacred duty to engineer a weapon to ensure Forsaken survival against the living and the Scourge.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing out aggressive coastal murloc settlements helps secure the outskirts of Tirisfal Glades for Horde messengers and travelers."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill","murloc","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades","vile fin scale"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","quest:367","subzone:brill","topic:murloc","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Vile Fin Scale","A New Plague (2)"}}
E["npc:apothecary-johaan"] = {t="npc",n="Apothecary Johaan",z="tirisfal",s="Apothecary Johaan is an alchemist and researcher of the Royal Apothecary Society stationed in Brill. Tasked by his order and Lady Sylvanas, he conducts lethal experiments on local flora and fauna to develop the Forsaken's new plague.",h="Apothecary Johaan is an alchemist and researcher of the Royal Apothecary Society stationed in Brill.",sec={{t="A Servant of the Society",b="Working openly out of the Forsaken settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, Apothecary Johaan represents the scientific ambitions of the newly formed Royal Apothecary Society. Like his colleagues across Lordaeron, Johaan operates under direct orders to craft biological and alchemical weapons capable of securing the Forsaken's fragile future against the living and the Scourge alike.",sp=0},{t="Brewing the New Plague",b="Johaan coordinates early field trials for what the Society calls the 'New Plague'. Using raw materials harvested from Tirisfal's corrupted wildlife and agricultural blights—such as gloom weed and the tainted soil of nearby farmsteads—he tests toxin mixtures and evaluates their potency on living subjects before dispatching results down south to Silverpine Forest.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Apothecary Johaan?",a="Apothecary Johaan is an alchemical researcher stationed in Brill. He belongs to the Royal Apothecary Society and directs young Forsaken champions to gather vile reagents across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"apothecary johaan","who is johaan","johaan brill"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Apothecary Johaan?",a="He can be found standing outside near the gallows and alchemical apparatus in the town of Brill, just north of the Undercity in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"johaan location","where is johaan","find johaan"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Johaan working on?",a="Johaan is formulating and refining early strains of the New Plague, testing biological agents and venomous extracts from Tirisfal's blighted fields and beasts.",al={"johaan experiments","what does johaan do","johaan plague"},it="wants"},{q="What does he do with the plague samples?",a="Once test mixtures are concocted and their lethality assessed, Johaan packages the samples and sends couriers to deliver them to allied apothecaries stationed in Silverpine Forest.",al={"delivery to silverpine","where do plague samples go"},it="purpose"},{q="What organization does Johaan belong to?",a="He is an active member of the Royal Apothecary Society, the alchemical guild serving Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken in the Undercity.",al={"johaan faction","who does johaan work for"},it="ties"},{q="Why is he interested in the local farms?",a="Human farms like the Solliden Farmstead and the nearby pumpkin patches contain fungal growths and blighted crops that provide key chemical precursors for his experimental toxins.",al={"fields of grief johaan","why send me to farms"},it="motive"},{q="Whose side is Apothecary Johaan on?",a="Johaan serves Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken as a member of the Royal Apothecary Society. Operating out of Brill, he labors on behalf of the Undercity to develop chemical weapons and plagues meant to secure their domain against both the Scourge and the living.",al={"johaan faction","who does johaan serve","apothecary johaan loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Apothecary Johaan in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Johaan is stationed in Brill to conduct field research and harvest local ingredients across Tirisfal Glades. His proximity to nearby blighted flora, wildlife, and contested human settlements lets him gather testing reagents and experiment directly on subjects away from the Undercity's main chambers.",al={"why is johaan here","why is apothecary johaan in brill","johaan tirisfal purpose"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Johaan's research is central to the Forsaken cause, developing the very weapons needed to carve out a permanent homeland free of Scourge and living threats.",["faction:Horde"]="While orcs and tauren might balk at alchemical blights, Johaan's deadly concoctions serve the strategic security of the Horde's foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"apothecary","brill","gloom weed","johaan","plague","royal apothecary society","silverpine","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:brill","topic:royal-apothecary-society","quest:367","quest:365","quest:445","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","zone:silverpine"},src={"Apothecary Johaan"}}
E["npc:aramis-hammerhand"] = {t="npc",n="Aramis Hammerhand",z="tirisfal",s="Aramis Hammerhand is an undead holy warrior situated within the desecrated chapel of Deathknell. As a Forsaken paladin trainer, he instructs newly risen dead who seek to reclaim their bond with the Holy Light. Walking this path in undeath requires immense discipline, as wielding such radiant energy inflicts agonizing pain upon their decayed flesh.",h="Aramis Hammerhand is an undead holy warrior situated within the desecrated chapel of Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Beacon in Deathknell",b="Standing within the somber walls of Deathknell's chapel, Aramis Hammerhand represents an anomaly among the recently awakened Forsaken. In life, many citizens of Lordaeron answered the call of the Silver Hand, only to perish when the Scourge swept across the north. Aramis survived the fall only to be raised into undeath, yet he chose not to abandon the martial and spiritual teachings of his former existence. Now, he offers guidance to others who refuse to surrender their devotion.",sp=0},{t="The Agony of the Holy Light",b="For an undead being, channeling the Holy Light is an ordeal of supreme willpower. The power burns necrotic flesh and reawakens long-dead nerves to excruciating pain. Aramis instructs his initiates that holding to this faith requires enduring continual torment. Through tasks like 'A Difficult Path' and 'Rediscovering the Light', he demands that young paladins confront the physical and spiritual costs of wielding a force inherently opposed to their present nature.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Aramis Hammerhand?",a="Aramis Hammerhand is a Forsaken paladin trainer located inside the chapel at Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He assists newly risen undead who wish to pursue the path of holy combat.",al={"aramis lore","who is hammerhand","deathknell paladin trainer"},it="identity"},{q="Can the Forsaken actually wield the Holy Light?",a="Yes, through sheer willpower and conviction, Forsaken can call upon the Holy Light. However, doing so causes immense physical agony, as the radiant energy burns and scours their rotting flesh while healing or bolstering them.",al={"can undead be paladins","how do undead use light","forsaken light"}},{q="Where can I find Aramis Hammerhand?",a="He can be found standing inside the chapel of Deathknell, the introductory settlement for the Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is aramis","hammerhand location","paladin trainer deathknell"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What quests does Aramis Hammerhand offer?",a="Aramis offers several class-specific quests for newly risen paladins, including 'A Difficult Path', 'Rediscovering the Light', 'A Light in the Darkness', 'Coming to Terms', and 'Continue Your Training'.",al={"aramis quests","deathknell paladin quests"},gp=true},{q="Why is a paladin trainer working for the Forsaken?",a="Before the plague, Lordaeron was the cradle of the Knights of the Silver Hand. Many of the newly liberated Forsaken remember their oaths and refuse to abandon their sacred calling, regardless of their undead state.",al={"why are there forsaken paladins","forsaken silver hand"},it="purpose"},{q="Does using the Light heal the undead in the normal way?",a="While the Light can mend their bodies and grant them martial strength, the process is excruciating. It reignites their sensory nervous systems, forcing them to feel the rot of their own bodies.",al={"does light hurt undead","light healing undead lore"}},{q="Whose side is Aramis Hammerhand on?",a="Aramis Hammerhand stands firmly with the Forsaken. Though he embraces the discipline of a paladin and teaches newly risen undead to wield the Holy Light, his loyalty belongs to Queen Sylvanas Windrunner and the kingdom she is forging in the ruins of Lordaeron.",al={"aramis hammerhand allegiance","who does aramis serve","aramis hammerhand faction"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Aramis Hammerhand here in Deathknell?",a="You will find Aramis stationed in the ruined chapel of Deathknell to receive newly awakened Forsaken. He awaits those freshly freed from the Scourge's grasp who carried holy vows in life, guiding them through the agony and discipline required to rekindle their sacred power in undeath.",al={"why is aramis in deathknell","aramis hammerhand deathknell location","what brought aramis here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For Forsaken paladins, Aramis is the first mentor they meet, guiding them through the agony and spiritual discipline required to wield the Holy Light in an undead vessel.",["race:Undead"]="Aramis demonstrates that rising into undeath does not require abandoning Lordaeron's proud heritage of holy warfare, even if it comes with immense personal suffering."},kw={"aramis","chapel","deathknell","forsaken","hammerhand","light","paladin","tirisfal","trainer"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:light","quest:98601","quest:90902","quest:98389","quest:91208"},src={"Aramis Hammerhand"}}
E["npc:calvin-montague"] = {t="npc",n="Calvin Montague",z="tirisfal",s="Calvin Montague is an opportunistic Forsaken rogue loitering near the exit gate of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. Preying on newly awakened undead as they leave the graveyard, he attempts to trick passersby into running errands on his behalf.",h="Calvin Montague is an opportunistic Forsaken rogue loitering near the exit gate of Deathknell in Tirisfal...",sec={{t="A Grifter by the Gate",b="Positioned just outside the gates leading away from Deathknell, Calvin Montague embodies the ruthless pragmatism common among Lordaeron's restless dead. Rather than handling his own correspondence or paying fair courier rates, he attempts to smooth-talk wandering adventurers into delivering letters under false pretenses. While several other prominent undead bearing the Montague family name manage banking interests within the Undercity, Calvin pursues a decidedly less reputable trade, preferring petty schemes and deception.",sp=0},{t="A Rogue's Scheme",b="Calvin's proposition involves taking a sealed message on his behalf, though those with sharp wits or underworld training quickly discern the con behind his words. Even among the freshly risen who struggle to find purpose in the bleak ruins of Tirisfal, Calvin reminds newcomers that trust is a scarce commodity in the service of the Dark Lady.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Calvin Montague?",a="Calvin Montague is a Forsaken rogue found standing near the exit gate of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He looks to exploit newly risen undead by conning them into running an errand for him without pay.",al={"calvin montague","who is calvin montague","calvin deathknell"},it="identity"},{q="What does Calvin want adventurers to do?",a="Calvin attempts to talk passing travelers into delivering a letter for him under deceptive pretenses, hoping to secure free courier services through smooth manipulation.",al={"calvin montague quest","calvin letter","what does calvin want"},it="wants"},{q="Is Calvin Montague related to the Undercity bankers?",a="He shares his surname with several prominent bankers in the Undercity, such as Ophelia, Mortimer, William, and Randolph Montague. Unlike his reputable kin managing coin in the vaults, Calvin operates as an outcast scoundrel out on the road.",al={"montague family","undercity bankers montague","calvin related to bankers"},it="ties"},{q="Where can Calvin Montague be found?",a="He lurks along the road just outside the wooden gate marking the northern exit of Deathknell, intercepting adventurers as they head out into the wider Tirisfal Glades.",al={"calvin location","where is calvin montague","find calvin montague"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Calvin Montague on?",a="Calvin is an independent Forsaken rogue looking out primarily for himself. While he resides within the lands of the Forsaken and interacts with its travelers, his schemes and shady dealings show he serves his own pocket rather than any noble cause or state enterprise.",al={"calvin montague faction","who does calvin montague serve","is calvin montague forsaken"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Calvin Montague stationed outside Deathknell?",a="Calvin positions himself right along the road leading out of Deathknell to prey upon newly awakened undead. Freshly risen Forsaken heading into the wider reaches of Tirisfal Glades make easy marks for an opportunistic rogue looking to trick someone into doing his courier work for free.",al={"why is calvin montague here","calvin montague deathknell location","why is calvin at the deathknell exit"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Forsaken rogues will quickly spot Calvin Montague's transparent attempts at manipulation, recognizing a fellow cutpurse trying to run a cheap hustle on the uninitiated.",["race:Undead"]="For the newly risen departing Deathknell, Calvin serves as an early lesson that not all of Sylvanas's subjects are interested in mutual solidarity."},kw={"a rogue's deal","calvin montague","con artist","deathknell","forsaken rogue","montague","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:8","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","zone:undercity","topic:forsaken"},src={"Calvin Montague"}}
E["npc:captured-mountaineer"] = {t="npc",n="Captured Mountaineer",z="tirisfal",s="The Captured Mountaineer is an Ironforge dwarf held prisoner in the basement of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. Bound and guarded by the Forsaken, he serves as an unwilling subject for the Royal Apothecary Society's grim experiments.",h="The Captured Mountaineer is an Ironforge dwarf held prisoner in the basement of the Gallows' End Tavern in...",sec={{t="A Prisoner in the Cellar",b="Tucked away beneath the floorboards of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, this Ironforge Mountaineer sits captive far from the safety of Khaz Modan. While patrons drink above, the Royal Apothecary Society puts him to work as an unwitting test subject for their concoctions. Apothecary Johaan and his peers test the lethal potency of their developing plague against living Alliance physiology, using the prisoner to gauge how well their toxins bring a swift and agonizing end to the living.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Captured Mountaineer?",a="He is an Alliance scout from Ironforge who fell into Forsaken hands. He is currently imprisoned in the basement of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill.",al={"who is this dwarf","dwarf in brill tavern","captive dwarf"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Forsaken keeping him alive in the basement?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society uses him as a live test subject. Rather than executing him outright, apothecaries like Johaan administer experimental mixtures to study their effectiveness against living dwarven biology.",al={"why keep him alive","why is he in the tavern","what are they doing to the dwarf"},it="why_here"},{q="What happens to him during the tests?",a="As part of the research into developing a new plague, he is forced to ingest concoctions brewed by the apothecaries. The lethal mixture quickly proves effective, terminating the prisoner as proof of the toxin's progress.",al={"does the dwarf die","what happens in a new plague"},sp=1},{q="Who else interacts with him down here?",a="Aside from the apothecaries conducting experiments, locals like Renee Lauer have spent time down in the cellar, sharing stories with the captive dwarf while he remains bound.",al={"who talks to the dwarf","renee lauer"}},{q="Whose side is the Captured Mountaineer on?",a="He belongs to Ironforge and the Grand Alliance. Dwarven mountaineers typically patrol the mountain passes of Khaz Modan and contested borders, making him a sworn enemy of the Forsaken and the Horde.",al={"captured mountaineer faction","captured mountaineer alliance or horde","what side is the mountaineer on"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does the Captured Mountaineer want?",a="Locked in chains inside a damp cellar, his primary hope is survival and escape. Unfortunately for him, the Royal Apothecary Society views him not as a prisoner of war to ransom, but as raw material for refining their deadly New Plague.",al={"what does the captured mountaineer want","captured mountaineer goal","captured mountaineer desires"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Seeing a proud brother of Ironforge caged beneath a Forsaken tavern and subjected to horrific alchemical trials is a bitter reminder of the Forsaken's ruthless hostility toward the living.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, live Alliance captives are vital resources for the Royal Apothecary Society's goal of perfecting a plague capable of wiping out Scourge and living foes alike."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill basement","captured mountaineer","dwarf prisoner","gallows end tavern","ironforge scout"},rel={"quest:367","subzone:gallows-end-tavern","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Captured Mountaineer"}}
E["npc:captured-scarlet-zealot"] = {t="npc",n="Captured Scarlet Zealot",z="tirisfal",s="The Captured Scarlet Zealot is a crusader prisoner held captive in the basement of Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. Watched over by Forsaken apothecaries and townsfolk, he serves as a living test subject for the Royal Apothecary Society's developing concoctions.",h="The Captured Scarlet Zealot is a crusader prisoner held captive in the basement of Gallows' End Tavern in...",sec={{t="A Bitter Captive in Brill",b="Confined beneath Gallows' End Tavern, this member of the Scarlet Crusade embodies the unyielding fanaticism of his order. Even in chains, surrounded by the undead he views as an abomination, he offers no remorse and venomously denounces anyone who speaks to him—such as Renee Lauer, whom he reviles as a traitor. For the Forsaken, capturing a living zealot is not an act of mercy; it provides valuable human test material to evaluate the lethality of experimental plagues and toxins under development by Apothecary Johaan.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is a Scarlet Zealot kept alive in Brill?",a="The Forsaken keep him captive in the tavern basement as a living subject for testing new poisons and chemical agents developed by the Royal Apothecary Society.",al={"why is scarlet zealot captured","why keep zealot alive","who is the zealot in brill cellar"},it="why_here"},{q="Where can I find this captive?",a="He is imprisoned in the damp cellar beneath Gallows' End Tavern, situated in the center of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is captured scarlet zealot","scarlet zealot location","zealot in brill"},gp=true},{q="How does he react to the Forsaken around him?",a="He remains fanatically defiant despite his confinement, lashing out with insults and labeling locals like Renee Lauer traitors to humanity and the Light.",al={"what does the zealot say","zealot dialogue","renee lauer and zealot"}},{q="Who is the Captured Scarlet Zealot?",a="He is a captured human soldier belonging to the fanatical Scarlet Crusade. Imprisoned in the dark cellar beneath the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, he serves as an involuntary subject for Forsaken interrogation, taunting, and alchemical trials.",al={"captured scarlet zealot who","who is the zealot in the basement","scarlet prisoner brill"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is the Captured Scarlet Zealot on?",a="He is fiercely loyal to the Scarlet Crusade, an uncompromising order dedicated to eradicating all undead. Even bound and surrounded by Forsaken captors, he refuses to recant his allegiance, spitting defiance and branding the Forsaken as unnatural abominations.",al={"captured scarlet zealot faction","what side is the zealot on","zealot loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does the Captured Scarlet Zealot want?",a="Above all, he seeks the absolute destruction of the Forsaken and the Scourge alike. Held helpless in chains, he wants nothing more than to see the Crusade burn the Undercity and purge what he views as blasphemous monsters from Lordaeron.",al={"zealot goal","what does the prisoner want","captured zealot motive"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is the Captured Scarlet Zealot tied to?",a="He is bound to the Scarlet Crusade, answering to its commanders who wage war across Tirisfal Glades. Locally, he is under the close watch of Apothecary Johaan and the patrons of the Gallows' End Tavern, who treat him as an enemy captive and an alchemical test subject.",al={"captured zealot connections","who does the zealot answer to","captured zealot ties"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="To the Forsaken of Brill, this fanatic in chains represents the relentless crusaders seeking their extermination, serving a far more useful role as an apothecary's guinea pig.",["class:Priest"]="Scarlet zealots claim exclusive dominion over the Holy Light, yet this prisoner's unwavering zeal offers no defense against the grim reality of Forsaken captivity."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","captured scarlet zealot","gallows' end tavern","prisoner","renee lauer","scarlet crusade","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:gallows-end-tavern","zone:tirisfal","quest:365"},src={"Captured Scarlet Zealot"}}
E["npc:carolai-anise"] = {t="npc",n="Carolai Anise",z="tirisfal",s="Carolai Anise is an eccentric Forsaken alchemist residing in the village of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. She trains novice apothecaries and herbalists, though her hazardous concoctions have drawn nervous murmurs from local guards.",h="Carolai Anise is an eccentric Forsaken alchemist residing in the village of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Volatile Craft",b="Operating out of Brill, Carolai Anise devotes her unlife to the volatile art of alchemy. While many Forsaken researchers operate under the Royal Apothecary Society's grim mandate, Carolai pursues her own strange brews right inside the settlement. Her unpredictable experiments and smoking retorts are notorious enough that the Deathguards keeping watch over Brill openly grumble that she seems bent on blowing up her own house.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Carolai Anise?",a="Carolai Anise is an undead alchemy trainer living in the settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. She teaches aspiring Forsaken how to brew potions, elixirs, and oils.",al={"carolai anise","brill alchemist","who is carolai"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find her?",a="She can be found in Brill, the primary Forsaken town in central Tirisfal Glades, working around her alchemy supplies inside one of the town's buildings.",al={"where is carolai anise","carolai anise location","where is brill alchemy trainer"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the Deathguards complain about her?",a="Local Deathguards warn that Carolai's unstable alchemy experiments are an accident waiting to happen, often joking or fretting that she will eventually blow up her entire house.",al={"deathguards on carolai","blowing up house","carolai anise danger"},it="fame"},{q="Whose side is Carolai Anise on?",a="She is loyal to the Forsaken and the Dark Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. Operating openly out of the settlement of Brill, she serves the broader Forsaken community by instructing fellow undead and Horde travelers in the foundational arts of brewing and distillation.",al={"carolai anise faction","is carolai anise forsaken","who does carolai anise serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Carolai Anise want?",a="Carolai is consumed by her alchemical crafts and volatile experimentation. While she passes on her trade to apprentices, she seems intent on pursuing reckless, bubbling concoctions—so much so that the local town guard worries her experiments might level her workshop.",al={"carolai anise motive","what is carolai anise doing","carolai anise goal"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Carolai Anise here in Brill?",a="She resides in Brill to teach alchemy to newly awakened Forsaken and Horde travelers stepping forth from Deathknell. Tirisfal Glades offers plenty of peace and raw ingredients for her to pursue her brewing and push her volatile craft to dangerous extremes.",al={"why is carolai anise in brill","carolai anise tirisfal glades why","why is carolai anise here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly liberated Forsaken learning to manipulate reagents, Carolai provides the basic foundations of alchemical craft in Tirisfal Glades.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Gatherers harvesting wild flora throughout Tirisfal often bring peacebloom, silverleaf, and earthroot to Carolai to be refined into potent mixtures."},kw={"alchemy","apothecary","brill","carolai anise","forsaken trainer","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","quest:95314","topic:forsaken"},src={"Carolai Anise"}}
E["npc:coleman-farthing"] = {t="npc",n="Coleman Farthing",z="tirisfal",s="Coleman Farthing is a bitter Forsaken survivor residing in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. In life, he worked for the wealthy Agamand dynasty at their extensive flour mills in northern Tirisfal. Convinced to stay and defend the estate against the Scourge, he died alongside his fellow laborers, and now dedicates his undeath to exacting vengeance on his former masters.",h="Coleman Farthing is a bitter Forsaken survivor residing in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill.",sec={{t="A Bitter Servant",b="Before the Third War, Coleman Farthing earned an honest living as a laborer at the Agamand Mills, the largest milling and agricultural operation in Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge poured across Lordaeron, the aristocratic Agamand family refused to flee their valuable lands. Instead, they urged their household and laborers to take up arms alongside them, promising they could hold the farmsteads against the undead horde. Coleman and his fellow workers stayed, believing their masters—a decision that cost every last one of them their lives.",sp=0},{t="Vengeance from the Grave",b="Raised into undeath only to break free under the banner of the Forsaken, Coleman harbors a burning hatred for the Agamands. Rather than blaming only the Scourge, he blames the sheer hubris and greed of his employers for tricking their staff into an impossible last stand. Now loitering in Brill's tavern, Coleman enlists Horde champions to march into the blighted mills, cut down the risen Agamand kin, and shatter the heirloom relics housed within their family crypt so that their legacy is utterly erased.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can Coleman Farthing be found?",a="Coleman Farthing spends his days inside the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, located in central Tirisfal Glades.",al={"coleman farthing location","where is coleman farthing","coleman farthing brill"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Coleman hate the Agamand family so much?",a="When the Scourge invaded Lordaeron, the Agamand patriarchs convinced their workers—including Coleman—to stay and fight to protect the mills rather than flee to safety. The entire workforce was slaughtered as a result. Coleman views their rallying cry as arrogant manipulation that led him directly to his death.",al={"why does coleman hate agamands","coleman farthing motivation","agamand mills grudge"},it="motive"},{q="What happened to the Agamand Mills?",a="The sprawling estate was completely overrun by the Scourge. Its wind-driven mills stand ruined and haunted, while the Agamand family members and their slaughtered workers wander the blighted fields as mindlessly hostile undead under the Lich King's sway.",al={"what happened to agamand mills","agamand mills lore","who owns agamand mills"},it="history"},{q="What tasks does Coleman give to adventurers?",a="Coleman directs adventurers to journey north to the Agamand Mills to cull the roaming undead, slay the risen members of the Agamand lineage to recover their remains for desecration, and raid the Agamand Family Crypt for ancestral weaponry.",al={"coleman farthing quests","agamand quests brill"},gp=true},{q="Why does Coleman want the Agamand heirloom weapons?",a="Coleman sees the Agamands' ancestral weaponry as symbols of the hollow pride that led his fellow workers to their deaths. He sends skilled warriors to plunder these relics from the family crypt, both to strip the dynasty of its honor and put the weapons to practical use against their enemies.",al={"agamand heirlooms quest lore","why steal agamand weapons"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Is Coleman Farthing related to Bishop Farthing?",a="They share a surname and origins in the fallen Kingdom of Lordaeron, but there is no confirmed record of their specific familial ties.",al={"bishop farthing relation","coleman farthing family"},it="ties"},{q="Who is Coleman Farthing?",a="Coleman Farthing is a Forsaken residing in Brill's Gallows' End Tavern. In life, he was a laborer employed by the wealthy Agamand family at their mills in western Tirisfal Glades. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, his masters convinced him and their other workers to stand and fight rather than flee. The mills were overrun, slaughtering everyone present. Now reanimated into free will under the Dark Lady, Coleman is consumed by bitter resentment toward the family that led him to his gruesome death.",al={"who is coleman farthing","coleman farthing lore","who is coleman"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Coleman Farthing on?",a="Coleman Farthing stands firmly with the Forsaken and the Horde. He dwells among his undead brethren in Brill, safe from the mindless Scourge that plagues the ruins of his former life. While he supports the Forsaken's struggle to secure Tirisfal Glades, his focus remains deeply personal: directing passing adventurers to settle his old grievances against the masters who doomed him.",al={"coleman farthing faction","is coleman horde","who does coleman serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Coleman Farthing want?",a="Coleman wants absolute vengeance against the Agamand family. He seeks to see the reanimated remains of his former masters—Gregor, Nissa, Thurman, and Devlin—utterly destroyed so he can burn their remains. Beyond their deaths, he wants their ancestral crypt plundered of its valuable heirloom weapons, stripping the proud dynasty of their dignity, their lineage, and their resting place.",al={"what does coleman want","coleman farthing goal","coleman goals"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Coleman Farthing here in Brill?",a="Coleman stays at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill because it serves as the primary gathering place for newly risen undead and Horde travelers journeying north. Lacking the martial strength to assault the overrun Agamand Mills on his own, he bides his time among travelers, recruiting capable warriors and spellcasters to march northwest and enact vengeance on his behalf.",al={"why is coleman in brill","why is coleman at gallows end","coleman farthing brill"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, Coleman represents the shared resentment many commoners hold toward Lordaeron's old nobility, whose arrogance and pride often hastened their subjects' demise during the plague.",["class:Warrior"]="Coleman points aspiring warriors directly toward the Agamand Family Crypt, urging them to plunder the martial heirlooms of a fallen aristocratic house to arm themselves for the wars ahead."},kw={"agamand heirlooms","agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","deaths in the family","gallows' end tavern","lordaeron","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:gallows-end-tavern","subzone:agamand-mills","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","quest:354","quest:362","quest:1821","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Coleman Farthing"}}
E["npc:dark-cleric-beryl"] = {t="npc",n="Dark Cleric Beryl",z="tirisfal",s="Dark Cleric Beryl is a Forsaken priest trainer stationed in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. He guides newly awakened undead priests as they learn to wield shadow magic and adapt to their new, unholy existence under the Dark Lady.",h="Dark Cleric Beryl is a Forsaken priest trainer stationed in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill.",sec={{t="A Guide in the Shadows",b="Residing on the upper floor of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, Dark Cleric Beryl serves the Forsaken by mentoring nascent priests. Once dedicated to the Holy Light in life, many Forsaken priests found their connection to holy magic agonizing or severed upon being raised into undeath. Beryl assists these newly risen practitioners as they turn their focus toward shadow and the dark arts, guiding them through early trials like crafting their Garments of Darkness and proving their dedication to the new path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Dark Cleric Beryl?",a="Dark Cleric Beryl is a Forsaken priest trainer located upstairs in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. He instructs newly risen undead priests in expanding their spellcraft and embracing the shadows.",al={"beryl role","who is beryl","cleric beryl lore"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Dark Cleric Beryl?",a="He is located in Tirisfal Glades inside the town of Brill. You can find him upstairs within the Gallows' End Tavern.",al={"beryl location","where is beryl","find dark cleric beryl"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do Forsaken priests use the title Dark Cleric?",a="Forsaken priests who abandon the traditional dogmas of the Holy Light or balance it with darker disciplines take up the title of Dark Cleric. Because channeling the Light is excruciating for their undead flesh, many lean heavily into shadow magic and the Cult of Forgotten Shadows.",al={"what is a dark cleric","forsaken dark clerics","why dark cleric"}},{q="What quests does Beryl offer?",a="Beryl is involved in early Forsaken priest training quests, including 'In Favor of Darkness' and 'Garments of Darkness', which teach young acolytes how to aid their allies and attire themselves in vestments suitable for the shadow.",al={"beryl quests","dark cleric beryl quests"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Dark Cleric Beryl on?",a="Beryl is loyal to the Forsaken and the Dark Lady, Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. Operating out of Brill, he serves the interests of Undercity and the Horde by instructing newly awakened undead who hear the call of the priesthood, helping them master shadow and discipline in service to their people.",al={"beryl faction","who does beryl serve","beryl allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Dark Cleric Beryl in Brill?",a="Beryl is stationed in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill to guide young Forsaken priests emerging from Deathknell. As fledglings venture out into Tirisfal Glades, he provides them with the training and robes necessary to survive against the Scourge, the Scarlet Crusade, and other perils threatening Lordaeron.",al={"why is beryl in brill","why is beryl here","beryl gallows end"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Dark Cleric Beryl want?",a="Beryl seeks to ensure that the Forsaken's fledgling priests are properly equipped and disciplined in their craft. He charges practitioners to understand their dark calling, testing their readiness and outfitting them in the garments suited to channel the powers of shadow and unyielding will.",al={"what is beryl's goal","what does beryl want","beryl motives"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="For Forsaken priests venturing into Tirisfal Glades, Beryl serves as an essential mentor in Brill, providing guidance on how to manipulate shadow magic and survive the harsh transition into unholy power.",["race:Undead"]="Beryl represents the Forsaken's adaptation to their cursed condition, trading the blind devotion of Lordaeron's former faiths for practical, shadowy resilience."},kw={"beryl","brill","dark cleric beryl","forsaken priest","gallows' end tavern","priest trainer","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","subzone:gallows-end-tavern","zone:tirisfal","quest:5650","quest:5651","topic:forsaken"},src={"Dark Cleric Beryl"}}
E["npc:david-trias"] = {t="npc",n="David Trias",z="tirisfal",s="David Trias is the Forsaken rogue trainer stationed in Deathknell. He instructs freshly awakened undead in the arts of stealth, subterfuge, and bladecraft to aid Queen Sylvanas and the Forsaken.",h="David Trias is the Forsaken rogue trainer stationed in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Guide from the Shadows",b="Positioned amidst the tombstones and ruins of Deathknell, David Trias is tasked with identifying and training those newly risen undead who possess a talent for unseen murder. For the fledgling Forsaken, survival requires more than sheer force of arms; it demands precise strikes from the dark. Trias decrypts orders and teaches initiates how to wield daggers, conceal their movements, and turn their reanimated reflexes into lethal precision against the living and the Scourge.",sp=0},{t="A Familiar Name",b="The surname Trias carries peculiar echoes across the continent. Far to the south in the kingdom of Stormwind sits Elling Trias, a well-known cheese merchant who secretly maintains deep ties to master rogues and spymasters. While David is now an undead agent of the Dark Lady, his surname hints at connections to that same lineage of covert operators—though whether he remembers or cares about his former family remains a secret kept in the shadows.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is David Trias?",a="David Trias is a Forsaken rogue trainer found in Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He teaches newly awakened Forsaken rogues their initial combat techniques and delivers their first covert assignments.",al={"david trias","who is david trias","rogue trainer deathknell"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find David Trias?",a="He is located in Deathknell, the starting village for the Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades, standing near the settlement's structures and assisting newly risen undead.",al={"david trias location","where is david trias","deathknell rogue trainer"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Is David Trias related to Elling Trias in Stormwind?",a="He shares his surname with Elling Trias and Lucian Trias of Stormwind. Given that all three possess strong ties to roguecraft, David may be related to them, though no connection is confirmed.",al={"elling trias relation","trias family","is he related to elling trias"},it="ties"},{q="What role does he play in Forsaken training?",a="Initiate rogues emerging from Deathknell's crypt are directed to David Trias with an encrypted scroll. He deciphers their instructions, guides their foundational training, and sets them on the path to becoming stealth operatives for the Forsaken.",al={"david trias quests","encrypted scroll","what does he teach"},it="purpose"},{q="Whose side is David Trias on?",a="David Trias is loyal to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken. Stationed in Deathknell, he dedicates his undeath to training newly risen rogues, helping them master stealth, cunning, and backstabbing to defend their free will against the Scourge and living threats.",al={"david trias faction","who does david trias serve","david trias loyalties"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is David Trias in Deathknell?",a="Deathknell serves as the primary awakening ground for newly raised Forsaken. David Trias remains here to evaluate newcomers who possess a knack for stealth and subtlety, providing them with basic guidance and instruction before they venture further into Tirisfal Glades.",al={"why is david trias here","david trias deathknell","what brings david trias to deathknell"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does David Trias want?",a="Trias wants newly awakened rogues to adapt quickly to their undead condition and become effective agents for the Dark Lady. He expects you to learn the value of striking from behind and mastering essential roguish arts so you can survive the harsh struggles awaiting the Forsaken.",al={"david trias motives","what does david trias need","david trias goal"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="For Forsaken rogues, David Trias is your very first mentor, helping you decipher secret scrolls and teaching you how to turn the cold precision of undeath into lethal bladecraft.",["race:Undead"]="David Trias represents the grim reality of the Forsaken: former humans of Lordaeron whose past lives and lineage are severed by the plague, redirected now into serving Queen Sylvanas."},kw={"david trias","deathknell","elling trias","encrypted scroll","forsaken","rogue trainer","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:3096","npc:elling-trias","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},src={"David Trias"}}
E["npc:deathguard-bartholomew"] = {t="npc",n="Deathguard Bartholomew",z="tirisfal",s="Deathguard Bartholomew is a vigilant Forsaken soldier stationed in the town of Brill. Tasked with maintaining security across Tirisfal Glades, he helps travelers navigate the blighted settlement and assists in enforcing discipline among local defenders.",h="Deathguard Bartholomew is a vigilant Forsaken soldier stationed in the town of Brill.",sec={{t="Sentinel of Brill",b="Standing watch in the settlement of Brill, Deathguard Bartholomew represents the stalwart military backbone of the Forsaken. Like his fellow Deathguards throughout Tirisfal Glades, he defends reclaimed territory from lingering Scourge, fanatical Scarlet Crusaders, and any other threats to Lady Sylvanas's domain. In addition to keeping watch, he provides directions to travelers attempting to navigate the town's facilities and participates in maintaining orderly conduct among the newly risen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Deathguard Bartholomew?",a="Deathguard Bartholomew is a Forsaken guard stationed in Brill within Tirisfal Glades. He stands as a sentry safeguarding the town and guides wayfarers to points of interest.",al={"bartholomew","who is bartholomew","deathguard bartholomew lore"},it="identity"},{q="What is the role of the Deathguard?",a="The Deathguard serves as the primary infantry and defense force of the Forsaken. They patrol the territories of Lordaeron, defend settlements like Brill from the Scourge and Scarlet Crusade, and enforce order on behalf of the Dark Lady.",al={"what are deathguards","deathguard faction","forsaken guards"},it="purpose"},{q="Can Deathguard Bartholomew give directions?",a="Yes. Like many town sentries across Azeroth, Bartholomew can direct travelers to essential facilities throughout Brill, including trainers, the inn, and profession vendors.",al={"where is the inn","brill directions","guard directions brill"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Deathguard Bartholomew on?",a="Deathguard Bartholomew serves the Forsaken and the Dark Lady, Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. As part of the military force defending Tirisfal Glades, he stands loyal to the Horde, protecting the township of Brill against the Scourge, the Scarlet Crusade, and trespassing Alliance intruders.",al={"bartholomew faction","bartholomew allegiance","who does bartholomew serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Deathguard Bartholomew here?",a="Bartholomew is stationed in Brill as part of the local garrison. Following the Forsaken reclamation of the town's ruins, he remains on guard to secure the settlement, maintain military order, and guide travelers and newly awakened undead navigating Tirisfal Glades.",al={"why is bartholomew in brill","bartholomew duty","what is bartholomew doing here"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Deathguard Bartholomew want?",a="Bartholomew's primary duty is keeping Brill safe and orderly for the Forsaken. Beyond keeping watch over the town and offering directions to arriving travelers, he relies on capable adventurers to assist with local threats and military tasks that ensure the township remains fortified.",al={"bartholomew goals","what does bartholomew need","bartholomew motive"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Deathguard Bartholomew tied to?",a="Bartholomew is a sworn soldier within the Deathguard, the standing military and town defense of the Forsaken. He answers to the local Forsaken leadership stationed in Brill and ultimately serves the Undercity and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner alongside his fellow sentries.",al={"bartholomew allies","bartholomew connections","who does bartholomew answer to"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly liberated Forsaken emerging from Deathknell, guards like Bartholomew in Brill are the first organized face of Lady Sylvanas's standing army.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies arriving from Kalimdor can rely on Bartholomew to find their bearings in the mist-shrouded streets of Brill."},kw={"bartholomew","brill","deathguard","forsaken","guard","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:86784","quest:99134"},src={"Deathguard Bartholomew"}}
E["npc:deathguard-burgess"] = {t="npc",n="Deathguard Burgess",z="tirisfal",s="Deathguard Burgess is a Forsaken sentry stationed in the town of Brill within Tirisfal Glades. Standing watch over the settlement, he keeps order and directs travelers to vital services across the hamlet.",h="Deathguard Burgess is a Forsaken sentry stationed in the town of Brill within Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Watchman in Brill",b="Positioned in the settlement of Brill just north of the Undercity, Deathguard Burgess serves as one of the town guards maintaining security against the Scourge and Scarlet Crusade. Like other Deathguards throughout Tirisfal Glades, Burgess aids arriving Forsaken by providing directions to local tradesfolk, class trainers, and civic services, keeping the vulnerable outpost running smoothly.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Deathguard Burgess?",a="Deathguard Burgess is an undead sentry serving the Forsaken in the town of Brill. He helps keep order in the hamlet and assists visitors with directions to local trainers and points of interest.",al={"burgess brill","who is burgess","deathguard burgess lore"},it="identity"},{q="What role do the Deathguards serve in Brill?",a="The Deathguard are the standard military and police force of the Forsaken. In places like Brill, they protect the local populace from outside threats like the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade while maintaining civil order.",al={"what are deathguards","deathguards role"},it="purpose"},{q="Can Deathguard Burgess provide directions?",a="Yes. Like many town guards across Azeroth, talking to Burgess allows travelers to ask for the location of class trainers, the local inn, profession masters, and other essential facilities in Brill.",al={"burgess directions","where to go in brill"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Deathguard Burgess on?",a="Deathguard Burgess serves Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken as part of the nascent kingdom based in the Undercity. Standing sentry in Brill, he defends the township against lingering Scourge threats, hostile Scarlet crusaders, and any outside interlopers who dare threaten the Dark Lady's newly claimed realm.",al={"burgess faction","who does deathguard burgess serve","is burgess forsaken"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Deathguard Burgess want?",a="Burgess wants order, vigilance, and safety for the settlement of Brill. As a member of the town guard, his duty is to watch the roads, keep hostile threats from breaching the township's borders, and ensure that newly risen Forsaken and visitors find their bearings across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what is burgess goal","burgess purpose","what does deathguard burgess do"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Deathguard Burgess here?",a="Burgess is stationed in Brill because it serves as the crucial forward settlement north of the Capital City ruins and the Undercity. Guarding the paths through Tirisfal Glades ensures that Forsaken interests remain secure against Scarlet zealots, mindless undead prowling the wilderness, and encroaching beasts.",al={"why is burgess in brill","deathguard burgess location","why is deathguard burgess stationed here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly awoken Forsaken leaving Deathknell, guards like Burgess in Brill provide your first orientation to the wider infrastructure of Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="Visiting allies from Kalimdor can speak to Burgess to navigate the unfamiliar, blighted layout of Brill when seeking out supply vendors or resting at the inn."},kw={"brill","burgess","deathguard burgess","forsaken","guard","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:374","quest:99134","topic:lordaeron"},src={"Deathguard Burgess"}}
E["npc:deathguard-dillinger"] = {t="npc",n="Deathguard Dillinger",z="tirisfal",s="Deathguard Dillinger is a Forsaken sentry stationed in Brill within Tirisfal Glades. Standing watch over the town's perimeter, he directs newly arrived undead toward necessary services and tasks recruits with keeping the local Scourge remnants at bay.",h="Deathguard Dillinger is a Forsaken sentry stationed in Brill within Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Sentinel of Brill",b="As a member of the Forsaken Deathguard, Dillinger stands at the entrance of Brill, serving as both a frontline protector and an orienting guide for travelers emerging from Deathknell. He provides directions to town amenities such as the inn, the town hall, and local trainers, helping newly awakened undead navigate their new reality under the Dark Lady's rule.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Borderlands",b="Dillinger actively enlists capable adventurers to purge lingering mindless Scourge from the nearby farms and mills. Through assignments like cullling rotting ghouls and reclaiming infested facilities, he seeks to establish a buffer zone of security around Brill, ensuring the fledgling Forsaken settlements can endure against unending undead predation.",sp=0},{t="Containing Mindless Horrors",b="Beyond routine patrols, Dillinger coordinates responses against persistent Scourge threats lurking in the ruins around Brill. When summoned horrors such as Ulag the Cleaver emerge from nearby tombs or neglected homesteads, Dillinger ensures able fighters are dispatched to put the rampaging monstrosities down before they breach the settlement's borders.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can I find Deathguard Dillinger?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is posted on the outskirts of Brill in central Tirisfal Glades, watching the path leading into town.",al={"dillinger location","where is dillinger","deathguard dillinger coordinates"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is the role of the Deathguard?",a="The Deathguard serves as the military standing army and constabulary of the Forsaken, guarding settlements like Brill and the Undercity against the Scourge, the Scarlet Crusade, and other regional threats.",al={"what is deathguard","who are the deathguards","deathguard purpose"},it="people"},{q="What tasks does Deathguard Dillinger offer?",a="Dillinger tasks adventurers with slaying mindless Scourge wandering the surrounding fields and mills, reclaiming vital local infrastructure, and dealing with dangerous entities like Ulag the Cleaver.",al={"dillinger quests","what quests does dillinger have"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Can Deathguard Dillinger provide directions?",a="Yes, speaking with Dillinger allows players to request directions to essential services, profession trainers, and class mentors located throughout Brill.",al={"dillinger directions","ask dillinger for help"},gp=true},{q="Why are the Forsaken fighting other undead near Brill?",a="The mindless undead wandering Tirisfal remain under the thrall of the Scourge and the Lich King. Having broken free of that mental control, the Forsaken must eliminate these feral remnants to protect their own territory.",al={"why fight scourge","undead fighting undead","brill ghouls"},it="conflict"},{q="Who is Ulag the Cleaver?",a="Ulag the Cleaver is a dangerous Scourge horror tied to nearby tombs outside Brill. Dillinger coordinates with Forsaken recruits to summon and destroy Ulag to keep the surrounding area safe.",al={"ulag quest","who is ulag","ulag the cleaver"},it="target"},{q="Who is Deathguard Dillinger?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is a Forsaken soldier stationed to guard the settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Like the rest of the Deathguard, he was once a human of Lordaeron who fell to the plague of undeath before breaking free from the Lich King's iron will under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. Now, he stands vigil on the front lines to defend newly awakened Forsaken and protect the realm from mindless Scourge prowling the outskirts.",al={"who is dillinger","deathguard dillinger lore","dillinger identity"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Deathguard Dillinger on?",a="Dillinger is fiercely loyal to the Forsaken and the Banshee Queen, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. As a sworn guardian of the Undercity's outer perimeter, he serves the Horde by holding the line against the lingering remnants of the Scourge and anyone else who threatens the hard-won sovereignty of the free undead in Lordaeron.",al={"dillinger faction","who does dillinger serve","dillinger loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Deathguard Dillinger here?",a="Dillinger is posted outside Brill to maintain order, provide sentry duty, and direct newly arrived Forsaken fresh from the crypts of Deathknell. Tirisfal Glades is crawling with mindless undead, wild beasts, and hostile encroachers, so experienced Deathguards like him are stationed at town crossroads to organize defenses and enlist capable travelers to cull nearby threats.",al={"why is dillinger in brill","what is dillinger doing here","dillinger post"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Deathguard Dillinger want?",a="Dillinger wants the lands surrounding Brill cleared of mindless Scourge and dangerous monstrosities. He enlists promising new Forsaken to thin out the rotting ghouls infesting the nearby countryside and eliminate unruly threats, ensuring that Brill remains a secure foothold for Queen Sylvanas's growing nation.",al={"what does dillinger want","dillinger goals","dillinger purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Deathguard Dillinger tied to?",a="Dillinger is tied directly to the Deathguard, the standing military force charged with securing Forsaken settlements. He answers to the commanders of Brill and the magistrates of Undercity, serving as a frontline intermediary between local authorities, like Magistrate Sevren, and arriving adventurers who can carry out the Forsaken's military orders.",al={"dillinger ties","who does dillinger answer to","dillinger leadership"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly freed Forsaken arriving from Deathknell, Dillinger is often the first face of military authority they meet, directing them toward training and their first duties in service of Sylvanas.",["faction:Horde"]="Dillinger represents the Forsaken's struggle to carve out a secure foothold in northern Lordaeron, showing Horde allies the relentless menace posed by mindless Scourge at their doorsteps.",["class:Warrior"]="Aspiring Forsaken warriors frequently work directly with Dillinger to test their martial mettle against the ferocious ghouls and skeletal horrors prowling the outskirts of Brill."},kw={"a putrid task","brill","deathguard","deathguard dillinger","dillinger","the mills overrun","tirisfal glades","ulag the cleaver"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:404","quest:426","quest:1819","quest:1818","subzone:cold-hearth-manor"},src={"Deathguard Dillinger"}}
E["npc:deathguard-linnea"] = {t="npc",n="Deathguard Linnea",z="tirisfal",s="Deathguard Linnea is a Forsaken soldier stationed at a Deathguard waystation in southeastern Tirisfal Glades. She helps secure the roads between Brill and the eastern borders, watching for threats posed by the Scarlet Crusade and lingering Scourge. Adventurers passing along the main thoroughfares often assist her unit with patrol duties.",h="Deathguard Linnea is a Forsaken soldier stationed at a Deathguard waystation in southeastern Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Vigil on the Roadside",b="Deathguard Linnea stands sentinel at a small fortified post southeast of Brill. Life—or unlife—on patrol in Tirisfal Glades is treacherous, as the Forsaken must vigilantly defend their fledgling realm against Scarlet Crusade infiltrators and mindless Scourge prowling the wilderness. Stationed alongside her fellow guards, Linnea oversees patrol efforts along the roadway, coordinating reports with local authorities such as Magistrate Sevren in Brill to keep the realm's southern approach secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Deathguard Linnea?",a="Linnea is an undead soldier serving in the Deathguard, the military force charged with defending Forsaken territory. She is posted at a Deathguard waystation along the road southeast of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"linnea","deathguard linnea lore","who is linnea"}},{q="Why does she need help patrolling?",a="Forsaken military resources are stretched thin across Tirisfal Glades. Linnea relies on passing travelers and rising champions to help clear out surrounding threats, ensuring messengers and supply runners can travel safely back and forth to Brill.",al={"rear guard patrol lore","why help linnea"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken newly risen from Deathknell, aiding Deathguard Linnea at the waystation is one of the earliest duties undertaken to secure the borders of their new kingdom.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies of the Forsaken traversing Tirisfal Glades can earn goodwill by assisting Linnea in stabilizing the outlying roadways against hostile forces."},kw={"brill","deathguard linnea","forsaken","magistrate sevren","rear guard patrol","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:356","quest:360"},src={"Deathguard Linnea"}}
E["npc:deathguard-simmer"] = {t="npc",n="Deathguard Simmer",z="tirisfal",s="Deathguard Simmer is a Forsaken soldier stationed just outside Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He sizes up newly risen Forsaken and sends them to raid the human pumpkin farm to the west, patrolled by the Scarlet Crusade, for reagents the apothecaries of Brill need.",h="Deathguard Simmer is a Forsaken soldier stationed just outside Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Guardian Outside Deathknell",b="Posted just outside Deathknell, Deathguard Simmer represents the Forsaken military line holding the border between the safe haven of Deathknell and the wider perils of Tirisfal Glades. The region crawls with living humans—most notably the fanatical Scarlet Crusade—who view all undead as abominations to be purged with fire and blade. Simmer tests fledgling Forsaken by sending them west to the Solliden Farmstead, where the Scarlet Crusade patrols from its nearby tower, to steal ten pumpkins and carry them to Apothecary Johaan in Brill.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Deathguard Simmer?",a="Deathguard Simmer is a Forsaken sentry stationed just outside Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He tests fledgling Forsaken by sending them to steal pumpkins from a human farm patrolled by the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"deathguard simmer","who is simmer","simmer npc"},it="identity"},{q="Where is Deathguard Simmer located?",a="He stands just outside Deathknell in southwestern Tirisfal Glades, along the road newly risen Forsaken take out of the crypts.",al={"deathguard simmer location","where is simmer","find deathguard simmer"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What threats does Deathguard Simmer fight?",a="Simmer despises the living humans who still farm Tirisfal and the Scarlet Crusade that patrols near them. He sends fledgling Forsaken to raid the Solliden Farmstead for pumpkins, teaching those humans a lesson while supplying the apothecaries of Brill.",al={"simmer enemies","what does simmer fight","scarlet palisade quests"},it="threat"},{q="Whose side is Deathguard Simmer on?",a="Deathguard Simmer serves the Forsaken and the Dark Lady, Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. As a sworn member of the Deathguard, the standing military force of the Undercity, he is dedicated to defending Forsaken territory and stamping out all threats to their newborn kingdom.",al={"simmer faction","who does simmer serve","deathguard simmer allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Deathguard Simmer here?",a="Deathguard Simmer is posted just outside Deathknell, watching over the road that newly risen Forsaken take into Tirisfal Glades. With living humans still farming the nearby fields and the Scarlet Crusade patrolling from its tower to the west, he tests fledgling Forsaken by sending them to raid the Solliden Farmstead.",al={"why is deathguard simmer here","simmer calston estate","why is simmer stationed here"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Deathguard Simmer want?",a="Simmer wants fledgling Forsaken to prove themselves to the Dark Lady. He sends them to steal pumpkins from the human farm to the west, where the Scarlet Crusade patrols, teaching the living a lesson while supplying Apothecary Johaan in Brill with much-needed reagents.",al={"what does deathguard simmer need","simmer goals","what does simmer want you to do"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Deathguard Simmer tied to?",a="Simmer belongs to the Deathguard, the soldiers who defend Forsaken lands in Tirisfal Glades. He works with Apothecary Johaan in Brill, sending fledgling Forsaken to him with the pumpkins they steal from the nearby human farm.",al={"simmer connections","who does simmer report to","deathguard simmer ties"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Simmer offers some of the first missions directly targeting the Scarlet Crusade, marking an undead adventurer's graduation from the crypts of Deathknell into the broader defense of Lordaeron."},kw={"calston estate","deathguard","deathguard simmer","deathknell","scarlet palisade","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:365"},src={"Deathguard Simmer"}}
E["npc:elder-graveborn"] = {t="npc",n="Elder Graveborn",z="tirisfal",s="Elder Graveborn is an ancestral spirit who manifests just southeast of Brill during the Lunar Festival. Appearing as a spectral earthen elder, he honors travelers and guides pilgrims searching for other ancient spirits scattered throughout northern Lordaeron.",h="Elder Graveborn is an ancestral spirit who manifests just southeast of Brill during the Lunar Festival.",sec={{t="A Spirit in Tirisfal",b="Appearing just beyond the Forsaken town of Brill, Elder Graveborn stands among the haunted woods of Tirisfal Glades as part of the worldwide Lunar Festival. Despite the blighted surroundings and the troubled state of the kingdom, the spectral elder offers peaceful greetings to travelers seeking the festival's traditional blessings. In addition to bestowing his own ancestral wisdom, Graveborn shares news of other elders lingering nearby—pointing pilgrims toward spirits abiding in the Undercity, the Sepulcher, and across the Plaguelands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elder Graveborn?",a="Elder Graveborn is a revered ancestral ghost who appears during the Lunar Festival. He materializes in Tirisfal Glades just southeast of Brill to grant festival blessings to passing adventurers.",al={"elder graveborn lore","who is graveborn","brill ghost elder"},it="identity"},{q="Why is he in Tirisfal Glades?",a="During the Lunar Festival, ancestral spirits appear across Azeroth to celebrate past heroes and communal heritage. Elder Graveborn watches over the lands outside Brill, maintaining an ancient presence amidst the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron.",al={"why brill","where is elder graveborn"},it="why_here"},{q="Can Alliance adventurers speak to him?",a="Yes. Festival elders welcome travelers of both factions regardless of territory. However, Alliance characters must exercise caution when approaching his resting ground, as he stands in close proximity to the Forsaken hub of Brill.",al={"can alliance talk to graveborn","alliance elder brill"},it="side"},{q="What information does he offer travelers?",a="Elder Graveborn guides pilgrims toward other local ancestors, sharing rumors of spirits residing in the Undercity, Silverpine's Sepulcher, Light's Hope Chapel, the Weeping Cave, and Scholomance.",al={"elder graveborn directions","graveborn quotes"}},{q="What does Elder Graveborn want?",a="Elder Graveborn desires to share his ancient blessings and ancestral wisdom with those who travel the lands during the Lunar Festival. As an earthen spirit returned to honor the world's heritage, he asks travelers to pay their respects to the past, bestowing his blessing and directing them toward other honored elders scattered across the continent.",al={"elder graveborn goal","why visit elder graveborn","what does graveborn want"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Elder Graveborn tied to?",a="Graveborn is bound to the ancient spirits of the Lunar Festival, specifically the scattered circle of earthen elders wandering the northern kingdoms. He maintains spiritual communion with his kin across Lordaeron, keeping track of Elder Darkcore in Undercity, Obsidian in Silverpine, Snowcrown in Light's Hope Chapel, and others watching over troubled ruins.",al={"elder graveborn relations","who does elder graveborn know","graveborn connections"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken walking the road between Brill and the Undercity, Graveborn serves as a tranquil reminder of Azeroth's ancient past, resting untroubled by the Scourge or the blight.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance pilgrims must skirt the outskirts of Brill carefully to pay their respects to Graveborn without drawing the wrath of Forsaken town guards and patrols."},kw={"ancestor","brill","earthen","elder graveborn","graveborn","lunar festival","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:8652","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","zone:undercity","npc:elder-darkcore","npc:elder-obsidian","subzone:sepulcher","subzone:light-s-hope-chapel"},src={"Elder Graveborn"}}
E["npc:eleanor-shackleton"] = {t="npc",n="Eleanor Shackleton",z="tirisfal",s="Eleanor Shackleton is a Forsaken outdoorswoman found camping southwest of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Rather than lingering within town or the Undercity, she assists wandering travelers in learning wilderness survival and field crafts. She shares her surname with other Forsaken surviving in the region, suggesting lingering ties from life before the plague.",h="Eleanor Shackleton is a Forsaken outdoorswoman found camping southwest of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Guide to the Glades",b="While many newly awakened Forsaken head straight to Brill or delve into the Undercity, Eleanor Shackleton makes her camp in the haunted woods to the southwest. Surviving out in Tirisfal requires pragmatic self-reliance, and Eleanor serves as an instructor for fledgling adventurers seeking to live off the blighted land. Through her various field lessons, she guides travelers in establishing campsites, gathering firewood, and maintaining trade crafts while roaming far from civilization's forge fires and kitchens.",sp=0},{t="Ties from a Past Life",b="In the shattered remnants of Lordaeron, family names often serve as the sole surviving tether to an undead's mortal past. Eleanor carries the surname Shackleton, shared by Pierce Shackleton in Brill and Samantha Shackleton elsewhere in Tirisfal. Whether the three maintain close bonds or merely carry common ancestry from their mortal days, each has carved out a distinct role in serving the Forsaken cause across the blighted kingdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Eleanor Shackleton?",a="Eleanor Shackleton makes her camp southwest of Brill, tucked into the woods of Tirisfal Glades away from the main paved road.",al={"eleanor shackleton location","where is eleanor","shackleton spot"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What does Eleanor Shackleton teach adventurers?",a="Eleanor guides travelers through the basics of wilderness survival, offering instruction on camping, foraging, and practicing professions out in the wild.",al={"eleanor quests","what does eleanor do","camping quests tirisfal"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Is Eleanor related to other Shackletons in Lordaeron?",a="She shares her family name with Pierce Shackleton in Brill and Samantha Shackleton, indicating potential kinship from their lives before the kingdom fell to the Scourge.",al={"pierce shackleton relative","samantha shackleton","shackleton family lore"},it="ties"},{q="Why is she camping outside Brill instead of staying in town?",a="Eleanor focuses on fieldcraft and wilderness survival. Setting up her camp in the woods allows her to meet wayfarers and train them directly where those skills are needed.",al={"why camp outside brill","eleanor shackleton camp"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is Eleanor Shackleton?",a="Eleanor Shackleton is a Forsaken outdoorswoman who has set up camp in the woods southwest of Brill. Rather than lingering in town, she embraces survival in the wilds of Tirisfal Glades, guiding fledgling undead on the fundamentals of enduring the outdoors and practicing basic trades in the field.",al={"who is eleanor shackleton","eleanor shackleton identity","eleanor shackleton info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Eleanor Shackleton on?",a="Eleanor is loyal to the Forsaken and the Dark Lady. She operates just outside Brill, helping newly risen members of her people adapt to their unlife by teaching them how to survive, harvest materials, and hone useful crafts across Lordaeron's haunted glades.",al={"eleanor shackleton faction","is eleanor shackleton horde","eleanor shackleton allegiances"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Eleanor Shackleton want?",a="Eleanor wants adventurers to learn essential survival skills and wilderness trades. By sending travelers out to practice professions like herbalism, skinning, and cooking under open skies, she ensures that Forsaken who venture beyond the safety of settlements are properly equipped to sustain themselves.",al={"what does eleanor want","eleanor shackleton goals","eleanor shackleton motives"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, learning to survive in the wilderness without living comforts is vital, and Eleanor ensures that newly risen brethren adapt quickly to roughing it in Tirisfal.",["profession:Cooking"]="Eleanor's instruction covers setting up a field campfire and preparing meals away from civilized hearths, making her camp a key stop for traveling cooks."},kw={"brill","camping 101","eleanor shackleton","shackleton","sticks and bones","the adventurer","the great outdoors","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:96658","quest:96607","quest:86784","quest:96627"},src={"Eleanor Shackleton"}}
E["npc:executor-arren"] = {t="npc",n="Executor Arren",z="tirisfal",s="Executor Arren is a high-ranking Forsaken officer stationed in Deathknell. He oversees the immediate defense and consolidation of the settlement as newly awakened undead emerge from their graves. Arren coordinates the local campaign against mindless Scourge zombies, feral beasts, and the encroaching zealots of the Scarlet Crusade.",h="Executor Arren is a high-ranking Forsaken officer stationed in Deathknell.",sec={{t="Commander of Deathknell",b="Standing resolute just outside the chapel in Deathknell, Executor Arren serves as the primary military voice for the nascent Forsaken within their awakening grounds. While the caretakers in the crypts guide newly raised undead through their disorientation, Arren directs their focus outward toward survival. Under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner's banner, he organizes local operations to sweep away mindless zombies that threaten the village borders, establishing secure territory in the shadow of Lordaeron's fall.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Borderlands",b="Deathknell is surrounded by perils far deadlier than confused fledglings. Under Arren's orders, scouts and initiates strike at the vicious arachnids inhabiting Night Web's Hollow and eliminate dangerous predators haunting the woodland fringes. By demanding decisive action from every capable hand, Arren tests the fortitude of new arrivals, ensuring only those capable of defending their second chance at existence remain in the service of the Dark Lady.",sp=0},{t="The Crimson Threat",b="Arren's greatest strategic concern lies with the Scarlet Crusade. The zealots consider all undead to be monstrosities requiring total eradication, failing or refusing to distinguish between the Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken. Arren organizes targeted strikes against Scarlet patrols encamped in the Tirisfal wilderness, intercepting couriers and analyzing stolen dispatches to safeguard Deathknell from being purged in holy fire.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Executor Arren?",a="Executor Arren is the commanding military officer overseeing Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He organizes newly risen Forsaken into an effective fighting force to defend the village and secure the surrounding countryside.",al={"arren role","who is arren","deathknell executor"},it="identity"},{q="What rank is an Executor among the Forsaken?",a="Executors are trusted military and administrative commanders within the Forsaken hierarchy. They govern local garrisons, direct operations in the field, and enforce the will and law of Queen Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"executor title","what is an executor","forsaken executor rank"}},{q="Why does Arren send recruits against the zombies?",a="The zombies prowling near Deathknell are mindless Scourge remnants left behind after the plague devastated Lordaeron. Arren orders them cleared so that newly awakened undead are not torn apart upon leaving the crypts.",al={"why kill zombies","the damned quest","mindless scourge"},it="motive"},{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is a radical human faction dedicated to exterminating all undead. They hold outposts across the ruins of Lordaeron and actively seek to purge the Forsaken, considering them identical to the Scourge.",al={"scarlet crusade tirisfal","why crusade here","crusaders in deathknell"},it="threat"},{q="What is Night Web's Hollow?",a="Night Web's Hollow is a spider-infested cave north of Deathknell. Arren directs recruits to clear out the aggressive arachnids to eliminate a major local hazard and demonstrate martial competence.",al={"spider cave deathknell","night webs hollow","arren spider quest"},it="place"},{q="Where does Arren send recruits once they finish his tasks?",a="Once Deathknell's immediate threats are neutralized, Arren directs capable recruits outward to Brill to report to Executor Zygand and aid the broader Forsaken effort across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"next after deathknell","where to go after arren","the executor in the field"},it="next_step"},{q="Whose side is Executor Arren on?",a="Executor Arren is utterly loyal to the Forsaken and the Dark Lady, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. As the local military commander in Deathknell, his duty is to secure the starting grounds for his newly awakened brethren and eliminate any threats to their young nation, whether mindless Scourge zombies, local beasts, or the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"arren allegiance","who does executor arren serve","arren faction"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Executor Arren want?",a="Arren seeks to firmly establish Forsaken control over Deathknell and test the resolve of the newly risen. He wants the mindless undead cleared from the graveyard, the encroaching arachnids purged, and the nearby camps of the Scarlet Crusade crushed so that fresh recruits can safely depart for Brill and join the broader defense of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what does arren want","arren goals","executor arren purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Executor Arren in Deathknell?",a="Arren is stationed in Deathknell to command the initial defense where newly freed Forsaken first break from the grave. Without military oversight, confused recruits could easily be overrun by mindless Scourge wandering the hills or fall prey to Scarlet Crusade scouts probing the settlement's perimeter.",al={"why is arren in deathknell","arren location reason","why is executor arren here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Executor Arren is one of the first commanders every Forsaken meets upon emerging into Tirisfal, giving them their earliest taste of duty under the Dark Lady.",["faction:Horde"]="Arren's frontline commands in Deathknell represent the Horde's initial foothold in northern Lordaeron, securing survival amidst hostile surroundings."},kw={"deathknell","executor arren","forsaken","night web's hollow","scarlet crusade","sylvanas","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:night-web-s-hollow","quest:96627"},src={"Executor Arren"}}
E["npc:executor-zygand"] = {t="npc",n="Executor Zygand",z="tirisfal",s="Executor Zygand is a senior Forsaken commander stationed at the town hall in Brill, overseeing defensive and military operations across central Tirisfal Glades. Reporting directly to the Undercity's high command, he coordinates the purge of Scarlet Crusade forces pressing into Forsaken territory.",h="Executor Zygand is a senior Forsaken commander stationed at the town hall in Brill, overseeing defensive and...",sec={{t="Command in Brill",b="Positioned at the administrative heart of Brill, Executor Zygand coordinates regional defense for the Forsaken after their initial awakening in Deathknell. Serving as the direct superior to regional officers like Executor Arren and field sentries such as Deathguard Linnea, Zygand synthesizes scout intelligence and dispatches able recruits to safeguard Lordaeron's borders from encroachment.",sp=0},{t="The War on the Crusade",b="Zygand's primary military concern is the fanatic presence of the Scarlet Crusade, which infests outposts across Tirisfal Glades. Under his direction, agents are sent systematically to strike at Crusader scouts, encampments, and forward bases, ensuring the zealots never muster sufficient strength to threaten the approaches to the Undercity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Executor Zygand?",a="Executor Zygand is a senior Forsaken officer operating out of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. He commands local Deathguard garrisons, receives tactical reports from lower-ranking officers, and directs military campaigns against local threats.",al={"zygand","who is executor zygand","zygand role"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Executor Zygand?",a="He is located inside or near the main town hall of Brill, the central Forsaken settlement in Tirisfal Glades situated just north of the Undercity.",al={"zygand location","where is zygand","executor zygand map"},gp=true},{q="What is Zygand's main goal in Tirisfal Glades?",a="His primary focus is orchestrating the regional war against the Scarlet Crusade. He monitors the zealots' movements throughout Tirisfal and issues orders to dismantle their encampments and leadership before they can march on the Undercity.",al={"zygand quest goal","why fight crusade","zygand purpose"},it="wants"},{q="Who reports to Executor Zygand?",a="Zygand acts as the superior officer to Executor Arren, who directs initial recruits in Deathknell, as well as field sentries like Deathguard Linnea patrolling the main crossroads of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"zygand subordinates","who serves zygand","arren superior"},it="ties"},{q="Does Executor Zygand handle rogue undead threats?",a="Yes. In addition to fighting the Scarlet Crusade, Zygand issues regional bounties to eliminate feral undead and dangerous rogue scavengers, such as the corpse-stealing gnoll Maggot Eye, that endanger travel through the glades.",al={"maggot eye","zygand bounties","wanted quests"}},{q="What rank does an 'Executor' hold among the Forsaken?",a="An Executor serves as a regional governor and military commander within the Forsaken hierarchy. They manage settlements, command local units of the Deathguard, and enforce Queen Sylvanas's rule across Lordaeron's contested regions.",al={"what is an executor","executor rank","forsaken executor"}},{q="Whose side is Executor Zygand on?",a="Executor Zygand is wholly devoted to the Forsaken and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. As a military officer in Brill, he serves the Undercity by coordinating local defenses, directing deathguards, and organizing strategic strikes to secure Tirisfal Glades against the Forsaken's relentless enemies.",al={"zygand faction","who does zygand serve","executor zygand side"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Executor Zygand stationed in Brill?",a="Zygand is stationed in Brill because the settlement serves as the Forsaken's primary military bastion directly north of the Undercity. From here, he monitors critical threats across Tirisfal Glades, managing frontline patrols and deploying seasoned fighters to keep vital supply lines and borders secure.",al={"why is zygand here","zygand brill purpose","why is executor zygand in brill"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken venturing out of Deathknell, Executor Zygand represents the wider military authority of the Undercity, welcoming new citizens into active service against their bitterest enemies.",["faction:Horde"]="Travelers from Kalimdor visiting Tirisfal Glades see in Zygand the structured, disciplined military leadership driving the Forsaken's struggle to secure Lordaeron."},kw={"arren","brill","deathguard","executor","maggot eye","scarlet crusade","tirisfal","zygand"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","quest:370","npc:executor-arren","npc:deathguard-linnea","quest:398","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:forsaken"},src={"Executor Zygand"}}
E["npc:gunther-arcanus"] = {t="npc",n="Gunther Arcanus",z="tirisfal",s="Gunther Arcanus is an exceptionally powerful necromancer dwelling on an isolated island in Brightwater Lake. Slain during the Third War and raised into undeath, he managed to shatter the Lich King's mental grip entirely on his own. Long believing he was the sole undead possessing free will, he defended his retreat against all outsiders until discovered by the Forsaken.",h="Gunther Arcanus is an exceptionally powerful necromancer dwelling on an isolated island in Brightwater Lake.",sec={{t="A Student of Dalaran",b="Before the fall of Lordaeron, Gunther was a promising apprentice among the Kirin Tor in Dalaran. Alongside close friends Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw, he studied the arcane arts, secretly delving into necromancy despite the Kirin Tor's strict prohibitions against dark magic. To commemorate their brotherhood, the trio once bound their apprentice wands together into a single heirloom.",sp=0},{t="Breaking the Shackles",b="When the Scourge swept across the north during the Third War, Gunther perished to the Plague of Undeath and was raised into servitude. Unlike most who required the Lich King's power to weaken or Lady Sylvanas to break their chains, Gunther's raw arcane mastery and sheer willpower allowed him to shake off the Lich King's mental domination entirely by himself. Convinced that every other walking corpse remained a mindless thrall of the Scourge, he retreated to an island in Tirisfal Glades, surrounding himself with shambling guardians and cutting off all contact with the living and dead alike.",sp=0},{t="The Prodigal Lich",b="Seeking powerful allies against the Scourge, the Forsaken sent emissaries to claim the mysterious necromancer's spellbook. Upon examining the arcane tome in the Undercity, Bethor Iceshard recognized the unique handwriting of his long-lost peer. By enchanting the returned grimoire and demanding the destruction of local Scourge commanders, Gunther finally realized he was not alone in his freedom. Convinced that Sylvanas's faction represents a true rebellion against the Scourge, he has forged a pact with his former schoolmate to aid the Forsaken.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Gunther Arcanus?",a="He is a former wizard of the Kirin Tor from Dalaran who perished during the Third War. Raised into undeath, he broke free of the Lich King's domination on his own and retreated to an island in Brightwater Lake in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"who is gunther","gunther lore","gunther arcanus story"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Gunther look like a regular Forsaken instead of a skeletal lich?",a="While traditional liches transfer their spirits into phylacteries and generate monstrous, floating skeletal forms, Gunther achieved his lich-like necromantic status without abandoning his original reanimated corpse, preserving his physical mortal appearance.",al={"why does gunther look human","gunther not a skeleton","why is gunther a lich"}},{q="Why was Gunther living alone on an island?",a="After breaking the Lich King's mental control, Gunther believed he was the only undead in existence with free will. Assuming all other walking dead were still mindless slaves of the Scourge, he secluded himself and ordered his minions to strike down anyone who approached.",al={"why is gunther alone","gunther island isolation","why does gunther hide"},it="why_here"},{q="How does Gunther know Bethor Iceshard?",a="They were classmates and close friends in Dalaran studying under the Kirin Tor, along with a third mage named Thule Ravenclaw. The three friends even wove their apprentice wands together as a symbol of their bond.",al={"bethor and gunther","how does gunther know bethor","bethor iceshard history"},it="ties"},{q="Does Gunther join the Forsaken?",a="Yes. Once adventurers return his enchanted spellbook and execute the local Scourge agent Lillith Nefara to prove their independence from the Lich King, Gunther pledges his allegiance to Bethor and the Forsaken cause.",al={"does gunther join horde","gunther faction","is gunther forsaken"},sp=1,it="side"},{q="Who is Lillith Nefara?",a="Lillith Nefara is an agent of the Scourge stationed in Tirisfal Glades. Gunther requires her death as proof that the adventurer truly serves an enemy of the Lich King rather than the Scourge itself.",al={"lillith nefara","nefara","gunther target"},sp=1},{q="What does Gunther Arcanus want?",a="Above all, Gunther values his independence from the Lich King. Having broken free through sheer willpower, he spent years in isolation guarding against what he presumed were Scourge thralls. He desires solitude from the Scourge and seeks proof before trusting anyone claiming to share his freedom, wanting assurance that you truly oppose the Lich King's dominion.",al={"what does gunther want","gunther arcanus goal","gunther arcanus motivation"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Gunther Arcanus famous or feared?",a="Even as a mortal Dalaran mage, Gunther mastered the forbidden art of necromancy. In undeath, he achieved the rare status of a lich while retaining his original mortal form. Most terrifying of all, he shattered the Lich King's psychic control entirely on his own, commanding undead minions under his own absolute will.",al={"is gunther arcanus powerful","why is gunther arcanus feared","gunther arcanus reputation"},it="fame",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Gunther's self-liberation makes him an anomaly among the Forsaken, proving that intense arcane training and iron will alone could sometimes shatter the Lich King's mental dominion.",["class:Mage"]="Trained in Dalaran, Gunther represents the Kirin Tor wizards who covertly dabbled in forbidden necromantic spells before the Scourge invaded Lordaeron.",["class:Warlock"]="Gunther's commanding grasp of dark arts and reanimation demonstrates how far forbidden scholarship could flourish outside the watchful eyes of Dalaran's leadership."},kw={"arcanus","bethor","brightwater","dalaran","gunther","iceshard","kirin tor","lich","necromancer","thule","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:gunther-s-retreat","npc:bethor-iceshard","quest:405","quest:366","quest:409","quest:411","npc:thor","quest:446"},src={"Gunther Arcanus"}}
E["npc:high-executor-derrington"] = {t="npc",n="High Executor Derrington",z="tirisfal",s="High Executor Derrington commands the Forsaken vanguard stationed at the Bulwark on the eastern border of Tirisfal Glades. A no-nonsense military officer, he oversees the Horde's front-line defense against the Scourge and Scarlet Crusade threats emanating from the Western Plaguelands. From this fortified barricade, he directs seasoned champions toward contested ruins like Andorhal and Scholomance.",h="High Executor Derrington commands the Forsaken vanguard stationed at the Bulwark on the eastern border of...",sec={{t="Commander at the Threshold",b="Standing directly before the fortified barricades of the Bulwark, High Executor Derrington serves as the gatekeeper between the haunted safety of Tirisfal Glades and the rotting horrors of the Western Plaguelands. Derrington holds little patience for political maneuvering in the Undercity, viewing the front lines as a crucible of sheer survival. For Horde soldiers and sellswords seeking to test their mettle, Derrington provides direct military orders, sending scouts and strike teams deep into plagueridden territory to blunt enemy incursions before they breach Tirisfal.",sp=0},{t="A War on Two Fronts",b="Derrington's defensive position is beleaguered on multiple axes. To the east lies the Scourge, entrenched in the shattered town of Andorhal and spilling through the countryside via corrupting cauldrons. Simultaneously, the zealous fanatics of the Scarlet Crusade contest northern fortifications and skirmish relentlessly with Forsaken patrols. Derrington coordinates surgical counterattacks, sabotage against the Scarlet lines, and the systematic disruption of Scourge cauldron fields to safeguard the eastern border.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is High Executor Derrington?",a="High Executor Derrington is the senior Forsaken commander at the Bulwark, the military outpost separating Tirisfal Glades from the Western Plaguelands. He coordinates the Horde's strategic push eastward against both the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"derrington","who is derrington","high executor derrington lore"},it="identity"},{q="Where is Derrington located?",a="He is stationed at the Bulwark, the fortified choke point located at the far eastern edge of Tirisfal Glades along the road leading into the Western Plaguelands.",al={"where is derrington","derrington location","find high executor derrington"},gp=true},{q="What is Derrington's objective at the Bulwark?",a="Derrington's goal is to ensure the survival of the Forsaken by maintaining a strong defensive perimeter against the Scourge and striking eastward into the Western Plaguelands. He tasks adventurers with scouting Andorhal, disabling plague cauldrons, and neutralizing Scarlet Crusade operations.",al={"what does derrington want","bulwark purpose","derrington mission"},it="wants"},{q="Does Derrington care about Undercity politics?",a="No. Derrington expresses open disdain for court schemers and politicians, noting that such luxuries do not exist on the front lines where survival must be fought for every day.",al={"derrington personality","is derrington political"}},{q="What major threats does Derrington send soldiers against?",a="Derrington primarily targets the Scourge holding the ruins of Andorhal, the dark necromancers inside Scholomance, and nearby Scarlet Crusade garrisons that threaten the Horde's eastern supply routes.",al={"derrington quests","what does derrington fight"},it="threat"},{q="Why is the Bulwark so heavily fortified?",a="The Bulwark stands as the primary chokepoint between the relative security of Tirisfal Glades and the active combat zones of the Plaguelands. Derrington's forces hold the pass to prevent massive Scourge swarms or Scarlet Crusade crusades from sweeping directly toward the Undercity.",al={"why is the bulwark here","bulwark lore"},it="place"},{q="Whose side is High Executor Derrington on?",a="High Executor Derrington stands firmly with the Forsaken and the Horde. As a high-ranking military commander under the Dark Lady, he is dedicated to defending their realm and securing their place in Lordaeron against all who would see them eradicated.",al={"derrington faction","who does derrington serve","is derrington horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is High Executor Derrington stationed at the Bulwark?",a="Derrington commands the frontline defense of Tirisfal Glades. Stationed at the border between Tirisfal and the Western Plaguelands, he oversees the fortification that halts Scourge incursions and prepares the Forsaken's advance eastward into the heart of the blighted lands.",al={"why is derrington here","derrington at the bulwark","why is derrington at the border"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="For Horde travelers arriving in Tirisfal, Derrington is the principal military commander directing forces into the dangerous conflicts of the Western Plaguelands.",["race:Undead"]="Derrington represents the martial backbone of the Forsaken military, focused squarely on preserving their reclaimed lands from Scourge reclamation and Scarlet fanaticism.",["class:Warrior"]="Derrington embodies the grim, practical grit of a front-line soldier who values martial competence and battlefield results over Court scheming."},kw={"andorhal","bulwark","derrington","forsaken","high executor derrington","scourge","tirisfal glades","western plaguelands"},rel={"subzone:bulwark","zone:tirisfal","subzone:andorhal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:scholomance","subzone:cauldron"},src={"High Executor Derrington"}}
E["npc:magistrate-sevren"] = {t="npc",n="Magistrate Sevren",z="tirisfal",s="Magistrate Sevren is the chief administrative official presiding over the settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Standing inside the town hall, he organizes early Forsaken defense and directs arriving champions to stabilize the territory around the Undercity.",h="Magistrate Sevren is the chief administrative official presiding over the settlement of Brill in Tirisfal...",sec={{t="Town Magistrate of Brill",b="Operating from the town hall in Brill, Magistrate Sevren coordinates civil authority and strategic efforts in the lands just north of the Undercity. As new Forsaken step forth into Tirisfal Glades, Sevren assesses local crises and dispatches adventurers to restore order, secure ruined infrastructure, and suppress persistent threats lingering in the surrounding hills.",sp=0},{t="Securing the Graves",b="Sevren's primary concerns center on the preservation and recovery of the Tirisfal dead. Local graverobbers, rot hides, and rogue undead disrupt burial grounds, threatening the emerging dominion of Sylvanas Windrunner. Sevren ensures that ancestral tombs—such as the Agamand Family Crypt—are reclaimed and that deserters or uncooperative forces do not subvert Forsaken authority in the region.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Magistrate Sevren?",a="Magistrate Sevren is the administrative head of Brill, stationed within the town hall. He serves as an overseer for Forsaken territorial management, handing out tasks to adventurers who arrive from Deathknell to aid the fledgling realm.",al={"magistrate sevren","who is sevren","brill magistrate"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Magistrate Sevren be found?",a="He is located inside the Town Hall in the center of Brill, in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"sevren location","where is magistrate sevren","find sevren"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What threats does Magistrate Sevren deal with?",a="Sevren contends with local threats to Tirisfal Glades, including human graverobbers, rogue rot hides, Scourge remnants desecrating local crypts, and rogue undead figures who refuse allegiance to Lady Sylvanas.",al={"sevren quests","what does sevren want","brill problems"},it="threat"},{q="What is Magistrate Sevren's allegiance?",a="He is loyal to the Forsaken and governs Brill on behalf of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Undercity.",al={"sevren faction","who does sevren serve"},it="side"},{q="Why does Sevren send adventurers to the family crypt?",a="The crypts belonging to the old Agamand dynasty are overrun with undead and scavenging threats. Sevren demands that adventurers secure the ancestral resting places to prevent enemies from exploiting them.",al={"agamand crypt","sevren crypt quest"},it="motive"},{q="Does Sevren have ties to the Undercity leadership?",a="Yes. As the local magistrate, he frequently liaises with agents and officials from the Undercity, dispatching messengers and directing adventurers deeper into the capital once their competence in Brill is proven.",al={"sevren undercity","sevren raleigh"},it="ties"},{q="What does Magistrate Sevren want?",a="As Brill's chief civil authority, Sevren wants to secure Tirisfal Glades for the Forsaken. He coordinates efforts to root out lingering Scourge, put down grave robbers desecrating Forsaken resting grounds, and maintain clear lines of command and supply heading deeper into the kingdom.",al={"magistrate sevren goals","sevren motives","what does sevren want"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Magistrate Sevren in Brill?",a="Sevren presides over Brill's Town Hall to oversee local administration and law just outside the gates of the Undercity. He serves as the primary municipal authority directing freshly awakened and arriving Forsaken into service to protect and organize the realm.",al={"why is sevren in brill","magistrate sevren town hall","sevren location purpose"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly liberated Forsaken leaving Deathknell, Magistrate Sevren represents the civic face of Sylvanas's realm, providing your first major assignments to safeguard your homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies of the Horde arriving in Tirisfal will find Sevren coordinating the front-line pacification of Tirisfal Glades against bandits, Scourge remnants, and rebel factions."},kw={"agamand","brill","forsaken","magistrate","sevren","tirisfal","town hall","undercity"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:355","quest:360","quest:358","quest:408","zone:undercity"},src={"Magistrate Sevren"}}
E["npc:novice-elreth"] = {t="npc",n="Novice Elreth",z="tirisfal",s="Novice Elreth is an undead member of the Forsaken stationed inside the chapel at Deathknell. As newly risen undead awaken into their second life, she directs them to confront the mindless Scourge haunting the nearby graves and tend to the tragic aftermath of the plague.",h="Novice Elreth is an undead member of the Forsaken stationed inside the chapel at Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Guide in the Church of the Damned",b="Inside the desecrated chapel at Deathknell, Novice Elreth assists the emerging undead as they struggle to understand their horrific awakening. Rather than succumbing to despair or the mindless hunger of the Scourge, the Forsaken must organize quickly to secure their territory. Elreth provides newly freed souls with early tasks, directing them to scavenge grave dust from mindless corpses and deal with the lingering remnants of Lordaeron's tragic downfall, including the lingering sorrow of Samuel Fitts and his departed wife Marla.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Novice Elreth located?",a="Novice Elreth stands inside the chapel in Deathknell, the ruined village in Tirisfal Glades where newly created Forsaken awaken.",al={"elreth location","where is elreth","novice elreth church"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Novice Elreth's role in Deathknell?",a="She acts as a local guide and taskmaster for newly arisen Forsaken. She gives out vital early directives, teaching fresh undead to strike against mindless Scourge prowling the village cemetery.",al={"what does elreth do","elreth role","novice elreth purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="What quests does Novice Elreth offer?",a="She assigns missions such as The Damned, asking adventurers to harvest scavenging materials from mindless zombies, and assists Samuel Fitts with his tragic request regarding his late wife in Marla's Last Wish.",al={"novice elreth quests","elreth tasks"},gp=true},{q="Why is she titled 'Novice'?",a="Given her location in the chapel and her title, Elreth is an initiate among the Forsaken's religious or civic ranks, learning the dark practicalities of managing Tirisfal's freshly raised undead under the shadow of the Forsaken order.",al={"why novice","novice meaning elreth","is elreth a priest"},it="name"},{q="Who is Novice Elreth?",a="Novice Elreth is an undead attendant stationed inside the desecrated church of Deathknell. As one of the early caretakers assisting freshly awakened Forsaken, she helps guide newly risen souls through the harsh reality of their undeath and integrates them into service under the Dark Lady.",al={"who is novice elreth","novice elreth lore","about novice elreth"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Novice Elreth on?",a="She is entirely loyal to the Forsaken and Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. Operating out of Deathknell, her work directly supports the Undercity's efforts to secure Tirisfal Glades by evaluating and directing the newly risen.",al={"novice elreth faction","who does novice elreth serve","novice elreth loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Novice Elreth want?",a="Elreth wants to ensure that newly freed undead understand their state, confront the truth of their graves, and find their purpose within the Forsaken. She tests the resolve of the newly risen and directs capable hands to the local leadership to aid the war effort.",al={"what does elreth want","novice elreth goal","why does elreth give quests"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Novice Elreth here in Deathknell?",a="Deathknell is where the Forsaken uncover and raise new corpses to join their ranks. Elreth remains in the chapel to minister to these disoriented souls immediately upon their awakening, ensuring they do not succumb to despair or turn against their new kin.",al={"why is elreth in deathknell","why is novice elreth in the church"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For fresh Forsaken crawling out of the grave, Novice Elreth offers some of the first practical steps toward survival, focusing cold hatred against the mindless Scourge.",["class:Priest"]="Standing within the former holy grounds of Lordaeron, Elreth embodies the stark, pragmatic shift from holy worship to grim undead necessity."},kw={"chapel","church","deathknell","forsaken","marla's last wish","novice elreth","the damned","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","quest:376","quest:6395","topic:forsaken"},src={"Novice Elreth"}}
E["npc:renee-lauer"] = {t="npc",n="Renee Lauer",z="tirisfal",s="Renee Lauer serves as the innkeeper of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, offering shelter, food, and drink to travelers passing through Tirisfal Glades. In life, she was a zealous Scarlet Crusade captain whose violent past ended in a Forsaken counterattack that claimed her life and her nephew's.",h="Renee Lauer serves as the innkeeper of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, offering shelter, food, and drink to...",sec={{t="From Zealot to Innkeeper",b="Before her undeath, Renee was an accomplished captain in the Scarlet Crusade. Convinced that Lordaeron belonged strictly to the living and to humans, she readily slew non-humans without remorse. Her fanaticism unravelled when relatives of her victims tracked her camp down for revenge. Although her nephew Stefan intervened, the skirmish drew the attention of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and Varimathras. The Forsaken host wiped out the camp, killing Stefan and raising Renee into undeath. Traumatized by how her crusade brought ruin upon her own kin, she abandoned martial life entirely to run the Gallows' End Tavern, determined to nurture rather than slaughter.",sp=0},{t="A Message for the Living",b="Rather than seeking vengeance against the Forsaken who struck her down, Renee directs her regret toward the fanaticism that doomed her family. Whenever captive Scarlet Crusaders or travelers end up in Brill, she shares her history to caution them against senseless hatred. She views the Crusade's dogma as an endless cycle of slaughter that guarantees only grief. While zealots rarely welcome her counsel, Renee persists in trying to convince them, offering hospitality and patient words to captive and patron alike.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Renee Lauer?",a="Renee Lauer is the Forsaken innkeeper of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. She sells food, drink, and general supplies to travelers across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"renee lauer","brill innkeeper","who is the innkeeper in brill"},it="identity"},{q="What did Renee do before she became Forsaken?",a="In life, she was a skilled captain and combat instructor in the Scarlet Crusade. She fervently persecuted and killed non-humans, believing Lordaeron belonged exclusively to living mankind.",al={"renee lauer past","was renee scarlet crusade","renee backstory"},it="history"},{q="How did Renee Lauer die?",a="After her violent raids sparked a retaliatory feud that endangered her nephew Stefan, their camp was overrun by Forsaken led by Sylvanas Windrunner and Varimathras. Stefan was slain, and Renee was killed and raised as an undead.",al={"how did renee die","renee lauer death","who killed renee"},it="ruin"},{q="Why did she become an innkeeper instead of a soldier?",a="Her death made her realize that the Scarlet Crusade's obsessive violence had achieved nothing except the destruction of her loved ones. In undeath, she chose a profession centered on providing care and shelter rather than taking lives.",al={"why is renee an innkeeper","why did renee stop fighting"},it="motive"},{q="What is Renee's connection to Calvin Montague?",a="In the task 'A Rogue's Deal', adventurers running errands for Calvin Montague are sent to deliver a letter directly to Renee at the Gallows' End Tavern.",al={"a rogue's deal renee","calvin montague letter"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Renee Lauer on?",a="Renee is loyal to the Forsaken and the Horde, tending the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. In life, she fought fiercely as a captain in the Scarlet Crusade, convinced Lordaeron belonged solely to living humans. After being killed and raised into undeath by Sylvanas Windrunner's forces, she renounced the Crusade's violent extremism and now dedicates her unlife to serving her fellow Forsaken.",al={"renee lauer faction","who does renee lauer serve","is renee horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Renee Lauer want?",a="Having realized in death that the Scarlet Crusade's hatred brought only needless slaughter, Renee seeks to end that cycle of bloodshed. Beyond providing comfort and sustenance to travelers passing through Brill, she is determined to reach captured Scarlet Crusaders, sharing her tragic past in hopes of convincing them to abandon their fanatical crusade.",al={"renee lauer goals","what does renee want","renee lauer purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Renee Lauer in Brill?",a="Renee manages the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, offering shelter, provisions, and a resting place for Forsaken citizens and travelers. She deliberately chose this quiet, nurturing role instead of returning to war, seeking to provide hospitality in Tirisfal Glades while remaining close enough to speak with prisoners captured from the Crusade.",al={"why is renee lauer here","why is renee in brill","renee lauer gallows end"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Renee is a stark reminder of how quickly the Scarlet Crusade's human zealots can end up on the other side of the grave, shedding their old dogma once freed from life's delusions."},kw={"brill","gallows' end tavern","innkeeper renee","renee lauer","scarlet crusade","stefan","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","subzone:gallows-end-tavern","topic:scarlet-crusade","quest:8","npc:calvin-montague","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","npc:varimathras","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Renee Lauer"}}
E["npc:shadow-priest-sarvis"] = {t="npc",n="Shadow Priest Sarvis",z="tirisfal",s="Shadow Priest Sarvis stands inside the ruined chapel of Deathknell, serving as one of the primary guides for newly awakened Forsaken. He introduces fresh corpses to their grim reality, directing them to purge mindless undead and gather intelligence on local threats. Under Lady Sylvanas's banner, Sarvis works tirelessly to shape bewildered undead into disciplined servants of the Forsaken.",h="Shadow Priest Sarvis stands inside the ruined chapel of Deathknell, serving as one of the primary guides for...",sec={{t="Shepherd of the Newly Awakened",b="Within the shadowy chapel of Deathknell, Shadow Priest Sarvis waits for those who have just clawed their way out of the grave. The awakening of a Forsaken soul is jarring and horrific, with many immediately losing their minds to mindless frenzy or despondency. Sarvis acts as an anchor, giving purpose to the newly risen. He assigns new recruits to put down the shambling, mindless corpses roaming the cemetery and directs them to secure Deathknell's borders against the encroaching Scourge.",sp=0},{t="Securing Deathknell",b="Beyond acclimating new recruits, Sarvis oversees crucial local defenses against lingering threats in Tirisfal Glades. He coordinates the containment of Scourge forces probing Deathknell's perimeter. By directing novices toward vital intelligence and martial trials, Sarvis ensures that the town remains a fortified bastion for the Forsaken.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Shadow Priest Sarvis?",a="Sarvis is a Forsaken shadow priest stationed in Deathknell. He guides freshly awakened undead, giving them their initial orders and helping secure the town against the Scourge.",al={"sarvis","who is sarvis","deathknell priest"}},{q="Where can I find Shadow Priest Sarvis?",a="He is located inside the ruined church at the center of Deathknell, the Forsaken starting area in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"sarvis location","where is sarvis","find sarvis"}},{q="Why is Sarvis having players kill mindless zombies?",a="Not every corpse raised from the grave retains their free will and sanity. Those who succumb to madness are deemed a hazard to the settlement and must be destroyed before they endanger the living dead.",al={"why kill mindless ones","the mindless ones"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Sarvis is one of the very first voices you hear after waking from the grave, setting you on your path to serve the Dark Lady.",["class:Priest"]="Sarvis exemplifies the Forsaken's embrace of shadow magic, serving as a dark mentor figure in a chapel stripped of Holy Light."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken","novice elreth","sarvis","shadow priest sarvis","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},src={"Shadow Priest Sarvis"}}
E["npc:shari-stilwell"] = {t="npc",n="Shari Stilwell",z="tirisfal",s="Shari Stilwell is a Forsaken paladin trainer stationed in the settlement of Brill within Tirisfal Glades. Bearing a surname shared with notable figures in the Eastern Kingdoms, she instructs Forsaken who take up the mantle of holy warriors. Her presence marks a rare and unconventional discipline among the undead under the Banshee Queen.",h="Shari Stilwell is a Forsaken paladin trainer stationed in the settlement of Brill within Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Beacon in Brill",b="Found among the blight-touched buildings of Brill, Shari Stilwell stands out as a mentor to Forsaken holy warriors. While the holy Light is excruciating to the undead, Shari guides fledgling Forsaken who endure that agonizing path. She aids newcomers seeking their footing in Tirisfal Glades through their early training and adjustments, offering guidance to those looking to secure a place within the Forsaken kingdom.",sp=0},{t="Ties of the Stilwell Name",b="Shari shares her family name with several living figures scattered across the southern kingdoms, including Daphne, Merissa, and the renowned smith Jordan Stilwell. Whether she was torn away from these kin during the fall of Lordaeron or shares only a distant lineage remains an unspoken tragedy of the Scourge's devastation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Shari Stilwell?",a="Shari Stilwell is an undead paladin trainer stationed in Brill in Tirisfal Glades. She assists Forsaken paladins with their early martial and holy instruction as they learn to survive in Lordaeron.",al={"who is shari stilwell","shari stilwell lore","brill paladin trainer"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Shari Stilwell be found?",a="She is located in the town of Brill, just north of the Capital City ruins and the Undercity in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"shari stilwell location","where is shari stilwell","find shari stilwell"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Is Shari related to Jordan Stilwell?",a="She shares her surname with Jordan Stilwell, the famous human smith, as well as Daphne and Merissa Stilwell. Their exact family connection is unconfirmed, but likely severed by the plague that destroyed Lordaeron.",al={"jordan stilwell relation","stilwell family ties","shari stilwell relatives"},it="ties"},{q="Can Forsaken wield the Light as paladins?",a="Channeling the Holy Light is deeply painful for undead beings, causing intense agony and burning sensations. However, trainers like Shari Stilwell guide determined Forsaken through the mental fortitude and devotion required to wield both martial skill and holy power.",al={"forsaken paladins","how can undead be paladins","undead paladin lore"}},{q="Whose side is Shari Stilwell on?",a="Shari Stilwell stands firmly with the Forsaken and the Horde. Based in the town of Brill, she serves Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom by instructing and guiding Forsaken who walk the demanding, paradoxical path of the paladin in undeath.",al={"shari stilwell faction","who does shari serve","is shari stilwell horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Shari Stilwell in Brill?",a="Shari Stilwell is stationed in Brill to serve as a paladin trainer for the newly awakened undead of Tirisfal Glades. As fledglings emerge from Deathknell and seek refuge or purpose in a second home, she ensures those drawn to the paladin's calling receive proper discipline and training.",al={"why is shari in brill","shari stilwell purpose","what is shari stilwell doing here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For Forsaken paladins beginning their journey in Tirisfal Glades, Shari Stilwell serves as the primary trainer and mentor in Brill.",["race:Undead"]="Shari Stilwell reflects the tragic splintering of families wrought by the Third War, standing as an undead mentor while potential kin endure far to the south."},kw={"brill","forsaken","light","paladin","shari","stilwell","tirisfal","trainer"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","npc:jordan-stilwell","npc:daphne-stilwell","npc:merissa-stilwell","quest:91209"},src={"Shari Stilwell"}}
E["npc:shelene-rhobart"] = {t="npc",n="Shelene Rhobart",z="tirisfal",s="Shelene Rhobart is a Forsaken leatherworking trainer stationed along the road in Tirisfal Glades. Operating out of a Deathguard waystation, she supplies cured hides and leathercraft training to support the Forsaken's military and logistical efforts across the blighted lands.",h="Shelene Rhobart is a Forsaken leatherworking trainer stationed along the road in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Crafting for the Forsaken",b="Shelene Rhobart plies her trade at a Deathguard waystation, an outpost situated southeast of Brill along the main road toward the ruins of Lordaeron. Even in undeath, practical crafts remain essential for survival and war. Shelene works with animal pelts collected from the prowling beasts of Tirisfal, refining tough hides into armor, straps, and field gear to supply local Forsaken patrols contending with both the Scourge and Scarlet crusaders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Shelene Rhobart?",a="Shelene Rhobart is stationed at a small Forsaken post located southeast of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, along the road leading toward the Undercity.",al={"shelene rhobart location","where is shelene rhobart","shelene rhobart map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What role does Shelene Rhobart serve?",a="She is a skilled leatherworking trainer. She teaches fledgling leatherworkers the fundamentals of curing pelts, stitching garments, and assembling protective leather gear for the Forsaken.",al={"what does shelene rhobart do","shelene rhobart trainer","shelene profession"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Why does she need beast hides?",a="Undead skin does not heal, so when the Forsaken are injured they must patch themselves with animal hides. Shelene's stocks are running low, and she wants duskbat wing membranes, darkhound hides and Vile Fin murloc skin, since different thicknesses suit different parts of the body.",al={"hides for the forsaken quest","why collect hides tirisfal"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Who is Shelene Rhobart?",a="Shelene Rhobart is an undead leatherworker serving the Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades. Like many of her kin, she retained the craft and skills she practiced in life, now offering her expertise to teach budding leatherworkers and craft essential supplies for her people.",al={"shelene rhobart lore","who is shelene","shelene rhobart info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Shelene Rhobart on?",a="She is staunchly loyal to the Forsaken and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, as well as the broader Horde. She works directly to support Tirisfal's defenders and tradespeople as they secure their homeland.",al={"shelene rhobart faction","who does shelene rhobart serve","shelene rhobart loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Shelene Rhobart want?",a="Shelene wants to supply the Forsaken war effort and support travelers across Tirisfal Glades. She seeks sturdy beast hides from local wildlife to craft armor, gear, and supplies essential for survival against the realm's enemies.",al={"what does shelene want","shelene rhobart goal","shelene rhobart purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Shelene Rhobart here?",a="Shelene is stationed at Death's Watch Waystation, southeast of Brill, to maintain an outpost along the vital road through Tirisfal Glades. From here, she assists patrolling scouts and passes her leatherworking knowledge to travelers passing through the glades.",al={"why is shelene at deaths watch","why is shelene rhobart here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Shelene represents the pragmatic foundation of Forsaken industry, ensuring local defenders are properly outfitted despite the decay and desolation of Tirisfal."},kw={"brill","hides for the forsaken","leatherworking","shelene rhobart","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","topic:forsaken","quest:97558"},src={"Shelene Rhobart"}}
E["npc:undertaker-mordo"] = {t="npc",n="Undertaker Mordo",z="tirisfal",s="Undertaker Mordo is the caretaker of the crypt of Deathknell, serving as the very first face newly awakened Forsaken see when they open their eyes. He tells those who wake that they are the Lich King's slaves no more and sends them on toward the Forsaken cause; the bodies that never stir go to the fire.",h="Undertaker Mordo is the caretaker of the crypt of Deathknell, serving as the very first face newly awakened...",sec={{t="The Awakening at Deathknell",b="Keeping the crypt of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades, Undertaker Mordo watches over the dead as corpses break free from the Scourge's shattered grip. For every newly liberated Forsaken who wakes in the cold of the crypt, Mordo provides their initial bearing in an unrecognizable second life. He assesses their sanity, introduces them to the reality of their condition, and immediately directs them toward duty under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner.",sp=0},{t="Those Who Do Not Wake",b="Not every body brought to the crypt wakes. Mordo does not wait long: the corpses that stay still are tossed into the fire, and those who do stir are greeted with dry impatience and little ceremony. Anyone who rises with their wits intact is sent up the steps and down the road to Shadow Priest Sarvis in the chapel, who explains their new existence. The mindless zombies that overrun the north of the village are Sarvis's concern, not Mordo's; the shadow priest sends the newly risen out to destroy them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Undertaker Mordo?",a="Undertaker Mordo is the Forsaken caretaker of the crypt of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. He greets freshly risen undead as they wake and directs them into service.",al={"who is mordo","undertaker mordo lore","mordo deathknell"},it="identity"},{q="Why is he standing in a graveyard?",a="Mordo is the caretaker of Deathknell's crypt, where newly freed undead wake after breaking free from the Lich King's control. He keeps watch there to greet them the moment they stir.",al={"why is mordo at the graves","what does mordo do","mordo job"},it="why_here"},{q="What happens to the undead who lose their minds upon waking?",a="Bodies that never wake are tossed into the fire. The mindless zombies that shamble through the north of Deathknell are not Mordo's charge: Shadow Priest Sarvis sends the newly risen out to destroy them, former brothers and sisters or not.",al={"those that couldnt be saved","mad undead deathknell","feral undead deathknell"}},{q="Where does Mordo send newly risen Forsaken?",a="After ensuring a newly awakened soul has their wits about them, Mordo sends them out of the crypt and down the road to the chapel to speak with Shadow Priest Sarvis, who explains what it means to join the ranks of the Forsaken.",al={"where do i go after mordo","mordo quest direction","next step after mordo"},it="next_step"},{q="Whose side is Undertaker Mordo on?",a="Undertaker Mordo serves the Forsaken and the Dark Lady, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. Operating on the front line of newly awakened undead in Deathknell, he ensures that freshly raised corpses who retain their sanity are inducted into the ranks of the Forsaken rather than falling sway to the Lich King or mindless frenzy.",al={"mordo faction","who does mordo serve","is mordo forsaken"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Undertaker Mordo want?",a="Mordo's immediate concern is managing the grave sites of Deathknell. He wants newly risen Forsaken to regain their bearings, report to local leadership like Shadow Priest Sarvis, and assist in putting down mindless undead that clawed their way from the dirt without their sanity intact.",al={"what is mordo trying to do","mordo goals","mordo purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Undertaker Mordo tied to?",a="Mordo works alongside other caretakers of Deathknell, such as Caretaker Caice, under the broader direction of Shadow Priest Sarvis and the Forsaken leadership overseeing the rebirth of Lordaeron's dead.",al={"mordo allies","who does mordo work with","mordo connections"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Undertaker Mordo is the very first person you speak with upon opening your eyes in death, guiding your first tentative steps in service to Queen Sylvanas."},kw={"deathknell","rude awakening","shadow grave","tirisfal glades","undertaker mordo"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","subzone:shadow-grave","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:363"},src={"Undertaker Mordo"}}
E["npc:yvette-farthing"] = {t="npc",n="Yvette Farthing",z="tirisfal",s="Yvette Farthing is a Forsaken resident of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, dwelling in the Gallows' End Tavern alongside her father, Coleman. Though she appears as hardened and detached as any of Sylvanas's people, she carries the lingering ghost of a mortal romance cut short by the Scourge.",h="Yvette Farthing is a Forsaken resident of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, dwelling in the Gallows' End Tavern...",sec={{t="Echoes of the Mills",b="Before the Third War swept Lordaeron into ruin, Yvette was in love with Thurman Agamand, one of the heirs of the prosperous Agamand Mills. When the Scourge plague approached, Yvette and her father, Coleman, begged Thurman to abandon the estate and flee with them. Thurman refused out of devotion to his family's legacy. Both would eventually perish and rise into undeath, but while Yvette found a place among the free-willed Forsaken in Brill, Thurman's tragic final fate remained unknown to her.",sp=0},{t="A Final Farewell",b="Before the mills were overrun, Thurman penned an urgent love letter to Yvette, detailing the treachery of his brother Devlin and lamenting his choice to stay. The Scourge intercepted the message, leaving it lost among the ruins of Agamand Mills until recovered by adventurers. Upon reading Thurman's dying confession, Yvette briefly displays genuine mortal despair before forcing herself back into cold detachment, bitter that such past attachments have no place in the grim reality of the Forsaken.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Yvette Farthing?",a="Yvette Farthing is a Forsaken woman living in Brill's Gallows' End Tavern. She is the daughter of Coleman Farthing and formerly lived in the farmlands of Tirisfal Glades before the Third War destroyed the kingdom.",al={"yvette farthing lore","who is yvette","brill yvette"},it="identity"},{q="What is Yvette's connection to Agamand Mills?",a="In life, Yvette was romantically involved with Thurman Agamand. When the plague threatened the region, she tried to convince him to flee with her, but he chose to remain and defend his family's estate.",al={"yvette agamand","thurman agamand and yvette","yvette lover"},it="ties"},{q="What happens when you deliver Thurman's letter to her?",a="When an adventurer delivers Thurman's long-lost letter, Yvette is momentarily crushed by sorrow upon reading his final words of love and betrayal. However, she quickly hardens her demeanor, declaring that she is Forsaken now and that mortal sentiment is useless.",al={"a letter undelivered turn in","thurmans letter reward","yvette reaction letter"},sp=1},{q="Who is Yvette's father?",a="Her father is Coleman Farthing, a Forsaken also residing in the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. They both managed to survive long enough to flee together initially, though both eventually succumbed to the plague and became Forsaken.",al={"yvette father","coleman and yvette","who is coleman farthing"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Yvette Farthing on?",a="Yvette stands fully with the Forsaken and the Horde. Though the remnants of her mortal life and past loves can still stir up painful memories, she has cast aside those human emotions. She now embraces her identity as one of the free-willed undead serving Lady Sylvanas.",al={"yvette farthing faction","yvette farthing side","who does yvette serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Yvette Farthing want?",a="Yvette seeks closure from the tragedy that consumed her former life. Having fled Agamand Mills during the Scourge invasion while her lover Thurman stayed behind, she harbored unspoken grief. Receiving word of his final moments allows her to finally shut the door on her mortal heart and embrace her cold existence among the Forsaken.",al={"what does yvette want","yvette farthing goal","yvette farthing motives"},sp=1,it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Yvette Farthing here in Brill?",a="Following the devastation of the Agamand Mills and the fall of Lordaeron to the Plague, Yvette and her father Coleman took refuge in Brill. In undeath, Brill serves as the primary Forsaken town outside the capital, making it a natural sanctuary for survivors reclaiming their former homeland.",al={"why is yvette farthing in brill","yvette farthing location why","why is yvette here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Yvette represents the deep emotional conflict many Forsaken endure, struggling between lingering human memories of love and the bitter, hardened reality of undeath."},kw={"a letter undelivered","agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","gallows' end tavern","thurman agamand","tirisfal glades","yvette farthing"},rel={"npc:coleman-farthing","quest:361","subzone:agamand-mills","subzone:brill","subzone:gallows-end-tavern","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},src={"Yvette Farthing"}}
E["quest:1470"] = {t="quest",n="Piercing the Veil",z="tirisfal",s="Piercing the Veil is an introductory warlock trial given by Venya Marthand in Deathknell. It tests a freshly risen warlock's resolve by directing them to collect skulls from nearby mindless skeletons. Completing the task earns the novice the knowledge required to summon and bind an imp minion.",h="Piercing the Veil is an introductory warlock trial given by Venya Marthand in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Novice's First Step",b="Newly awakened Forsaken warlocks begin their journey in Deathknell under the instruction of Venya Marthand. She teaches that true strength lies not in raw brute force, but in knowledge and the precise mastery of chaotic powers. To prove they possess the focus and capability required to pierce the veil between worlds and subjugate nether beings, novices are dispatched to harvest the skulls of restless Rattlecage Skeletons roaming near Deathknell's abandoned smithy.",sp=0},{t="Subjugating the Nether",b="Once the skeletal remains are secured, Venya considers the initiate ready to step beyond simple dark spells. The recovered materials serve as a rite of passage, demonstrating that the novice can overcome mindless undead and withstand the bleak realities of their craft. In return, Venya imparts the rituals needed to bend an imp to the summoner's command, warning that despite the demon's diminutive stature, its cunning and fire will make it a dangerous ally.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Piercing the Veil?",a="The quest is given by Venya Marthand, the warlock trainer situated inside the crypt at Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"who starts piercing the veil","piercing the veil questgiver","venya marthand quest"},gp=true},{q="What must you collect to complete this quest?",a="You must collect three Rattlecage Skulls, which are recovered by destroying the Rattlecage Skeletons wandering near the ruined smithy in Deathknell.",al={"piercing the veil objective","what do i collect for piercing the veil","rattlecage skulls"},gp=true},{q="Why does Venya Marthand want skeleton skulls?",a="Hunting the mindless skeletons tests the novice warlock's control, resolve, and basic magical abilities before Venya trusts them with more dangerous demonic rituals.",al={"why rattlecage skulls","purpose of skulls quest"},it="motive"},{q="What power or minion do you learn to summon?",a="Completing the lesson prepares the warlock to summon and bind an imp, the first demonic companion a practitioner learns to command.",al={"piercing the veil reward spell","what demon do i get","imp summon quest"},it="for_you"},{q="Where are the Rattlecage Skeletons located?",a="They wander the outskirts of Deathknell, particularly around the crumbling, abandoned smithy located to the northwest of the main town clearing.",al={"rattlecage skeleton location","where are the skeletons deathknell","abandoned smithy deathknell"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is eligible to undertake this quest?",a="Only Forsaken warlocks who have reached the appropriate level of introductory training in Deathknell can undertake this quest.",al={"who can take piercing the veil","can other classes do this","is this warlock only"},gp=true},{q="What is the Rattlecage Skull for?",a="Venya Marthand uses the skulls as a trial to test your emerging command over dark magic. Warlocks believe knowledge and will are their greatest weapons, and proving you can harvest these remains from the restless dead shows you possess enough discipline to summon and bind an imp to your service.",al={"what are rattlecage skulls for","why rattlecage skulls","rattlecage skull purpose"},it="item",add=true},{q="Who are the Rattlecage Skeletons?",a="Rattlecage Skeletons are mindless Scourge undead lingering around the abandoned smithy in Deathknell. Like much of Tirisfal Glades, the grounds remain haunted by the remnants of the plague and the Lich King's ruined forces, making them convenient fodder for novice Forsakenwarlocks looking to test their growing might.",al={"what are rattlecage skeletons","rattlecage skeletons deathknell","why rattlecage skeletons here"},it="target",add=true},{q="Why does Venya Marthand send you instead of handling it herself?",a="Venya Marthand is your warlock trainer, not a mercenary contractor. Gathering the skulls is not a chore she needs completed for herself, but an essential lesson for you. To pierce the veil and bend chaotic beings of the void to your will, you must first prove your power and determination on your own.",al={"why me piercing the veil","why does venya make you get skulls","why cant venya marthand get them"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="This rite of passage marks your first true foray into demonology, demonstrating the fundamental lesson that chaotic magic must be bent to iron will.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken warlocks draw upon their uncanny detachment from mortality to fearlessly study chaotic and dark magics in the shadows of Deathknell."},kw={"deathknell","imp","piercing the veil","rattlecage skull","tirisfal glades","venya marthand","warlock"},rel={"zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Venya Marthand",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1470,ri={"Rattlecage Skull"}},src={"Piercing the Veil"}}
E["quest:1819"] = {t="quest",n="Ulag the Cleaver",z="tirisfal",s="Ulag the Cleaver is a rite of passage for young Forsaken warriors in Tirisfal Glades. Deathguard Dillinger tasks you with releasing and executing an enslaved Scourge warrior bound inside a northern mausoleum, testing your martial resolve and earning the respect of the Deathguard.",h="Ulag the Cleaver is a rite of passage for young Forsaken warriors in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Bound Scourge Thrall",b="When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, the Forsaken managed to capture a peculiar undead warrior who named himself Ulag the Cleaver. While Ulag possessed a conscious, lucid mind, his spirit remained inextricably dominated by the psychic thrall of the Lich King. Seeking to understand how the Scourge binds free will, the Forsaken kept him restrained under a magical seal beneath a mausoleum north of Brill, hoping to break the entity's spiritual chains.",sp=0},{t="The Executioner's Trial",b="All efforts to sever Ulag from the Lich King's mental grip ended in failure. Recognizing that true freedom could only come through destruction, Deathguard Dillinger repurposed the captive as a trial of combat for rising Forsaken warriors. By disturbing the skull stone trigger outside the crypt, a warrior breaks the protective wards, releases Ulag from his imprisonment, and grants him release at the edge of an axe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ulag the Cleaver?",a="Ulag the Cleaver was an undead warrior captured by the Forsaken during the Scourge's invasion of Tirisfal Glades. Uniquely, he retained his free mind and identity, yet remained completely enthralled by the Lich King's mental command.",al={"ulag lore","who is ulag","ulag the cleaver identity"},it="target"},{q="Why was Ulag imprisoned under the mausoleum?",a="Forsaken scholars and soldiers imprisoned him beneath a magical seal to study how the Lich King's mind control functioned. They had hoped to discover a method to sever the psychic link that bound him, though their experiments proved unsuccessful.",al={"why is ulag locked up","mausoleum prisoner tirisfal"},it="origin"},{q="Why do the Deathguards want Ulag killed now?",a="Having admitted defeat in breaking the Lich King's grip on Ulag, the Forsaken decided death was the only mercy remaining. Deathguard Dillinger chose to use the execution as a test of martial courage and discipline for aspiring warriors.",al={"why kill ulag","reason to slay ulag"},it="motive"},{q="How is Ulag summoned?",a="He is imprisoned beneath a mausoleum just north of Brill. Interacting with the skull stone near the tomb releases the magical seal that binds him, causing him to rise and attack.",al={"how to summon ulag","where is ulag","ulag skull stone"},gp=true},{q="Who gives the quest to slay Ulag?",a="Deathguard Dillinger, who guards the western road leading out of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, assigns this mission specifically to Forsaken warriors seeking further instruction.",al={"quest giver ulag","who sends me to ulag"},gp=true},{q="What happens after Ulag is defeated?",a="Upon confirming Ulag's demise, Deathguard Dillinger acknowledges your valor and sends you to speak with Coleman Farthing in Brill to continue your training.",al={"after ulag","ulag quest follow up"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger have you face Ulag instead of dealing with him himself?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is testing your mettle. Ulag was no minor servant of the Scourge, and facing him alone proves whether you possess the skill, courage, and resolve demanded of a true warrior in service to Lady Sylvanas. Dillinger guards Brill and oversees your training; sending you into the crypt serves as your trial by combat to show you are worthy of advanced instruction.",al={"why me dillinger","why kill ulag yourself","why send player ulag"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="This trial serves as an early proving ground for Forsaken warriors, testing your courage against a powerful, mind-controlled champion of the Scourge.",["race:Undead"]="Ulag's fate illustrates the horrifying reality of Scourge control, showing the tragic distinction between Forsaken who broke free and those whose minds remain enslaved to the Lich King."},kw={"brill","deathguard dillinger","dillinger","mausoleum","scourge","skull stone","ulag","ulag the cleaver"},rel={"npc:deathguard-dillinger","quest:1818","quest:1820","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Dillinger",level="10",faction="Horde",id=1819},src={"Ulag the Cleaver (quest)"}}
E["quest:1820"] = {t="quest",n="Speak with Coleman",z="tirisfal",s="Speak with Coleman is a transitional task in Tirisfal Glades offered to champions who have proven their worth against the Scourge. Deathguard Dillinger directs capable warriors from the town square to the local tavern to assist Coleman Farthing with personal and factional vendettas.",h="Speak with Coleman is a transitional task in Tirisfal Glades offered to champions who have proven their worth...",sec={{t="A Word from the Guard",b="After assisting in the containment of severe local threats—such as laying to rest dangerous Scourge remnants like Ulag the Cleaver—adventurers earn the personal trust of Deathguard Dillinger. As one of the sentinels watching the roads into Brill, Dillinger judges who is capable of handling more than routine perimeter patrols and points capable fighters toward citizens in need of specialized service.",sp=0},{t="A Private Vendetta",b="Not every conflict in Tirisfal Glades is fought on high command orders from the Undercity. Across the ruined kingdom of Lordaeron, surviving undead carry deep grudges tied to the lands, families, and fortunes they left behind. Coleman Farthing bides his time within the Gallow's End Tavern, seeking an instrument of vengeance to strike back at the threats lingering near former settlements like the Agamand Mills.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Coleman Farthing?",a="Coleman Farthing waits inside the Gallow's End Tavern, located on the eastern side of the town of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"coleman farthing location","where is coleman farthing","find farthing brill"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who sends you to talk to Coleman?",a="Deathguard Dillinger, who guards the entrance path into Brill, sends you to speak with Coleman after you prove your martial prowess.",al={"who gives speak with coleman","dillinger quest"},it="origin"},{q="What kind of work does Coleman Farthing have?",a="Coleman seeks a capable combatant to serve as an instrument of revenge against the enemies of the Forsaken, specifically concerning matters around the old Agamand properties.",al={"what does coleman want","coleman farthing task"},it="purpose"},{q="What prerequisites are needed before receiving this task?",a="Deathguard Dillinger only refers you to Coleman once you have proven your loyalty and combat skill to the Forsaken by defeating Ulag the Cleaver.",al={"prereqs for speak with coleman","ulag requirement"},gp=true},{q="Does this mission grant substantial rewards directly?",a="This assignment simply connects you with Farthing in the tavern, opening up his personal line of requests rather than providing substantial gear or coin on its own.",al={"speak with coleman rewards","is it worth doing"},gp=true},{q="What is the Gallow's End Tavern?",a="The Gallow's End Tavern is the central inn of Brill, serving as a gathering spot, resting point, and informal center for private contracts among the local undead.",al={"brill tavern","gallows end tavern"},it="place"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger want me to speak with Coleman?",a="Deathguard Dillinger recognizes that you have proven your strength and loyalty to the Dark Lady by handling threats in Brill. Coleman Farthing has personal grievances and needs an able combatant to exact vengeance on enemies of the Forsaken. By directing you to him, Dillinger ensures capable champions assist fellow citizens while furthering the realm's wider security.",al={"why send to coleman","dillinger motive speak with coleman","why see coleman farthing"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger send me instead of handling Coleman's request himself?",a="Dillinger is a Deathguard stationed to guard Brill's town perimeter against immediate threats like the Scourge. He cannot abandon his post to resolve the personal vendettas or private concerns of local townsfolk. You have demonstrated your combat prowess and independence, making you the ideal agent to assist Coleman Farthing directly.",al={"why me coleman","why not dillinger","why dillinger sends you"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Many Forsaken hold personal grudges alongside their loyalty to the Dark Lady; Coleman Farthing's requests reflect how personal loss and national defense intertwine in Tirisfal.",["faction:Horde"]="For travelers helping secure Lordaeron, connecting with local townsfolk in Brill provides vital footing for operations throughout Tirisfal Glades."},kw={"agamand","brill","coleman farthing","deathguard dillinger","forsaken","gallow's end tavern","horde","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:deathguard-dillinger","npc:coleman-farthing","quest:1821","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},m={start="Deathguard Dillinger",["end"]="Coleman Farthing",level="10",faction="Horde",id=1820},src={"Speak with Coleman"}}
E["quest:1821"] = {t="quest",n="Agamand Heirlooms",z="tirisfal",s="Coleman Farthing tasks adventurers with plundering the Agamand Family Crypt at the northern end of Agamand Mills. Driven by bitter resentment over his mortal demise, he seeks to strip his former masters of their ancestral weapons and erase their final remnants of dignity.",h="Coleman Farthing tasks adventurers with plundering the Agamand Family Crypt at the northern end of Agamand...",sec={{t="A Servant's Bitter Grudge",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, Coleman Farthing served the wealthy and influential Agamand family at their expansive mills in Tirisfal Glades. Whatever occurred during the fall of their estate, Farthing blames his former lords entirely for his death. Now raised into undeath among the Forsaken, he views the Agamands' downfall with spiteful satisfaction, rejoicing that the once-proud dynasty has been reduced to mindless minions enslaved to the Lich King.",sp=0},{t="Desecrating the Crypt",b="To completely extinguish what remains of the family's prestige, Farthing sends adventurers into the Agamand Family Crypt nestled beneath the northern bluffs of the mills. Within its haunted stone halls, the restive dead wander alongside lingering Scourge. Farthing demands the extraction of four storied relics—the Agamand family sword, axe, dagger, and mace—ensuring their bloodline's legacy is stripped of every remaining shred of honor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Coleman Farthing in life?",a="Coleman Farthing was a retainer employed by the wealthy Agamand family at Agamand Mills prior to the Scourge invasion of Lordaeron. He died during the calamity and blames his masters for his demise, harboring an intense resentment that carried over into his undeath.",al={"who is coleman farthing","coleman backstory","farthing history"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want the heirlooms?",a="Farthing seeks complete vengeance against his former masters. By pillaging their ancestral sword, axe, dagger, and mace from the family tomb, he aims to strip the fallen lineage of its last vestiges of honor and memory.",al={"why steal heirlooms","coleman motive","why take weapons"},it="motive"},{q="What happened to the Agamand family?",a="The Agamand family perished when the Scourge swept across Tirisfal Glades. Rather than finding peace in death, they were raised as mindless undead thralls bound to the will of the Lich King, wandering their ruined mills and crypt.",al={"agamand fate","are agamands alive","what are agamands now"},it="history"},{q="Where is the Agamand Family Crypt located?",a="The crypt lies at the northern edge of Agamand Mills, set into the rocky cliffs overlooking the blighted farmsteads in northern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"crypt location","where is crypt","agamand crypt map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What weapons are kept within the crypt?",a="The crypt holds four distinct ancestral weapons belonging to the bloodline: the Agamand Family Sword, the Agamand Family Axe, the Agamand Family Dagger, and the Agamand Family Mace.",al={"which heirlooms","four heirlooms","agamand weapons"},it="item"},{q="Why are the Forsaken attacking the Agamands if both are undead?",a="The Forsaken possess free will and fight for their own survival, whereas the undead Agamands remain mindless slaves of the Scourge, serving the Lich King that the Forsaken despise.",al={"undead vs undead","why fight agamands","forsaken scourge conflict"},it="conflict"},{q="What will Coleman Farthing do with the Agamand heirlooms?",a="To Coleman Farthing, the four weapons—the Agamand family sword, axe, dagger, and mace—represent the final remnants of his former masters' pride and lineage. By desecrating their tomb and taking them, he strips away the last vestiges of honor and memory left to the cursed Agamand lands, and declares that the family truly is no more.",al={"agamand heirlooms use","what are the heirlooms for","agamand family weapons purpose"},sp=1,it="item",add=true},{q="Who guards the Agamand family crypt?",a="The crypt and the surrounding mills are infested with mindless Scourge minions, including the reanimated remains of the Agamand family itself. Bound to the will of the Lich King rather than free-willed Forsaken, these undead lurk in the dark tombs of their ancestors, fiercely guarding the family relics against any intruders.",al={"agamand crypt enemies","who protects the heirlooms","agamand family crypt monsters"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Coleman Farthing's bitter quest embodies the lingering grudges and unhealed wounds that many Forsaken carry from their mortal lives into undeath.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing out the crypt undermines the Scourge's entrenched hold over northern Tirisfal Glades, making the realm safer for Horde travelers.",["class:Rogue"]="Slipping past lurking ghouls to pilfer ancient armaments from guarded burial vaults is second nature to stealthy operatives."},kw={"agamand family crypt","agamand heirlooms","agamand mills","coleman farthing","heirloom weapon","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","quest:1820"},m={start="Coleman Farthing",level="11",faction="Horde",id=1821,ri={"Agamand Family Axe","Agamand Family Dagger","Agamand Family Mace","Agamand Family Sword"}},src={"Agamand Heirlooms"}}
E["quest:3096"] = {t="quest",n="Encrypted Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Encrypted Scroll is a discreet summons delivered to freshly awakened Forsaken rogues in Deathknell. Passed along by Shadow Priest Sarvis, the cipher directs the recruit to David Trias, a rogue mentor operating quietly out of the local inn.",h="The Encrypted Scroll is a discreet summons delivered to freshly awakened Forsaken rogues in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Quiet Summons",b="While newly raised Forsaken battle mindless zombies in the cemetery, the settlement's leadership takes note of those with particular inclinations toward stealth and subterfuge. Shadow Priest Sarvis passes this sealed parchment to promising scouts once their initial readiness is proven. Stamped with the discreet seal of David Trias, it serves as an early introduction to the shadowy network operating beneath the Forsaken hierarchy.",sp=0},{t="Eyes in the Shadows",b="David Trias watches the crossroads and inn of Deathknell, scouting prospects for future clandestine operations. Rather than seeking common thieves or reckless cutpurses, the Forsaken require disciplined covert operatives to serve the fledgling kingdom. Trias hints that capable rogues may eventually catch the eye of the Shadowstalkers, a dedicated cadre within the Forsaken who prize cunning, precision, and absolute loyalty to the Dark Lady.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Encrypted Scroll?",a="The scroll is a private invitation for newly awakened Forsaken rogues. Shadow Priest Sarvis hands it over once you have proved capable, directing you to report to David Trias for specialized training.",al={"what is encrypted scroll","what does the scroll do","why encrypted scroll"}},{q="Who is David Trias?",a="David Trias is the rogue trainer stationed at the inn in Deathknell. He evaluates fresh recruits and scouts potential agents for larger Forsaken intelligence operations.",al={"david trias","who is trias","rogue trainer deathknell"}},{q="Who are the Shadowstalkers mentioned by Trias?",a="The Shadowstalkers are an organization of Forsaken stealth operatives and assassins. Trias warns new rogues that the Shadowstalkers recruit only talented, disciplined agents rather than clumsy pickpockets.",al={"who are the shadowstalkers","shadowstalkers forsaken","shadowstalkers"}},{q="Why is the message encrypted?",a="Forsaken rogues prize secrecy and operational security. Directing prospects via encrypted dispatches ensures that sensitive communications remain shielded from careless eyes in a vulnerable settlement like Deathknell.",al={"why encrypted","why cipher"}},{q="Where do you find Shadow Priest Sarvis?",a="Shadow Priest Sarvis oversees the initial intake of new Forsaken within the central crypt and chapel complex of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is sarvis","sarvis location","find shadow priest sarvis"}},{q="Where is David Trias located?",a="David Trias stands inside the damaged inn building just down the path from the central chapel in Deathknell.",al={"where is trias","david trias location","deathknell inn trainer"}}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="This missive marks your formal induction into the Forsaken's clandestine network, bridging the gap between a freshly risen corpse and an aspiring covert agent.",["race:Undead"]="As the newly formed Forsaken secure Tirisfal Glades against the Scourge and living remnants, scouts and covert operatives are vital to the Dark Lady's survival strategy."},kw={"david trias","deathknell","encrypted scroll","forsaken rogue","shadow priest sarvis","shadowstalkers","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:david-trias","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={start="Shadow Priest Sarvis",["end"]="David Trias",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3096},src={"Encrypted Scroll (quest)"}}
E["quest:3097"] = {t="quest",n="Hallowed Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Hallowed Scroll is an introductory summons directed to newly awoken Forsaken priests in Deathknell. Handed over by Shadow Priest Sarvis, the missive calls the novice to Dark Cleric Duesten inside the shattered village chapel to learn how the undead wield faith after death.",h="The Hallowed Scroll is an introductory summons directed to newly awoken Forsaken priests in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Summons in Deathknell",b="As newly awakened undead stagger from the graves of Deathknell, their minds are often reeling from the trauma of the plague, death, and sudden liberation from the Lich King's grip. For those who were once devout followers of the Holy Light in life, confusion runs exceptionally deep. While fledgling undead battle the mindless Scourge haunting the settlement's perimeter, Shadow Priest Sarvis intercepts incoming messages. Bearing the mark of Dark Cleric Duesten, the hallowed scroll bids newly risen priests to abandon their wandering and report to the desecrated town chapel.",sp=0},{t="Faith Redefined",b="Upon delivering the scroll, a novice encounters the harsh theology of Forsaken priesthood. Dark Cleric Duesten pulls no punches: the Holy Light abandoned them in death, ancestral spirits are hollow tales, and planar demons have no interest in their plight. Instead, Forsaken clergy teach that sheer willpower and radical self-reliance are the only true sources of salvation. Under Duesten's tutelage, newly risen practitioners learn to channel both divine discipline and shadow, wielding power not out of devotion to distant gods, but out of absolute conviction in their own survival.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Hallowed Scroll?",a="It is a sealed document given by Shadow Priest Sarvis to newly risen Forsaken priests in Deathknell. It instructs them to seek out Dark Cleric Duesten in the town chapel to begin their formal priestly training.",al={"hallowed scroll","scroll item","priest scroll"}},{q="Who sends the scroll to the player?",a="The scroll bears the personal seal of Dark Cleric Duesten, the resident Forsaken priest mentor residing in Deathknell's chapel, though it is handed to you by Shadow Priest Sarvis.",al={"who sent scroll","duesten scroll","sarvis letter"}},{q="Can Forsaken still wield the Holy Light?",a="Dark Cleric Duesten tells new priests that the Holy Light no longer concerns them. As he explains, traditional veneration of the Light is abandoned in favor of faith in oneself and mastery over Shadow.",al={"undead use light","forsaken holy light","can undead cast light"}},{q="Where is Dark Cleric Duesten located?",a="He is located inside the ruined church at the center of Deathknell in western Tirisfal Glades, standing near other Forsaken trainers.",al={"find duesten","duesten location","where is cleric duesten"}},{q="Why does Duesten dismiss ancestors and demons?",a="Duesten seeks to strip away all illusions held by newly raised undead. Forsaken culture rejects external divine saviors, ancestral aid, and demonic pacts, preaching instead that their survival depends solely upon their own collective willpower.",al={"duesten speech","fairy tales","nether dont want you"}},{q="What happens after reading the scroll?",a="You read the parchment and present yourself to Dark Cleric Duesten in the Deathknell chapel, unlocking your initial priest abilities and establishing him as your trainer while in the starting area.",al={"hallowed scroll objective","turning in scroll"}}},ang={["class:Priest"]="This quest marks your philosophical baptism into the Forsaken priesthood, rejecting traditional pious devotion to embrace self-reliance, suffering, and shadow magic.",["race:Undead"]="The scroll exemplifies the Forsaken worldview: severed from their old human faiths, they survive strictly through iron will and mutual necessity."},kw={"dark cleric duesten","deathknell","forsaken priest","hallowed scroll","priest quest","shadow priest sarvis","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:light","topic:scourge"},m={start="Shadow Priest Sarvis",["end"]="Dark Cleric Duesten",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3097},src={"Hallowed Scroll (quest)"}}
E["quest:3098"] = {t="quest",n="Glyphic Scroll",z="tirisfal",s="The Glyphic Scroll is an official summons delivered to newly awakened Forsaken mages in Deathknell. Bearing the seal of the mage trainer Isabella, the parchment directs promising practitioners of the arcane to seek her out in the desecrated village church to hone their volatile powers.",h="The Glyphic Scroll is an official summons delivered to newly awakened Forsaken mages in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Arcane",b="When a freshly raised Forsaken demonstrates an affinity for arcane magic while culling mindless Scourge around Deathknell, they quickly catch the attention of their elders. Delivered by hand, the Glyphic Scroll is marked with the arcane sigil of Isabella, the local mage instructor. It bids the newly risen practitioner to report to her station within the ruined church, recognizing that unregulated magical aptitude poses as much danger to the Forsaken as it does to their enemies.",sp=0},{t="Wielding Magic in Undeath",b="Within the ruined chapel of Deathknell, Isabella instructs fledglings that death has not exempted them from the perils of the arcane. While mortality has stripped away their physical frailties, the volatile pull and corrupting nature of magic linger just as fiercely. For the Forsaken, pursuing arcane dominance is not merely an intellectual pursuit, but an instrument of survival and vengeance—a means to reclaim their destiny and obliterate any who challenge their fragile reclamation of Lordaeron.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Glyphic Scroll?",a="The Glyphic Scroll is an instructional writ bearing the personal seal of Isabella, the Forsaken mage trainer in Deathknell. It instructs newly awakened mages to report to the ruined church to advance their study of the arcane.",al={"glyphic scroll quest","what is this scroll","deathknell scroll"}},{q="Who sends the Glyphic Scroll to you?",a="The scroll is dispatched by Isabella, the resident mage trainer in Deathknell, delivered to you after you prove your utility by dealing with the nearby mindless Scourge.",al={"who sent the scroll","who wrote glyphic scroll","sender of glyphic scroll"}},{q="Where is Isabella located?",a="Isabella is stationed inside the ruined village church in the center of Deathknell, situated alongside other class mentors offering guidance to newly risen Forsaken.",al={"where is isabella","find isabella","deathknell mage trainer location"}},{q="Why does Isabella warn you about magical corruption?",a="Isabella warns that although the Forsaken are undead, the volatile nature of arcane power remains unchanged. Magic still carries the constant risk of corruption and addiction, requiring discipline and will to master without being consumed.",al={"magic corruption undead","arcane corruption lore","why is magic dangerous to undead"}},{q="Why is arcane magic important to the Forsaken in Deathknell?",a="Forsaken mages view arcane power as a vital tool to reclaim Lordaeron and secure their autonomy against the mindless Scourge, hostile living mortals, and the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"why do forsaken use magic","undead mages lore"}},{q="Can any Forsaken class receive this scroll?",a="No. The Glyphic Scroll is given exclusively to Forsaken mages as an introductory summons to their primary class trainer in Deathknell.",al={"is glyphic scroll mage only","can warlocks get glyphic scroll","who gets glyphic scroll"}}},ang={["class:Mage"]="This scroll marks your formal initiation into the ranks of Forsaken mages, linking your magical craft directly to the survival and resurgence of your newly freed people.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken mages wield the arcane with dark purpose, treating magic not as a scholarly game of Dalaran, but as an indispensable weapon to reclaim their stolen homeland."},kw={"arcane","deathknell","forsaken mage","glyphic scroll","isabella","mage trainer","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={level="3",faction="Horde",id=3098},src={"Glyphic Scroll (quest)"}}
E["quest:354"] = {t="quest",n="Deaths in the Family",z="tirisfal",s="Coleman Farthing, a former mill worker now dwelling in Brill, seeks personal vengeance against the aristocratic Agamand family. Convinced to stay and defend their estates against the Scourge during the Third War, Farthing and his fellow workers perished alongside their stubborn employers, only for the Agamands to rise as mindless thralls of the Lich King.",h="Coleman Farthing, a former mill worker now dwelling in Brill, seeks personal vengeance against the...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Pride",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, the Agamand family stood as the wealthiest and most influential agrarian dynasty in Tirisfal Glades. Their sprawling mills and fertile pastures produced bountiful grain, commanding the labor of dozens of tenant farmers and millers. When the Plague of Undeath crept across the kingdom, the Agamands refused to flee or abandon their ancestral holdings, convinced that stone walls and loyal workers could hold off the horrors advancing from the east.",sp=0},{t="The Price of Loyalty",b="Believing their lords' assurances, loyal employees like Coleman Farthing stood their ground to defend the estate. The defense proved futile against the Scourge's relentless onslaught. The Agamands and their workers were slaughtered, their bodies rotting where they fell before being raised into undeath. While Farthing eventually broke free through Sylvanas Windrunner's liberation, the Agamand patriarchs and heirs remained trapped under the Lich King's mental grip, wandering the ruined mills as mindless Scourge sentries.",sp=0},{t="Bitter Retribution",b="Now residing in Brill, Coleman Farthing harbors intense resentment toward the former masters whose arrogance cost him his mortal life. Seeking closure and retribution, he sends Forsaken champions to Agamand Mills to destroy Gregor, Nissa, and Thurman Agamand. Farthing openly hopes that behind their rotting visages, his former masters retained just enough consciousness to feel terror as their unlife was violently brought to an end.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was the Agamand family?",a="The Agamands were the wealthiest and most prosperous landowning family in Tirisfal Glades before the fall of Lordaeron. They owned and operated the vast Agamand Mills, employing numerous local farmers and millers.",al={"who are the agamands","agamand backstory","agamand mills history"},it="people"},{q="Why is Coleman Farthing so angry at them?",a="Farthing was employed at their mills in life. When the Scourge arrived, the Agamands convinced their workers to remain and fortify the estate rather than flee. Farthing views their aristocratic pride as the direct reason he and his peers died and became undead.",al={"coleman farthing grudge","why does farthing hate the agamands","farthing motive"},it="motive"},{q="Did the Agamands join the Forsaken?",a="No. Unlike Coleman Farthing and the other free-willed Forsaken, the Agamands never broke free from the Lich King's control. They remain mindless Scourge minions haunting the ruined family mills.",al={"are agamands forsaken","why are the agamands scourge","who controls the agamands"},it="threat"},{q="Which Agamand family members must be destroyed?",a="The quest tasks you with hunting down Gregor Agamand, Nissa Agamand, and Thurman Agamand, collecting their remains from the family estate to confirm their destruction.",al={"who do you kill","agamand targets","which agamands"},it="target",gp=true},{q="Where are Agamand Mills located?",a="Agamand Mills lies in the northern reaches of Tirisfal Glades, northwest of Brill. The once-fertile estate consists of several ruined windmills and farmhouses infested with Scourge.",al={"where is agamand mills","agamand location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What does Coleman Farthing do with their remains?",a="Coleman asks for their remains purely to confirm their destruction. The trophy represents the closure of his mortal grudges and proof that the proud lords who doomed him have been permanently extinguished.",al={"why collect remains","farthing remains"},it="item"},{q="Who are you sent to destroy at the mills?",a="You are sent to destroy the undead remnants of the Agamand family: Gregor, Nissa, and Thurman. In life, they owned the expansive Agamand Mills and convinced their laborers to stay and defend the estate against the Scourge. Their stand failed, and the family members were raised as mindless thralls of the Lich King, haunting the grounds where their workers perished.",al={"who are the targets","who are the agamands","agamand targets"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task reflects the profound personal grudges many Forsaken still bear from their mortal lives, illustrating the lingering trauma of Lordaeron's fall and the bitterness toward those who led them to ruin.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing the Agamand leadership removes dangerous Scourge commanders lurking uncomfortably close to Brill and the Undercity's northern approaches."},kw={"agamand","agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","deaths in the family","gregor agamand","nissa agamand","scourge","thurman agamand","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},m={start="Coleman Farthing",level="11",faction="Horde",id=354,ri={"Gregor's Remains","Nissa's Remains","Thurman's Remains"}},src={"Deaths in the Family (Classic)"}}
E["quest:355"] = {t="quest",n="Speak with Sevren",z="tirisfal",s="Following the settling of Coleman Farthing's personal score at Agamand Mills, Magistrate Sevren summons the adventurer to Brill. The mills house far more than bitter family ghosts; they represent an active Scourge peril on the settlement's northern border.",h="Following the settling of Coleman Farthing's personal score at Agamand Mills, Magistrate Sevren summons the...",sec={{t="From Vengeance to Civic Duty",b="Coleman Farthing sought personal retribution against his former neighbors, the Agamands, sending travelers north to tear through the undead lurking about the flour mills. Once those remains were delivered, Coleman recognized that his private grudge intersected with a wider tactical problem: the entire Agamand estate remains swarming with mindlessly aggressive Scourge minions right on Brill's doorstep.",sp=0},{t="The Magistrate's Call",b="Magistrate Sevren oversees civic and military security in Brill for the Dark Lady. Impressed by reports of an adventurer capable of surviving deep incursions into the haunted farmlands, Sevren steps in to direct those capable hands toward the root of the infestation—the ancient Agamand family crypt itself.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Coleman Farthing send me to Magistrate Sevren?",a="Coleman satisfied his own vengeance against the resurrected Agamand family, but he knows the undead presence at the mills threatens all of Brill. He reported your combat prowess to Magistrate Sevren, who organizes local defense.",al={"why go to sevren","coleman farthing referral","who is magistrate sevren"},it="motive"},{q="Where can Magistrate Sevren be found?",a="Magistrate Sevren is stationed in Brill, Tirisfal Glades, usually found standing outside the town hall directing local affairs for the Forsaken.",al={"sevren location","where is magistrate sevren","find sevren"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What threat do the Agamand Mills pose?",a="The mills lie just north of Brill and remain thoroughly infested by mindless Scourge. Left unchecked, the walking dead and restless spirits wandering the fields could easily overrun the town.",al={"agamand mills danger","why are mills a threat"},it="threat"},{q="What happens after speaking with Magistrate Sevren?",a="Sevren enlists you for a direct strike into the Agamand Family Crypt to eliminate the deeper Scourge threat anchoring the undead to the mills.",al={"next quest after sevren","what does sevren want"},it="next_step"},{q="Does this quest require returning to the mills immediately?",a="This brief assignment is simply a bridge. You report to Sevren in Brill to conclude Coleman's lead before accepting your next mission into the crypt.",al={"do i go back to the mills","is this quest just talking"},gp=true},{q="Why does Brill care about the Agamand family?",a="In life, the Agamands were wealthy regional mill owners and prominent neighbors in Tirisfal. In death, their unhallowed estate serves as a fortified staging ground for mindless Scourge.",al={"agamand importance","who are the agamands to brill"},it="why_wanted"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Proving capable against the mindless Scourge earns rapid recognition in Brill, moving you from freelance odd-jobs to direct civic defense under Magistrate Sevren.",["faction:Horde"]="Brill represents the first line of defense for the Undercity; securing the surrounding farmland from the Scourge is vital for the Horde's hold on northern Lordaeron."},kw={"agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","magistrate sevren","sevren","the family crypt","the haunted mills","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:magistrate-sevren","npc:coleman-farthing","quest:362","quest:408","subzone:brill","subzone:agamand-mills","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},m={start="Coleman Farthing",["end"]="Magistrate Sevren",level="10",faction="Horde",id=355},src={"Speak with Sevren (Classic)"}}
E["quest:356"] = {t="quest",n="Rear Guard Patrol",z="tirisfal",s="Rear Guard Patrol is a military scouting mission tasked by Deathguard Linnea to protect the Forsaken defenses along Tirisfal's eastern border. It directs operatives to purge stray Scourge horrors and lingering spirits haunting the Balnir Farmstead before they can compromise the supply lines supporting the Bulwark.",h="Rear Guard Patrol is a military scouting mission tasked by Deathguard Linnea to protect the Forsaken defenses...",sec={{t="Securing the Bulwark's Flank",b="The Bulwark stands as the primary bulwark holding back the pestilence and armies of the Western Plaguelands from spilling into Tirisfal Glades. Deathguard Linnea oversees rear-echelon security along this frontier. Because the line cannot afford to divide its forces against ambushes from behind, any undead that bypass the choke point must be destroyed before they can sever the supply lines feeding the front.",sp=0},{t="The Ruins of Balnir Farmstead",b="Once famous across Lordaeron for breeding prize royal steeds, the Balnir Farmstead fell to ruin during the Third War. Now thoroughly blighted, the overgrown fields and crumbling stables provide cover for interloping Bleeding Horrors and restless, Wandering Spirits. Clearing the site denies the Lich King a staging post inside Tirisfal itself and safeguards the road for Horde reinforcements.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is this patrol important for the Bulwark?",a="The Bulwark holds the narrow border pass against the Western Plaguelands. If wandering Scourge flank the fortification or settle behind it, the defenders would be caught in a two-front battle and lose the supply lines connecting them to Brill and the Undercity.",al={"why do rear guard patrol","bulwark importance","why secure balnir"},it="motive"},{q="Who orders this mission?",a="Deathguard Linnea, an officer stationed near the road leading east into the Plaguelands, assigns the patrol and forwards reports of success to Executor Zygand.",al={"who gives rear guard patrol","deathguard linnea"},it="identity"},{q="What creatures haunt the Balnir Farmstead?",a="The blighted farmstead crawls with Bleeding Horrors—reanimated corpses stitched together by necromancy—and Wandering Spirits of former inhabitants who died during the Scourge's initial conquest.",al={"balnir farmstead mobs","what monsters are at balnir","bleeding horror"},it="target"},{q="What was the Balnir Farmstead before the plague?",a="Before the Third War, the Balnir family bred prized horses renowned throughout the Kingdom of Lordaeron, including the noble steeds favored by Lordaeron's royalty and chivalry.",al={"balnir lore","what was balnir farmstead","balnir history"},it="history"},{q="Who is Executor Zygand?",a="Executor Zygand is a senior Forsaken Deathguard commander based in Brill and the superior of Deathguard Linnea.",al={"executor zygand lore","who is zygand"},it="leader"},{q="Why don't the Forsaken just abandon the eastern border?",a="Abandoning the Bulwark would open all of Tirisfal Glades and the gates of the Undercity directly to the endless hordes of the Scourge marching from the Plaguelands.",al={"why defend the bulwark","why fight at the border"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Deathguard Linnea need an adventurer instead of handling the patrol herself?",a="The defenders stationed at the Bulwark are stretched thin holding the eastern frontier against constant assaults from the Plaguelands. Linnea and the garrison cannot afford to abandon their fortified posts to sweep the rear without weakening the main line. Turning to capable travelers allows the Forsaken to clear out infiltrators behind them while maintaining an unbroken wall of defense against the Scourge.",al={"why me linnea","why send an adventurer rear guard patrol","why doesnt linnea do it"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, safeguarding the Bulwark's rear is a matter of sheer survival; breaking the Scourge's presence at Balnir Farmstead keeps the homelands free from the Lich King's renewed grasp.",["faction:Horde"]="Holding Tirisfal's eastern perimeter ensures the Horde maintains its tenuous foothold in the northern Eastern Kingdoms against the undead legions."},kw={"balnir","bleeding horror","bulwark","linnea","scourge","tirisfal","wandering spirit","zygand"},rel={"npc:deathguard-linnea","subzone:balnir-farmstead","subzone:bulwark","npc:executor-zygand","quest:1882","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Linnea",level="11",faction="Horde",id=356},src={"Rear Guard Patrol"}}
E["quest:358"] = {t="quest",n="Graverobbers",z="tirisfal",s="Magistrate Sevren of Brill tasks adventurers with purging Rot Hide gnolls from the Mass Grave southwest of Garren's Haunt. These undead beasts were sent by the Scourge to dig up the corpses of early plague victims to swell their ranks. Slaying them and recovering their embalming ichor protects the fallen and aids the Royal Apothecary Society's research.",h="Magistrate Sevren of Brill tasks adventurers with purging Rot Hide gnolls from the Mass Grave southwest of...",sec={{t="Desecration of the Mass Graves",b="When the Plague of Undeath first tore through Tirisfal Glades, the dead piled up faster than formal burials could accommodate. Immense mass graves were dug across the north to contain the staggering loss. Until recently, those buried remains escaped resurrection. Now, however, the Scourge has unleashed Rot Hide gnolls upon the trenches southwest of Garren's Haunt, sending them to exhumate the decayed remains to reinforce the Lich King's armies.",sp=0},{t="Turning Scourge Alchemy Inward",b="The Rot Hides are sustained by embalming ichor, an alchemical concoction that preserves and animates their rotting frames. By commanding scouts to slay the graverobbers and extract this fluid, Magistrate Sevren strikes two blows at once: halting the Scourge's harvest of raw bodies and delivering valuable biological specimens directly to the apothecaries in Brill, who seek to turn the Lich King's own craft against him.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Scourge digging up the Mass Grave?",a="The Mass Grave holds countless victims from the initial arrival of the Plague in Tirisfal. The Scourge intends to unearth these preserved bodies to raise new soldiers and swell their armies against the Forsaken.",al={"why are gnolls digging corpses","what does the scourge want with bodies","mass grave purpose"},it="threat"},{q="What are the Rot Hide gnolls?",a="They are undead gnolls enslaved by the Scourge. Animated and preserved using alchemical embalming ichor, they serve as scavengers and shock troops around Garren's Haunt and the neighboring graves.",al={"rot hide lore","undead gnolls tirisfal","what are rot hides"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the Mass Grave located?",a="The Mass Grave lies southwest of Garren's Haunt, in the northern hills of Tirisfal Glades, just north of Brill.",al={"where are graverobbers","mass grave location","garren's haunt mass grave"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What do the Forsaken want with the Embalming Ichor?",a="Magistrate Sevren hands the collected ichor over to Forsaken apothecaries to study how the Scourge animates and maintains its lesser thralls, searching for weaknesses to exploit in the ongoing war.",al={"why collect embalming ichor","what is ichor for","ichor lore"},it="item"},{q="Who orders the death of the Graverobbers?",a="Magistrate Sevren, the Forsaken overseer of Brill, issues the bounty to defend Tirisfal's borders and secure useful reagents for the Apothecaries.",al={"who gives graverobbers","magistrate sevren role"},it="in_charge"},{q="Why do the Forsaken care if dead plague victims are raised?",a="Every corpse claimed and raised by the Scourge becomes an enemy soldier marching under the Lich King's will, directly threatening the newly liberated Forsaken kingdom.",al={"why do forsaken protect graves","why care about dead bodies"},it="motive"},{q="Who are you being sent against at the Mass Grave?",a="You are sent to destroy the Rot Hide gnolls—specifically the graverobbers and mongrels—haunting Garren's Haunt and the nearby mass grave. These undead beasts act as vile thralls for the Scourge, tasked with exhuming the countless victims of the original plague to bolster the ranks of the Lich King's armies right on Brill's doorstep.",al={"who are the enemies","what are rot hides doing","target of graverobbers"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, preventing the Scourge from raising Tirisfal's former citizens spares their fallen countrymen the horrific fate of mindless servitude.",["faction:Horde"]="Halting the Scourge's resupply efforts secures northern Lordaeron, strengthening the Horde's foothold against the Lich King's remaining forces."},kw={"brill","embalming ichor","garren's haunt","graverobbers","magistrate sevren","mass grave","rot hide","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:magistrate-sevren","subzone:garren-s-haunt","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Magistrate Sevren",level="8",faction="Horde",id=358,ri={"Embalming Ichor"}},src={"Graverobbers (Classic)"}}
E["quest:360"] = {t="quest",n="Return to the Magistrate",z="tirisfal",s="Return to the Magistrate concludes an urgent reconnaissance dispatch between the eastern border of Tirisfal Glades and the town of Brill. Having reported to Deathguard Linnea at her waystation east of Brill, adventurers are sent back to deliver troubling news directly to Magistrate Sevren. The message confirms that the mindless minions of the Lich King continue to test the borders of the young Forsaken nation.",h="Return to the Magistrate concludes an urgent reconnaissance dispatch between the eastern border of Tirisfal...",sec={{t="Frontier Reports",b="The eastern road out of Tirisfal Glades leads directly into the blighted ruins of the Western Plaguelands, where the Scourge holds devastating sway. To keep the undead hordes from spilling over into their newly claimed homeland, the Forsaken established the Bulwark—a fortified checkpoint watched from a waystation behind the line by grim sentries like Deathguard Linnea. After gathering reports of what presses against the eastern gate, Linnea sends messengers racing back west along the King's Road toward Brill before enemy infiltrators can capitalize on any gaps.",sp=0},{t="Sevren's Vow",b="When Magistrate Sevren receives the dispatch in Brill, it confirms his worst fears: Scourge scouts are finding pathways around or through the frontline perimeter. For the Forsaken, every creeping ghoul and mindless skeleton is not merely a military nuisance, but a reminder of the enslavement they broke free from during the fall of Lordaeron. Sevren remains adamant that no matter how ceaseless the Lich King's onslaught becomes, the free undead will never surrender their newfound autonomy.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where does this report come from?",a="The report comes from Deathguard Linnea, stationed at a Deathguard waystation east of Brill, on the road toward the Bulwark. She gathers reports from the barricades separating Tirisfal from the Scourge-choked Western Plaguelands.",al={"quest origin","who sent this report","where is linnea"},it="origin"},{q="Why is Magistrate Sevren concerned about this news?",a="The dispatch informs Sevren that Scourge forces have managed to slip past the Bulwark's fortifications. For the Forsaken in Brill, enemy incursions threaten their fledgling towns and serve as a constant reminder of the Lich King's efforts to reclaim them.",al={"why is sevren worried","what does the report say","scourge threat"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Magistrate Sevren?",a="Magistrate Sevren is the civil administrator of Brill. Operating out of the town hall, he oversees local defenses, infrastructure, and the mobilization of newly raised Forsaken citizens across central Tirisfal Glades.",al={"sevren lore","who is the magistrate"},it="identity"},{q="What is the Bulwark?",a="The Bulwark is a fortified outpost constructed along the main thoroughfare between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands. It serves as the primary barrier preventing Scourge incursions into Forsaken lands.",al={"bulwark lore","eastern border tirisfal"},it="place"},{q="Why do the Forsaken hate the Scourge so much?",a="The Forsaken were once mindless thralls of the Scourge, slaughtered in life and raised in undeath to butcher their own kin. Having regained their free will, they despise the Lich King and fear losing their minds to his control once again.",al={"forsaken vs scourge","why hate lich king"},it="conflict"},{q="What path does this message travel?",a="Messengers travel west along the main road from Deathguard Linnea's waystation, heading through central Tirisfal Glades until reaching the town center of Brill.",al={"route from bulwark to brill","road to brill"}},{q="Why does Deathguard Linnea want this done?",a="Deathguard Linnea is stationed at the Bulwark, the vital fortified border holding back the horrors of the Western Plaguelands. When Scourge forces breach these eastern defenses and infiltrate Tirisfal Glades, Brill's leadership must know immediately. Linnea needs Magistrate Sevren warned without delay so local authorities can prepare for incursions and mobilize defenders against the Lich King's relentless minions.",al={"why linnea report","why send report to sevren","deathguard linnea motive"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Linnea need you to deliver the message?",a="Linnea and her fellow Deathguards are tasked with guarding the Bulwark against nonstop Scourge incursions spilling from the Plaguelands. With enemy forces actively slipping past the perimeter, every soldier at the barricade is essential to prevent a complete breakthrough. She cannot spare guards from their posts to act as couriers, making a capable traveling adventurer the fastest and most practical option.",al={"why not send a deathguard","why linnea needs me","why deliver message to sevren"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Carrying intelligence from the Bulwark brings home the precarious reality of Forsaken freedom: the master you escaped still clawing at the doorstep of your new home.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde's foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms relies on the stability of Tirisfal Glades, making the Bulwark's defenses critical against the undead horrors of the Plaguelands."},kw={"brill","bulwark","forsaken duties","linnea","scourge","sevren","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:brill","subzone:bulwark","npc:magistrate-sevren","npc:deathguard-linnea","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Linnea",["end"]="Magistrate Sevren",level="9",faction="Horde",id=360},src={"Return to the Magistrate (Classic)"}}
E["quest:361"] = {t="quest",n="A Letter Undelivered",z="tirisfal",s="A faded, bloodstained missive found among the skeletal ruins of Agamand Mills, addressed to Yvette Farthing. It reveals a tragic parting between two lovers as the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, offering a glimpse into what the Forsaken left behind when they traded life for undeath.",h="A faded, bloodstained missive found among the skeletal ruins of Agamand Mills, addressed to Yvette Farthing.",sec={{t="A Message Lost in the Fall",b="Written by Thurman Agamand, the eldest son of patriarch Gregor Agamand, this unsent parchment never reached its intended recipient before the family estate collapsed into ruin. When the Plague of Undeath consumed Tirisfal Glades, Thurman refused to abandon his ancestral home and kin. Amid the horrors of the onslaught, he took up quill to write one last message to Yvette Farthing, whom he had loved before the kingdom turned to ash.",sp=0},{t="The Severed Bond",b="When the letter is finally placed into Yvette Farthing's hands at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, it provokes a brief, agonizing flash of memory. She remembers begging Thurman to flee Agamand Mills alongside her and her father, and she laments that Thurman's brother, Brand, bore ill will toward him. Yet the warmth is fleeting. Like so many of the Forsaken, Yvette steels her heart, casting aside mortal remorse and declaring that love has no place in her new existence.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who wrote this letter?",a="The letter was penned by Thurman Agamand, the eldest son of Gregor Agamand. Thurman perished defending his family's mills against the Scourge alongside the rest of his cursed household.",al={"author","thurman agamand","who is thurman"},it="identity"},{q="Who is Yvette Farthing?",a="Yvette Farthing is a Forsaken woman currently residing inside the tavern in Brill. In life, she and her father fled the countryside before the Scourge, leaving behind her lover, Thurman Agamand.",al={"yvette","who is yvette","recipient"},it="people"},{q="Where did the letter come from?",a="The letter is recovered from the walking skeletons prowling the devastated grounds and fields of Agamand Mills in western Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to find letter","letter drop","letter location"},it="origin"},{q="What happened between Thurman and Yvette?",a="They were lovers before the Third War. Yvette begged Thurman to abandon his family's mills and escape with her, but Thurman chose loyalty to his kin and remained behind to face the Plague.",al={"thurman and yvette","yvette backstory","what happened to them"},it="history"},{q="What does Yvette do when she reads the letter?",a="She grieves briefly for Thurman and curses both the Scourge and his brother Brand, but quickly suppresses her emotions. She insists that regrets are for the weak and that romance has no place in her Forsaken existence.",al={"yvette reaction","what does yvette say"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Who is Brand Agamand?",a="Brand was Thurman's brother. According to Yvette, Brand harbored deep resentment and ill intent toward Thurman even before the estate fell to the Plague.",al={"brand","brand agamand","who is brand"},it="threat"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The letter embodies the sharp, painful division between a Forsaken's mortal memories and their cold, purpose-driven existence under the Banshee Queen.",["faction:Horde"]="For travelers visiting Brill, this recovered parchment offers a stark demonstration of the emotional detachment that defines the Forsaken people."},kw={"agamand","brill","farthing","letter","scourge","tavern","thurman","undelivered","yvette"},rel={"npc:yvette-farthing","subzone:agamand-mills","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","npc:farthing"},m={start="A Letter to Yvette",["end"]="Yvette Farthing",level="7",faction="Horde",id=361},src={"A Letter Undelivered"}}
E["quest:362"] = {t="quest",n="The Haunted Mills",z="tirisfal",s="Coleman Farthing of Brill seeks vengeance against his former landowners, the Agamands, by having their restless undead remains collected and destroyed. This task sends you north to Agamand Mills to put down Devlin Agamand, the sharp-tongued younger son of the estate. It begins a bitter vendetta against the family who failed to protect Tirisfal's common folk from the Plague.",h="Coleman Farthing of Brill seeks vengeance against his former landowners, the Agamands, by having their...",sec={{t="A Bitter Harvest",b="Before the Third War, Agamand Mills stood as the breadbasket of northern Tirisfal, owned by a wealthy landed family. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, the common folk looked to the noble landowners for safety, only to find neither sanctuary nor salvation. Coleman Farthing survived in undeath, but his heart remains consumed by hatred for the family he served. Rather than letting them roam in eternal torment, Farthing desires the morbid satisfaction of collecting their bones to desecrate them under his own heel.",sp=0},{t="The Younger Brother",b="Devlin Agamand was the younger brother of Thurman Agamand. In life, Devlin was widely remembered as a weak, spiteful, and sharp-tongued youth who stood in stark contrast to his gentle elder brother. When the Plague of Undeath crept into the mill estate, Devlin was among the quickest to fall prey to the corruption. Now, stripped of whatever fragile wit he possessed in life, his mindless corpse chatters incessantly along the northern road leading into the family mills.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Coleman Farthing want Devlin's remains?",a="Coleman Farthing blames the wealthy Agamand family for abandoning and failing him and his relatives when the Scourge overran Lordaeron. Seeking personal revenge in undeath, he wants to gather the Agamand bones so he can display them on his mantle and personally grind them into dust.",al={"why coleman want bones","coleman farthing motive","why kill devlin"},it="motive"},{q="Where can Devlin Agamand be found?",a="Devlin wanders near the road just south of the Agamand Mills in northern Tirisfal Glades, gibbering madly among the undead that prowl the outskirts of the estate.",al={"devlin location","find devlin agamand","where is devlin"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What was Devlin like before the Plague?",a="According to Coleman Farthing, Devlin was the smaller, weaker, and distinctly sharp-tongued younger son of the Agamand household, very unlike his tall and mild-mannered older brother Thurman.",al={"who was devlin","devlin agamand lore","devlin agamand in life"},it="identity"},{q="Is Devlin part of the Scourge or Forsaken?",a="Devlin is a mindless thrall of the Scourge. Unlike the Forsaken of the Undercity, he never broke free of the Lich King's grip and mindlessly stalks his family's ruined lands.",al={"is devlin scourge","devlin faction"},it="side"},{q="What other quests take place at Agamand Mills?",a="The quest 'Deaths in the Family' also directs Forsaken adventurers to Agamand Mills to eliminate the rest of the Agamand household, making it natural to pursue both tasks concurrently.",al={"agamand mills quests","quests near devlin"},gp=true},{q="What happens after turning in Devlin's remains?",a="After accepting the bones, Coleman Farthing directs you to speak with Sevren in Brill, which eventually leads deeper into the Agamand family crypt.",al={"quest continuation","next quest after haunted mills"},sp=1,it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What does Coleman Farthing do with Devlin's Remains?",a="Coleman intends to mount Devlin's bones right on his mantle, keeping them as a grim trophy of vengeance. He harbors deep, bitter hatred toward the Agamand family for failing him and his relatives when the Scourge swept through Tirisfal. Rather than granting the remains a proper burial or study, Coleman seeks the petty satisfaction of gloating over what is left of the weak, sharp-tongued younger brother, ultimately swearing to grind the Agamand bones under his heel.",al={"devlins remains use","why keep devlins remains","what happens to devlins bones"},it="item",add=true},{q="Who is Devlin Agamand?",a="Devlin was the younger son of the prominent Agamand dynasty, known in life as weak and viciously sharp-tongued in contrast to his gentle older brother, Thurman. When the Plague of Undeath engulfed Agamand Mills, Devlin was among the quickest to succumb. Now risen as a mindless undead monster under the Scourge's thrall, he wanders the road just south of his family's ruined mills, chattering madly to himself in the Tirisfal gloom.",al={"who is devlin agamand","who is devlin","devlin agamand lore"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task reflects the deep class resentments that survived Lordaeron's fall, showing how Forsaken retain mortal grudges against the landed nobility who failed to protect them from the Plague.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the Forsaken in Tirisfal means helping them purge dangerous pockets of the Scourge from the borders of Brill and Undercity."},kw={"agamand mills","brill","coleman farthing","devlin agamand","haunted mills","plague","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:coleman-farthing","subzone:agamand-mills","quest:354","quest:355","quest:408","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Coleman Farthing",level="10",faction="Horde",id=362,ri={"Devlin's Remains"}},src={"The Haunted Mills (Classic)"}}
E["quest:363"] = {t="quest",n="Rude Awakening",z="tirisfal",s="Rude Awakening is the first task given to newly awakened Forsaken within the crypts of Deathknell. After rising from death freed from the Lich King's iron grip, you are directed away from the incinerators and sent down the hill to learn the reality of your new existence from Shadow Priest Sarvis.",h="Rude Awakening is the first task given to newly awakened Forsaken within the crypts of Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Grave Beginning",b="Opening your eyes in the damp subterranean chill of Deathknell's crypt is an unsettling introduction to undeath. Undertaker Mordo stands watch over the freshly interred, sorting the revived into those who have reclaimed their free will and those destined for the burning pits. For many, rising from the grave means waking to rot, cold stone, and total disorientation, having slipped through the grasp of the Scourge by sheer circumstance.",sp=0},{t="Breaking the Grip of the Frozen Throne",b="Before reaching the chapel, newly awakened dead must grasp a grim truth: they were once mindless tools of the Lich King. It was Lady Sylvanas Windrunner who shattered that psychic enslavement, allowing remnants of former humanity to reclaim independent thought. Stepping up from the tomb and heading down into Deathknell marks the first step into a world where both the living and the Scourge view you as an abomination to be eradicated.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was I in a crypt?",a="You died, succumbed to the plague or violence of the Third War, and were buried in Tirisfal Glades. Deathknell serves as the primary gathering point where bodies are monitored to see if they regain sapience or succumb to mindless rot.",al={"why in crypt","where did i wake up","deathknell tomb"},it="arrival"},{q="Who is Undertaker Mordo?",a="Mordo is the caretaker of the crypt in Deathknell. His duty is to watch over the newly awakened, direct those who maintain their sanity toward the town chapel, and dispose of those who fail to awaken with their minds intact.",al={"undertaker mordo","who is mordo","gravekeeper"},it="identity"},{q="Why did Mordo mention throwing people into the fire?",a="Not every corpse raised from the grave regains free will or stable thoughts. Those who awaken completely mad or purely feral cannot be integrated into Forsaken society and are destroyed and incinerated to prevent rampages.",al={"burn bodies","toss into fire","cremation"},it="history"},{q="Who freed me from the Lich King?",a="Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen, broke free from the Lich King's psychic domination as his power waned. She rallied other free-willed undead under her banner, establishing the Forsaken to battle both their former master and hostile living remnants.",al={"who freed me","sylvanas freed undead","why am i free"},it="origin"},{q="Where is Shadow Priest Sarvis located?",a="Sarvis resides inside the damaged chapel at the base of the hill in Deathknell. Walking out of the crypt and following the cobblestone path downhill brings you directly to the chapel entrance.",al={"find sarvis","sarvis location","where is the chapel"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who are the Forsaken fighting against?",a="The Forsaken wage a two-front war for survival: against the Scourge, who seek to reclaim or obliterate them, and against human zealots and surviving settlements who refuse to distinguish them from mindless monsters.",al={"forsaken enemies","who do we fight","undead enemies"},it="conflict"},{q="Why does Undertaker Mordo send me to Shadow Priest Sarvis?",a="Undertaker Mordo's duty is merely to tend the crypts and sort the salvageable undead from the truly lost. He is no statesman or spiritual guide. Newly awakened Forsaken are disoriented, traumatized, and dangerously unaware of the war around them. By sending you immediately down the hill to Shadow Priest Sarvis in the chapel, Mordo ensures you are educated, integrated into Lady Sylvanas's cause, and trained for survival before you wander off or fall into the hands of enemies.",al={"why visit sarvis","mordo motive","why send me to church"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This moment defines your rebirth: shedding the shackles of the Scourge to carve out a spiteful, free existence among the Forsaken.",["class:Priest"]="Reporting to Shadow Priest Sarvis introduces you immediately to the Cult of Forgotten Shadow, which reinterprets spiritual devotion in the wake of undeath.",["faction:Horde"]="This awakening places you in the fragile orbit of the Horde, an alliance born out of sheer necessity against mutual extinction."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken","lich king","mordo","rude awakening","sarvis","scourge","shadow priest sarvis","sylvanas","tirisfal glades","undertaker mordo"},rel={"npc:undertaker-mordo","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","subzone:deathknell","quest:364","zone:tirisfal","topic:lich-king","topic:scourge","topic:human"},m={start="Undertaker Mordo",["end"]="Shadow Priest Sarvis",level="1",faction="Horde",id=363},src={"Rude Awakening"}}
E["quest:364"] = {t="quest",n="The Mindless Ones",z="tirisfal",s="Shadow Priest Sarvis tasks newly awakened Forsaken with culling the Mindless Zombies and Wretched Zombies that wander the northern edges of Deathknell. These shambling corpses are Scourge thralls, devoid of free will and utterly bound to the Lich King's command.",h="Shadow Priest Sarvis tasks newly awakened Forsaken with culling the Mindless Zombies and Wretched Zombies...",sec={{t="Slaves to the Frozen Throne",b="Not all who rise from the dirt of Lordaeron regain their minds. While the Forsaken threw off the mental domination of Ner'zhul when his power waned, countless corpses remain hollow husks chained to his will. In northern Deathknell, these Mindless and Wretched Zombies wander without purpose other than raw violence. For a newly risen undead, putting them down is the first bitter trial of existence—proving one's autonomy by severing the chains of those who never broke free.",sp=0},{t="Mercy Through Destruction",b="Though Shadow Priest Sarvis laments that these vast legions of dead cannot be recruited to bolster the Forsaken's desperate struggle, there is no curing them. The mindless undead are permanently broken instruments of slaughter. Leaving them to roam invites chaos to Deathknell's doorstep and provides the Lich King with easy fodder. Eliminating them is both a tactical necessity and an act of grim finality for Lordaeron's fallen people.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why can't the mindless zombies be recruited into the Forsaken?",a="They lack the cognitive will to break free from the Lich King. Shadow Priest Sarvis notes that while their numbers would be invaluable, their minds are entirely degraded. Because they cannot choose to join Lady Sylvanas, they remain dangerous Scourge thralls that must be purged.",al={"recruit mindless zombies","why kill them","can mindless zombies be cured","free the zombies"}},{q="Who are these zombies originally?",a="They are the former human inhabitants of Lordaeron who fell during the Third War to the plague of undeath. Unlike the player character, they never recovered their memories, personalities, or individual agency when the Lich King's grip slipped.",al={"who were the mindless ones","identity of mindless zombies","deathknell corpses"},it="origin"},{q="Why is Shadow Priest Sarvis commanding the defense here?",a="Shadow Priest Sarvis oversees the initial intake and survival of newly risen undead in Deathknell. He assesses whether newly awakened Forsaken have the grit and clarity of mind to survive the harsh reality of their existence.",al={"who is sarvis","sarvis deathknell role","priest sarvis"},it="in_charge"},{q="What is the difference between Forsaken and Scourge zombies?",a="Free will. Forsaken broke away from the Lich King's telepathic domination and possess self-determination, intelligence, and emotion. Scourge zombies are empty vessels puppeteered directly by the Lich King's malice.",al={"scourge vs forsaken","difference between forsaken and mindless","why are some zombies mindless"},it="identity"},{q="Are the mindless zombies a threat to Deathknell?",a="Yes. Wandering just north of the village, their aggression poses an immediate hazard to new arrivals emerging from the graves and the Forsaken priests trying to organize the settlement.",al={"deathknell danger","why clear northern deathknell","zombie threat"},it="danger"},{q="Where does this task lead next?",a="Proving competence against the mindless dead leads to further duties securing Deathknell, such as culling skeletal Scourge in Rattling the Rattlecages and pursuing initial training with your class mentor.",al={"next quest after mindless ones","mindless ones followup","what comes next"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Shadow Priest Sarvis want the zombies destroyed?",a="Deathknell is surrounded and under constant pressure from the Lich King's minions. Sarvis sees the overrun northern side of the village as an immediate danger to the Forsaken foothold here. The Scourge cannot be reasoned with or swayed to your cause, so destroying them is the only way to secure the area and ensure the survival of your newly awakened people.",al={"sarvis motive","why kill mindless zombies","why destroy mindless ones"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are the Mindless Zombies wandering northern Deathknell?",a="They are slain residents of Lordaeron raised by the Scourge who never regained their free will. Trapped as feral thralls to the Lich King's will, these mindless and wretched corpses now shamble through the ruins of northern Deathknell, attacking anyone who crosses their path.",al={"who are the mindless zombies","wretched zombies","what are mindless ones"},it="target",add=true},{q="Why does Sarvis send you to kill the zombies instead of the guards?",a="Sarvis is testing your resolve and capability in this new existence. Deathknell's established guardians must maintain their posts around the chapel and perimeter. Proving that you can destroy even mindless former kin demonstrates you possess the stomach and martial strength needed to serve the Dark Lady.",al={"why me","why send me to kill zombies","why sarvis asks you"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every zombie roaming the village outskirts could have been your neighbor, kin, or friend in life, serving as an immediate reminder of what you were spared—and what happens if the Forsaken fall.",["class:Priest"]="Serving under Shadow Priest Sarvis introduces you to the pragmatic, shadow-tinged philosophy the Forsaken have adopted in place of the Holy Light that abandoned them.",["faction:Horde"]="The existence of thralls like these illustrates why the Horde viewed the Forsaken with such deep suspicion before granting them entry into the faction."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken","lich king","mindless ones","mindless zombie","scourge","shadow priest sarvis","wretched zombie"},rel={"npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","quest:363","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Shadow Priest Sarvis",level="2",faction="Horde",id=364},src={"The Mindless Ones"}}
E["quest:365"] = {t="quest",n="Fields of Grief",z="tirisfal",s="Fields of Grief is an early Forsaken mission assigned by Deathguard Simmer along the road north of Deathknell. It tasks fledglings with raiding a living human farmstead to harvest Tirisfal pumpkins for the Royal Apothecary Society in Brill.",h="Fields of Grief is an early Forsaken mission assigned by Deathguard Simmer along the road north of Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Lesson in Survival",b="For new Forsaken adventurers freshly risen from the crypts of Deathknell, stepping onto the misty roads of Tirisfal Glades is an immediate lesson in hostility. Deathguard Simmer represents the pragmatic malice of Sylvanas Windrunner's forces: survival means seizing what the living possess and turning it toward the Undercity's darker endeavors.",sp=0},{t="Raiding the Living",b="The targets of the raid are the mortal human farmers attempting to work the blighted soil west of the road, north of Deathknell. Described by the Deathguard as infesting the realm like mold on a corpse, these peasants cling to their homesteads under the uneasy protection of nearby Scarlet Crusade towers. Snatching their crops deals a spiteful blow to their supplies while feeding the Royal Apothecary Society's endless demand for reagents.",sp=0},{t="Supplies for the Society",b="The stolen pumpkins are delivered directly to Apothecary Johaan, who conducts experimental research in Brill. The Royal Apothecary Society frequently uses local agricultural produce and flora as bases for alchemical brews, poisons, and plague research intended to secure the Forsaken's precarious foothold in northern Lordaeron.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest Fields of Grief?",a="Deathguard Simmer gives the quest. He is stationed guarding the road in southern Tirisfal Glades, just north of the exit path leading from Deathknell.",al={"quest giver","fields of grief quest starter","who starts fields of grief"},gp=true},{q="Where do I take the Tirisfal Pumpkins?",a="You deliver the ten pumpkins to Apothecary Johaan, located in the town of Brill further north in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to turn in pumpkins","pumpkin turn in","fields of grief hand in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are there living humans farming in Tirisfal Glades?",a="These human peasants are remnants of Lordaeron's original population who survived the Third War and the Scourge plague. They stubbornly cling to their homesteads in the shadow of nearby Scarlet Crusade guard posts, resisting Forsaken dominion.",al={"why humans here","living humans tirisfal","why are humans in undead zone"},it="people"},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want pumpkins?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society utilizes various plants, blighted crops, and biological matter as testing agents and base components for their alchemical mixtures, poisons, and experimental plagues.",al={"why pumpkins","what are pumpkins for","apothecary johaan pumpkins"},it="item"},{q="Who patrols near the farmstead?",a="Zealots of the Scarlet Crusade patrol down from their watchtower nearby, defending the human settlement and attempting to purge any undead that approach the fields.",al={"scarlet crusade near farm","who protects the farm"},it="threat"},{q="What comes after Fields of Grief?",a="After turning in the pumpkins, Apothecary Johaan immediately gives a follow-up quest of the same name, handing you a pumpkin laced with his latest formula to deliver to a captured Scarlet Zealot held in the cellar of the Gallows' End Tavern.",al={"next quest","fields of grief followup","what is after fields of grief"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Simmer want this done?",a="Deathguard Simmer sees surviving humans farming the glades as mold on a rotting corpse—an intolerable infestation on land that now belongs to the Forsaken. Stealing their crops strikes at their meager livelihood while simultaneously aiding the Royal Apothecary Society with fresh reagents, proving whether newly awakened Forsaken have the grit to serve the Dark Lady.",al={"why simmer pumpkins","deathguard simmer motive","fields of grief reason"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task marks your first military assignment outside Deathknell, proving your willingness to strip your former mortal kin of their livelihood to advance the Royal Apothecary Society.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Tirisfal Glades requires breaking the lingering human presence and dismantling the Scarlet Crusade's influence along the southern roadways.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Apothecaries rely heavily on the twisted flora of Tirisfal to formulate their deadly concoctions, making every local root and crop a reagent of war."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","deathguard simmer","deathknell","fields of grief","royal apothecary society","scarlet crusade","tirisfal pumpkin"},rel={"npc:deathguard-simmer","npc:apothecary-johaan","subzone:brill","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scarlet-crusade"},m={start="Deathguard Simmer",["end"]="Apothecary Johaan",level="7",faction="Horde",id=365,ri={"Tirisfal Pumpkin"}},src={"Fields of Grief (Classic)"}}
E["quest:366"] = {t="quest",n="Return the Book",z="tirisfal",s="Bethor Iceshard enchants Gunther Arcanus's stolen spellbook to prove his own freedom and recruit his former Kirin Tor colleague to the Forsaken cause. Taking the tome back to Gunther's island retreat reveals that neither former wizard is a thrall to the Lich King.",h="Bethor Iceshard enchants Gunther Arcanus's stolen spellbook to prove his own freedom and recruit his former...",sec={{t="A Message in Arcane Ink",b="After discovering that the renegade necromancer on Brightwater Lake was his old companion Gunther Arcanus, Bethor Iceshard devised an ingenious method of communication. Having previously ordered the theft of Gunther's spellbook to uncover the necromancer's identity, Bethor weaves an intricate dweomer into its pages. Rather than a demand or an armed parley, the enchantment serves as an arcane signature, proving that Bethor is not only active in Lordaeron, but completely liberated from the Lich King's mental yoke.",sp=0},{t="The Old Circle of Dalaran",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, Bethor Iceshard, Gunther Arcanus, and Thule Ravenclaw were fellow students and friends within the Kirin Tor in Dalaran. All three succumbed to the horrors of the Plague, but their paths diverged sharply in death. Thule embraced the Scourge and continues to serve the Lich King, while Bethor broke free alongside Queen Sylvanas. Believing Gunther was either dead or enslaved, Bethor's realization that Gunther also achieved self-willed undeath sparked an urgent effort to draw him into the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="Mistrust on the Island",b="When approached on his solitary island, Gunther initially lashes out in fury, assuming anyone trespassing on Brightwater Lake is an agent of the Scourge seeking to re-enslave him or the thief who plundered his magic. The sight of his personal grimoire, accompanied by Bethor's familiar magical resonance, catches the solitary mage off guard. Recognizing that someone from his mortal past broke the Scourge's shackles forces Gunther to reconsider his absolute isolation.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why did Bethor enchant Gunther's spellbook?",a="Bethor enchanted the stolen grimoire with a personal magical signature to prove to Gunther that he was truly free from the Lich King. Knowing Gunther's deep paranoia and hatred of the Scourge, a mundane messenger would have been rejected or destroyed on sight.",al={"why enchant the book","gunthers book spell","what did bethor do to the book"},it="item"},{q="Who was Gunther Arcanus before he died?",a="Gunther Arcanus was a human mage and scholar of the Kirin Tor in Dalaran. He studied alongside Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw before the Third War, eventually falling victim to the Scourge's Plague of Undeath.",al={"who is gunther arcanus","gunther backstory","gunther lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why is Gunther hiding on Brightwater Lake?",a="After breaking free from the Lich King's control, Gunther believed he was entirely alone in his freedom. He secluded himself on an island in Brightwater Lake to avoid being hunted down and re-enslaved by the Scourge or killed by living enemies.",al={"why is gunther on the island","gunthers retreat lore","why did gunther leave"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is Thule Ravenclaw?",a="Thule Ravenclaw was the third member of Bethor and Gunther's circle of Kirin Tor students in Dalaran. Unlike Bethor and Gunther, who broke their shackles, Thule willingly remains a loyal necromancer serving the Scourge in Silverpine Forest.",al={"who is thule","thule ravenclaw lore","third friend"},it="history"},{q="Why does Gunther think the player is a thief?",a="Earlier on, Bethor instructed Forsaken adventurers to sneak into Gunther's sanctum and steal his spellbook so Bethor could verify who was practicing necromancy on the lake. Gunther correctly recognizes the returning adventurer as the person who robbed him.",al={"why does gunther call me a thief","did i steal gunthers book","stolen book"},it="hostile"},{q="Does Gunther immediately join the Forsaken?",a="Not right away. While stunned to learn Bethor lives and possesses free will, Gunther remains skeptical of the Forsaken and requires proof that you are an enemy of the Scourge: summoning and destroying the Scourge agent Lillith Nefara at an old altar off his island's southern coast.",al={"does gunther join sylvanas","gunther allegience","what happens next with gunther"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Bethor Iceshard want to recruit Gunther?",a="Bethor and Gunther were close friends and fellow mages within Dalaran's Kirin Tor before both succumbed to the Plague. Having learned that Gunther managed to break free from the Lich King's grasp on his own, Bethor desperately wants to bring his powerful former colleague into the Forsaken. By returning Gunther's enchanted spellbook, Bethor hopes to prove that other undead have reclaimed their free will and convince the reclusive necromancer that he no longer needs to stand alone.",al={"why does bethor want gunther","bethor iceshard motives","why recruit gunther"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Gunther's plight mirrors the foundational struggle of the Forsaken: waking from the nightmare of mindless servitude only to face an isolated, hostile world.",["class:Mage"]="This exchange highlights the deep bonds among former Kirin Tor magi, who continue to recognize one another's distinct magical signatures across the veil of undeath.",["faction:Horde"]="Recruiting independent undead sorcerers like Gunther bolsters Undercity's arcane strength against both the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade."},kw={"bethor iceshard","brightwater lake","dalaran","gunther arcanus","gunther's retreat","gunther's spellbook","kirin tor","return the book","thule ravenclaw","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:gunther-arcanus","npc:bethor-iceshard","quest:357","quest:409","subzone:gunther-s-retreat","quest:446","subzone:dalaran","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Bethor Iceshard",["end"]="Gunther Arcanus",level="8",faction="Horde",id=366},src={"Return the Book"}}
E["quest:367"] = {t="quest",n="A New Plague",z="tirisfal",s="A New Plague is an introductory task in Brill that reveals the Forsaken's overarching strategic ambition. Apothecary Johaan tasks recruits with harvesting the blood of wild darkhounds to lay the groundwork for a virulent pathogen commissioned directly by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner.",h="A New Plague is an introductory task in Brill that reveals the Forsaken's overarching strategic ambition.",sec={{t="A Royal Mandate",b="In the damp village of Brill, Apothecary Johaan works on behalf of the Royal Apothecary Society under a mandate directly from the Dark Lady. Sylvanas Windrunner has charged her alchemists with crafting a disease unlike anything Azeroth has ever seen. While the original plague of undeath served the Lich King by slaughtering humanity and binding their souls, this new creation is explicitly designed to annihilate Arthas Menethil and lay waste to his Scourge legions.",sp=0},{t="Raw Ingredients",b="Developing a biological weapon capable of destroying the undead requires grim experimentation. Johaan believes that the blood of indigenous beasts, starting with the fierce darkhounds roaming the gloom of Tirisfal Glades, holds vital chemical properties. By harvesting vials of this tainted animal blood, Johaan begins isolating elements to test whether the natural resilience and predatory biology of native creatures can catalyze a lethal contagion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the goal of the new plague?",a="Lady Sylvanas tasked the Royal Apothecary Society with brewing a disease deadlier than any other on Azeroth. Its primary strategic objective is the complete destruction of Arthas and his Scourge army.",al={"why make a new plague","purpose of new plague","what does the plague do"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Apothecary Johaan?",a="Johaan is a member of the Royal Apothecary Society stationed in the town of Brill. He handles early field experiments, testing biological compounds gathered from Tirisfal's local fauna.",al={"who is johaan","apothecary johaan lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Johaan need darkhound blood?",a="Johaan's research indicates that the blood of native beasts may serve as a crucial foundation for his alchemical formulas. He needs darkhound blood to test his preliminary theories in the laboratory.",al={"why darkhound blood","what is the blood for"},it="item"},{q="How does this plague differ from the Scourge's plague?",a="The original Plague of Undeath was designed by the Lich King to slaughter the living and raise them as thralls. Sylvanas's new plague is specifically engineered as a weapon intended to bring ruin to Arthas's undead legions.",al={"difference between plagues","scourge plague vs forsaken plague"}},{q="Who ordered the creation of this plague?",a="Lady Sylvanas Windrunner directly commanded the Royal Apothecary Society to invent it, believing that Forsaken alchemy combined with newfound magic is the key to defeating Arthas.",al={"who started the plague research","who wants the plague"},it="origin"},{q="What happens after gathering the blood?",a="Gathering darkhound blood is only the first step of an ongoing series of experiments. Johaan uses the samples to begin refining the pathogen through further field tests across the Glades.",al={"what is the next step","does the plague work"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want this done?",a="Lady Sylvanas herself called upon the Royal Apothecary Society to create a weapon deadlier than any affliction on Azeroth, engineered to bring Arthas and his Scourge army to ruin. As an apothecary of the Society stationed in Brill, Johaan is striving to answer the Dark Lady's mandate by testing his theories on indigenous beast reagents.",al={"johaan motive","why does johaan want a new plague","why help johaan"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are darkhounds and why hunt them here?",a="Darkhounds are fierce, corrupted predatory beasts that roam the blighted woods and pathways of Tirisfal Glades. Their blood has absorbed the corruptive energies seeping into the land, making them prime biological specimens for the Royal Apothecary Society's toxicological experiments.",al={"what are darkhounds","darkhounds tirisfal","why darkhounds"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This quest marks your first direct contribution to the Royal Apothecary Society's master project: creating the weapon that Sylvanas plans to wield against Arthas.",["faction:Horde"]="While the Forsaken have joined the Horde, their alchemical endeavors in Tirisfal focus entirely on a grim biological weapon aimed at Northrend."},kw={"apothecary","arthas","brill","darkhound","johaan","plague","scourge","sylvanas","tirisfal"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","topic:royal-apothecary-society","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={start="Apothecary Johaan",level="6",faction="Horde",id=367,ri={"Darkhound Blood"}},src={"A New Plague"}}
E["quest:368"] = {t="quest",n="A New Plague",z="tirisfal",s="Tasked by Apothecary Johaan in Brill, this errand requires gathering scales from the local Vile Fin murlocs prowling the Tirisfal coast. The Royal Apothecary Society aims to harness unique biological reagents to refine their budding new contagion, ensuring it can spread beyond isolated victims to engulf larger populations.",h="Tasked by Apothecary Johaan in Brill, this errand requires gathering scales from the local Vile Fin murlocs...",sec={{t="Scaling Up the Contagion",b="Poisoning a single target is simple alchemy, but formulating a plague capable of sweeping across entire lands demands more complex components. Apothecary Johaan's ongoing research in Brill hinges on creating a disease that can self-propagate and endure in the wild. To engineer this transmissibility, he turns his attention toward the shoreline wildlife of Tirisfal Glades, identifying the resilient biology of the coastal Vile Fin murlocs as a promising source of raw material.",sp=0},{t="The Vile Fin Reagents",b="The northern and western shores of Tirisfal Glades are plagued by aggressive murloc tribes known as the Vile Fin. Harvesting their scales provides Johaan with organic compounds adapted to damp, harsh conditions. By incorporating the murlocs' slick, resilient scales into his developing cauldron, the apothecary seeks to bolster the virulence and environmental stability of the Royal Apothecary Society's new plague.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the goal of Apothecary Johaan's research?",a="Johaan is developing a new plague on behalf of the Royal Apothecary Society and Lady Sylvanas. His goal goes far beyond crafting simple toxins; he is working on a contagion designed to spread across entire populations and lands, targeting both the living and the Scourge.",al={"why make a new plague","johaan plague goal","what is johaan doing"},it="motive"},{q="Why does Johaan need murloc scales specifically?",a="Johaan discovered during his initial brewing that simple poisons lack the biological complexity to spread effectively. The tough, slime-coated scales of the Vile Fin murlocs provide specific biological compounds needed to refine the contagion's ability to survive in the wild and transmit between hosts.",al={"why murloc scales","vile fin scale purpose","why scales for plague"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can the Vile Fin murlocs be found?",a="The Vile Fin murlocs inhabit the coasts of Tirisfal Glades, primarily concentrated along the northern beaches bordering the Great Sea and along the western coast.",al={"where are murlocs tirisfal","vile fin location","murloc spots"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who does Apothecary Johaan answer to?",a="Apothecary Johaan is an operative of the Royal Apothecary Society, an alchemical guild headquartered in the Undercity and answering directly to Queen Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"who leads the apothecaries","johaan boss","royal apothecary society leader"},it="leader"},{q="What is the next step in this quest chain?",a="After bringing the Vile Fin scales to Johaan, he continues refining the formula, which requires sourcing further biological components and eventually putting the newly brewed strain to the test.",al={"what comes next","after vile fin scales","next plague quest"},it="next_step"},{q="Are the Forsaken concerned about using murloc parts?",a="Not at all. The Royal Apothecary Society is notoriously pragmatic and amoral, readily harvesting parts from whatever local flora, fauna, or beasts yield the biochemical traits needed for their experiments.",al={"murloc alchemy dangerous","why harvest murlocs"}},{q="What is the Vile Fin Scale for?",a="Apothecary Johaan requires Vile Fin Scales as a vital reagent for formulating the Royal Apothecary Society's new plague. While simple poisons can easily fell an isolated victim, Johaan realizes that engineering a virulent disease capable of spreading across an entire world requires far more complex biological ingredients to ensure proper contagion.",al={"vile fin scale purpose","why vile fin scales","what are the murloc scales for"},it="item",add=true},{q="Who are the Vile Fin murlocs?",a="The Vile Fin are a tribe of primitive murlocs dwelling along the northern coast and western waters of Tirisfal Glades. Like most of their kind, they aggressively defend their seaside settlements and scavenging grounds against any outsiders who wander near their shores.",al={"vile fin murlocs","who are vile fins","tirisfal murlocs"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, helping Apothecary Johaan is a direct contribution to their race's ultimate weapon and defense: the creation of a deadly plague engineered specifically to protect their sovereign realm and destroy their foes.",["faction:Horde"]="While Forsaken apothecaries pursue these dark concoctions openly in Tirisfal Glades, knowledge of the plague's full intent and potency remains a delicate matter kept mostly quiet from their less ruthless allies in the Horde."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill","murloc","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades","vile fin scale"},rel={"quest:367","npc:apothecary-johaan","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:murloc"},m={start="Apothecary Johaan",level="9",faction="Horde",id=368,ri={"Vile Fin Scale"}},src={"A New Plague (2)"}}
E["quest:369"] = {t="quest",n="A New Plague",z="tirisfal",s="In Brill, Apothecary Johaan turns his attention toward developing a potent new biological agent for the Forsaken. By studying ancient texts, he discovers that a historic, deadly plague survived through the venom of eastern Tirisfal's night web spiders, prompting an urgent retrieval effort.",h="In Brill, Apothecary Johaan turns his attention toward developing a potent new biological agent for the...",sec={{t="Unearthing Ancient Contagions",b="While pursuing his alchemy for the Royal Apothecary Society in Brill, Apothecary Johaan scours aged tomes in search of deadlier mixtures. He uncovers records of an ancient pestilence that once ravaged thousands of lives. Crucially, the texts indicate that the deadly pathogen survived across generations by harboring within the local arachnids of Tirisfal Glades, making their venom an invaluable biological reagent.",sp=0},{t="Hunting the Venomweb Vale",b="To acquire a viable sample of the pathogen, Johaan directs agents toward the eastern reaches of Tirisfal Glades. South of the Scarlet Monastery lies Venomweb Vale, a fog-shrouded hollow infested with vicious night web spiders. Harvesters must venture into their dens to extract fresh venom glands, securing the final ingredient needed to finalize Johaan's experimental brew.",sp=0},{t="Brewing the Final Formulation",b="Upon receiving the harvested venom, Johaan incorporates it into a simmering cauldron alongside previously gathered reagents. With the mixture boiled down and primed, the contagious element from the spiders completes the compound, bringing the Royal Apothecary Society one step closer to practical field trials.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want spider venom?",a="Johaan uncovered an old text describing an ancient plague that killed thousands, whose virulent strain was naturally preserved in the venom of Tirisfal's night web spiders. He intends to harness that contagious element for his concoction.",al={"why spider venom","apothecary johaan spiders","spider venom use"},it="item"},{q="Where do the vicious night web spiders live?",a="They dwell in eastern Tirisfal Glades within Venomweb Vale, located just south of the Scarlet Monastery.",al={"where are night web spiders","night web spider location","venomweb vale location"},gp=true},{q="What is the goal of the Royal Apothecary Society's research here?",a="The society seeks to synthesize a lethal new strain capable of exterminating both the Scourge and the living, cementing the Forsaken's survival and independence in Lordaeron.",al={"why make a new plague","forsaken plague goals","royal apothecary society plague"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Apothecary Johaan?",a="Johaan is a Forsaken researcher stationed in Brill under the Royal Apothecary Society. He oversees local chemical and biological experiments, blending dark alchemy with field-harvested Tirisfal reagents.",al={"who is johaan","apothecary johaan lore"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after this venom is gathered?",a="Once Johaan boils the venom down into his brew, he prepares to test the compound directly to determine if the resulting toxic agent produces the desired lethality.",al={"what happens next","new plague quest sequel","after a new plague 3"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Is this the same plague that created the Scourge?",a="No. The Scourge spread the Plague of Undeath created by the Lich King. The Forsaken's apothecaries are deliberately formulating an entirely distinct biological weapon of their own design.",al={"is this scourge plague","plague of undeath difference"}},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan need this venom right now?",a="While consulting his tomes, Apothecary Johaan discovered that an ancient plague which slew thousands of innocents was preserved within the venom of Night Web spiders. He needs this final reagent to test whether its contagious properties can make his concoction truly lethal and infectious.",al={"johaan motive","why does johaan want this done","why gather spider venom"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are Vicious Night Web Spiders and why are they targeted?",a="Vicious Night Web Spiders are deadly arachnids lurking in the eastern reaches of Tirisfal Glades, particularly around Venomweb Vale. Johaan targets them because ancient texts record their venom as naturally carrying and preserving the lethal, virulent properties of a forgotten historical plague.",al={"why vicious night web spiders","what are night web spiders","who are we sent against"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, crafting an original weapon offers the only lasting security against relentless Scourge remnants and Scarlet crusaders on their doorstep.",["faction:Horde"]="While Forsaken researchers welcome assistance from orc or tauren allies, the visceral nature of their biological experiments frequently unsettles the wider Horde."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill","night web spider","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades","venom","venomweb vale"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","subzone:brill","subzone:venomweb-vale","zone:tirisfal","zone:scarlet","quest:367"},m={start="Apothecary Johaan",level="11",faction="Horde",id=369,ri={"Vicious Night Web Spider Venom"}},src={"A New Plague (3)"}}
E["quest:370"] = {t="quest",n="At War With The Scarlet Crusade",z="tirisfal",s="Executor Zygand directs Forsaken champions to strike down Captain Perrine and his garrison stationed at a ruined tower southwest of Brill. Using intelligence recovered in Deathknell, the Forsaken aim to push back the zealous crusaders threatening their borders.",h="Executor Zygand directs Forsaken champions to strike down Captain Perrine and his garrison stationed at a...",sec={{t="Intelligence Put to Use",b="Documents recovered from the Scarlet Crusade in Deathknell by Executor Arren revealed active troop movements across Tirisfal Glades. Rather than waiting for the fanatics to mount an offensive against the newly fortified town of Brill, Executor Zygand uses this captured intelligence to orchestrate preemptive strikes against their field commanders.",sp=0},{t="The Threat at the Ruined Tower",b="Stationed at a dilapidated watchtower southwest of Brill, Captain Perrine oversees a contingent of Scarlet Zealots and Missionaries. From this vantage, the Crusade attempts to monitor Forsaken movements and project power deeper into Tirisfal, making Perrine's death crucial to the defense of the Dark Lady's young kingdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Captain Perrine?",a="Captain Perrine is a Scarlet Crusade officer stationed at a ruined watchtower southwest of Brill, tasked with commanding a detachment of Zealots and Missionaries encroaching upon Tirisfal Glades.",al={"captain perrine","perrine","who is perrine"},it="target"},{q="Where did the Forsaken get information on Captain Perrine?",a="Executor Arren recovered strategic Crusade documents during the initial defense of Deathknell, which detailed troop assignments and outpost locations, including Perrine's post.",al={"strategic documents zygand","arren's documents","scarlet documents deathknell"},it="origin"},{q="Why are Scarlet Missionaries and Zealots in Tirisfal?",a="The Scarlet Crusade considers all undead abominations that must be exterminated, regardless of whether they serve the Scourge or the Forsaken. Their missionaries and zealots push deep into Tirisfal to wage a holy war and reclaim Lordaeron for the living.",al={"why is scarlet crusade in tirisfal","scarlet missionaries tirisfal","scarlet zealots"},it="why_here"},{q="How does this quest fit into the campaign against the Crusade?",a="This is the second step in the Tirisfal campaign against the Crusade. Slaying Perrine dismantles their local scouting post before Executor Zygand escalates operations against larger encampments further across the glades.",al={"at war with the scarlet crusade chain","what comes next after perrine"},it="conflict"},{q="Can this quest be done together with anything else nearby?",a="Yes, it pairs directly with Proof of Demise, in which Deathguard Burgess asks for Scarlet Insignia Rings taken from the same crusaders as proof of loyalty to Varimathras and the Dark Lady.",al={"proof of demise pairing","quest pairing perrine","brill tower quests"},gp=true},{q="Who is Executor Zygand?",a="Executor Zygand is the primary military commander stationed in Brill, responsible for securing Tirisfal Glades and directing strategic offensives on behalf of Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"zygand","executor zygand brill"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want Captain Perrine killed?",a="Executor Zygand intends to purge the lands around Brill of what he views as a human infestation. Having analyzed intercepted strategic Crusade documents, he knows Captain Perrine commands a fortified brigade at the ruined tower southwest of town. Striking Perrine and his retinue breaks the Crusade's local command and directly serves the Dark Lady's conquest of Tirisfal.",al={"why kill perrine","zygand motives","why target the scarlet brigade"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, eliminating the Scarlet Crusade is an existential necessity; the zealots draw no distinction between free-willed undead and the Scourge.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde's foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms relies on the Forsaken securing Tirisfal Glades against hostile human zealots."},kw={"brill","captain perrine","executor arren","executor zygand","proof of demise","ruined tower","scarlet crusade","scarlet missionary","scarlet zealot","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","topic:scarlet-crusade","quest:374","subzone:brill","npc:executor-arren","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="9",faction="Horde",id=370},src={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (2)"}}
E["quest:371"] = {t="quest",n="At War With The Scarlet Crusade",z="tirisfal",s="This military operation tasks Forsaken defenders with striking at the Scarlet Crusade encampment near the Balnir Farmstead in southeastern Tirisfal Glades. Under orders from Executor Zygand, players eliminate Captain Vachon and his accompanying friars to stall Crusader raids threatening the Dark Lady's domain.",h="This military operation tasks Forsaken defenders with striking at the Scarlet Crusade encampment near the...",sec={{t="A Looming Encroachment",b="As the Forsaken strive to secure the perimeter around Brill and the Undercity, the Scarlet Crusade maintains fortified pockets across the countryside. Reports funneling into town from Executor Arren alert the military command to active Crusader incursions operating out of a crumbling guard tower in southeastern Tirisfal Glades, uncomfortably close to the abandoned Balnir Farmstead.",sp=0},{t="Severing the Chain of Command",b="Recognizing that disorganized zealots are far less perilous than coordinated squads, Executor Zygand focuses the offensive on their field leadership. By eliminating Captain Vachon alongside the Scarlet Friars who bolster his men with holy magic, the Forsaken aim to shatter this cell's operational cohesion and suppress human raids into nearby Forsaken territories.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who orders this strike?",a="Executor Zygand, stationed in the town of Brill, issues the orders. He acts on frontline intelligence compiled and delivered by Executor Arren regarding Crusade movements near the Balnir Farmstead.",al={"quest giver","who gives this quest","who is zygand"},it="in_charge"},{q="Where is Captain Vachon located?",a="Captain Vachon and his retinue of Scarlet Friars are based at a ruined watchtower in southeastern Tirisfal Glades, just east of the Balnir Farmstead.",al={"vachon location","where is vachon","where are scarlet friars"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Executor Zygand target Scarlet Friars specifically?",a="Scarlet Friars provide essential magical and spiritual support to the rank-and-file zealots. Cutting down their spellcasters deprives the Crusade squad of defensive wards and healing, leaving their field operations completely crippled.",al={"why kill friars","scarlet friars"}},{q="What threat does the ruined tower pose to Brill?",a="The tower provides the Scarlet Crusade with elevated sightlines and a fortified launching point. From there, Crusader patrols can easily ambuscade Forsaken travelers and disrupt supply roads leading east out of Brill.",al={"tower threat","why attack tower","balnir tower"},it="threat"},{q="How does this fit into the broader conflict with the Crusade?",a="This mission forms the third phase of Executor Zygand's tactical campaign to systematically eradicate Scarlet Crusade strongholds from the Tirisfal Glades, pushing zealot forces back before they can besiege the Undercity.",al={"scarlet war chain","war with the scarlet crusade lore"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens after Captain Vachon is slain?",a="While Vachon's defeat slows Crusader advances in southeastern Tirisfal, Executor Zygand promptly directs attention toward larger Scarlet Crusade fortifications elsewhere in the region.",al={"next quest","what after vachon","vachon aftermath"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want Captain Vachon and the friars dead?",a="Executor Arren sent word that Scarlet Crusaders have entrenched themselves in the ruined tower near the Balnir Farmstead and are actively staging raids across southeastern Tirisfal. The Scarlet Crusade continues to grow stronger on the glades' borders, threatening Brill and the Dark Lady's broader campaign. By cutting down Captain Vachon and his accompanying friars, Zygand intends to blunt their offensive momentum and deliver a crippling blow to their local command structure before they can push any deeper into Forsaken lands.",al={"why kill captain vachon","zygand motives","why target scarlet tower"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are you being sent against?",a="You are sent to eliminate Captain Vachon and five Scarlet Friars occupying the ruined watchtower overlooking southeastern Tirisfal. Vachon commands this forward raiding force for the Scarlet Crusade, relying on zealot friars for holy support and field leadership. Their presence so close to the Balnir Farmstead grants them an fortified staging ground to disrupt Forsaken travel and harry the Dark Lady's subjects throughout the countryside.",al={"who is captain vachon","who are the scarlet friars","who do i kill"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every Crusader post cleared around Tirisfal Glades secures your newborn nation's borders and protects Lady Sylvanas's realm from living purifiers.",["faction:Horde"]="Neutralizing Scarlet zealots keeps the approach to the Undercity open and secures Horde communication routes through northern Lordaeron.",["class:Priest"]="Clashing with the Scarlet Friars pits your mastery of the Shadow and distorted Holy power against the dogmatic, unyielding fanaticism of human zealots."},kw={"arren","balnir","brill","crusader","friar","tirisfal","tower","vachon","zygand"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:balnir-farmstead","subzone:brill","npc:executor-arren","quest:370","quest:1882","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="10",faction="Horde",id=371},src={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (3)"}}
E["quest:372"] = {t="quest",n="At War With The Scarlet Crusade",z="tirisfal",s="This mission represents the culmination of Executor Zygand's early campaign to secure Tirisfal Glades against Scarlet Crusade incursions. Operating out of Brill, Forsaken agents are directed to strike directly at Captain Melrache and his personal vanguard at a ruined watchtower past Faol's Rest.",h="This mission represents the culmination of Executor Zygand's early campaign to secure Tirisfal Glades against...",sec={{t="A Strike at the Vanguard",b="While the Forsaken consolidate their hold over the ruins of Lordaeron, the Scarlet Crusade relentlessly probes the borders of Tirisfal. From an elevated watchtower situated in northern Tirisfal past Faol's Rest, zealous crusaders have organized raiding parties against Forsaken patrols and territory. Rather than waiting to repel another assault, Executor Zygand chooses decapitation: eliminating the command structure that directs the local raids.",sp=0},{t="Captain Melrache's Hold",b="Commanding this northern encampment is Captain Melrache, an uncompromising officer flanked constantly by two sworn bodyguards. Melrache's position gives the Crusade observation over the coastal roads and surrounding woods. Slaying him breaks the immediate coordination of the crusaders near Brill, neutralizing a direct threat to Lady Sylvanas's fledgling kingdom and driving the zealots back toward their stronger fortifications further east.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Captain Melrache?",a="Captain Melrache is a field commander of the Scarlet Crusade overseeing a raiding camp at a ruined tower in northern Tirisfal Glades, beyond Faol's Rest. He leads aggressive incursions into Forsaken lands alongside his personal bodyguards.",al={"melrache","captain melrache lore","who is the scarlet captain"},it="target"},{q="Where is the ruined tower located?",a="The tower stands in the northern wilderness of Tirisfal Glades, perched on the bluffs just past the graveyard of Faol's Rest, overlooking the northern coast.",al={"melrache location","where is the tower","faol's rest tower"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want Melrache dead?",a="Melrache's contingent has been actively raiding out of their tower base, harassing Forsaken troops and threatening the security of Brill and the Undercity. Zygand orders his assassination to break the Crusade's local offensive momentum.",al={"why kill melrache","zygand's motive"},it="motive"},{q="How does this fight connect to the wider war against Arthas?",a="Executor Zygand views the Scarlet Crusade as a dangerous distraction. Removing aggressive Crusade officers allows the Forsaken to solidify control over Tirisfal and preserve strength for their ultimate vengeance against the Lich King.",al={"arthas connection","why mention arthas"},it="conflict"},{q="Does slaying Melrache clear the Scarlet Crusade from Tirisfal?",a="No. Melrache's death eliminates an immediate raiding cell and weakens northern pressure on Brill, but the Crusade maintains massive strongholds nearby, including the Scarlet Monastery to the east.",al={"is scarlet crusade defeated","do they leave tirisfal"},it="next_step"},{q="What is Faol's Rest?",a="Faol's Rest is a secluded, solemn burial site in northern Tirisfal named after Alonsus Faol, the former Archbishop of the Church of the Holy Light. Melrache's tower sits in the rugged hills just beyond it.",al={"faols rest","faol resting place"},it="place"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand send an adventurer rather than his own guards?",a="Brill's standing garrison is already stretched thin guarding the town and maintaining patrols against constant incursions. Striking deep into the northern wilderness past Faol's Rest to breach an enemy-held ruined tower requires surgical assassination rather than a clumsy troop deployment. Zygand relies on capable, independent champions who have already proven their ruthlessness to cut off the enemy command quietly.",al={"why send me","why not use guards","why not royal dreadguard"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, every crusader slain is one less fanatic denying their right to exist in the homeland they inhabited in life.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing the perimeter of Tirisfal protects the Horde's solitary, vulnerable foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Rogue"]="Striking deep behind enemy lines to isolate a commander from his surrounding army is the quintessential work of Deathstalkers."},kw={"brill","captain melrache","executor zygand","faol's rest","scarlet bodyguard","scarlet crusade","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","subzone:brill","subzone:faol-s-rest","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:forsaken","zone:tirisfal","quest:370"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="12",faction="Horde",id=372},src={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (4)"}}
E["quest:374"] = {t="quest",n="Proof of Demise",z="tirisfal",s="Proof of Demise is a military directive issued by Deathguard Burgess in Brill to thin the ranks of the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades. Initiated on the orders of Varimathras, Sylvanas Windrunner's dreadlord lieutenant, it tests a soldier's loyalty to the Forsaken cause. Recruits are ordered to strip signet rings from fallen crusaders as tangible evidence of their slaughter.",h="Proof of Demise is a military directive issued by Deathguard Burgess in Brill to thin the ranks of the...",sec={{t="A Mandate from the Dreadlord",b="The campaign across northern Tirisfal Glades is not merely defensive; it is a coordinated war of eradication. Varimathras, the dreadlord who surrendered and pledged his blade to Sylvanas Windrunner, oversees many of the Forsaken's aggressive military maneuvers against the living. His directives trickle down to commanders like Executor Zygand and frontline officers such as Deathguard Burgess. Burgess makes the directive clear: the human race is viewed as vermin to be purged from Lordaeron, and the zealous crusaders nearby are the immediate target.",sp=0},{t="Tokens of Slaughter",b="Words alone do not satisfy the commanders in Brill. To prove obedience to the Dark Lady and her lieutenant, soldiers must plunder Scarlet Insignia Rings straight from the fingers of slain zealots. These rings bear the iconography of the Scarlet Crusade, marking their bearers as sworn enemies of the Forsaken. Returning with a handful of these trophies serves as unassailable proof of dead crusaders and a demonstration of uncompromising allegiance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who assigns the task to collect these rings?",a="Deathguard Burgess, a Forsaken guard stationed in the town of Brill, gives the order. He acts on reports from Executor Zygand and follows the overarching military goals set by Varimathras.",al={"who gives proof of demise","quest giver","deathguard burgess quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Burgess demand Scarlet Insignia Rings specifically?",a="Scarlet Insignia Rings serve as indisputable physical proof that a crusader has been slain. They are personal tokens carried by the zealots, making them convenient, undeniable trophies of war that confirm a soldier's loyalty to the Forsaken leadership.",al={"why rings","scarlet rings purpose","what are insignia rings"},it="item"},{q="Who is Varimathras and why is he mentioned?",a="Varimathras is a dreadlord who allied with Queen Sylvanas Windrunner after the Forsaken broke free from the Lich King. Currently serving as her chief lieutenant in the Undercity, he directs military strategy against remaining human enclaves in Lordaeron, including the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"who is varimathras","dreadlord in undercity","why varimathras"},it="leader"},{q="Where are the Scarlet Crusaders located for this task?",a="Scarlet zealots maintain several armed encampments across Tirisfal Glades, particularly around their watchtowers, garrisons, and outposts in the northern and eastern reaches of the woods.",al={"where to find scarlet crusade","scarlet crusader locations","crusader camps tirisfal"},it="place",gp=true},{q="How does this connect to Executor Zygand's orders?",a="Executor Zygand oversees the broader campaign against the Scarlet Crusade from Brill. While Zygand directs recruits to break the crusaders' field presence, Burgess follows up by requiring personal trophies as proof of obedience directly to Varimathras and the Dark Lady.",al={"executor zygand connection","at war with the scarlet crusade link"},it="ties"},{q="Why is the Forsaken leadership so hostile to the Scarlet Crusade?",a="The Scarlet Crusade considers all undead abominations destined for holy fire, making no distinction between the mindless Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken. Because they aggressively hunt down the Forsaken on their own borders, Sylvanas and her officers view them as a dangerous existential threat that must be annihilated.",al={"why fight scarlet crusade","forsaken vs scarlet crusade","why do forsaken hate humans"},it="conflict"},{q="Why does Deathguard Burgess want the Scarlet Crusade killed?",a="Deathguard Burgess serves Varimathras and the Dark Lady directly on the front lines of Brill. In Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Crusade poses an immediate, fanatical threat right on the Undercity's doorstep. Burgess wants their presence eradicated to secure the territory and demands tangible tokens of their defeat to measure your loyalty to the Forsaken cause.",al={"burgess motive","why kill scarlet crusade burgess","why proof of demise"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are the Scarlet Crusaders targeted in this mission?",a="You are sent against the zealous members of the Scarlet Crusade occupying encampments throughout Tirisfal Glades. These fanatical humans view all undead as abominations requiring total eradication, actively waging a campaign to reclaim Lordaeron and wipe out the Forsaken.",al={"who are scarlet crusade target","proof of demise target","who do i kill proof of demise"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, every crusader ring brought to Brill represents one fewer fanatic sworn to cleanse Lordaeron of free-willed undead.",["faction:Horde"]="This task demonstrates the ruthless, total-war mindset of Sylvanas's regime in the north, orchestrated in part by the dreadlord Varimathras."},kw={"brill","dark lady","deathguard burgess","executor zygand","proof of demise","scarlet crusade","scarlet insignia ring","tirisfal glades","varimathras"},rel={"npc:deathguard-burgess","quest:370","npc:executor-zygand","npc:varimathras","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Burgess",level="7",faction="Horde",id=374,ri={"Scarlet Insignia Ring"}},src={"Proof of Demise (quest)"}}
E["quest:375"] = {t="quest",n="The Chill of Death",z="tirisfal",s="In the town of Brill, a Forsaken tailor named Gretchen Dedmar struggles against the creeping cold of mortality and decay. Fearing that the encroaching mindless state will rob her of the ability to serve Lady Sylvanas, she asks for simple materials to sew herself a warm blanket.",h="In the town of Brill, a Forsaken tailor named Gretchen Dedmar struggles against the creeping cold of...",sec={{t="A Creeping Frost",b="For the Forsaken, undeath is not a static condition of eternal preservation. As Gretchen Dedmar describes, the lingering Plague of Undeath runs through her body like an icy serpent, draining the warmth from her limbs and threatening to pull her into the mindless state that afflicts so many wandering corpses in Tirisfal Glades. Her shivering hands make ordinary work nearly impossible.",sp=0},{t="Service to the Dark Lady",b="Despite her failing strength, Gretchen refuses to abandon her duty to the Forsaken kingdom. When Sylvanas Windrunner first rallied the liberated dead, Gretchen contributed by sewing burial shrouds and body bags for fallen soldiers. By gathering sturdy duskbat pelts and coarse thread, travelers help keep her hands steady enough to craft a blanket and prolong her useful service to the Forsaken cause.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gretchen Dedmar?",a="Gretchen Dedmar is a Forsaken woman residing in Brill. She previously contributed to the nascent Forsaken war effort by stitching body bags for Lady Sylvanas's fallen troops.",al={"gretchen dedmar","who is gretchen","brill tailor questgiver"}},{q="What is the 'Mindless State' Gretchen fears?",a="The mindless state refers to the mental and physical decay that overtakes some undead when the remaining will and necrotic energies fail, leaving them as feral, shambling creatures like those wandering outside Brill and Deathknell.",al={"what is mindless state","why is gretchen losing her mind","forsaken becoming mindless"}},{q="Why does Gretchen feel cold if she is dead?",a="While undead bodies lack living circulation, Gretchen perceives the creeping plague within her veins as an overwhelming, freezing chill that stiffens her joints and leaves her hands shaking.",al={"can undead feel cold","why are undead cold","chill of death meaning"}},{q="Why does she need duskbat pelts and thread?",a="Gretchen needs five duskbat pelts and coarse thread to sew herself a thick blanket. She hopes the added warmth will keep her shivering hands functional so she can continue working.",al={"why duskbat pelts","what is the blanket for"}},{q="Where can duskbats be found?",a="Duskbats roost throughout the woods and open glades surrounding Brill and northern Tirisfal.",al={"where are duskbats","duskbat pelt location"}},{q="Does this quest connect to any other duties in Brill?",a="It is a standalone task of aid for a struggling resident of Brill, demonstrating the quiet, personal struggles among the newly liberated Forsaken.",al={"chill of death follow up","gretchen dedmar chain"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Gretchen's struggle is a grim reminder for all Forsaken that liberation from the Lich King does not protect against physical rot and the ever-present threat of mindlessness.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Gretchen relies on humble needlecraft, having stitched body bags for Sylvanas's army before turning her needle toward a simple pelt blanket."},kw={"brill","chill of death","coarse thread","duskbat pelt","gretchen dedmar","mindless state","plague of undeath","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:plague-of-undeath"},m={level="8",id=375},src={"In-game quest text: The Chill of Death","The Chill of Death (Classic)"},fv=true}
E["quest:376"] = {t="quest",n="The Damned",z="tirisfal",s="The Damned is an early task given to newly awakened Forsaken by Novice Elreth in the chapel of Deathknell. In exchange for the materials needed to fashion crude clothing and battlefield wraps, Elreth provides salvageable armor to protect fledgling undead from the perils surrounding the cemetery.",h="The Damned is an early task given to newly awakened Forsaken by Novice Elreth in the chapel of Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Pragmatic Trade",b="Awakening into undeath leaves little room for vanity or sentimentality. Novice Elreth attends to the steady stream of fresh Forsaken pulled from the dirt of Deathknell, serving as an assistant to Shadow Priest Sarvis. Between tending to battered soldiers and mending garments, supplies run perilously thin. To ensure new arrivals do not fall apart during their initial trials, Elreth sends them into the surrounding glades to hunt native duskbats and scavenger wolves, trading salvaged armor for the raw pelts, wings, and sinew required to keep the camp functioning.",sp=0},{t="Inherited Protection",b="The armor offered by Elreth is far from pristine forge-work; it is hand-me-down gear pulled from those who failed to endure their awakening. Her grim parting words—hoping the garments serve the recipient better than the previous owner—reflect the cold calculus of Forsaken survival. In Tirisfal Glades, nothing is wasted, and every piece of leather and rusted chain has already outlived at least one corpse.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Novice Elreth?",a="Novice Elreth is an acolyte stationed in Deathknell's chapel. She serves as an aide to Shadow Priest Sarvis, responsible for tending wounded Forsaken, tailoring field gear, and equipping new recruits before they are sent into danger.",al={"novice elreth","who gives the damned quest","elreth"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Elreth need scavenger paws and duskbat wings?",a="Deathknell lacks fresh shipments of raw materials. Elreth relies on native Tirisfal wildlife like duskbats and scavenger wolves to gather leather, sinew, and tailoring scraps to repair uniforms and bandage wounded soldiers.",al={"why bat wings","why scavenger paws","elreth materials"},it="motive"},{q="Where did the armor reward come from?",a="The armor is stripped from fallen Forsaken recruits who did not survive previous skirmishes around Deathknell. Elreth recycles whatever functional gear remains to protect the next wave of newly risen dead.",al={"where does armor come from","elreth armor origin"},it="item"},{q="Where are the creatures found?",a="Young Scavengers and Duskbats roost immediately south of Deathknell. Slightly tougher variants roam eastward toward the Scarlet Crusade outpost.",al={"where to find scavengers","where are duskbats","duskbat locations"},gp=true},{q="How does this task fit into the defence of Deathknell?",a="It serves as basic field readiness. Shadow Priest Sarvis expects all newly risen Forsaken to combat nearby threats like mindless undead and the Scarlet Crusade, and Elreth ensures they are clad in sturdy armor before marching out.",al={"why do this quest","deathknell defense"},it="purpose"},{q="What happens after helping Novice Elreth?",a="Proving capable of surviving the local wildlife opens up further tasks around Deathknell, including honoring Marla's last wish, addressing the spider infestation at Night Web's Hollow, and recovering supplies across the settlement.",al={"next quests after the damned","what comes after the damned"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Novice Elreth need Scavenger Paws and Duskbat Wings?",a="Novice Elreth is tasked with tailoring armor and clothes for Deathknell's defenders, alongside attending to the wounded and aiding Shadow Priest Sarvis. Natural materials like the leather and hides harvested from scavenger paws and duskbat wings serve as essential raw crafting stock. Without fresh supplies, Elreth cannot properly reinforce the ragged garments worn by newly risen Forsaken heading into battle.",al={"what are the scavenger paws for","duskbat wings use","why paws and wings"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why does Novice Elreth ask you instead of the Deathknell guards?",a="Novice Elreth already carries an overwhelming burden caring for battered soldiers, tailoring supplies, and assisting Shadow Priest Sarvis. Deathknell's established guards cannot abandon their posts while the Scourge and Scarlet Crusade press in. As a newly risen Forsaken facing immediate deployment against the Mindless Ones, gathering your own protective materials serves both the settlement's needs and your own survival.",al={"why me elreth","why doesnt elreth get the paws himself","why not send guards"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For fresh Forsaken, this task is an early demonstration of their people's grim practicality, showing that survival means stripping gear from fallen predecessors and living off whatever the blighted land provides.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Novice Elreth highlights the brutal necessity of battlefield tailoring in Lordaeron, where wing membranes and scavenged pelts substitute for clean cloth."},kw={"deathknell","duskbat wing","forsaken","novice elreth","scavenger paw","shadow priest sarvis","the damned","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:novice-elreth","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","subzone:deathknell","quest:363","quest:364","quest:380","quest:3902","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Novice Elreth",level="2",faction="Horde",id=376,ri={"Duskbat Wing","Scavenger Paw"}},src={"The Damned (Classic)"}}
E["quest:380"] = {t="quest",n="Night Web's Hollow",z="tirisfal",s="Night Web's Hollow is an early assignment from Executor Arren to clear out the spiders infesting a nearby gold mine northwest of Deathknell. Facing critical shortages of raw materials, the fledgling Forsaken need the mine's wealth to sustain their settlement and war effort. Newly risen recruits are sent into the tangled cavern to cull the arachnid infestation piece by piece.",h="Night Web's Hollow is an early assignment from Executor Arren to clear out the spiders infesting a nearby...",sec={{t="A Nation in Need of Coin",b="Survival for the newly risen Forsaken involves more than just defending against the Scourge and Scarlet crusaders. Building a fledgling society requires raw resources, and precious metals like gold were scarce in northern Lordaeron even at the height of the Alliance's strength. Executor Arren, overseeing operations in Deathknell, recognizes that the Forsaken cannot hope to arm, equip, and establish themselves without secured wealth.",sp=0},{t="The Infested Mine",b="Directly northwest of the cemetery grounds lies Night Web's Hollow, a natural cavern containing a viable gold vein. Unfortunately, monstrous arachnids have overrun the tunnels, filling the depths with thick webbing and deadly venom. Lacking the seasoned troops to conduct a full-scale purge, Arren relies on fresh recruits to whittle down the young and adult spiders until mining crews can eventually burn away the webs and extract the ore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do the undead need a gold mine?",a="Even in undeath, the Forsaken must fund their war effort, pay for supplies, and rebuild settlements across Tirisfal Glades. Lordaeron always struggled with scarce natural resources, and establishing a new realm independent of both the Alliance and the Scourge requires valuable currency to trade and procure equipment.",al={"why do forsaken need gold","why gold in deathknell","undead gold mine"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Executor Arren?",a="Executor Arren is the ranking military authority in Deathknell. Tasked with organizing newly risen undead and securing the immediate perimeter, he directs recruits toward essential tasks like hunting Scourge remnants, gathering supplies, and opening up local resources.",al={"executor arren lore","who gives night webs hollow","arren role"},it="identity"},{q="Where is Night Web's Hollow located?",a="The hollow is situated just northwest of the main settlement and graveyard of Deathknell, nestled in the rocky bluffs that frame the secluded valley in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is night webs hollow","spider cave deathknell","night web mine location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Are the Forsaken able to mine the gold immediately after this quest?",a="Not right away. Executor Arren notes that completely eradicating the infestation will take weeks or months of sustained effort, followed by crews using torches to burn out the pervasive webbing before miners can safely excavate the veins.",al={"is the mine cleared","do they get the gold","what happens to the spiders"},it="next_step"},{q="Why doesn't Arren send a full detachment of soldiers to clear the cave?",a="Deathknell's garrison is stretched thin guarding against mindless Scourge prowling the outskirts and patrolling against Scarlet Crusade scouts. Arren simply lacks the spare manpower to commit a regular squad to pest control, leaving it to newly risen recruits to clear the mine bit by bit.",al={"why send recruits","why not use deathknell guards","arren lack of manpower"},it="why_me"},{q="Can spider venom harm the undead?",a="While the Forsaken do not bleed or breathe like the living, caustic spider venom can still burn through preserved flesh and weaken reanimated limbs. Executor Arren specifically warns recruits to watch for sharp burning from venom and seek treatment if afflicted.",al={"does venom hurt undead","forsaken spider poison","are forsaken immune to poison"},it="danger"},{q="Why does Executor Arren want Night Web's Hollow cleared?",a="Deathknell and the nascent Forsaken war effort desperately need natural resources to survive and fortify their lands. Gold was scarce in Tirisfal Glades even at the height of the old Alliance's reign, making the local mine in the hollow an indispensable asset. However, the spiders nesting within make excavation impossible. Arren wants the infestation culled so the Forsaken can eventually secure and harvest the precious metal.",al={"why does arren want this done","why clear night webs hollow","executor arren motive"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are the Night Web spiders infesting the mine?",a="The target of your mission is the brood of Night Web spiders and their young that have overrun the old gold mine. In the wake of Lordaeron's fall and the plague's blight, predatory wildlife expanded aggressively into abandoned mortal settlements and subterranean caves. The spiders have aggressively nested in the mine tunnels, stringing webs and driving out any miners seeking to tap into the vein.",al={"what are night web spiders","who are we sent against","why are spiders in night webs hollow"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task highlights the harsh reality facing Lordaeron's reclaimed dead: survival requires securing mundane materials like metal and coin, not just seeking vengeance against the living and the Scourge.",["profession:Mining"]="The cavern represents one of Deathknell's few nearby sources of gold ore, though the thick webs and predatory spiders make prospecting perilous.",["faction:Horde"]="Establishing secured resources in Tirisfal Glades is critical for cementing the Forsaken's position as a reliable northern bastion for the Horde."},kw={"deathknell","executor arren","forsaken","gold mine","night web spider","night web's hollow","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","npc:executor-arren","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:alliance"},m={start="Executor Arren",level="4",faction="Horde",id=380},src={"Night Web's Hollow (Classic)"}}
E["quest:381"] = {t="quest",n="The Scarlet Crusade",z="tirisfal",s="This early mission from Executor Arren tasks newly awakened Forsaken with ambushing a nearby camp of Scarlet Crusade zealots southeast of Deathknell. It serves as a grim introduction to one of the Forsaken's most fanatical and persistent enemies.",h="This early mission from Executor Arren tasks newly awakened Forsaken with ambushing a nearby camp of Scarlet...",sec={{t="A Fanatical Threat",b="Once the immediate peril of the Night Web spider infestation is handled, Executor Arren turns Deathknell's attention toward an encroaching threat. A detachment of the Scarlet Crusade has established a foothold just southeast of the settlement. These zealots refuse to acknowledge any distinction between mindless Scourge minions and free-willed Forsaken, meaning their presence on the border poses an existential threat to the newly risen dead.",sp=0},{t="Preemptive Retaliation",b="Understanding that dialogue is impossible with holy fanatics who view all dead flesh as an abomination, Arren orders a preemptive strike. Adventurers are sent to cut down Scarlet Initiates and Converts before the encampment can grow into an organized forward assault on Deathknell. The trophies brought back—their crimson armbands—stand as stark proof that survival in Tirisfal must be claimed by force.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Scarlet Crusade?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is an extremist religious movement dedicated to the utter extermination of the undead. Driven mad by grief and religious zeal after the fall of Lordaeron, they refuse to distinguish between mindless Scourge monsters and self-aware Forsaken, seeking to purge both indiscriminately.",al={"scarlet crusade lore","what is the scarlet crusade","scarlet zealots"},it="people"},{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade camping near Deathknell?",a="Deathknell is where new Forsaken are being raised and gathered. The Scarlet Crusade has established a small forward outpost to survey the area, catch newly risen undead unawares, and muster forces to destroy the fledgling Forsaken settlement.",al={"why are scarlets in deathknell","scarlet camp deathknell"},it="why_here"},{q="Why do the Forsaken strike first rather than talk?",a="As Executor Arren points out, the Crusaders are blinded by zeal and consider all undead to be abominations that must be wiped out. They do not listen to reason or negotiate with the dead, leaving preemptive slaughter as the only viable defense.",al={"can forsaken negotiate with scarlet crusade","why attack scarlets"},it="conflict"},{q="What rank are the Crusaders found in this camp?",a="The camp is staffed largely by recruits and trainees, specifically Scarlet Converts and Scarlet Initiates. Despite their low standing in the Crusade's hierarchy, their fervor makes them dangerous to newly risen Forsaken.",al={"who is in the camp","scarlet converts initiates"},it="target"},{q="What does completing this mission lead to?",a="Reports from Executor Arren's superiors in Brill reveal that a Crusade messenger has been sent from the Scarlet Monastery to the camp. This prompts Executor Arren to assign you to intercept The Red Messenger to uncover their battle plans.",al={"what quest comes after","quest chain after scarlet crusade"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Who is Executor Arren?",a="Executor Arren is the primary military commander overseeing the security and defense of Deathknell, directing freshly awakened Forsaken against surrounding threats.",al={"arren role","who gives the quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Executor Arren want the Scarlet Crusade camp eliminated?",a="Deathknell is the fragile cradle of the newly awakened Forsaken, and having a hostile human outpost right on its doorstep poses an immediate survival threat. The Scarlet Crusade refuses to distinguish between the mind-controlled Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken, sworn to obliterate any undead they encounter. With the mine recently secured, Executor Arren insists on striking preemptively before this detachment can gather strength or launch an assault on the settlement.",al={"arren motive","why kill scarlet crusade","why attack scarlet camp"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Scarlet Armband for?",a="The Scarlet Armbands serve as undeniable physical proof that you routed the camp and decimated their ranks. Each armlet is stripped from the fallen zealots—Converts and Initiates who wear their vibrant red colors with blinding fanaticism. Bringing twelve of them back confirms to Executor Arren that the immediate encroachment near Deathknell has been crushed.",al={"scarlet armband use","why collect armbands","scarlet armband"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly arisen Forsaken, this mission is an early lesson in how the living view them: as monsters deserving only extermination, regardless of free will.",["faction:Horde"]="The relentless aggression of the Scarlet Crusade underscores why the Forsaken sought the mutual defense and protection offered by the Horde.",["class:Priest"]="Confronting the Scarlet Crusade forces Forsaken priests to face how the Holy Light they once venerated in Lordaeron has been twisted into fanatical hatred."},kw={"deathknell","executor arren","scarlet armbands","scarlet converts","scarlet crusade","scarlet initiates","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:executor-arren","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:deathknell","quest:382","quest:380","quest:383","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={start="Executor Arren",level="4",faction="Horde",id=381,ri={"Scarlet Armband"}},src={"The Scarlet Crusade (quest)"}}
E["quest:382"] = {t="quest",n="The Red Messenger",z="tirisfal",s="Executor Arren tasks newly risen Forsaken with intercepting a courier dispatched from the Scarlet Monastery to the crusade camp outside Deathknell. Intercepting the messenger secures critical intelligence on local Scarlet movements before the zealots can solidify their presence in the valley. This mission marks one of the earliest offensive operations undertaken to secure the Forsaken's fledgling sanctuary.",h="Executor Arren tasks newly risen Forsaken with intercepting a courier dispatched from the Scarlet Monastery...",sec={{t="Eyes in the East",b="Word travels quickly through Tirisfal Glades, even among the dead. Superiors in Brill alerted Executor Arren that the Scarlet Crusade sent an emissary from their stronghold at the Scarlet Monastery westward into Deathknell. While the Crusaders maintain an aggressive presence across Lordaeron, dispatching a courier directly to the edge of the Forsaken's waking grounds indicates an organized push to eradicate newly risen undead before they can muster an organized defense.",sp=0},{t="Intercepting the Courier",b="The courier, Meven Korgal, blended into the small Scarlet encampment east of Deathknell without bearing distinctive heraldry. Executor Arren directed a targeted strike to assassinate Korgal and confiscate his dispatch. Stripping the courier of his missives denies the local zealots further tactical coordination with their eastern headquarters.",sp=0},{t="Vital Dispatches",b="Rifling through the recovered Scarlet Crusade Documents reveals a mixture of propaganda, scouting notes, and direct orders telling the zealots to expand their camp near the ruined capital. Crucially, the documents contain a strategic map outlining the positions of field commanders and agents throughout the glades—a discovery valuable enough to command immediate delivery to higher authorities in Brill.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is the Red Messenger?",a="The messenger is Meven Korgal, a human courier in service to the Scarlet Crusade. He was sent directly from the Scarlet Monastery to deliver strategic orders to the zealots entrenched near Deathknell.",al={"meven korgal","who is meven korgal","who is the messenger","scarlet messenger identity"},it="target"},{q="Where did the messenger come from?",a="He was dispatched from the Scarlet Monastery, the massive fortified headquarters of the Scarlet Crusade located in northeastern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where did korgal come from","messenger origin","scarlet courier source"},it="origin"},{q="What information was found in the documents?",a="The dispatches contain scouting summaries, orders instructing Crusaders to build up fortifications near the ruined city of Lordaeron, and a strategic map detailing the locations of Crusade field commanders and agents.",al={"scarlet documents contents","what are the orders","what was korgal carrying"},sp=1,it="item"},{q="Where does this intelligence go next?",a="Executor Arren immediately forwards the map and gathered intel to his superiors in Brill via the follow-up mission Vital Intelligence, providing the broader Forsaken military with actionable targets across Tirisfal.",al={"what happens to the documents","where to deliver scarlet papers","vital intelligence quest"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade encroaching so close to Deathknell?",a="The Scarlet Crusade considers all undead abominations to be eradicated indiscriminately. Deathknell is where the newly free undead first claw their way from graves, making it an ideal target for zealots attempting to snuff out the Forsaken before they organize.",al={"why are scarlets in deathknell","crusade in deathknell","why are zealots here"},it="conflict"},{q="Who gave Executor Arren the tip about the messenger?",a="Forsaken command stationed in Brill intercepted news of the courier's departure from the monastery and relayed word down to Arren so his local forces could intercept him.",al={"who warned arren","who sent report to arren","how did forsaken know"},it="allies"},{q="Why does Executor Arren want this done?",a="Deathknell is the Forsaken's fledgling sanctuary, and the Scarlet Crusade has brazenly pitched camp right at its threshold. Word from Arren's superiors in Brill warned that an enemy courier slipped in directly from the Scarlet Monastery with critical correspondence. Intercepting those dispatches allows the Forsaken to anticipate Crusade movements, blunting the zealots' assault before their foothold can choke Deathknell completely.",al={"arren motive","why kill the messenger","why does arren want scarlet documents"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Executor Arren send you instead of his guards?",a="Deathknell's garrison is stretched thin simply holding the line against Scourge remnants, prowling beasts, and aggressive Scarlet patrols surrounding the haven. Having already proved your mettle by thinning the crusaders at their camp, Executor Arren considers you the most capable operative at hand to slip past their perimeter and strike down the courier.",al={"why send me red messenger","why not use deathknell guards","why me arren"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Intercepting this messenger strikes a crucial blow against the zealots who seek to execute you a second time before you have even found your footing in Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="By dismantling Scarlet operations at Deathknell's borders, new recruits protect the northern perimeter of the Horde's newly established foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Rogue"]="Infiltrating the Crusade's camp to assassinate Meven Korgal and steal his dispatches is quintessential covert work for newly awakened rogues."},kw={"brill","deathknell","executor arren","meven korgal","red messenger","scarlet crusade","scarlet documents","scarlet monastery","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:381","quest:383","quest:380","npc:executor-arren","subzone:deathknell","subzone:brill","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Executor Arren",level="5",faction="Horde",id=382,ri={"Scarlet Crusade Documents"}},src={"The Red Messenger"}}
E["quest:383"] = {t="quest",n="Vital Intelligence",z="tirisfal",s="Vital Intelligence marks the end of a freshly risen Forsaken's training in Deathknell. Entrusted with intercepted Scarlet Crusade documents, the courier is sent out into Tirisfal Glades to report to Executor Zygand in Brill.",h="Vital Intelligence marks the end of a freshly risen Forsaken's training in Deathknell.",sec={{t="Leaving Deathknell",b="Having neutralized local threats and intercepted a courier of the Scarlet Crusade, newly recruited Forsaken are deemed ready for broader service. Executor Arren entrusts the recovered intelligence to the player, directing them out through the sheltered canyon of Deathknell and east along the road toward Brill, past Cold Hearth Manor.",sp=0},{t="A Decisive Strike",b="Upon arrival in Brill, Executor Zygand evaluates the captured correspondence. The documents contain rosters and details identifying active Scarlet Crusade commanders operating throughout Tirisfal Glades, laying the groundwork for targeted assassinations intended to fracture the zealots' leadership and morale.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What information is inside the Scarlet Crusade Documents?",a="The captured documents contain names and assignments of Scarlet Crusade officers stationed across Tirisfal Glades. Executor Zygand intends to use these rosters to target and eliminate their local leadership in swift, demoralizing strikes.",al={"what are the scarlet documents","scarlet crusade documents contents","what intelligence was gathered"},it="item"},{q="Where is Executor Zygand located?",a="Executor Zygand is stationed in Brill, positioned just outside the Brill Town Hall along the town's main square.",al={"where is zygand","find executor zygand","executor zygand location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="How do I get to Brill from Deathknell?",a="Follow the road north out of Deathknell. At the fork leading into greater Tirisfal Glades, take the eastern path and continue past Cold Hearth Manor and across the covered bridge straight into Brill.",al={"path to brill","how to get to brill","directions to brill"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does this quest signal the end of Deathknell?",a="Deathknell serves as the initial proving ground for newly awakened Forsaken. Once you have helped secure the hamlet and gathered actionable intelligence on the Scarlet Crusade, Executor Arren transfers your services to higher-ranking commanders in Brill.",al={"done with deathknell","why leave deathknell","is this the last deathknell quest"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Executor Arren?",a="Executor Arren is the Forsaken officer overseeing operations, security, and the initial integration of newly risen undead within Deathknell.",al={"who is arren","arren lore"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after turning in these documents?",a="Delivering the documents integrates you into the broader Forsaken military efforts operating out of Brill, opening assignments across Tirisfal Glades aimed at dismantling local Scarlet Crusade encampments and Scourge remnants.",al={"what comes next","next quest after vital intelligence"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Executor Arren want the documents delivered to Brill?",a="Executor Arren knows that the recovered documents contain crucial intelligence on Scarlet Crusade leadership in Tirisfal Glades. Deathknell is merely a staging ground for the newly risen, so tactical decisions on a regional scale fall to his superior, Executor Zygand in Brill. Arren wants the papers in Zygand's hands immediately so the Forsaken military command can coordinate a decisive, morale-shattering strike against the zealots.",al={"why deliver documents to zygand","executor arren motive","why send documents to brill"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Executor Arren send you instead of his own deathguards?",a="Deathknell is constantly besieged by Mindless Ones, Scourge remnants, and Scarlet zealots, leaving Executor Arren unable to spare his deathguards from their defense posts. You, on the other hand, have already proven your competence by intercepting the Scarlet messenger and securing the intelligence. Arren recognizes that you have outgrown the sheltered valley of Deathknell and sees this mission as both an essential courier run and an introduction to the wider Forsaken war effort under Executor Zygand.",al={"why send me to zygand","why not use guards to deliver documents","why does arren trust me"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Carrying these intercepted plans represents a rite of passage: having proven viable after rising from the grave, you are dispatched from your cradle in Deathknell into the wider war for Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="The intelligence gathered here helps the Forsaken consolidate their hold on Tirisfal Glades, protecting the northern gateway to the Undercity from crusade incursions."},kw={"brill","cold hearth manor","deathknell","documents","executor arren","executor zygand","scarlet crusade","tirisfal glades","vital intelligence"},rel={"quest:382","npc:executor-arren","npc:executor-zygand","subzone:brill","subzone:deathknell","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:cold-hearth-manor","quest:5481"},m={start="Executor Arren",["end"]="Executor Zygand",level="5",faction="Horde",id=383},src={"Vital Intelligence (Classic)"}}
E["quest:3901"] = {t="quest",n="Rattling the Rattlecages",z="tirisfal",s="Rattling the Rattlecages is an early trial assigned by Shadow Priest Sarvis to newly awakened Forsaken in Deathknell. Tasked with destroying twelve Rattlecage Skeletons, fresh undead prove their resilience against the Scourge and demonstrate that they possess the willpower to defend their newly won freedom.",h="Rattling the Rattlecages is an early trial assigned by Shadow Priest Sarvis to newly awakened Forsaken in...",sec={{t="A Test of Resolve",b="Having culled the mindless zombies stumbling through Deathknell, newly risen Forsaken are immediately pushed into a harsher trial. Shadow Priest Sarvis directs recruits to confront the Rattlecage Skeletons roaming the outskirts of the settlement. These animated bones lack any trace of sapience or self-control, yet they hit harder and fight with greater ferocity than the decaying corpses nearby. Surviving their clattering ambushes demonstrates that a new recruit will not falter when placed under immediate pressure.",sp=0},{t="Defending Newly Won Freedom",b="To Sarvis and the leadership in Deathknell, culling the skeletons serves a dual purpose. It protects the fragile refuge from being overrun by the lingering grip of the Lich King while weeding out those recruits who cannot cut it. Those who fail or hesitate risk meeting the same fate as the mindless wretches wandering the woods. Overcoming the Rattlecages proves to Sarvis that the recruit possesses the mettle required to navigate a world that despises their very existence.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Rattlecage Skeletons?",a="Rattlecage Skeletons are mindless undead still held in thrall to the Lich King. Stripped of flesh and cognitive will, they wander the edges of Deathknell, attacking anyone who crosses their path.",al={"rattlecages","what are rattlecage skeletons","who are the skeletons"},it="target"},{q="Why does Shadow Priest Sarvis want the skeletons destroyed?",a="Sarvis wants to test the combat ability and willpower of newly awakened Forsaken under pressure. Thinning out the aggressive skeletons also protects Deathknell from being overwhelmed by the Scourge.",al={"why kill skeletons","sarvis purpose","reason for rattling rattlecages"},it="motive"},{q="Where are the Rattlecage Skeletons found?",a="They wander the northern and northeastern borders of the Deathknell valley, just beyond the village where the mindless zombies roam.",al={"rattlecage location","where to find rattlecage skeletons","skeleton spawns deathknell"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after completing this quest?",a="Sarvis considers your initial training complete, declaring that you have proven worthy of your freedom. He then directs you toward other leaders in Deathknell to aid the Forsaken's broader operations.",al={"next quest after rattling rattlecages","what to do after","sarvis next step"},it="next_step"},{q="Why are some undead free while these skeletons remain mindless?",a="When the Lich King's telepathic hold wavered after the Third War, a fraction of undead broke free and reclaimed their sentience, forming the Forsaken. Constructs and simple animated bones like the Rattlecages retained no personality or will, remaining purely bound to the Scourge.",al={"why are skeletons mindless","forsaken vs scourge free will","how did forsaken wake up"},it="origin"},{q="Who is Shadow Priest Sarvis?",a="Shadow Priest Sarvis is the Forsaken overseer in Deathknell's chapel. He evaluates newly raised undead, guiding them through their awakening and ensuring they are strong enough to survive as free beings.",al={"who is sarvis","sarvis lore","shadow priest sarvis role"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Shadow Priest Sarvis send you instead of the Deathknell guards?",a="Sarvis is testing your mettle and fitness to serve Lady Sylvanas. Deathknell's established protectors are already occupied guarding the settlement's perimeter against the relentless Scourge. As a newly awakened Forsaken, you must prove you possess the focus and martial resolve to handle dangerous foes before you are granted leave to venture into the wider kingdom.",al={"why me sarvis","why not use the guards","why does sarvis test you"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Confronting the Rattlecages is a harsh reminder of what awaits any Forsaken whose mind slips, contrasting your hard-won autonomy with the mindless servitude of the Scourge.",["faction:Horde"]="The trials in Deathknell temper new Forsaken soldiers into capable fighters ready to defend their fledgling faction and secure Tirisfal Glades.",["class:Priest"]="Working directly for Shadow Priest Sarvis offers newly risen Priests an early glimpse into the ruthless pragmatism defining the Forsaken's spiritual path."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken","rattlecage","sarvis","scourge","skeletons","tirisfal","undead"},rel={"npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","quest:364","subzone:deathknell","topic:forsaken","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Shadow Priest Sarvis",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3901},src={"Rattling the Rattlecages"}}
E["quest:3902"] = {t="quest",n="Scavenging Deathknell",z="tirisfal",s="Scavenging Deathknell is an early logistical task assigned to newly awakened Forsaken by Deathguard Saltain in Deathknell. As freshly raised undead claw their way from the soil, the Forsaken must scavenge abandoned boxes and crates throughout the blighted settlement to equip incoming recruits. It illustrates the raw scarcity facing Sylvanas's nascent people, who must reclaim discarded goods simply to survive.",h="Scavenging Deathknell is an early logistical task assigned to newly awakened Forsaken by Deathguard Saltain...",sec={{t="A Pragmatic Resurrection",b="Newly raised Forsaken awaken into a grim reality of decay and deprivation. Unlike established nations with bustling supply lines and armories, the free undead in Deathknell possess next to nothing. Deathguard Saltain's blunt demand reflects the ruthless practicality required to build an army from the graveyard dirt: every piece of salvageable cloth, leather, and iron must be hauled back to clothe and arm those still crawling out of their plots.",sp=0},{t="Scraping by in Tirisfal",b="The settlement of Deathknell is littered with remnants of its former human inhabitants alongside supplies abandoned during the kingdom's fall to the Scourge. Rather than letting usable materials rot away in crates across town, the Deathguards press newborn undead into service as salvagers. As Saltain makes clear, the Forsaken expect no charity from the living world and must rely entirely on their own industriousness to stand on their feet.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Deathguard Saltain need scavenged goods?",a="Deathguard Saltain is gathering equipment to outfit the constant stream of newly awakened Forsaken rising in the nearby cemetery. Without scavenged clothing, boots, and tools, fresh recruits would wander the blighted hills unarmed and defenseless.",al={"why scavenge goods","saltain purpose","why look for boxes"},it="motive"},{q="Where are the scavenged goods found?",a="The goods are found scattered in stacks of boxes and crates throughout the town of Deathknell, resting near abandoned buildings and quiet corners of the settlement.",al={"where are boxes","scavenged goods locations","deathknell boxes"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Deathguard Saltain?",a="Saltain is a pragmatic Deathguard stationed in Deathknell tasked with evaluating and putting fresh undead recruits to work, enforcing order and survival through harsh discipline.",al={"who is saltain","deathguard saltain lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why don't the Forsaken have standard military supplies here?",a="The Forsaken are a young, beleaguered faction carving out an existence amid hostile living enemies and mind-controlled Scourge. In Deathknell, resources are stretched thin, forcing them to reclaim and repurpose anything left behind in the ruins.",al={"why no gear","forsaken supply shortage","deathknell equipment"},it="history"},{q="What happens after completing this task?",a="Saltain rewards the recruit with a piece of salvaged armor from the communal stockpile, solidifying the recruit's transition from an unproven corpse into a functional soldier of the Dark Lady.",al={"next quest","scavenging reward"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are so many new undead rising in Deathknell right now?",a="Residual necromantic magic and the lingering curse in Tirisfal's soil cause dead Lordaeronians to slowly break free of the Lich King's shattered grasp, emerging from their graves confused and requiring immediate guidance.",al={"why rising","new recruits deathknell","why fresh undead"},it="threat"},{q="What are the Scavenged Goods used for?",a="The scavenged goods are recovered equipment and supplies meant to outfit newly awakened Forsaken recruits rising in Deathknell. With supplies scarce and no outside aid, the Forsaken must salvage whatever discarded gear they can find to ensure new soldiers are clothed and armed.",al={"what are scavenged goods for","scavenged goods use","why gather scavenged goods"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Saltain need recruits to scavenge instead of doing it himself?",a="Deathguard Saltain and the other seasoned guards are tasked with holding Deathknell's perimeter and managing the flow of newly risen undead. Scavenging the ruined settlement is low-risk labor, serving both as a practical way to gather supplies and a test to see if fresh recruits are capable of following orders.",al={"why does saltain need me","why send recruits to scavenge","why not have deathguards scavenge"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task demonstrates the stark ethos of your newly embraced unlife: no one in Azeroth will offer pity or charity, and the Forsaken must salvage their own future from the dirt.",["faction:Horde"]="Though the Forsaken are allied with the distant Horde across the sea, outposts like Deathknell must manage day-to-day survival through desperate local scavenging rather than foreign aid."},kw={"deathguard saltain","deathknell","forsaken supplies","scavenged goods","scavenging deathknell","tirisfal glades","undead starter quest"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},m={start="Deathguard Saltain",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3902,ri={"Scavenged Goods"}},src={"Scavenging Deathknell"}}
E["quest:398"] = {t="quest",n="Wanted: Maggot Eye",z="tirisfal",s="Wanted: Maggot Eye is an official death bounty posted on the Brill notice board by the town's Forsaken leadership. A notorious gnoll has been looting nearby graveyards and farms to supply dead bodies directly to the Lich King's Scourge. Executor Zygand requires proof of the creature's execution to secure the lands around Garren's Haunt.",h="Wanted: Maggot Eye is an official death bounty posted on the Brill notice board by the town's Forsaken...",sec={{t="A Grave Crime",b="In Tirisfal Glades, the dead are far from resting peacefully. The gnoll known as Maggot Eye earned a severe death sentence not merely for scavenging, but for active collaboration with the Lich King. While gnolls are generally viewed as savage brutes driven by simple hunger, Maggot Eye has been systematically digging up corpses and collecting remains to fuel the ranks of the Scourge army.",sp=0},{t="Justice in Brill",b="For the Forsaken, whose entire existence hinges on breaking free from the Lich King's dominion, feeding dead kin back into the Scourge war machine is among the highest offenses possible. Executor Zygand, overseeing local military enforcement in Brill, commissioned this bounty to put an end to the grave robberies near Garren's Haunt. To collect the prize, the executioner must sever Maggot Eye's paw as unmistakable proof that the beast will trouble Tirisfal no longer.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Maggot Eye?",a="Maggot Eye is an infamous gnoll prowling the northern regions of Tirisfal Glades. Considered vile even among his kind, he pillages the farms near Garren's Haunt and gathers corpses for the Scourge.",al={"maggot eye lore","who is maggot eye","maggot eye gnoll"},it="target"},{q="Why do the Forsaken want him dead?",a="He was sentenced to death for directly supplying freshly looted corpses to the Lich King's Scourge army. Denying soldiers and resources to the Scourge is essential to Forsaken survival.",al={"why kill maggot eye","maggot eye crimes","why bounty maggot eye"},it="motive"},{q="Where is Maggot Eye usually found?",a="He can be found lurking inside an old ruined hut in Garren's Haunt, a ruined farmstead located to the north of Brill.",al={"maggot eye location","where is maggot eye","find maggot eye"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who pays the reward for the bounty?",a="Executor Zygand in the center of Brill accepts proof of the kill and distributes the town's reward on behalf of the Forsaken military authority.",al={"who turns in maggot eye","maggot eye quest turn in","brill bounty rewarder"},it="leader"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want his paw?",a="In classic frontier justice, taking a unique physical trophy like Maggot Eye's paw ensures indisputable proof of death before the bounty funds are paid out.",al={"why maggot eye paw","paw proof","maggot eyes paw"},it="item"},{q="Are gnolls allied with the Scourge?",a="Generally gnolls act independently out of hunger and greed, but Maggot Eye specifically trades or funnels dead bodies to the Scourge, making him an exceptionally dangerous collaborator.",al={"gnolls and scourge","do gnolls work for lich king","scourge gnolls"},it="allies"},{q="Why does Brill need an adventurer to deal with Maggot Eye?",a="The Forsaken in Brill have their hands full maintaining order and defending against active Scourge incursions and Scarlet Crusade threats across Tirisfal Glades. Rather than stretching their town guard thin by sending soldiers out to track down rogue scavengers, Executor Zygand and the town leadership post public bounties. Calling upon capable travelers and rising champions allows Brill to eliminate regional threats without weakening its local garrison.",al={"why me maggot eye","why don't guards kill maggot eye","why hire adventurer maggot eye"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every corpse stolen by Maggot Eye is a fallen sibling turned back into an enslaved puppet of the Lich King. Putting him down is a matter of both national defense and personal vengeance.",["faction:Horde"]="Tirisfal's northern borders must be pacified if the Horde is to maintain a secure foothold in Lordaeron against remnants of the Scourge."},kw={"bounty","brill","executor zygand","garren's haunt","gnoll","graverobber","lich king","maggot eye","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:garren-s-haunt","npc:executor-zygand","subzone:brill","quest:358","topic:gnoll","topic:forsaken","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Wanted!",["end"]="Executor Zygand",level="10",faction="Horde",id=398,ri={"Maggot Eye's Paw"}},src={"Wanted: Maggot Eye"}}
E["quest:404"] = {t="quest",n="A Putrid Task",z="tirisfal",s="A Putrid Task is an early military assignment offered by Deathguard Dillinger in Brill. It directs adventurers to push back the mindlessly aggressive Scourge encroaching upon the old bridge west of the settlement. Collecting proof of the monsters' destruction secures the road and eases pressure on Tirisfal's defenders.",h="A Putrid Task is an early military assignment offered by Deathguard Dillinger in Brill.",sec={{t="Holding the Bridge",b="Brill sits at a critical crossroads in eastern Tirisfal Glades, making any enemy approach a direct threat to the Forsaken power base. Mindless Scourge constantly seep westward across the waterways, clustering around the broken remnants of the old stone bridge. Without regular patrols beating them back, the shambling corpses would quickly choke transit between Brill and the western farmsteads.",sp=0},{t="A Grudge Against the Mindless",b="For the Forsaken, Scourge roaming their borders is not merely a strategic nuisance; it is an insult. Every mindless ghoul and rotting corpse represents the servitude and torment that Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's followers broke away from. Deathguards like Dillinger take grim satisfaction in crushing their former captors, demanding severed claws as brutal proof that the mindless dead have been put to rest for good.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Deathguard Dillinger, an officer of the Forsaken guard stationed at the western exit of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, assigns this mission.",al={"quest giver","where to get putrid task","deathguard dillinger quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger want putrid claws?",a="The claws serve as gruesome proof of death. The Deathguards need confirmation that wandering Scourge have been thoroughly destroyed, rather than merely driven slightly off the main road.",al={"why putrid claws","claws reason"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where are the rotting dead found?",a="They wander the overgrown fields and riverbanks immediately west of Brill, clustered heavily around the old stone bridge.",al={"rotting dead location","where are the ghouls","old bridge location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are there Scourge in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Although the Forsaken wrested control of Lordaeron's capital and the surrounding glades from the Scourge, Scourge have trickled into Tirisfal Glades and infested the area west of Brill, near the old bridge.",al={"why scourge here","scourge in tirisfal"},it="why_here"},{q="What happens after completing this task?",a="Clearing the bridge uncovers deeper trouble on the nearby farmsteads, prompting Deathguard Dillinger to dispatch you further west to inspect the overrun mills.",al={"next quest","what follows putrid task"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Are the Forsaken and the Scourge not the same thing?",a="No. The Forsaken are free-willed undead who broke the Lich King's telepathic control and fight to protect their autonomy. The Scourge remain mindless thralls bound to the Iron Will of the Frozen Throne.",al={"difference between forsaken and scourge","aren't they all undead"},it="conflict"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger want this done?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is charged with defending Brill and securing the surrounding approaches for the Forsaken. Mindless Scourge have trickled into Tirisfal Glades and gathered around the old bridge just west of town. Left unchecked, these shambling corpses threaten travelers along the road and test the settlement's outer perimeters, making their destruction an urgent matter of regional defense.",al={"dillinger motive","why kill rotting dead","why clear the scourge near brill"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are you sent against at the old bridge?",a="You are sent against the rotting dead and ravaged corpses infesting the area near the old bridge west of Brill. Unlike the Forsaken, who broke free from the Lich King's grasp to reclaim their free will, these creatures are mindless remnants of the Scourge plague wandering the glades and attacking anyone who crosses their path.",al={"rotting dead target","ravaged corpses old bridge","who are the scourge near brill"},it="target",add=true},{q="Why does Dillinger need you instead of the town guard?",a="The Deathguards stationed in Brill are bound to their watch posts, guarding the village gates, outbuildings, and supply lines against sudden incursions. With Scourge roaming the western roads and hostile forces pressing elsewhere, Dillinger cannot abandon his post to patrol the bridge, requiring able-bodied recruits to hunt down the invaders in the field.",al={"why send me dillinger","why dont guards kill the scourge","why cant dillinger do it"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Destroying Scourge is deeply personal for the Forsaken, serving as vengeance against the relentless force that once held their minds captive.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Brill ensures that the fledgling Horde presence in the Eastern Kingdoms maintains a secure foothold against the Scourge."},kw={"a putrid task","brill","deathguard dillinger","putrid claw","ravaged corpse","rotting dead","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={start="Deathguard Dillinger",level="6",faction="Horde",id=404,ri={"Putrid Claw"}},src={"A Putrid Task (Classic)"}}
E["quest:407"] = {t="quest",n="Fields of Grief",z="tirisfal",s="Fields of Grief concludes Apothecary Johaan's experiment to weaponize ordinary pumpkins gathered from Solliden Farmstead. Having infused the crop with his latest alchemical concotion, Johaan dispatches you to feed the laced vegetable to a captive Scarlet Crusader languishing in Brill.",h="Fields of Grief concludes Apothecary Johaan's experiment to weaponize ordinary pumpkins gathered from...",sec={{t="A Weapon of Ingenuity",b="Apothecary Johaan of the Royal Apothecary Society believes the Forsaken must exploit every tactical advantage to fend off the Scourge from the north and human incursions from the south. Rather than relying solely on raw blade-work or dark sorcery, the Society seeks to harness ordinary staples of the land. By tainting the pumpkins recovered from the mortal farmers of Solliden Farmstead, Johaan intends to prove that even common produce can be transformed into an instrument of death on behalf of the Dark Lady.",sp=0},{t="The Cellar Test",b="The test subject awaits beneath the floorboards of the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. Chained and starved, the captured Scarlet Zealot greets Forsaken callers with typical fanatic defiance, invoking the Holy Light to purge the undead. Hunger quickly breaks the crusader's resolve, however. Mistaking the tainted pumpkin for genuine relief, the captive devours it eagerly, only to rapidly deform into a rotting ghoul before collapsing lifeless in the dirt.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan use a pumpkin for his poison?",a="Johaan wants to demonstrate that mundane crops can be turned into subtle, effective weapons against the living. By lacing everyday produce from nearby human farms with alchemical mixtures, the Royal Apothecary Society tests delivery methods that prey on the basic survival needs of mortal foes.",al={"why a pumpkin","why poison pumpkin","laced pumpkin lore"},it="item"},{q="Who is the prisoner in the tavern cellar?",a="The prisoner is a Captured Scarlet Zealot, a fanatic of the Scarlet Crusade captured by Forsaken forces in Tirisfal Glades. Weakened by starvation and isolation, the zealot clings to religious fervor until presented with food.",al={"who is captured zealot","zealot in gallows end","brill cellar prisoner"},it="target"},{q="What happens to the Scarlet Zealot after eating the pumpkin?",a="Upon consuming the laced pumpkin, the zealot mutates rapidly into a ravenous ghoul, wanders aimlessly across the cellar floor, and collapses dead shortly thereafter, proving the fatal potency of Johaan's formulation.",al={"what happens to zealot","zealot transformation","does zealot turn into ghoul"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Where is the Gallows' End Tavern cellar?",a="The cellar is located directly beneath the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. A ramp behind the front counter leads down into the holding area where Forsaken guards keep prisoners.",al={"how to find cellar","where is zealot located","gallows end cellar entrance"},gp=true},{q="What is the Royal Apothecary Society trying to achieve here?",a="The Society works under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner to invent biological and alchemical agents capable of destroying both the Scourge and mortal threats like the Scarlet Crusade. Small-scale experiments on captives in Brill serve as trial runs for these concoctions.",al={"apothecary society goal","why test plague","royal apothecary society tirisfal"},it="purpose"},{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade active in Tirisfal Glades?",a="The Scarlet Crusade considers all undead abominations that must be exterminated, making no distinction between the mindless Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken. They maintain camps and garrisons across the former kingdom of Lordaeron to reclaim the land by holy fire.",al={"scarlet crusade tirisfal","why are scarlets here","crusade presence brill"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want this done?",a="Apothecary Johaan views the Forsaken's condition of undeath as a potent weapon against their enemies. Caught between the Scourge marching from the north and human forces encroaching from the south, the Forsaken need ruthless methods to survive. By delivering a lethal, chemically altered pumpkin to a captured enemy, Johaan aims to field-test his latest concoction and prove that even mundane farm produce can be weaponized in service of the Dark Lady.",al={"johaan motive","why does johaan want the pumpkin delivered","why test the pumpkin"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, this experiment showcases the ruthless pragmatism required to survive surrounded by mortal zealots to the south and the Lich King's armies to the north.",["faction:Horde"]="The Royal Apothecary Society's biochemical research often unsettles the Forsaken's new allies, yet it remains central to securing Tirisfal Glades for the Horde."},kw={"apothecary johaan","brill","captured scarlet zealot","fields of grief","gallows end tavern","laced pumpkin","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","npc:captured-scarlet-zealot","quest:365","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal","topic:light"},m={start="Apothecary Johaan",["end"]="Captured Scarlet Zealot",level="7",faction="Horde",id=407},src={"Fields of Grief (2)"}}
E["quest:408"] = {t="quest",n="The Family Crypt",z="tirisfal",s="The Family Crypt is a Horde quest tasking players with confronting the Scourge inside the Agamand Family Crypt. Commanded by Magistrate Sevren in Brill, adventurers must destroy the raised Agamand ancestors and slay the skeletal Scourge commander, Captain Dargol.",h="The Family Crypt is a Horde quest tasking players with confronting the Scourge inside the Agamand Family...",sec={{t="A Foxtrot in Tirisfal",b="While the Forsaken have carved out a fragile dominion in Tirisfal Glades, remnants of the Lich King's Scourge remain deeply entrenched in the countryside. The Agamand Mills, once a prosperous flour-producing heartland of Lordaeron, have become a dangerous Scourge stronghold. Magistrate Sevren in Brill watches this military buildup with grave concern, knowing that every inch yielded to mindless undead undermines the Forsaken's independence.",sp=0},{t="Defiling the Tomb",b="The center of the local Scourge occupation is the Agamand Family Crypt, where the skeletal commander Captain Dargol has set up headquarters. Using dark necromancy, Dargol has dragged generations of Agamand family dead from their rest, twisting them into Rotting and Wailing Ancestors to swell his ranks. Sevren dispatches Forsaken agents to purge the crypt, lay the disturbed dead back to rest, and severed Dargol's head to shatter Scourge coordination in northern Tirisfal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Captain Dargol?",a="Captain Dargol is a skeletal Scourge officer stationed within the Agamand Family Crypt. From this tomb, he commands local Scourge forces and uses necromancy to reanimate the Agamand family ancestors to march against the Forsaken.",al={"captain dargol","who is dargol","dargol lore"},it="target"},{q="Why does Magistrate Sevren care about the Agamand Mills?",a="Agamand Mills represents a major Scourge foothold dangerously close to Brill and the Undercity. Left unchecked, Captain Dargol's growing force of reanimated ancestors could mount an offensive against the newly liberated Forsaken settlements.",al={"why brill cares","sevren agamand","agamand importance"},it="motive"},{q="Who are the Wailing and Rotting Ancestors?",a="They are the deceased forebears of the wealthy Agamand lineage. Rather than resting peacefully in their ancestral burial grounds, their bodies and spirits have been corrupted and bound to the will of the Scourge by Captain Dargol's dark arts.",al={"wailing ancestors","rotting ancestors","agamand ancestors"},it="target"},{q="What happens after Dargol is killed?",a="Returning Captain Dargol's skull to Magistrate Sevren breaks the Scourge's centralized leadership at the mills. Sevren hails the victory as a critical strike for the Forsaken and an early step toward purging the Lich King's influence from Lordaeron entirely.",al={"crypt aftermath","end of agamand chain","dargol defeated"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Magistrate Sevren want Dargol's skull?",a="Magistrate Sevren demands Captain Dargol's skull as undeniable proof that the Scourge commander has been eliminated. The Agamand Family Crypt serves as the Scourge's primary stronghold in western Tirisfal Glades, and Sevren cannot afford mere rumors of Dargol's defeat. Bringing his severed skull back to Brill confirms that the necromancer's leadership has been shattered and his ritual raising of the Agamand ancestors halted.",al={"dargols skull purpose","why take dargols skull","what is dargols skull for"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, Captain Dargol represents everything they fought to escape: enslavement to the Lich King's will. Putting down the raised ancestors is both an act of self-defense and a grim duty to break the Scourge's grip on their homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Tirisfal Glades is vital to anchoring the Horde's presence in the Eastern Kingdoms, removing immediate threats to their northern stronghold."},kw={"agamand family crypt","agamand mills","brill","captain dargol","magistrate sevren","rotting ancestor","scourge","the family crypt","tirisfal glades","wailing ancestor"},rel={"subzone:agamand-family-crypt","subzone:agamand-mills","npc:magistrate-sevren","quest:355","quest:362","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},m={start="Magistrate Sevren",["end"]="Magistrate Sevren",level="13",faction="Horde",id=408,ri={"Dargol's Skull"}},src={"The Family Crypt (Classic)"}}
E["quest:409"] = {t="quest",n="Proving Allegiance",z="tirisfal",s="Proving Allegiance is the crucial trial presented by Gunther Arcanus to determine whether you truly serve the free-willed Forsaken or the Lich King. To earn the solitary necromancer's trust, you are sent to summon and destroy Lillith Nefara, a dangerous Scourge operative lurking near his island.",h="Proving Allegiance is the crucial trial presented by Gunther Arcanus to determine whether you truly serve the...",sec={{t="A Recluse's Suspicions",b="Gunther Arcanus has isolated himself on an island in Brightwater Lake since rising into undeath, fiercely clinging to his autonomy. Though Bethor Iceshard's magical enchantments on Gunther's spellbook confirmed the emissary does not carry the foul taint of the Scourge, words and charms are not enough for the paranoid hermit. To earn his confidence, an outsider must demonstrate active hostility toward the minions of the Lich King through decisive violence.",sp=0},{t="The Slaying of Lillith Nefara",b="Just south of Gunther's refuge stands a bloodstained ritual altar used by Lillith Nefara, a formidable Scourge agent operating in the Tirisfal Glades. Using a specialized Candle of Beckoning, the petitioner awakens Lillith from the shadows to face her in open combat. Striking down the agent shatters Gunther's long-held conviction that he was the only undead creature capable of breaking free from the Lich King's iron grip.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why does Gunther Arcanus need proof?",a="Gunther has survived as an independent undead by trusting no one. Surrounded by the Scourge, the Scarlet Crusade, and other threats in Tirisfal, he assumes any undead visitor could be an agent sent by the Lich King to subjugate him until they shed Scourge blood.",al={"why does gunther doubt me","gunther trust"},it="motive"},{q="Who is Lillith Nefara?",a="Lillith Nefara is a powerful Scourge agent active along the southern shores of Brightwater Lake. She operates around an ancient, bloody altar near Gunther's island, serving the Lich King's reach in the glades.",al={"who is lillith","lillith nefara lore"},it="target"},{q="What is the Candle of Beckoning?",a="The Candle of Beckoning is a ritual item retrieved from a crate on Gunther's island. Placing and lighting it upon the bloody altar summons Lillith Nefara from her resting place so she can be engaged in battle.",al={"candle of beckoning","how to summon lillith"},it="item"},{q="What realization does Gunther come to upon Lillith's defeat?",a="Defeating Lillith forces Gunther to accept the astonishing truth that he is not unique. Until this trial, he genuinely believed he was the only undead in Lordaeron who had broken free from the Lich King's telepathic domination.",al={"gunther reaction","gunther epiphany"},sp=1},{q="Where does this confrontation take place?",a="The ritual occurs at an altar situated on the lakeshore just off the southern coast of Gunther's island in Brightwater Lake, within eastern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where is the altar","lillith location"},it="place"},{q="What happens after proving your allegiance?",a="With Gunther's doubts resolved, he finally agrees to listen to the emissaries of the Undercity and reconsider his solitary exile, setting the stage for his formal recruitment into Lady Sylvanas's Forsaken.",al={"what next after proving allegiance","after lillith dies"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Gunther need you to destroy Lillith instead of doing it himself?",a="Gunther Arcanus is not merely asking for a pest to be removed; he is testing your loyalty. He has lived in isolation believing himself to be the sole undead soul who broke free from the Lich King's iron grip. While you claim that you and the Forsaken share that independence, words and magical wards alone do not satisfy him. Sending you to summon and destroy an active Scourge commander forces you to demonstrate that you truly stand against the Lich King before he lowers his guard.",al={"why me gunther","why doesn't gunther kill lillith","why send me proving allegiance"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, breaking Gunther's isolation is personal; it proves to a brother in undeath that none of them have to face the wrath of the Scourge or the living alone.",["class:Mage"]="Bethor Iceshard and Gunther Arcanus represent significant arcane and necromantic talents in Lordaeron; demonstrating your capability earns the professional respect of a powerful fellow caster."},kw={"bethor iceshard","brightwater lake","candle of beckoning","gunther arcanus","lillith nefara","proving allegiance","scourge altar","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:411","quest:366","quest:431","npc:gunther-arcanus","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Gunther Arcanus",level="12",faction="Horde",id=409},src={"Proving Allegiance"}}
E["quest:410"] = {t="quest",n="The Dormant Shade",z="tirisfal",s="The Dormant Shade is a dark ritual performed within the ruined homesteads of Tirisfal Glades. By placing a mystic candle upon an old, blood-soaked table, a traveler summons forth a restless spirit tied to the Forsaken's dark rites of loyalty.",h="The Dormant Shade is a dark ritual performed within the ruined homesteads of Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Gruesome Altar",b="Amid the blighted ruins of Tirisfal Glades rests a battered, bloody dinner table. Far from an ordinary piece of neglected domestic furniture, it serves as the physical focus for a sinister conjuration. Stained with dried gore and abandoned to the shadows, the table acts as a makeshift ritual slab where death and dark magic linger.",sp=0},{t="The Candle of Beckoning",b="To awaken whatever lurks attached to the site, one must place the Candle of Beckoning upon the bloodied surface and ignite its flame. The candle's light cuts through the Tirisfal gloom, calling forth the spirit of Lillith Nefara from the veil of death. It is an intentional act of necromantic disturbance designed to challenge those who seek to prove their mettle and allegiance.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of lighting the candle on the table?",a="Lighting the Candle of Beckoning on the battered, bloody table summons the shade of Lillith Nefara, a dangerous apparition who must be vanquished.",al={"why light candle","dormant shade objective","table ritual"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is summoned by this ritual?",a="The ritual draws forth Lillith Nefara, an undead spirit haunting the ruined homesteads of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"who spawns","dormant shade ghost","who is the shade"},it="target"},{q="How does this relate to proving allegiance to the Forsaken?",a="Summoning and destroying Lillith Nefara is an essential test in the quest Proving Allegiance, demonstrating a Forsaken initiate's willingness and ability to root out stubborn apparitions in Tirisfal.",al={"proving allegiance connection","why kill lillith","forsaken test"},it="ties"},{q="Where does this ritual take place?",a="The ritual occurs at an old ruined homestead in Tirisfal Glades, at a battered wooden dinner table stained with dried blood.",al={"table location","where is the table","lilliths dinner table"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Can anyone light the candle?",a="Anyone possessing the Candle of Beckoning who is undertaking the trials of allegiance for the Forsaken can ignite the flame upon the altar.",al={"who can do the ritual","class requirements","candle ritual"},gp=true},{q="Is the table naturally haunted?",a="The table itself is a grim relic of Lordaeron's fall, steeped in violence and residual dark energies that allow the Candle of Beckoning to anchor and manifest the dormant shade.",al={"is the table magical","why the bloody table"},it="origin"},{q="What is the Candle of Beckoning used for?",a="The Candle of Beckoning is an enchanted item crafted specifically to bridge the veil and summon the dormant spirit lingering around this abandoned homestead. When placed atop the bloodied dinner table and lit, its dark flame acts as a beacon, calling forth the restless shade of Lillith Nefara into the physical world so you can confront her.",al={"candle of beckoning use","what does candle of beckoning do","beckoning candle purpose"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, performing rituals over the bloodied ruins of Lordaeron's former homes is a grim reminder that their new existence is forged through death, dark magic, and unyielding loyalty to the Dark Lady.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks understand the resonant power of binding spirits to blood-drenched altars, making this ritual a familiar exercise in manipulating the boundary between life and death.",["class:Priest"]="Shadow priests recognize the call of the candle as an exercise in shadowy communion, pulling a restless lingering soul back into physical conflict."},kw={"bloody table","candle of beckoning","dormant shade","forsaken ritual","lillith nefara","proving allegiance","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:409","zone:tirisfal","topic:light"},m={start="Lillith's Dinner Table",level="10",faction="Horde",id=410,ri={"Candle of Beckoning"}},src={"The Dormant Shade"}}
E["quest:426"] = {t="quest",n="The Mills Overrun",z="tirisfal",s="Tasked by Deathguard Dillinger in Brill, this mission sends Forsaken soldiers to break the Scourge's foothold at the nearby Agamand Mills. Mindless undead gather among the ruined windmills, threatening to establish a forward base dangerously close to Tirisfal Glades' settlements. To halt this staging ground, recruits must shatter the ranks of skeletal soldiers and bonecasters.",h="Tasked by Deathguard Dillinger in Brill, this mission sends Forsaken soldiers to break the Scourge's foothold...",sec={{t="A Looming Threat on the Mills",b="The Agamand Mills once anchored the agricultural heart of Tirisfal Glades, but in the wake of the Third War, their windmills overlook a far grimmer harvest. Scourge forces under the Lich King's thrall have begun massing on the grounds. If these mindless thralls successfully secure the mills, they will have an entrenched base of operations from which to push deeper into Tirisfal and strike directly at the fledgling Forsaken holdings.",sp=0},{t="Proof of Destruction",b="Deathguard Dillinger wastes little time on sentiment. Tasked with fortifying Brill and policing the territory, Dillinger orders recruits to hunt down Rattlecage and Cracked Skull Soldiers alongside Darkeye Bonecasters. By demanding notched ribs and blackened skulls as proof of their destruction, Dillinger ensures the skeletal ranks are being physically battered apart, throwing the mindless remnants back toward the Plaguelands before their offensive can truly begin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the Scourge gathering at Agamand Mills?",a="The Scourge are attempting to establish a forward staging ground in western Tirisfal. If they entrench themselves at Agamand Mills, they can stage coordinated raids deeper into the glades and directly threaten nearby Forsaken settlements like Brill.",al={"why agamand mills","scourge at the mills","why the mills"},it="target"},{q="Who is Deathguard Dillinger?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is a Forsaken sentry stationed in Brill. He coordinates tactical strikes against regional hazards in Tirisfal Glades, particularly the encroaching Scourge presence and hostile gnoll packs that threaten the road.",al={"deathguard dillinger lore","who is dillinger","dillinger brill"},it="identity"},{q="Where are the Agamand Mills located?",a="Agamand Mills sits north of the main western highway in Tirisfal Glades. Traveling west from Brill over the river bridge and following the north fork leads straight to the prominent ruined windmills.",al={"where is agamand mills","mills location","how to get to the mills"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Dillinger want ribs and skulls?",a="Dillinger requires tangible proof that the Scourge front line has been shattered. Bringing back notched ribs from the skeletal infantry and blackened skulls from the bonecasters guarantees the force has been battered and culled.",al={"notched ribs blackened skulls","why ribs and skulls"},it="item"},{q="What is the relationship between the Forsaken and the Scourge here?",a="The Forsaken are undead who broke free of the Lich King's mental domination. The Scourge remain mindless, obedient slaves bound to the Lich King's will, actively seeking to wipe out the Forsaken and reclaim Lordaeron for their master.",al={"forsaken vs scourge","why fight other undead"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens after clearing these Scourge?",a="Clearing the foot soldiers proves only an opening gambit against the infestation at Agamand Mills. Dillinger and the Deathguards continue pushing deeper into the mills to dismantle the lingering commanders and dark forces entrenched there.",al={"what comes next","after the mills overrun"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Deathguard Dillinger want this done?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is tasked with defending Brill and securing Tirisfal Glades for the Forsaken. The Scourge are attempting to establish a forward base of operations at Agamand Mills, which would allow the Lich King's mindless minions to stage deeper incursions into Tirisfal and threaten the Undercity's perimeter. Crushing them before they solidify their foothold is vital to holding the region.",al={"why clear agamand mills","dillinger motive","why destroy the scourge at the mills"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, driving the Scourge out of Agamand Mills is deeply personal: it protects their new sovereign lands while crushing the mindless slaves of the Lich King who once held them in thrall.",["faction:Horde"]="Holding Tirisfal Glades is critical to securing the Horde's solitary foothold in northern Lordaeron, making the elimination of Scourge staging grounds a tactical priority.",["class:Warrior"]="Clashing with the armored skeletons at Agamand Mills serves as a brutal trial by combat against relentless enemies who feel no pain and give no quarter."},kw={"agamand mills","blackened skulls","brill","darkeye bonecaster","deathguard dillinger","notched ribs","scourge","the mills overrun","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:agamand-mills","npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:brill","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Dillinger",level="8",faction="Horde",id=426,ri={"Blackened Skull","Notched Rib"}},src={"The Mills Overrun (Classic)"}}
E["quest:427"] = {t="quest",n="At War With The Scarlet Crusade",z="tirisfal",s="Executor Zygand in Brill coordinates the Forsaken campaign against the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades. Using intelligence recovered by Executor Arren, you are dispatched to eliminate Scarlet Warriors stationed at a guard tower west of the Solliden Farmstead. This assault marks the Forsaken military's opening offensive to purge the zealous crusaders from their borders.",h="Executor Zygand in Brill coordinates the Forsaken campaign against the Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Two-Front War",b="The fledgling Forsaken state under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner faces simultaneous threats on multiple fronts. While the Deathguard keeps a wary vigil against the swelling armies of the Lich King, the fanatical mortals of the Scarlet Crusade contest Forsaken dominance over their former homeland. The Crusade considers all undead abominations to be purged with fire and blade, blind to the fact that the Forsaken have broken free of the Scourge's control.",sp=0},{t="Striking the Western Outpost",b="Thanks to intelligence documents secured by Executor Arren at Deathknell, Executor Zygand now knows the exact encampments and outposts held by the zealots across Tirisfal Glades. With regular Deathguard garrisons occupied by Scourge containment, independent Forsaken champions are commissioned to strike out directly. The first strike falls upon the guard tower west of Solliden Farmstead, where Scarlet Warriors muster and threaten the western approaches to Brill.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade hostile toward the Forsaken?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is an extremist human order dedicated to eradicating the undead. They make no distinction between the mindless Scourge controlled by the Lich King and the free-willed Forsaken loyal to Lady Sylvanas, viewing every undead being as a plague-ridden monster that must be burned.",al={"why scarlet hate undead","scarlet crusade motivation","why are they attacking"},it="hostile"},{q="Who is Executor Zygand?",a="Executor Zygand is the senior military officer of the Forsaken Deathguard stationed in Brill. He directs tactical deployments, defends the roads surrounding the capital's approaches, and oversees offensive strikes against nearby Scarlet and Scourge encroachments.",al={"who is zygand","brill quest giver executor","zygand lore"},it="identity"},{q="How did the Forsaken get intelligence on Scarlet positions?",a="Crucial tactical documents were recovered by recruits serving Executor Arren in Deathknell. These papers laid out Scarlet patrol routes, outposts, and encampments throughout Tirisfal, giving Executor Zygand the information needed to orchestrate targeted counter-attacks.",al={"scarlet documents","arren intelligence","how did they find the tower"},it="origin"},{q="Where are the Scarlet Warriors located?",a="The Scarlet Warriors garrison a defensive tower located to the west of the Solliden Farmstead, in the western countryside of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"scarlet warrior location","where is the tower","solliden farmstead tower"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why doesn't the Deathguard handle the Crusade directly?",a="The Deathguard's formal ranks are stretched thin monitoring the Scourge, whose forces grow in number each night under the Lich King's reach. Because regular soldiers cannot be spared from containment, Zygand relies on ambitious, unassigned champions to cull the Crusaders.",al={"why send players","where is the deathguard","why help zygand"},it="why_me"},{q="Does this mission end the Scarlet presence in Tirisfal?",a="No. This attack on the western watchtower is merely the first strike in a four-part campaign directed by Executor Zygand to dismantle Scarlet command and outposts across the region.",al={"is this the last scarlet quest","scarlet crusade quest chain"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want the Scarlet Warriors killed?",a="With recovered intelligence revealing Scarlet Crusade camps across Tirisfal Glades, Executor Zygand sees a vital opening to strike back against these zealots. The Crusade seeks to eradicate all undead, making them a lethal threat to Brill and the Forsaken's borders if left unchecked.",al={"why kill scarlet warriors","zygand motive","why attack scarlet crusade"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are the Scarlet Warriors at the tower?",a="They are militant zealots of the Scarlet Crusade garrisoned at the watchtower west of the Solliden Farmstead. Driven by fanatical hatred, they view all Forsaken as mindless Scourge monsters deserving of eradication and wage relentless war to reclaim Lordaeron from the undead.",al={"who are scarlet warriors","scarlet warriors tirisfal","who am i killing"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the Scarlet Crusade represents a bitter irony: living former countrymen who brand them monsters and seek their eradication rather than mourning the tragic fall of Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="Establishing a secure border around Tirisfal Glades and the Undercity is essential to giving the Horde a permanent foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"brill","deathguard","scarlet crusade","scarlet warriors","solliden farmstead","sylvanas","tirisfal glades","zygand"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","subzone:solliden-farmstead","npc:executor-arren","topic:lich-king"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="8",faction="Horde",id=427},src={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (Classic)"}}
E["quest:431"] = {t="quest",n="Candles of Beckoning",z="tirisfal",s="Candles of Beckoning is a brief task centered around salvaging specialized ritual tapers discovered in Tirisfal Glades. These eerie tallow candles are steeped in dark practices and recovered directly from crates left among the shadows of Lordaeron's ruins.",h="Candles of Beckoning is a brief task centered around salvaging specialized ritual tapers discovered in...",sec={{t="Tallow and Dark Rites",b="Across Tirisfal Glades, dark magic lingers in neglected crypts, decaying farms, and cultist encampments. Tapers such as the Candles of Beckoning are not everyday illumination; they are crafted specifically to focus shadowy energies, invoke unseen presences, or channel forbidden incantations. Recovering them from discarded caches prevents them from fueling rogue practitioners or Scourge infiltrators.",sp=0},{t="A Reclaimed Relic",b="The crate holding these candles sits on Gunther's Retreat, the island in Brightwater Lake where the lich Gunther Arcanus dwells. A single candle is all that is needed for the test Gunther sets in Proving Allegiance: lit upon the old, bloody altar off the island's southern coast, it awakens the Scourge agent Lillith Nefara.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Candles of Beckoning?",a="These candles are ritualistic tapers imbued with shadowy intent, often utilized in occult ceremonies to focus magical summonings or invoke lingering entities throughout Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what are candles of beckoning","purpose of candles","what do candles do"},it="purpose"},{q="Where are these candles found?",a="They are stored in a Crate of Candles on Gunther's Retreat, Gunther Arcanus's island in Brightwater Lake in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"where to find candles of beckoning","candle crate location","crate of candles"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who crafted these candles?",a="While their exact origins often tie to occultists, cultists, or rogue necromancers operating in Lordaeron's ruins, they are typically found stored in nondescript supply crates intended for ritual use.",al={"who made candles of beckoning","candle creator","who made the crate"},it="origin"},{q="Why does the Horde or Forsaken want them?",a="A candle is needed for Gunther Arcanus's test in Proving Allegiance. Lit on the bloody altar off his island's southern coast, it awakens the Scourge agent Lillith Nefara, and destroying her proves you are the Lich King's foe.",al={"why forsaken need candles","why retrieve candles","why horde candles"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Can just anyone use the candle?",a="While physically simple to take and store in a pack, the magical effects tied to ritual beckoning require specific incantations and ceremonial preparation.",al={"can i light it","how to use candle","use candle of beckoning"},it="power"},{q="Are the candles linked to the Scourge?",a="Cults aligned with dark magic and Scourge remnants frequently employ ritual items of this kind to summon or direct the undead across the northern ruins.",al={"is this scourge magic","are candles scourge","scourge cult candles"},it="ties"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, salvaging occult implements in Tirisfal is a matter of both survival and study, ensuring dangerous artifacts do not fall into hostile hands.",["class:Warlock"]="Practitioners of the dark arts readily recognize the distinct tallow and magical resonance used in ritual beckoning.",["class:Priest"]="Shadow priests understand the delicate balance required when handling items crafted to invoke lingering spirits and call forth unseen presences."},kw={"candles of beckoning","crate of candles","forsaken","occult","ritual taper","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Crate of Candles",level="10",faction="Horde",id=431},src={"Candles of Beckoning"}}
E["quest:492"] = {t="quest",n="A New Plague",z="tirisfal",s="This quest marks the grim culmination of Apothecary Johaan's initial research in Brill on behalf of the Royal Apothecary Society. Having harvested venom, blood, and vile concoctions across Tirisfal Glades, Johaan sends you to the cellar of Gallows' End Tavern to test his lethal brew on a captive dwarven mountaineer.",h="This quest marks the grim culmination of Apothecary Johaan's initial research in Brill on behalf of the Royal...",sec={{t="A Deadly Concoction",b="Throughout your time aiding Apothecary Johaan, your efforts have steadily built toward refining a lethal toxin. The Royal Apothecary Society pursues an ambitious and shadowy agenda on behalf of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner: crafting a new plague that bypasses undead biology while annihilating the living. In this final step, Johaan brews what he euphemistically calls a 'special drink' and tasks you with administering it directly to an imprisoned enemy of the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="The Gallows' End Cellar",b="Beneath Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, the Deathguard maintains a damp cellar used as a makeshift holding cell for captives awaiting interrogation or disposal. Among them sits a captured Dwarven Mountaineer, an operative of the Ironforge reconnaissance units trespassing in Tirisfal Glades. Defiant and thirsty, he eagerly accepts a drink, completely unaware of its true alchemical payload.",sp=0},{t="A Chilling Demonstration",b="When offered the flask, the mountaineer gladly drinks, comparing it wistfully to Rhapsody Malt and boasting that dwarven steam tanks are coming to save him. The poison takes effect almost instantly, killing him on the spot. Johaan's parting words ring with chilling clarity: this lethal draft is merely a taste of what the Dark Lady envisions for the rest of Azeroth.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Apothecary Johaan?",a="Apothecary Johaan is a member of the Royal Apothecary Society stationed in the town of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. He directs early research into creating a new plague tailored for Forsaken warfare, employing adventurers to gather lethal reagents from the surrounding wilderness.",al={"apothecary johaan","who gives a new plague","johaan brilliance"},it="identity"},{q="Why is there a dwarf captured in Brill?",a="Ironforge Mountaineers actively scout the northern kingdoms to monitor Forsaken movements and investigate the plaguelands. The Deathguard apprehended this particular scout in Tirisfal and locked him in the tavern cellar until the apothecaries found a use for him.",al={"dwarf in cellar","captured mountaineer","why is dwarf in tirisfal"},it="target"},{q="What happens when you give the prisoner the drink?",a="The dwarf takes the flagon gladly, desperate to quench his thirst after his capture. Moments after drinking, the toxic brew kills him on the spot, demonstrating the ruthless effectiveness of Johaan's latest plague formulation.",al={"does the dwarf die","drink result","mountaineer death"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="What is the purpose of the New Plague research?",a="The Forsaken seek to invent a new plague under the orders of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. Their goal is to develop a biological weapon potent enough to destroy both the mindless Scourge and the living races who threaten their sovereign territory.",al={"why make new plague","royal apothecary society goal","dark lady plague"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Captured Mountaineer located?",a="The Captured Mountaineer is imprisoned downstairs in the cellar of Gallows' End Tavern, located in the center of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"find captive dwarf","gallows end cellar","where is mountaineer"},gp=true},{q="Does this quest chain continue beyond this dwarf?",a="This four-part assignment for Apothecary Johaan concludes with the mountaineer's death. However, research into the New Plague remains a central, ongoing endeavor across Lordaeron, leading you to further testing assignments in the Undercity and the Sepulcher.",al={"next quest","a new plague followup","after mountaineer dies"},gp=true},{q="Why does Apothecary Johaan want to poison the mountaineer?",a="Apothecary Johaan is conducting practical trials for the Royal Apothecary Society's new plague. Having refined his concoctions using local venom and blood, he needs a live living subject to observe its lethality firsthand. A captured enemy of the Dark Lady sitting helpless in the tavern cellar presents the perfect opportunity to test whether his brew can swiftly eradicate mortal foes.",al={"why does johaan want this done","johaan motive captured mountaineer","why test poison on dwarf"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Carrying out Johaan's experiment offers a firsthand glimpse into the uncompromising survival doctrine of the Forsaken and the lethal work of the Royal Apothecary Society.",["race:Dwarf"]="The unfortunate prisoner represents the far-reaching presence of Ironforge Mountaineers, who risk death and covert interrogation to keep watch over Tirisfal Glades.",["faction:Alliance"]="The fate of the captive mountaineer illustrates the absolute ruthlessness of the Forsaken toward Alliance intruders discovered in Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="While your Kalimdor allies may fight for honor and survival, the Forsaken pursue victory through cold-blooded alchemical warfare that spares neither friend nor foe."},kw={"a new plague","apothecary johaan","brill","captured mountaineer","gallows end","plague","poison","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:apothecary-johaan","npc:captured-mountaineer","quest:367","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Apothecary Johaan",["end"]="Captured Mountaineer",level="11",faction="Horde",id=492},src={"A New Plague (4)"}}
E["quest:5481"] = {t="quest",n="Gordo's Task",z="tirisfal",s="Gordo's Task is an errand in Tirisfal Glades given by Gordo, a wandering abomination trying to gather flora along the road to Brill. Unsuited for delicate fieldwork, he recruits travelers to pick Gloom Weed and deliver it to his handler, Junior Apothecary Holland.",h="Gordo's Task is an errand in Tirisfal Glades given by Gordo, a wandering abomination trying to gather flora...",sec={{t="A Clumsy Laborer",b="Gordo is an abomination who trudges back and forth along the countryside between Deathknell and Brill. While abominations excel at brute combat and heavy hauling, their massive, sutured hands make fine manipulation almost impossible. Sent into the meadows to gather ingredients for the Royal Apothecary Society, Gordo quickly realizes his blunt fingers crush more stems than they pluck, prompting him to bully or bribe passersby into doing the tedious work for him.",sp=0},{t="Apothecary Frustrations",b="Junior Apothecary Holland awaits the delivery in the graveyard behind Brill's town hall, but the errand goes awry. Gordo completely misunderstands his orders, directing travelers to collect harmless Gloom Weed instead of the volatile Doom Weed required for alchemical research. Holland is thoroughly exasperated by the stitched giant's incompetence, treating the mistaken delivery as proof of the abomination's uselessness before immediately assigning the player to correct the error.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Gordo?",a="Gordo is an abomination bound to the service of the Royal Apothecary Society. He patrols the meadows west of Brill, attempting to complete labor tasks assigned by his master, Junior Apothecary Holland.",al={"who is gordo","gordo tirisfal","gordo npc","what is gordo"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Gordo need help gathering weeds?",a="Gordo's hands are enormous, clumsy appendages of stitched dead flesh. He simply lacks the dexterity required to pick delicate plants without ruining them, so he recruits traveling Forsaken adventurers to gather the flora instead.",al={"why does gordo need weeds","why can't gordo pick weeds","gordo hands"},it="why_me"},{q="Who is Master Holland?",a="Junior Apothecary Holland is an alchemist with the Royal Apothecary Society stationed in the Brill graveyard. He oversees Gordo and conducts research on the local flora and blighted soil to aid the Forsaken war effort.",al={"master holland","who is holland","apothecary holland"},it="identity"},{q="Why is Holland upset when the Gloom Weed is delivered?",a="Holland never wanted Gloom Weed. Gordo completely misunderstood his orders and sent adventurers after the wrong plant. Holland actually needed Doom Weed, a far more potent and dangerous ingredient for his concoctions.",al={"why is holland mad","gloom weed mistake","wrong weed"},sp=1,it="conflict"},{q="Where can Gloom Weed be found?",a="Gloom Weed sprouts low to the ground in the wild fields between Deathknell and Brill, particularly in the open meadows east of the Solliden Farmstead.",al={"where to find gloom weed","gloom weed locations","finding gloom weed"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after completing Gordo's Task?",a="Upon receiving the useless Gloom Weed, Junior Apothecary Holland immediately sends you back out into the fields on the quest 'Doom Weed' to gather the actual volatile plants he originally requested.",al={"what comes next","gordo's task follow up","next quest after gordo"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Gordo want gloom weed collected?",a="Gordo was ordered by his master, Junior Apothecary Holland, to gather weeds from the glades. Being a clumsy abomination, Gordo is desperate to fulfill his master's commands and avoid trouble, but he confused the order and believes Holland asked for harmless gloom weed instead of what the apothecary actually requires.",al={"why does gordo want weeds","gordo motive","why collect gloom weed"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is Gloom Weed used for?",a="As far as Master Holland is concerned, gloom weed is utterly useless. It is simply a common, harmless wild weed that grows throughout Tirisfal Glades. Holland has no interest in picking daisies and needs Doom Weed for his concoctions instead.",al={"what is gloom weed for","gloom weed purpose","use of gloom weed"},sp=1,it="item",add=true}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Trained herbalists will find this task effortless, as their tracking sense easily highlights Gloom Weed patches nestled amidst the blighted grass.",["race:Undead"]="For newly risen Forsaken leaving Deathknell, this task provides a first encounter with the clunky realities of abomination labor and the sharp temper of the Royal Apothecary Society."},kw={"abomination","apothecary","brill","doom weed","gloom weed","gordo","holland","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","subzone:deathknell","subzone:solliden-farmstead","topic:horde"},m={level="5",faction="Horde",id=5481,ri={"Gloom Weed"}},src={"Gordo's Task (Classic)"}}
E["quest:5482"] = {t="quest",n="Doom Weed",z="tirisfal",s="Doom Weed is a task given by Junior Apothecary Holland in Brill to gather noxious flora sprouting near Tirisfal's northern mass graves. Holland recruits passing adventurers after becoming frustrated with the clumsy harvesting efforts of the abomination Gordo.",h="Doom Weed is a task given by Junior Apothecary Holland in Brill to gather noxious flora sprouting near...",sec={{t="Apothecary Ambitions",b="Junior Apothecary Holland conducts research on behalf of the Royal Apothecary Society from the settlement of Brill. Seeking to advance his experiments, he requires quantities of doom weed, a vile plant that infests the blighted soil of Tirisfal Glades. Finding Gordo incapable of discerning the proper reagents, Holland turns to sharper hands to salvage his harvest.",sp=0},{t="The Mass Grave Harvest",b="Doom weed thrives in places heavily corrupted by death and plague, proliferating densely around the mass gravesite situated just north of the Brill graveyard. Gathering the flora is made perilous by aggressive Rot Hide gnolls that prowl the desecrated burial mounds, scavenging among the remains and defending the overgrown trenches.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Junior Apothecary Holland need doom weed?",a="Junior Apothecary Holland needs doom weed to conduct his alchemical work for the Royal Apothecary Society. He claims the service directly aids Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, though his greedy enthusiasm suggests he is equally invested in furthering his own career within the society.",al={"holland doom weed","why doom weed","doom weed purpose"}},{q="Why doesn't Gordo gather the weeds himself?",a="Gordo is an abomination tasked with gathering herbs around Brill, but he lacks the intellect to identify specific plants. Holland complains that the lumbering creature brought back entirely the wrong weeds, forcing the apothecary to hire capable adventurers instead.",al={"why not gordo","gordo task fail","who is gordo"}},{q="Where does doom weed grow?",a="Doom weed grows around the blighted flora surrounding the mass gravesite, located directly north of the graveyard outside Brill. It spreads through soil heavily tainted by burial trenches and lingering plague.",al={"doom weed location","where is doom weed","mass grave weeds"}},{q="What dangers lurk near the doom weed patches?",a="The grounds around the mass gravesite are overrun by Rot Hide gnolls. These feral, disease-ridden scavengers aggressively attack anyone venturing close to the burial grounds while harvesting the weeds.",al={"doom weed monsters","mass grave enemies","gnolls near brill"}},{q="What is the Royal Apothecary Society's interest in Tirisfal flora?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society constantly studies plants, poisons, and blight-altered fungi across Tirisfal Glades. Reagents like doom weed are harvested to refine toxins, uncover alchemical cures, and develop new weapons to aid the Forsaken against their enemies.",al={"apothecary interest","royal apothecary society tirisfal","forsaken herb gathering"}},{q="Who is 'the Lady' mentioned by Holland?",a="The Lady refers to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen and ruler of the Forsaken. Apothecaries in Undercity and Brill frequently invoke her name, as their alchemical efforts officially serve her command.",al={"who is the lady","holland lady sylvanas","the lady undercity"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Serving junior members of the Royal Apothecary Society is a common rite of passage for newly awakened Forsaken seeking to establish their usefulness to Sylvanas's realm.",["profession:Herbalism"]="While ordinary harvestable flora grows throughout the glades, corrupted variants like doom weed illustrate the unnatural botanical shifts caused by the Scourge's plague.",["faction:Horde"]="The unsettling alchemical experiments taking place in Brill reveal how the Forsaken operate quite differently from their shamanic and nomadic allies in Kalimdor."},kw={"brill","doom weed","gordo","junior apothecary holland","mass grave","rot hide gnolls","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:5481","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:gnoll","topic:horde"},m={level="6",id=5482},src={"In-game quest text: Doom Weed","Doom Weed (Classic)"},fv=true}
E["quest:5650"] = {t="quest",n="Garments of Darkness",z="tirisfal",s="Garments of Darkness is a foundational rite of passage for fledgling Forsaken priests in Brill. Dark Cleric Beryl tasks initiates with healing and fortifying an injured Deathguard, testing their grasp of supportive magics before they delve deeper into the shadows.",h="Garments of Darkness is a foundational rite of passage for fledgling Forsaken priests in Brill.",sec={{t="A Balance of Dark and Light",b="While the Forsaken priesthood draws renown and distinct identity from shadow and darkness, its elders recognize the enduring pragmatic necessity of restoration. Dark Cleric Beryl teaches that understanding the dark demands an understanding of the light and restorative arts. A priest who seeks only ruin is cautioned to study arcane magic instead; the priesthood's true calling involves strengthening and preserving comrades just as much as afflicting enemies.",sp=0},{t="A Duty in the Graveyard",b="To demonstrate mastery, the initiate is sent north of Brill's cemetery to aid Deathguard Kel. Stationed against prowling gnoll packs, Kel has sustained injuries holding the line. By binding his wounds with healing spells and bolstering his stamina with protective enchantments, the acolyte proves capable of maintaining the defenders of Tirisfal Glades, earning Beryl's vestment and acceptance into the priesthood's higher mysteries.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do Forsaken priests use healing magic if their power comes from darkness?",a="Dark Cleric Beryl explains that while Forsaken priests draw exceptional strength from shadow, healing remains essential. Before mastering darkness, an acolyte must understand supportive and restorative magic to keep Forsaken defenders standing on the battlefield.",al={"why do undead heal","forsaken priest healing lore","shadow vs light undead"},it="power"},{q="Who is Deathguard Kel?",a="Deathguard Kel is a Forsaken sentry stationed north of the cemetery in Brill. He defends the local settlements against aggressive gnoll packs wandering the woods and requires aid when injured in the line of duty.",al={"deathguard kel lore","who is deathguard kel","wounded deathguard"},it="target"},{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Dark Cleric Beryl, the priest trainer located in Brill within Tirisfal Glades, offers the quest after you complete the preliminary task, In Favor of Darkness.",al={"who starts garments of darkness","garments of darkness quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="What vestments are granted upon finishing the trial?",a="Beryl awards a tailored robe as a badge of station and accomplishment, symbolizing that the priest has mastered their fundamental duties and is recognized by their peers.",al={"what robe do you get","garments reward priest"},it="item"},{q="What threats was Deathguard Kel fighting?",a="Kel was wounded while fighting the local gnolls that infest the surrounding wilderness of Tirisfal Glades north of the Brill cemetery.",al={"what attacked kel","kel gnolls"},it="threat"},{q="What darker powers does Dark Cleric Beryl hint at?",a="Beryl hints at shadow techniques unique to the Forsaken priesthood—distinctive manifestations of shadow magic and mental affliction not practiced by priests of other races.",al={"forsaken priest unique spells","darker powers hint"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Dark Cleric Beryl want this done?",a="Dark Cleric Beryl seeks to test your discipline and readiness within the Forsaken priesthood. While Forsaken priests draw profound strength from darkness and shadow, Beryl believes a priest cannot truly master the dark without first understanding the light and its restorative arts. By directing you to mend and fortify the injured Deathguard Kel, she tests your practical grasp of essential healing before welcoming you deeper into the priesthood's darker teachings.",al={"beryl motive","why does beryl send you","why heal kel beryl"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Dark Cleric Beryl send you instead of tending to Deathguard Kel herself?",a="This task serves specifically as a rite of passage for you as an initiate. Beryl already possesses these abilities, but evaluating your growth requires you to venture out into the field and apply your training directly. Mending and fortifying an active defender like Deathguard Kel proves to Beryl and your peers that you are capable of supporting the Forsaken in battle.",al={"why send me to heal kel","why not beryl herself","why does beryl need me"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="This rite encapsulates the Forsaken philosophy of the priesthood: balancing the grim utility of shadow with the irreplaceable duty of fortifying and healing allies.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, healing magic is a pragmatic tool of survival used to sustain front-line soldiers like Deathguard Kel against gnolls and Scourge remnants."},kw={"brill","dark cleric beryl","deathguard kel","fortitude","garments of darkness","lesser heal","priest","shadow","tirisfal glades"},rel={"quest:5651","npc:dark-cleric-beryl","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:light","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Dark Cleric Beryl",level="4",faction="Horde",id=5650},src={"Garments of Darkness"}}
E["quest:5651"] = {t="quest",n="In Favor of Darkness",z="tirisfal",s="In Favor of Darkness marks a Forsaken priest's transition beyond the starting crypts of Deathknell toward advanced instruction in Brill. Entrusted by Dark Cleric Duesten, the fledgling disciple travels to study under Dark Cleric Beryl to master the delicate balance between the Holy Light and the Shadow.",h="In Favor of Darkness marks a Forsaken priest's transition beyond the starting crypts of Deathknell toward...",sec={{t="A Path Carved in Rot",b="While priests of other races revere the Holy Light as an unblemished source of moral virtue, the Forsaken approach faith through the harsh lens of their own undeath. As Dark Cleric Duesten teaches, the Forsaken were forged in corruption and discarded like carrion. Their survival demands understanding both Light and Shadow without sentimentality. In Favor of Darkness directs new disciples out of Deathknell's cloistered graveyard to confront the wider reality awaiting them in Tirisfal Glades.",sp=0},{t="Instruction in Brill",b="The road leading northeast out of Deathknell brings the fledgling priest over a wooden covered bridge and into the ruined hamlet of Brill. There, in the upper quarters of the local inn, Dark Cleric Beryl waits to test the initiate's resolve. Beryl values ambition and cold practicality over blind devotion, demanding that a Forsaken priest wield divine power as a weapon and a tool rather than a moral crutch.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="It serves as a rite of passage for Forsaken priests leaving Deathknell. Dark Cleric Duesten sends you to Dark Cleric Beryl in Brill to continue your training and receive your next priestly challenge.",al={"what does this quest do","why in favor of darkness","in favor of darkness purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Why do Forsaken priests use the Light if they embrace darkness?",a="Forsaken clerics acknowledge that the world operates on a balance between Light and Shadow. Even though the Holy Light causes excruciating pain to undead flesh and their origins lie in rot and darkness, they study both forces out of practicality and necessity.",al={"why do undead priests use light","forsaken priest lore","light and shadow undead"}},{q="Where do I find Dark Cleric Beryl?",a="Dark Cleric Beryl is located in the town of Brill, northeast of Deathknell. He resides on the second floor of the Gallows' End Tavern on the eastern side of the settlement.",al={"where is dark cleric beryl","beryl location","find beryl"},gp=true},{q="What does this quest lead into?",a="Speaking with Dark Cleric Beryl completes this task and opens the follow-up quest, Garments of Darkness, where he tests your ability to mend and sustain allies using your newfound disciplines.",al={"what comes next","next quest after in favor of darkness","in favor of darkness followup"},it="next_step"},{q="Who is Dark Cleric Duesten?",a="Dark Cleric Duesten is the priest trainer stationed in Deathknell. He guides newly awakened Forsaken priests in understanding their painful existence and utilizing their spiritual powers.",al={"who gives this quest","duesten lore","dark cleric duesten"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Beryl mention ambition over faith?",a="Forsaken priests view traditional pious faith as insufficient after being abandoned by the powers they worshipped in life. Dark Cleric Beryl expects disciples to rely on personal ambition, urgency, and practical strength to survive.",al={"beryl dialogue meaning","why ambition not faith","forsaken faith"}},{q="Why does Dark Cleric Duesten send you to Brill?",a="Duesten has taught you the foundational truth of Forsaken priesthood: that while you may wield both the Light and the dark with discretion, your existence was forged in rot and darkness. Now that you have grasped these fundamentals in Deathknell, he sends you onward to Dark Cleric Beryl in Brill to advance your training in service of the Forsaken.",al={"why does duesten send me away","duesten motive in favor of darkness","why go to dark cleric beryl"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="This quest encapsulates the fundamental philosophy of Forsaken priesthood: wielding both Holy Light and Shadow with cold discretion rather than religious reverence.",["race:Undead"]="The journey from Deathknell to Brill is the standard first excursion into the broader Forsaken realm, showing initiates how their people rebuild in the ruins of Lordaeron."},kw={"beryl","brill","deathknell","duesten","light","priest","shadow","tirisfal"},rel={"quest:5650","npc:dark-cleric-beryl","subzone:deathknell","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:light"},m={start="Dark Cleric Duesten",["end"]="Dark Cleric Beryl",level="4",faction="Horde",id=5651},src={"In Favor of Darkness"}}
E["quest:590"] = {t="quest",n="A Rogue's Deal",z="tirisfal",s="A Rogue's Deal is a confrontation with Calvin Montague in Deathknell. After offering work to newly awakened Forsaken with the promise of wealth, the sly rogue's true intentions come to light when payment is demanded.",h="A Rogue's Deal is a confrontation with Calvin Montague in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Dishonorable Promise",b="Calvin Montague loiters near the crypts of Deathknell offering seemingly easy work for newly risen Forsaken. Claiming there is good coin to be earned by delivering a letter, Montague sends green adventurers into danger. However, once the errand is done, Montague conveniently forgets his promise of payment, feigning utter ignorance about any arrangement or owed coin.",sp=0},{t="Paying the Price",b="When pressed to honor his bargain, Montague shows his true colors. Rather than parting with his silver, he draws his weapons, betting that he can easily cut down an inexperienced fledgling. Beating sense into the rogue forces him to concede. Thoroughly battered, he coughs up a cut of coin, freely admitting that he lied about the payout simply because he thought he could get away with it.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why does Calvin Montague attack when asked for payment?",a="Calvin Montague is an unscrupulous rogue who never intended to share his coin. When confronted about his broken promise, he attempts to silence his associate permanently rather than pay up, assuming a newly raised Forsaken will be an easy mark.",al={"why calvin fight","calvin montague hostile","why rogue attacks"},sp=1,it="motive"},{q="Where does this confrontation take place?",a="The confrontation takes place directly in Deathknell, the ruined village in southwestern Tirisfal Glades where newly risen Forsaken first awaken.",al={"where is calvin montague","calvin montague location","where is rogue's deal"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Does Calvin Montague actually pay what he promised?",a="No. Once beaten in combat, Calvin surrenders and hands over some coin, but admits it is far less than he originally offered because he lied about the full amount from the start.",al={"calvin reward","does calvin pay","did he lie about coin"},sp=1},{q="How is this second task unlocked?",a="After completing the initial delivery errand from Calvin Montague, return and speak to him again to demand the coin he promised. Challenging his deceit immediately triggers the fight.",al={"how to get part 2","unlock rogue deal fight","secret rogue quest"},sp=1,gp=true},{q="Does Calvin Montague die when defeated?",a="No. Montague yields before taking a fatal blow, profusely complimenting your fighting skill and begrudgingly opening his coin pouch to save his own skin.",al={"did i kill calvin","is calvin dead","calvin surrender"},sp=1},{q="What lesson does this encounter offer to new Forsaken?",a="It serves as an early, practical lesson in the harsh realities of the Forsaken: honor is scarce among the undead, words are cheap, and respect is earned only through martial strength.",al={"what is the point of rogue deal","lore behind rogue's deal"}},{q="Why do you have to fight Calvin Montague?",a="Calvin Montague is a rogue through and through. After luring you into doing his dirty work with false promises of wealth, he feigns ignorance when you return to collect your coin. Rather than parting with his silver, he decides it is cheaper and easier to simply attack you, hoping you will be easy prey. To earn your rightful due, you have to beat respect—and your payment—out of him.",al={"why fight calvin montague","who is calvin montague target","why attack calvin"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Dealing with Calvin Montague offers young rogues an immediate lesson in professional hazard: trust no one, watch your purse, and never let another scoundrel walk away with your cut.",["race:Undead"]="Waking up in Deathknell quickly proves that death does not strip humanity's pettiest vices; Forsaken cutpurses are just as quick to swindle their own kin as living bandits."},kw={"a rogue's deal","brawl","calvin montague","deathknell","forsaken rogue","swindle","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:calvin-montague","quest:8","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Calvin Montague",level="5",faction="Horde",id=590},src={"A Rogue's Deal (2)"}}
E["quest:6395"] = {t="quest",n="Marla's Last Wish",z="tirisfal",s="Marla's Last Wish is an early Forsaken task given by Novice Elreth in Deathknell. It asks you to hunt down the Scourge-enslaved remnants of Samuel Fipps so he can be buried beside his murdered wife, Marla. The task reflects the fledgling Forsaken society's yearning to honor human ties and distinguish themselves from the mindless Scourge.",h="Marla's Last Wish is an early Forsaken task given by Novice Elreth in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Tragic Reunion",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, Marla and Samuel Fipps lived together as husband and wife. When the plague swept through their lands, Samuel succumbed to the contagion and was reanimated as a mindless thrall of the Scourge. Though Marla was spared undeath, she could not escape Samuel himself, who brutally slew her in his mindless frenzy. Despite dying at her husband's hands, Marla harbored no hatred in her final moments. Her dying wish, remembered by Novice Elreth, was to rest eternally beside her husband in the Deathknell graveyard.",sp=0},{t="Respecting the Dead",b="Novice Elreth dispatches Forsaken adventurers northeast of Deathknell to locate Samuel Fipps, who still wanders among a ruined campsite. Putting an end to Samuel's torment serves a dual purpose: it cleanses an encroaching Scourge minion from the valley and allows his physical remains to be carried back to Marla's resting place. As Elreth emphasizes, honoring the deceased and respecting their final wishes is a vital moral distinction separating the newly liberated Forsaken from the mindless malice of the Scourge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Marla Fipps?",a="Marla Fipps was a Lordaeron resident who survived the initial plague of undeath, only to be slain by her husband Samuel after he turned into a mindless Scourge ghoul. Her final dying wish was to be laid to rest alongside him.",al={"marla fipps lore","who is marla","marla background"},it="identity"},{q="Who is Samuel Fipps?",a="Samuel Fipps was Marla's husband before the plague. He succumbed to the Scourge, lost his mind to the Lich King's will, and murdered his wife. He wanders a ruined campsite northeast of Deathknell until put down by Forsaken adventurers.",al={"samuel fipps lore","who is samuel fipps","where is samuel fipps"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Novice Elreth care about burying them together?",a="Novice Elreth was Marla's friend in life. Beyond personal sentiment, Elreth believes that honoring the wishes of the dead and treating remains with dignity is proof that the Forsaken retain their humanity and morality, distinguishing them from the mindless Scourge.",al={"why help marla","novice elreth motive","why bury samuel"},it="motive"},{q="Did Marla rise as Forsaken?",a="No. According to Novice Elreth, Marla was spared undeath altogether. She died a mortal death at her husband's hands and was buried in the graveyard at Deathknell.",al={"is marla undead","did marla become forsaken","marla fipps undead"}},{q="Where is Marla's grave located?",a="Marla's grave is located in the first row of the cemetery just outside Deathknell's main chapel.",al={"marla's grave location","where is marla buried","where do i bury samuel"},gp=true},{q="Where can Samuel Fipps be found?",a="Samuel Fipps wanders around a ruined campsite along the road heading northeast out of Deathknell.",al={"samuel fipps location","find samuel fipps","where is samuel"},gp=true},{q="What are Samuel's Remains for?",a="You collect Samuel's earthly remains so they can finally be laid to rest alongside his wife, Marla. Even though Samuel was turned into a mindless scourge and took her life, Marla's dying wish was for them to share an eternal resting place in the Deathknell graveyard.",al={"samuels remains","why gather samuels remains","samuel remains purpose"},it="item",add=true},{q="Who is Samuel Fipps and why must you defeat him?",a="Samuel Fipps was once a living resident of Tirisfal Glades who succumbed to the plague of undeath and became an unthinking servant of the Scourge. After killing his own wife in his mindless state, he now roams the ruined camp northeast of Deathknell as a wandering ghoul, unable to find rest until destroyed.",al={"why kill samuel fipps","who do i fight in marlas last wish","samuel fipps lore"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the newly risen Forsaken, fulfilling Marla's wish is an emotional anchor to their former human lives, demonstrating that compassion and honor did not perish when Lordaeron fell.",["class:Priest"]="Novice Elreth's insistence on proper burial rites speaks directly to the spiritual duty of Forsaken priests, who grapple with mortality, grief, and the desecration of Lordaeron's dead."},kw={"deathknell","grave","marla fipps","marla's last wish","novice elreth","remains","samuel fipps","scourge"},rel={"npc:novice-elreth","subzone:deathknell","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Novice Elreth",level="5",faction="Horde",id=6395,ri={"Samuel's Remains"}},src={"Marla's Last Wish"}}
E["quest:8"] = {t="quest",n="A Rogue's Deal",z="tirisfal",s="A seemingly casual favor for Calvin Montague in Deathknell sends a traveler on the road to the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill. The errand involves ferrying a mysterious, unaddressed message to Innkeeper Renee under the guise of an ordinary courier run.",h="A seemingly casual favor for Calvin Montague in Deathknell sends a traveler on the road to the Gallows' End...",sec={{t="A Friendly Courier",b="Lounging amidst the solemn graves and ruined chapels of Deathknell, Calvin Montague presents himself as an exhausted fighter burdened by too many Scourge zombies and creeping spiders. Claiming he simply needs a hand delivering a message to Brill, he entrusts passing adventurers with an unmarked letter. His breezy demeanor and promise of generous coin make the task appear as nothing more than a harmless favor meant to point freshly awakened Forsaken toward the region's main town.",sp=0},{t="The Gallows' End Tavern",b="The road leads eastward through the blighted trees of Tirisfal Glades, passing Cold Hearth Manor to reach the settlement of Brill. Behind the counter of the local tavern, Innkeeper Renee initially greets newcomers with an unnervingly sunny disposition, doting on the comfort of her patrons. However, upon receiving Montague's correspondence, her hospitality immediately fractures. Dropping her forced pleasantries, she feigns receiving word from a beloved mother before scolding the courier for prying and demanding they buy something or leave.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Calvin Montague?",a="Calvin Montague is an unscrupulous rogue lingering around the cemetery and training grounds of Deathknell. He frequently recruits newly awakened undead into handling his personal errands with promises of quick payment.",al={"calvin montague","who gives a rogues deal","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="What is inside the Nondescript Letter?",a="The letter's exact contents are kept secret from the courier. Montague leaves it unmarked, and Innkeeper Renee quickly snatches it away, dismissing any curiosity with defensive sarcasm.",al={"nondescript letter contents","what is in the letter","calvins letter"},it="item"},{q="Why does Innkeeper Renee act so strange?",a="Renee maintains an exaggerated, cheery tavern-host persona for travelers in Brill, but drops the act the moment Montague's covert delivery arrives, revealing an irritable and secretive temperament.",al={"innkeeper renee attitude","why is renee fake","renee mother excuse"}},{q="Does Calvin Montague actually pay for the delivery?",a="Despite promising he would pay well for taking the message to Brill, Montague sends the courier off without upfront compensation, leaving travelers to discover the true nature of his deals upon their return.",al={"did calvin scam me","where is my money","montague reward"},it="for_you"},{q="Where is Brill located?",a="Brill sits northeast of Deathknell. Travelers follow the main road east out of the starter valley, veer right past Cold Hearth Manor, and cross a covered bridge into the settlement.",al={"how to get to brill","road to brill","brill directions"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after giving the letter to Renee?",a="After dropping off the letter, returning to Calvin Montague in Deathknell reveals that the rogue has no intention of paying up peacefully, escalating into a direct confrontation.",al={"next quest","a rogues deal follow up","what to do after renee"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Calvin Montague want the letter delivered?",a="Calvin Montague claims he has fought more than his fair share of mindless zombies and spiders around Deathknell and simply needs someone to run an errand for him. Rather than trek up the road to Brill himself, he offers to pay you handsomely to deliver his sealed, nondescript letter directly to Innkeeper Renee.",al={"calvin montague motive","why send letter to renee","why does calvin want this done"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For recently risen Forsaken, this errand marks their very first journey away from the safety of Deathknell's graveyard toward the bustling hub of Brill.",["class:Rogue"]="Calvin Montague's shifty requests and empty promises serve as an early lesson in how quickly underworld dealings can turn sour in Lordaeron.",["faction:Horde"]="The delivery leads straightforwardly to Brill, introducing newly arrived allies to the regional heart of Forsaken administration and respite in Tirisfal."},kw={"brill","calvin montague","deathknell","gallows end","innkeeper renee","nondescript letter","rogue","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:calvin-montague","subzone:brill","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","quest:5481","subzone:cold-hearth-manor","topic:forsaken"},m={start="Calvin Montague",["end"]="Innkeeper Renee",level="5",faction="Horde",id=8},src={"A Rogue's Deal"}}
E["quest:8652"] = {t="quest",n="Graveborn the Elder",z="tirisfal",s="Graveborn the Elder is a Lunar Festival encounter situated near the town of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. During the celebration, travelers seek out Elder Graveborn among the blighted groves to honor the ancient forebears of Azeroth. Paying respects to this spirit earns his blessing and a token of the holiday.",h="Graveborn the Elder is a Lunar Festival encounter situated near the town of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Ghostly Vigil in Brill",b="While the Forsaken dwell among ruined manors and grave sites in Tirisfal Glades, the Lunar Festival brings an unexpected communion with ancient times. Elder Graveborn appears as a spectral elder stationed just outside Brill. Even in a realm shattered by the Scourge and reclaimed by the undead, he welcomes pilgrims who pause to honor the ancient ways.",sp=0},{t="Honoring the Olden Races",b="The Lunar Festival marks a time when the great spirits of long-past eras return to offer wisdom and gratitude. When travelers approach Elder Graveborn, he remarks on how heartening it is that the inhabitants of Tirisfal still offer homage to the ancestors despite the land's current decay, bestowing a Coin of Ancestry upon those who greet him.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Elder Graveborn located?",a="Elder Graveborn is found near the settlement of Brill in Tirisfal Glades, standing quietly near the roadside outside the town.",al={"elder graveborn location","where is graveborn","graveborn coords"}},{q="What happens when you speak to Elder Graveborn?",a="Upon greeting him, Elder Graveborn commends you for honoring the ancient peoples of Azeroth and awards a Coin of Ancestry as part of the Lunar Festival celebrations.",al={"graveborn reward","lunar festival graveborn","what does he give you"}},{q="Why is an ancient elder standing in Tirisfal Glades?",a="During the Lunar Festival, the spirits of ancient ancestors manifest across all of Azeroth, from the kaldorei forests to lands torn by war and plague. Elder Graveborn watches over the northern reaches of Lordaeron to ensure its history is not entirely eclipsed by death.",al={"why is he in tirisfal","graveborn lore","who is elder graveborn"}},{q="Can Alliance players visit Elder Graveborn?",a="Elder Graveborn himself is a neutral spirit of wisdom and will speak with members of both the Alliance and the Horde. However, because he is right outside the Forsaken town of Brill, Alliance adventurers must beware the local guards.",al={"can alliance talk to graveborn","alliance graveborn the elder","graveborn hostile"}},{q="What is the Coin of Ancestry used for?",a="Coins of Ancestry gathered from elders like Graveborn can be traded in Moonglade during the Lunar Festival for festive garments, firework recipes, and other celebratory tokens.",al={"coin of ancestry use","what are lunar coins for"}},{q="Is Elder Graveborn one of the Forsaken?",a="No. Despite his name and ghostly visage near Brill, Elder Graveborn is one of the ancient spirits celebrated during the Lunar Festival, belonging to the long lineage of Azeroth's elders rather than Sylvanas's Forsaken.",al={"is graveborn undead","is elder graveborn forsaken"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken living in Brill, the arrival of ancient ancestral spirits provides a stark reminder of Azeroth's deeper history beyond the recent tragedies of the Scourge.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers seeking out Elder Graveborn must tread carefully through Tirisfal Glades, as Brill is a heavily fortified Forsaken haven.",["class:Druid"]="Druids reverence the Lunar Festival elders as links to the ancient world before the Sundering, heedless of the troubled faction boundaries they inhabit."},kw={"brill","coin of ancestry","elder graveborn","graveborn","lunar festival","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:elder-graveborn","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Elder Graveborn",id=8652},src={"Graveborn the Elder"}}
E["quest:86784"] = {t="quest",n="Sticks and Bones",z="tirisfal",s="Sticks and Bones is a local task offered by Deathguard Bartholomew in Tirisfal Glades. He sends travelers to gather dry branches across the blighted woodland to deliver to Eleanor Shackleton, an experienced survivalist camped in the western hills.",h="Sticks and Bones is a local task offered by Deathguard Bartholomew in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Friendly Gesture",b="While Deathguards in Tirisfal Glades are usually consumed by military patrol duty and guarding against the Scourge or Scarlet crusaders, Deathguard Bartholomew takes a moment to look out for an acquaintance. Knowing that Eleanor Shackleton spends her unlife out in the harsh elements, he encourages passing adventurers to collect dry branches for her camp fire.",sp=0},{t="Life in the Wilds",b="Eleanor Shackleton prides herself on surviving the blighted wilderness of Tirisfal alone. Despite being undead, the Forsaken still feel the damp, bitter chill that rolls off the northern moors. Eleanor's philosophy on wood gathering is simple: whenever you think you have collected enough firewood, collect five times more. Grateful for the extra timber, she lets the courier keep Bartholomew's basket and shares the warmth of her fire.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who starts the quest Sticks and Bones?",a="Deathguard Bartholomew in Tirisfal Glades gives this task to passing adventurers, asking them to help his associate Eleanor Shackleton.",al={"who gives sticks and bones","sticks and bones quest starter"},gp=true},{q="Where is Eleanor Shackleton camped?",a="Eleanor Shackleton maintains a small camp out in the hills to the west in Tirisfal Glades, surviving the elements away from the settlements.",al={"eleanor shackleton location","where is eleanor","where to turn in sticks and bones"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the Forsaken need firewood if they are undead?",a="While the Forsaken do not freeze to death like the living, they still feel the persistent damp and bitter cold of the Tirisfal wilderness. A campfire provides comfort, keeps their stiff joints warm, and maintains a livable camp in the wilds.",al={"do undead get cold","why firewood for undead","forsaken cold"}},{q="What advice does Eleanor give about gathering wood?",a="According to Deathguard Bartholomew, Eleanor's golden survival rule is: 'When you think you have enough firewood, gather five times more.'",al={"eleanor firewood rule","eleanor advice"}},{q="What item do you use to collect the branches?",a="Deathguard Bartholomew provides a collecting basket to carry the six dry branches found across the glades, which Eleanor lets you keep upon completing the delivery.",al={"basket for dry branch","how to gather branches"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Does this quest lead to further survival training?",a="Eleanor welcomes you to her camp and shares advice on traveling the wilds as an adventurer, providing a friendly contact point in the Tirisfal wilderness.",al={"sticks and bones followup","what does eleanor do next"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Deathguard Bartholomew want firewood brought to Eleanor?",a="Deathguard Bartholomew looks out for his associate Eleanor Shackleton, an experienced survivalist braving the dangerous hills to the west. Remembering her personal motto about always stocking up on ample firewood, he suggests you bring her extra fuel both to assist her vigil and to introduce yourself to a seasoned traveler who can teach you the ways of wilderness survival.",al={"why help eleanor","bartholomew motive","why sticks and bones"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are the Dry Branches for?",a="The dry branches serve as essential fuel for Eleanor Shackleton's campfire out in the wilderness. Despite the walking dead lacking living breath, Eleanor relies on a warm fire to ward off the biting chill of the Tirisfal nights while camped alone amid the hills.",al={"dry branch use","what is dry branch for","firewood for eleanor"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why does Bartholomew ask you instead of gathering the wood himself?",a="As a Deathguard stationed in Tirisfal Glades, Bartholomew's primary duty is maintaining watch and guarding the roads. He cannot abandon his post to forage for fuel, making an independent traveler like you the ideal courier to carry firewood out to Eleanor's wilderness camp.",al={"why me sticks and bones","why does bartholomew need me","why doesn't bartholomew do it"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Even in Lordaeron's blighted woods, Forsaken travelers like Eleanor Shackleton must adapt the old habits of survival to their new unlife.",["faction:Horde"]="Outlying campers like Eleanor offer friendly stopping points for young Horde adventurers striking out across Tirisfal Glades."},kw={"collecting basket","deathguard bartholomew","dry branch","eleanor shackleton","firewood","sticks and bones","survival","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:deathguard-bartholomew","npc:eleanor-shackleton","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Deathguard Bartholomew",["end"]="Eleanor Shackleton",level="7",faction="Horde",id=86784,ri={"Dry Branch"}},src={"Sticks and Bones (Forever)"},fv=true}
E["quest:90902"] = {t="quest",n="Rediscovering the Light",z="tirisfal",s="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this task challenges newly raised Forsaken paladins to embrace the Holy Light once more. Calling upon holy powers inflicts intense agony upon undead flesh, requiring fledgling paladins to channel healing into injured Deathguards despite the burning torment.",h="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this task challenges newly raised Forsaken paladins to embrace...",sec={{t="A Painful Reunion",b="For former champions of the Silver Hand and fallen knights of Lordaeron, awakening in undeath means confronting a horrifying reality: the Holy Light still answers their call, but it burns their withered bodies from within. Aramis Hammerhand instructs newly risen Forsaken paladins that abilities once practiced as second nature now bring excruciating pain. Because holy energy naturally conflicts with the foul magic sustaining undeath, channeling the Light requires raw willpower and agonizing perseverance.",sp=0},{t="Purification Through Suffering",b="Rather than allowing this torment to deter them, Forsaken paladins view the pain as an enduring testament of faith. Deathguard throughout Deathknell suffer grievous wounds combating mindless zombies, and it falls upon these undead knights to restore them. Aramis teaches that pushing through the burning pain is like exercising an atrophied muscle—the agony itself is treated as a badge of honor and living proof that the Light has not forsaken them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does using the Light hurt Forsaken paladins?",a="The Holy Light is fundamentally opposed to the necrotic energies that sustain undeath. While a Forsaken's will and conviction allow them to channel the power of the Light, circulating that sacred energy through dead flesh inflicts excruciating, burning pain.",al={"why does light hurt undead","holy light burning undead","why pain when healing"},it="power"},{q="Who is Aramis Hammerhand?",a="Aramis Hammerhand is an undead paladin trainer stationed in Deathknell. He guides newly risen Forsaken paladins as they reforge their shattered connection to the Holy Light.",al={"who is aramis hammerhand","aramis hammerhand lore","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Deathguards in Deathknell injured?",a="The Deathguard are the soldiers defending Deathknell from mindless undead, feral Scourge remnants, and hostile wildlife that surround the settlement as the newly risen awaken from their graves.",al={"why are deathguards hurt","injured deathguard lore","deathknell defenders"},it="danger"},{q="Can undead naturally wield the Holy Light?",a="Yes, provided they retain the faith, willpower, and memory of their former vows. The Light responds to genuine conviction, though it exacts a brutal physical toll upon dead bodies.",al={"can forsaken be paladins","undead using the light","forsaken paladin lore"},it="power"},{q="What does Aramis mean when he calls pain a badge of honor?",a="Aramis views the burning agony not as a curse, but as undeniable proof that the Light still listens. Forsaken paladins endure the searing heat of their spells to demonstrate their unyielding loyalty and purpose.",al={"pain purifying property","badge of honor meaning","aramis speech"}},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="It takes place entirely within Deathknell, the secluded starter haven in northwestern Tirisfal Glades where newly raised Forsaken first emerge into their undead existence.",al={"where is this quest","quest location","deathknell quest"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Aramis Hammerhand want this done?",a="Aramis aims to train newly risen Forsaken paladins to reconnect with the Holy Light. Because channeling the Light in undeath causes immense physical pain, he knows your abilities have atrophied. The only way to restore your mastery and return to full function is to push through the agony and exercise those spiritual muscles by mending the wounded soldiers defending Deathknell.",al={"why does aramis want this done","aramis hammerhand motive","why heal injured deathguards"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Aramis need you to heal the Deathguards?",a="This task serves as your trial and discipline as a newly awakened paladin. While established healers or Aramis himself could attend to the wounded, only by personally channeling the burning agony of the Light can you rebuild your own endurance and reclaim your former powers in undeath.",al={"why me rediscovering the light","why does aramis need me to heal deathguards","why can't aramis heal them"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For an undead paladin, reconnecting with the Light is a crucible of faith and endurance, proving that holy vows can survive death itself despite the blistering agony it brings.",["race:Undead"]="This rite demonstrates the fierce resilience of the Forsaken, who turn even the searing torment of the Light into a disciplined weapon for their survival."},kw={"aramis hammerhand","deathguard","deathknell","forsaken paladin","holy light","rediscovering the light","tirisfal glades","undead paladin"},rel={"npc:aramis-hammerhand","subzone:deathknell","topic:light","topic:forsaken","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Aramis Hammerhand",level="2",faction="Horde",id=90902,ri={"Injured Deathguard"}},src={"Rediscovering the Light"},fv=true}
E["quest:91208"] = {t="quest",n="Coming to Terms",z="tirisfal",s="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this quest tasks newly risen paladins with reaching out to a fellow knight struggling to accept undeath. Fleeing into the hills in terror, the newly returned warrior faces the agonizing realization of becoming what she once fought against.",h="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this quest tasks newly risen paladins with reaching out to a...",sec={{t="A Bitter Resurrection",b="Awakening from death into the ranks of the Forsaken is jarring for anyone, but for former paladins of the Silver Hand, the shock is visceral. Having dedicated their mortal lives to destroying the undead in service to the Holy Light, these individuals suddenly find their souls tethered back to decomposing flesh. While some adapt under the guidance of Aramis Hammerhand, others are overwhelmed by horror and confusion, fleeing into the Tirisfal wilderness rather than confronting their grim new existence.",sp=0},{t="A Merciful Release",b="Aramis Hammerhand hopes that a fellow knight can reach the panicked paladin who hid in the hills west of the chapel with compassion and understanding. Tragically, the weight of the transformation drives her into complete madness. When approached, she lashes out blindly in fear and fury, leaving no choice but to strike her down. For those Forsaken whose minds shatter upon the grave, a swift end is the only remaining mercy.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is the frightened paladin?",a="She is a recently risen Forsaken who practiced as a paladin in life. Overcome with dread upon waking as an undead creature, she fled Deathknell into the nearby western hills in total panic.",al={"frightened paladin","who is the paladin in the hills","who do i find in coming to terms"},it="target"},{q="Why does the frightened paladin attack?",a="The psychological agony of waking up as the exact monster she spent her life battling shatters her sanity. Consumed by terror and madness, she cannot be reasoned with and violently attacks anyone who tries to reach out.",al={"why does she turn hostile","why attack","can you save the frightened paladin"},sp=1,it="hostile"},{q="Who gives the quest Coming to Terms?",a="Aramis Hammerhand, a paladin mentor situated at the church in Deathknell, offers the task to newly risen knights who need to understand the fragile mental state of their resurrected peers.",al={"quest giver coming to terms","aramis hammerhand quest","where to start coming to terms"},it="identity"},{q="Can Forsaken still wield the Light?",a="Yes, through profound willpower and faith, though channeling holy energy causes intense physical agony to an undead vessel. Mentors like Aramis Hammerhand strive to preserve their spiritual bonds despite the pain of their condition.",al={"undead paladins light","do forsaken paladins use the light","undead use light"},it="power"},{q="Where is the frightened paladin located?",a="She fled directly west from the church in Deathknell, hiding among the wooded hills overlooking the settlement.",al={"frightened paladin location","where is she","where in deathknell"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after completing Coming to Terms?",a="After reporting the tragic outcome to Aramis Hammerhand, he offers a prayer for her departed soul and directs you toward further training to hone your discipline as a Forsaken paladin.",al={"next quest coming to terms","after coming to terms"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Aramis Hammerhand send you on this quest?",a="Aramis Hammerhand understands how harrowing it is for fallen holy warriors to awaken as undead. Paladins spent their mortal lives battling the Scourge, making the reality of their own resurrection nearly impossible to reconcile. Aramis hopes to save a newly risen sister with compassion, extending a sympathetic ear to guide her back to the Light before despair and madness consume her entirely.",al={"aramis hammerhand motive","why help frightened paladin","why does aramis want this done"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="This task strikes at the heart of the paladin's dilemma in undeath: confronting the horror of having become what you were sworn to purge.",["race:Undead"]="The psychological toll of awakening in Tirisfal breaks many minds; dealing with those lost to panic is a tragic reality of early Forsaken life."},kw={"aramis hammerhand","coming to terms","deathknell","frightened paladin","holy light","paladin","tirisfal glades","undead paladin"},rel={"npc:aramis-hammerhand","quest:91209","subzone:deathknell","topic:forsaken","topic:light","zone:tirisfal","quest:6381"},m={start="Aramis Hammerhand",level="4",faction="Horde",id=91208},src={"Coming to Terms (quest)"},fv=true}
E["quest:91209"] = {t="quest",n="Continue Your Training",z="tirisfal",s="This quest directs newly risen Forsaken paladins to leave Deathknell and travel to Brill for advanced instruction. Entrusted by Aramis Hammerhand, the journey introduces the fledgling holy warrior to the wider perils of Tirisfal Glades. It also serves as a stark warning about the deep suspicion that holy practitioners face among the undead.",h="This quest directs newly risen Forsaken paladins to leave Deathknell and travel to Brill for advanced...",sec={{t="Leaving Deathknell",b="Once a newly awakened soul has mastered the rudiments of martial combat and channelled holy power within Deathknell's cloistered graveyard, Aramis Hammerhand sends them east into the broader expanse of Tirisfal Glades. Their destination is Brill, a bustling regional outpost serving as the administrative hub for central Tirisfal. There, Shari Stilwell oversees the advancement of those who dare walk the path of the paladin in undeath.",sp=0},{t="A Precarious Calling",b="Aramis offers parting advice that underlines the fraught reality of being an undead paladin. While Lordaeron still requires defenders against external threats, wielding the Holy Light under Sylvanas Windrunner's banner draws bitter suspicion. Countless Forsaken suffered agony or death at the hands of holy zealots, and the mere presence of the Light breeds distrust among their undead peers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest and where do I find them?",a="The quest is provided by Aramis Hammerhand, the paladin trainer stationed in Deathknell within Tirisfal Glades.",al={"quest giver","where to start","aramis hammerhand location"},gp=true},{q="Where do I turn in Continue Your Training?",a="You turn the quest in to Shari Stilwell, the paladin trainer located in the town of Brill, just east along the main road from Deathknell.",al={"where to turn in","shari stilwell location","turn in spot"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why do the Forsaken distrust paladins?",a="Many Forsaken hold deep-seated grudges against the Holy Light, both because of the agony it causes undead bodies and due to brutal persecution by holy orders like the Scarlet Crusade. Walking the path of a paladin puts one in uncomfortable proximity to forces most undead fear or despise.",al={"why do undead hate paladins","forsaken paladin prejudice","proximity to light"},it="hostile"},{q="Can any Forsaken class take this quest?",a="No. This quest is explicitly the introductory transition quest for Forsaken paladins moving from the starting grounds of Deathknell to the wider world at Brill.",al={"is this quest paladin only","class requirement"},gp=true},{q="What is Brill?",a="Brill is a key town in central Tirisfal Glades serving as the primary hub for Forsaken travelers, guards, and tradesfolk just north of the Undercity ruins.",al={"what is brill","brill town lore"},it="place"},{q="What threats does Lordaeron face according to Aramis?",a="Though shattered, Lordaeron remains under constant threat from rampaging Scourge remnants, hostile wildlife, and vengeful living factions such as the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"threats to lordaeron","why protect lordaeron"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Aramis Hammerhand want me to go to Brill?",a="Aramis recognizes that Deathknell can teach you no more. Though Lordaeron has fallen to ruin, the Forsaken must defend their reclaimed home against countless perils, and that requires fully prepared champions. To temper your holy discipline and survive in a kingdom where your brethren view your wielded Light with deep suspicion, you must continue your instruction under Shari Stilwell in Brill.",al={"why send to brill","aramis hammerhand motive","why continue your training"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For an undead paladin, this transition is a sobering reminder that wielding holy power makes you an outsider even among your fellow Forsaken.",["race:Undead"]="The quest highlights the internal tensions within Forsaken society, where loyalty to Lordaeron's soil clashes with lingering trauma surrounding the Holy Light."},kw={"aramis hammerhand","brill","continue your training","deathknell","forsaken paladin","holy light","shari stilwell","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:aramis-hammerhand","npc:shari-stilwell","subzone:deathknell","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:light","topic:lordaeron"},m={start="Aramis Hammerhand",["end"]="Shari Stilwell",level="4",faction="Horde",id=91209},src={"Continue Your Training"},fv=true}
E["quest:95314"] = {t="quest",n="That Shadowvale Green Elixir",z="tirisfal",s="Carolai Anise in Brill seeks salvage from the abandoned settlement of Shadowvale, tucked deep within the secluded Whispering Forest. Before the kingdom fell, the village was infamous for distilling Whispering Elixir from strange local herbs—a potent draught that remained prized on the black market even after being outlawed in Lordaeron.",h="Carolai Anise in Brill seeks salvage from the abandoned settlement of Shadowvale, tucked deep within the...",sec={{t="A Banned Brew",b="Long before the Third War and the arrival of the Scourge, the reclusive inhabitants of Shadowvale brewed a distinct concoction known as Whispering Elixir. Distilled from rare herbs unique to Tirisfal Glades, the draught gained notoriety across northern Lordaeron. Despite being officially outlawed within the Capital City, its unusual properties made it eagerly sought after by mystics, poisoners, and practicing apothecaries willing to skirt royal decrees.",sp=0},{t="Ruins in the Whispering Forest",b="Shadowvale sits hidden deep within the Whispering Forest of western Tirisfal. The villagers were historically an insular and distrustful people who rarely welcomed outsiders. Following the Scourge invasion, the settlement fell into ruin, but forgotten stores of the aged green elixir remain among the wreckage. Carolai Anise operates out of Brill, taking advantage of persistent underworld demand to pay adventurers willing to brave the forest's unsettling silence.",sp=0},{t="The Whispering Curse",b="While Carolai prefers to dismiss rumors as simple superstition, the Whispering Forest bears a dark reputation. Local lore suggests the very soil and roots of the forest are tainted by an ancient curse, one that leaches directly into the flora harvested by Shadowvale's brewers. Whatever lingering malevolence haunts the abandoned hamlet, it has done little to dampen the appetite of distant buyers seeking its bottled remnants.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Shadowvale?",a="Shadowvale was a secluded village tucked away in the Whispering Forest of western Tirisfal Glades. Its folk were secretive isolationists known primarily for distilling rare concoctions from the local wilderness before the Scourge overran the region.",al={"where is shadowvale","who lived in shadowvale","shadowvale village"},it="place"},{q="What is Whispering Elixir?",a="It is a potent green draught brewed from unique herbs found in Tirisfal. Highly desired by mystics, poisoners, and apothecaries, the beverage was banned in Capital City before Lordaeron's fall.",al={"what does the elixir do","shadowvale green elixir lore","whispering elixir"},it="item"},{q="Who is Carolai Anise?",a="Carolai Anise is a resourceful Forsaken resident residing in Brill. She acts as a broker for valuable and banned curios, securing illicit reagents like Shadowvale's elixir to sell to interested buyers across the realm.",al={"carolai anise","who gives that shadowvale green elixir quest","brill quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Why was the elixir banned in the Capital City?",a="No reason is given. Carolai says only that the elixir was banned in the city, and that apothecaries, mystics, and poisoners sought it even so. Some believe the Whispering Forest is cursed and that the elixir distilled from its roots carries the same curse, but Carolai dismisses this as superstition and never ties it to the ban.",al={"why was whispering elixir illegal","is the elixir poison"},it="history"},{q="Why is the Whispering Forest considered dangerous?",a="The secluded woods west of Deathknell are widely considered eerie and cursed. Those who venture there speak of deep malevolence and strange woodland phenomena, which rumor claims tainted the herbs growing from its soil.",al={"is whispering forest cursed","whispering forest lore"},it="danger"},{q="Who are the buyers wanting this elixir?",a="According to Carolai Anise, a number of buyers are eager to obtain surviving pre-war bottles, though she does not say who they are.",al={"who buys the elixir","carolai anise buyers"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Carolai Anise want the Whispering Elixir collected?",a="Carolai Anise operates as a procurer of rare goods in Brill, and demand for the illicit brew has never truly died out. With numerous eager buyers—including apothecaries, mystics, and poisoners—pressing her for shipments, there is considerable profit to be made from whatever bottles remain intact among the ruins.",al={"why does carolai want the elixir","carolai anise motive","why collect whispering elixir"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, recovering Lordaeron's forgotten distillations serves both profitable commerce and apothecary experimentation in the fledgling Undercity.",["class:Rogue"]="Whispering Elixir was long prized by illicit poisoners and scoundrels operating beneath the notice of Lordaeron's former magistrates.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The elixir derives its potency from singular herbs that thrive only under the eerie canopy of the secluded Whispering Forest."},kw={"apothecary","brill","carolai anise","poison","shadowvale","tirisfal","whispering elixir","whispering forest"},rel={"npc:carolai-anise","subzone:whispering-forest","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={start="Carolai Anise",level="10",faction="Horde",id=95314,ri={"Bottle of Whispering Elixir"}},src={"That Shadowvale Green Elixir"},fv=true}
E["quest:96656"] = {t="quest",n="The Adventurer",z="tirisfal",s="The Adventurer is an introductory task given by Executor Arren to seasoned young Forsaken champions outside Deathknell. Having proven reliable in strikes against the Scarlet Crusade, you are dispatched up the road toward Brill to investigate a stranger lingering near the settlement. Rather than uncovering another zealot spy, the task introduces you to Eleanor Shackleton, an independent traveler eager to teach the practical survival skills of the wild.",h="The Adventurer is an introductory task given by Executor Arren to seasoned young Forsaken champions outside...",sec={{t="A Suspicious Watch",b="Executor Arren maintains a vigilant watch over Deathknell and the surrounding approaches. With Scarlet Crusaders actively spying on the region and infiltrating northern Lordaeron, any newcomer spotted wandering near Brill warrants immediate scrutiny. Having demonstrated your dedication by eliminating crusader threats along the border, Arren turns to you to investigate the unknown figure quietly, determining whether she poses a hidden danger to the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="A Traveler in the Woods",b="Upon tracking down Eleanor Shackleton along the roadside near Brill, her intentions prove far removed from Crusade fanatical plots or Scourge necromancy. An independent traveler pausing before her next journey into the wilds, Eleanor harbors no hostility toward the risen dead. Instead, she recognizes the spark of an adventurer in those sent to check on her, offering to share the practical essentials of surviving open wilderness and making camp.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Executor Arren want Eleanor investigated?",a="Deathknell and Brill are constantly threatened by Scarlet Crusade infiltrators and hostile spies. Anyone unfamiliar lurking along the high road is treated with suspicion until their motives are proven benign.",al={"why investigate eleanor","who sent me to eleanor","arren eleanor task"},it="motive"},{q="Who is Eleanor Shackleton?",a="Eleanor Shackleton is an independent adventurer camping along the road near Brill. She has no loyalty to the Scarlet Crusade and is focused purely on exploring the wilderness and sharing survival know-how with fellow travelers.",al={"who is eleanor","eleanor shackleton lore","who is the adventurer npc"},it="target"},{q="Is Eleanor connected to the Scarlet Crusade?",a="No. Upon arriving at her camp, Eleanor quickly clarifies that she has no affiliation whatsoever with the zealous crusaders threatening Tirisfal Glades.",al={"is eleanor scarlet crusade","eleanor enemy","is eleanor a spy"},it="threat"},{q="Where does this quest lead?",a="Reporting to Eleanor opens the quest 'The Great Outdoors,' where she begins teaching you the basics of wilderness survival and setting up camp.",al={"what comes after the adventurer","next quest after adventurer","eleanor shackleton follow up"},it="next_step"},{q="Where can Eleanor Shackleton be found?",a="She makes her camp just off the main road leading east toward the town of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"eleanor location","where is eleanor shackleton","eleanor camp tirisfal"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why did Executor Arren choose you for this mission?",a="You earned Arren's trust through your successful service rooting out Scarlet Crusade agents and messengers near Deathknell, proving you can handle sensitive reconnaissance.",al={"why pick me","arren trust","why executor arren sends you"},it="why_me"}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For fresh Forsaken venturing beyond Deathknell, meeting neutral travelers like Eleanor is an early lesson that not every living mortal in Lordaeron immediately raises a weapon against the reclaimed dead.",["faction:Horde"]="While Executor Arren guards the fledgling borders of the Forsaken realm, opening peaceful dialogue with wandering survivalists secures valuable practical skills for Horde initiates."},kw={"brill","camping","deathknell","eleanor shackleton","executor arren","scarlet crusade","the adventurer","the great outdoors","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:eleanor-shackleton","npc:executor-arren","quest:96607","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","topic:scarlet-crusade"},m={start="Executor Arren",["end"]="Eleanor Shackleton",level="6",faction="Horde",id=96656},src={"The Adventurer (Tirisfal Glades)"},fv=true}
E["quest:97558"] = {t="quest",n="Hides for the Forsaken",z="tirisfal",s="A grim necessity of undead existence in Tirisfal Glades, where the Forsaken must scavenge animal hides to replace their decaying flesh. Shelene Rhobart tasks adventurers with gathering darkhound hides, duskbat wing membranes, and murloc skin to patch damaged bodies.",h="A grim necessity of undead existence in Tirisfal Glades, where the Forsaken must scavenge animal hides to...",sec={{t="The Price of Undeath",b="Forsaken flesh does not heal naturally. Sustaining physical wounds leaves rotting gaps that cannot regenerate through normal biological means. To maintain their forms and keep their corpses functional, the Forsaken must source organic material from the living world and stitch replacement pieces over their injuries.",sp=0},{t="Specialized Materials",b="Not every hide serves the same anatomical purpose. Thick darkhound hide offers durability for broad, sturdy repairs. Delicate duskbat wing membranes are light and pliable enough to cover faces and fingers without creating stiff, clumsy layers. Meanwhile, the pliable skin of Vile Fin murlocs is gathered to line internal membranes and sensitive areas such as inner cheeks and mouths.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do the Forsaken need animal skins?",a="The skin of an undead does not naturally heal or regenerate when damaged. In order to survive physical injury and halt outward decay, the Forsaken harvest skins and membranes from living creatures to patch and sew over their wounded bodies.",al={"why forsaken need hides","undead healing lore","do undead heal"},it="motive"},{q="Who is Shelene Rhobart?",a="Shelene Rhobart is a Forsaken caretaker in Tirisfal Glades tasked with maintaining medical stocks and body parts. She collects various hides and biological materials to ensure damaged Forsaken have the resources needed to mend their rotting forms.",al={"shelene rhobart","who is shelene"},it="identity"},{q="Why are duskbat wing membranes specifically needed?",a="Duskbat wing membranes are exceptionally thin and supple compared to heavier pelts. This lightness makes them ideal for covering sensitive and intricate parts of the anatomy, such as facial features and fingers, preventing the grafted skin from becoming too thick or rigid.",al={"duskbat wings use","why duskbat membrane"},it="item"},{q="What is murloc skin used for?",a="Vile Fin murloc skin possesses unique moisture and flexibility, making it well-suited for internal membranes or delicate mucosal surfaces like the inside of cheeks and mouths.",al={"why murloc skin","murloc skin undead"},it="item"},{q="Where are these materials found?",a="All three creatures roam the haunted landscapes of Tirisfal Glades. Darkhounds and duskbats stalk the woods and roadsides, while Vile Fin murlocs nest along the northern coast and inland waterways.",al={"where to get hides","tirisfal beasts"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Is using animal hides common for the Forsaken?",a="Yes. Sourcing tissues from animals and scavenged remains is a fundamental part of everyday Forsaken survival. Without access to replacement flesh and hides, unhealed wounds would leave underlying bones, organs, and muscles entirely exposed to the elements.",al={"do all forsaken use hides","forsaken fleshcraft"}},{q="What is the Darkhound Hide used for?",a="Undead flesh does not naturally heal from injury, forcing the Forsaken to patch their bodies with harvested beast hides. While delicate tissues require thin membranes, thicker and hardier pelts like darkhound hide serve as durable grafts for broad, heavy-wear surfaces across the body that require tough, resilient replacement skin.",al={"darkhound hide use","why darkhound hide","darkhound hide forsaken"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="A stark reminder of the practical burdens of the Forsaken condition, where survival requires manual upkeep and scavenged grafts rather than natural healing.",["profession:Tailoring"]="Forsaken tailoring often overlaps with mortuary science, using stitching skills not just for garments, but to bind raw beast membranes to decaying frames."},kw={"darkhound hide","duskbat wing","hides for the forsaken","shelene rhobart","tirisfal glades","undead graft","vile fin murloc"},rel={"npc:shelene-rhobart","zone:tirisfal","item:duskbat-wing","topic:murloc"},m={start="Shelene Rhobart",level="11",faction="Horde",id=97558,ri={"Darkhound Hide","Duskbat Wing Membrane","Vile Fin Murloc Skin"}},src={"Hides for the Forsaken"},fv=true}
E["quest:97957"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Herbalism",z="tirisfal",s="Camping 101: Herbalism directs budding herbalists to local profession trainers across Azeroth, such as Faruza in Brill, to master the craft of Incense Candles. These aromatic creations provide vital restorative aid to spellcasters resting in wilderness camps.",h="Camping 101: Herbalism directs budding herbalists to local profession trainers across Azeroth, such as Faruza...",sec={{t="Flora for the Camp",b="While herbalism is commonly seen as the groundwork for alchemy, practical field survival relies just as heavily on gathered roots and blossoms. Experienced travelers point aspiring foragers toward regional master herbalists to learn how to prepare Incense Candles. In the Tirisfal Glades, Eleanor Shackleton directs promising foragers to visit Faruza in Brill once they demonstrate fundamental skill.",sp=0},{t="Sustenance for the Mind",b="Life on the road takes a heavy mental toll on spellcasters and spiritualists. Incense Candles release restorative fragrances meant to lift spirits and soothe weary minds. By infusing dried plants into candles, gatherers provide crucial aid to the healers and mages whose magical reserves falter during extended travel.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="The quest directs herbalists to reach a basic level of proficiency so they can learn how to craft Incense Candles from an herbalism trainer. These candles are used to sustain and assist spellcasters during wilderness rests.",al={"why do camping herbalism","what does camping 101 give","camping 101 herbalism objective"}},{q="Who offers this quest in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Eleanor Shackleton offers this guidance to herbalists in Tirisfal Glades, pointing them toward Faruza in the nearby settlement of Brill.",al={"who gives camping 101 herbalism tirisfal","eleanor shackleton quest"}},{q="What does an Incense Candle do?",a="Incense Candles provide ambient benefits to a campsite, raising spirits and assisting healers and magic users who need to recover their mental focus while resting in the field.",al={"incense candle effect","what are incense candles for"}},{q="Who teaches the Incense Candle recipe?",a="Local herbalism trainers instruct adventurers once they qualify. In Tirisfal Glades, Faruza in Brill handles training, while other cities and towns rely on trainers like Jandi in Orgrimmar, Reyna Stonebranch in Ironforge, and Malorne Bladeleaf in Dolanaar.",al={"who teaches camping herbalism","where to turn in camping 101 herbalism"}},{q="Can members of other factions and races take this quest?",a="Yes. While Eleanor Shackleton and Faruza cater to travelers in Tirisfal Glades, other questgivers and trainers provide identical instruction to herbalists across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"is camping 101 horde only","alliance camping 101 herbalism"}},{q="What skill requirement is needed to complete the training?",a="Herbalists must show true proficiency in their trade, reaching an herbalism skill of twenty before the trainer will teach the candle recipe.",al={"how much herbalism needed","camping 101 skill level"}}},ang={["profession:Herbalism"]="Rather than simply brewing potions or selling stalks, herbalists can directly support adventuring groups by fashioning fragrant Incense Candles for campfires.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken travelers operating out of Deathknell and Brill learn from Faruza how to make practical field craft out of Tirisfal's withered vegetation.",["class:Mage"]="Spellcasters value herbalists who craft Incense Candles, as their soothing smoke helps restore focus and spirits far from tavern comforts."},kw={"brill","camping","eleanor shackleton","faruza","herbalism","incense candle","tirisfal"},rel={"npc:eleanor-shackleton","subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","zone:orgrimmar","zone:thunderbluff","zone:ironforge","subzone:dolanaar"},m={level="6",id=97957},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Herbalism","Camping 101: Herbalism"},fv=true}
E["quest:97960"] = {t="quest",n="Camping 101: Skinning",z="tirisfal",s="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory wilderness instruction given to novice gatherers in Tirisfal Glades and across Azeroth. It directs skinners who have practiced their trade to regional craftsmen to learn how to fashion a durable camp chair out of harvested hides.",h="Camping 101: Skinning is an introductory wilderness instruction given to novice gatherers in Tirisfal Glades...",sec={{t="Comfort in the Wilds",b="Survival in the wilds demands more than a sharp blade; it requires knowing how to husband your strength between trials. Adventurers like Eleanor Shackleton emphasize that harvesting beast pelts provides more than raw trade goods. By developing basic field proficiency, skinners can fashion essential camp comforts like chairs out of treated skins, allowing weary wanderers to rest and recover properly before their next perilous encounter.",sp=0},{t="Mastering the Craft",b="In Tirisfal Glades, young Forsaken skinners who reach initial competency are pointed toward Rand Rhobart, who plies his trade along the road linking Brill to the Capital ruins of the Undercity. Rhobart shares practical techniques for turning rough leather into functional campsite seating, an art mirrored by skinning masters throughout the lands of both the Horde and the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="The quest directs novice skinners to seek out a skilled trade mentor who can teach them how to assemble a field camp chair using harvested animal pelts.",al={"what does camping 101 skinning do","why do camping 101 skinning","camping 101 reward"}},{q="Who offers this training in Tirisfal Glades?",a="In Tirisfal Glades, the task is handed out by Eleanor Shackleton to guide new skinners toward Rand Rhobart along the highway between Brill and the Undercity.",al={"who gives quest tirisfal","eleanor shackleton quest"}},{q="What skill level is required to complete this task?",a="You must raise your skinning skill to at least 20 before the trainer will deem you experienced enough to learn how to craft field campsite equipment.",al={"what skinning level do i need","camping 101 skinning requirements","skinning skill required"}},{q="Where can Rand Rhobart be found?",a="Rand Rhobart is stationed along the main road running south from Brill toward the entrance of the Undercity in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"rand rhobart location","where is rand rhobart","rand rhobart tirisfal"}},{q="Can members of other factions learn this technique?",a="Yes. While Forsaken learn from Rand Rhobart, equivalent instructors train skinners in Goldshire, Ironforge, Dolanaar, Orgrimmar, Bloodhoof Village, and Shen'dar Village.",al={"is this horde only","can alliance do camping 101 skinning","alliance skinning camp chair"}},{q="Why is crafting a camp chair useful?",a="A camp chair provides a comfortable place to sit and recover stamina in the wilderness, helping keep an adventurer rested and alert for upcoming battles.",al={"what does a camp chair do","why make camp chair"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Even the Forsaken, freed from mortal exhaustion, find practical utility in maintaining functional field encampments while hunting the plagued beasts of Tirisfal.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde hunters and gatherers utilize every scrap of a slain beast, ensuring nothing goes to waste on the unforgiving road."},kw={"brill","camp chair","camping","eleanor shackleton","rand rhobart","skinning","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"npc:eleanor-shackleton","subzone:brill","zone:undercity","zone:tirisfal","subzone:goldshire","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:orgrimmar","zone:ironforge"},m={level="6",id=97960},src={"In-game quest text: Camping 101: Skinning","Camping 101: Skinning"},fv=true}
E["quest:98389"] = {t="quest",n="A Light in the Darkness",z="tirisfal",s="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this quest tasks Forsaken adventurers with rescuing newly raised undead trapped in Night's Web Hollow. It highlights the delicate relationship between undead wielders of the Holy Light and a populace that deeply distrusts them. By aiding their fallen kin, these holy practitioners hope to prove their loyalty to Sylvanas and the Forsaken cause.",h="Offered by Aramis Hammerhand in Deathknell, this quest tasks Forsaken adventurers with rescuing newly raised...",sec={{t="A Precarious Faith",b="The Holy Light is notoriously agonizing and hazardous to the undead, yet some Forsaken retain their connection to the sacred magic they wielded in life. In Deathknell, figures like Aramis Hammerhand strive to demonstrate that their faith has not turned them against their people. To combat the suspicion that holy practitioners will turn on other undead as monstrosities, they actively use their abilities and compassion to safeguard their kin.",sp=0},{t="Night's Web Hollow",b="Northwest of Deathknell lies Night's Web Hollow, a dank cavern crawling with vicious spiders. Freshly raised undead, still disoriented by their awakening and lacking the wits to defend themselves, frequently stumble into the hollow and end up cocooned in sticky webbing. Rescuing these unfortunate Forsaken serves a dual purpose: saving new recruits for the Dark Lady and proving to leaders like Executor Arren that practitioners of the Light are devoted servants of the Forsaken.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the quest A Light in the Darkness?",a="The quest is given by Aramis Hammerhand, a holy practitioner stationed in Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"quest giver","who starts a light in the darkness","aramis hammerhand quest"},gp=true},{q="Where is Night's Web Hollow located?",a="Night's Web Hollow is located northwest of Deathknell within the hills of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"nights web hollow location","where are the webbed victims","where is the cave"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the Forsaken distrust wielders of the Light?",a="The Holy Light is deeply searing and dangerous to undead flesh. Many Forsaken fear that anyone wielding holy powers might view them as abominations to be eradicated rather than brethren to be embraced.",al={"why fear the light","why do forsaken hate paladins","forsaken holy fear"},it="conflict"},{q="Who are the victims trapped in the webs?",a="The victims inside the cocoons are newly awakened Forsaken adventurers who blundered into the spider caves before finding their bearings.",al={"who is webbed","what is inside the webs","trapped victims"},it="target"},{q="Who is Executor Arren, and why does Aramis want to impress him?",a="Executor Arren oversees military and civic order in Deathknell. Gaining his trust is essential for holy wielders who wish to integrate smoothly into the Forsaken forces.",al={"who is executor arren","why arren matters"}},{q="Can the undead really wield the Holy Light?",a="Yes, through incredible willpower and devotion, some undead retain their bond with the Light, even though channeling it causes immense discomfort and their own society views them with suspicion.",al={"can forsaken use light","undead light lore"},it="power"},{q="Why does Aramis Hammerhand want the webbed victims freed?",a="Aramis wants to prove to his fellow Forsaken that undead who wield the Holy Light are not a threat to their own kind. Many in Deathknell, including Executor Arren, fear or suspect that Light-wielders will turn their powers upon other undead as monstrosities. By rescuing freshly raised Forsaken from the spiders of Night's Web Hollow, Aramis hopes to demonstrate that their power exists to protect the Forsaken and serve the Dark Lady's vision.",al={"aramis hammerhand motive","why free the webbed victims","why does aramis want this done"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This task demonstrates the complex internal struggles of the Forsaken, showing how individuals can overcome prejudice among their own freshly risen people.",["class:Priest"]="For Forsaken who channel holy energy, this mission illustrates the delicate tightrope walked between lingering mortal faith and the harsh reality of undeath."},kw={"aramis hammerhand","deathknell","executor arren","forsaken","holy light","night's web hollow","tirisfal glades","webbed victims"},rel={"npc:aramis-hammerhand","subzone:deathknell","npc:executor-arren","topic:light","topic:forsaken","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Aramis Hammerhand",level="4",faction="Horde",id=98389,ri={"Webbed Forsaken"}},src={"A Light in the Darkness (Forever)"},fv=true}
E["quest:98601"] = {t="quest",n="A Difficult Path",z="tirisfal",s="A Difficult Path marks the formal beginning of a Forsaken paladin's training in Deathknell. After dealing with the mindless undead rising from the crypts, newly awakened champions of the Light are directed to Aramis Hammerhand within the village church to confront the agonizing reality of wielding holy energy in undeath.",h="A Difficult Path marks the formal beginning of a Forsaken paladin's training in Deathknell.",sec={{t="A Reeking Scroll",b="When a freshly awakened Forsaken paladin finishes culling the feral undead outside the crypt, Shadow Priest Sarvis hands over an unexpected delivery. The Consecrated Scroll radiates holy energy so intensely that the shadow priest visibly recoils, bluntly asking the bearer to step aside. To a practitioner of shadow and decay, the very aura of the Holy Light feels like an open flame.",sp=0},{t="The Crucible of Holy Fire",b="Inside the desecrated church of Deathknell awaits Aramis Hammerhand, the Forsaken paladin trainer. Where other races wield the Light naturally and joyfully, undead who call upon it endure searing, visceral agony. Aramis makes no attempt to soften the blow: channeling holy power through a rotting vessel is sheer torment, but he demands his disciples embrace the pain rather than flinch away, using it as a crucible to temper their newfound resolve.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Shadow Priest Sarvis dislike the scroll?",a="Shadow Priest Sarvis is devoted to shadow magic and the preservation of his fellow undead. The Consecrated Scroll radiates pure holy energy, which is inherently hostile to undead flesh and deeply unsettling to practitioners of the Shadow.",al={"sarvis holy scroll","why is sarvis uneasy","shadow priest reacts to scroll"}},{q="Can Forsaken truly wield the Holy Light without dying?",a="Forsaken can wield the Light through sheer willpower and faith, but doing so causes them intense internal pain and rekindles deadened nerve endings, making them feel their own decay. It demands immense mental endurance to channel.",al={"forsaken paladins lore","undead using the light","does the light hurt forsaken"}},{q="Who is Aramis Hammerhand?",a="Aramis Hammerhand is the paladin trainer stationed in the Deathknell chapel. He guides newly risen Forsaken paladins, teaching them to withstand the agony of wielding the Light in undeath and forge it into an effective weapon.",al={"who is aramis hammerhand","deathknell paladin trainer","aramis role"},it="identity"},{q="Where does this quest take place?",a="The quest begins with Shadow Priest Sarvis in the central clearing of Deathknell and concludes just steps away inside the village's ruined chapel, where Aramis Hammerhand resides.",al={"where is a difficult path","quest location","where is deathknell church"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Do I have to read the Consecrated Scroll to complete the quest?",a="No, reading the scroll is not strictly required to complete the objective; speaking directly to Aramis Hammerhand inside the church will advance your training.",al={"read consecrated scroll","is scroll reading required","skip reading scroll"},gp=true},{q="Why is the path called 'difficult'?",a="The name reflects the brutal path of the Forsaken paladin. While other warriors of the Light draw comfort and solace from their faith, an undead paladin must withstand searing holy fire coursing through dead flesh every time they cast a blessing or strike.",al={"why difficult path","meaning of quest name"}},{q="Why does Shadow Priest Sarvis want this done?",a="Sarvis merely wants the Holy Light out of his sight. The consecrated scroll arrived for you while you were culling the mindless Scourge, and its radiant magic naturally offends his shadow-touched senses. Rather than lingering around its presence or dealing with a budding paladin, he directs you to report to your own trainer, Aramis Hammerhand, so you can begin learning how to hone your painful discipline.",al={"why does sarvis send me to aramis","sarvis motive difficult path","why give me the scroll sarvis"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="This quest represents the foundational trial of the Forsaken paladin, framing holy power not as an easy blessing, but as an agonizing crucible of discipline and endurance.",["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, calling upon the Light proves that devotion and discipline can survive physical death, even if the magic actively burns the vessel channeling it."},kw={"a difficult path","aramis hammerhand","consecrated scroll","deathknell","forsaken paladin","holy light","shadow priest sarvis","tirisfal glades"},rel={"npc:aramis-hammerhand","npc:shadow-priest-sarvis","subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:light","topic:scourge"},m={start="Shadow Priest Sarvis",["end"]="Aramis Hammerhand",level="1",faction="Horde",id=98601},src={"A Difficult Path (Forever)"},fv=true}
E["quest:99134"] = {t="quest",n="Discipline",z="tirisfal",s="Discipline is a task issued by Executor Zygand in Tirisfal Glades. Unimpressed by the competence of the local Deathguard garrison, Zygand commissions outside adventurers to administer physical punishment to slackening undead sentries.",h="Discipline is a task issued by Executor Zygand in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Enforcing Order in Undeath",b="The Deathguard form the military spine of the Forsaken across Lordaeron, but freedom from the Lich King's iron will brings its own complications: apathy, fatigue, and negligence. Taskmasters like Executor Zygand have little patience for slackening discipline, especially with threats from the Scarlet Crusade and Scourge prowling Tirisfal's borders. To keep the troops sharp, officers rely on blunt instruments and aggressive oversight to maintain military readiness.",sp=0},{t="The Executor's Ledger",b="Executor Zygand deliberately employs independent operators rather than ranks from within the garrison to administer his punishments. Using the Executor's Motivator, outsiders remind negligent Deathguards that survival in service to the Dark Lady requires absolute vigilance. Those who fail in their duties quickly learn that unlife offers no shelter from consequence.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives the Discipline quest?",a="Discipline is given by Executor Zygand, an exacting Forsaken officer stationed in Tirisfal Glades who oversees regional defenses and demands strict obedience from his troops.",al={"discipline quest giver","who starts discipline","executor zygand quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want the Deathguard punished?",a="Zygand views the Deathguards under his command as incompetent and lacking proper drive. Because complacency threatens Forsaken survival in Tirisfal, he enforces harsh discipline to shock the sentries into alertness.",al={"why hit deathguards","why motivate deathguard","zygand deathguard reason"},it="motive"},{q="Why does Zygand use an adventurer instead of another soldier?",a="Zygand notes that as an outsider who is not yet part of the Deathguard, you have not had the opportunity to disappoint him. Employing a fresh hand also prevents internal garrison squabbles or soldiers showing mercy to their comrades.",al={"why me discipline","why use outsiders zygand"},it="why_me"},{q="What is the Executor's Motivator?",a="The Executor's Motivator is a punitive disciplinary tool provided by Executor Zygand. It is used to deliver a painful reprimand to idling Deathguards stationed across Tirisfal Glades.",al={"executors motivator","what is the motivator zygand"},it="item"},{q="What happens if you refuse Zygand's order?",a="Zygand warns that hesitation or refusal will result in your own name being inscribed in his ledger, marking you as the next target for someone else to discipline.",al={"zygand threats","ledger threat zygand"},gp=true},{q="How do the Forsaken view pain?",a="Though undead bodies are dulled to many sensations, sharp physical pain remains one of the few visceral feelings they retain. For officers like Zygand, inflicting and experiencing it serves as a morbid satisfaction and an effective motivator.",al={"do undead feel pain","forsaken pain lore"}},{q="Who are the Deathguards you are punishing?",a="The Deathguard are the standing military and sentinels of the Forsaken, tasked with defending Tirisfal Glades and enforcing the Dark Lady's rule. Executor Zygand views those stationed around Brill and the outlying posts as lazy and incompetent, demanding that they be reminded through physical chastisement of their sworn duty to protect Forsaken lands.",al={"who are the deathguards","deathguard targets discipline","who do i discipline"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, military discipline is a matter of species survival. Sentries who succumb to apathy endanger the entire territory of Tirisfal Glades.",["faction:Horde"]="The grim methods of Forsaken commanders illustrate the stark, unforgiving realities of life and governance within the northern reaches of the Horde."},kw={"dark lady","deathguard","discipline","executor zygand","forsaken","motivator","tirisfal glades","undead"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","zone:tirisfal"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="6",faction="Horde",id=99134,ri={"Deathguards"}},src={"Discipline (Forever)"},fv=true}
E["quest:99141"] = {t="quest",n="Patience",z="tirisfal",s="Patience is a task assigned by Executor Zygand in Tirisfal Glades, demanding strict military accountability for the Dark Lady. With routine field reports overdue, Zygand dispatches an adventurer to collect updates from nearby Deathguards and an unusual sentry. The assignment highlights the rigorous, uncompromising discipline enforced across Forsaken holdings.",h="Patience is a task assigned by Executor Zygand in Tirisfal Glades, demanding strict military accountability...",sec={{t="Enforcing the Dark Lady's Will",b="Executor Zygand holds command over local security in Tirisfal Glades and tolerates no lapses in vigilance. To Zygand, serving Lady Sylvanas requires nothing less than absolute efficiency. When regular field dispatches fail to arrive at his post on schedule, he sees it as an unacceptable breach of duty rather than a minor delay. Rather than abandon his post to investigate, he sends incoming Forsaken agents to rein in delinquent subordinates and ensure that the borders remain secure.",sp=0},{t="Sentinels of the Glades",b="The dispatches involve sentries stationed at critical vantage points surrounding Brill. Deathguard Dillinger keeps watch over the graveyard to the west, where rogue undead and Scourge remnants often stir. To the east, Deathguard Kristof oversees a waystation guarding the approaches to the Capital City and the eastern roads. Most unusual among them is Gordo, a hulking abomination wandering near Stillwater Pond whose simple mind makes military record-keeping a deeply dubious expectation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Executor Zygand?",a="Executor Zygand is a high-ranking Forsaken officer tasked with maintaining order and defense around Brill in Tirisfal Glades. He answers directly to the command hierarchy serving Lady Sylvanas and demands flawless execution from all troops under his oversight.",al={"zygand","who is zygand","executor zygand role"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Zygand send an adventurer to collect reports?",a="Several field sentries have failed to submit their scheduled reports on time. Lacking patience for delays and unwilling to abandon his central post, Zygand employs newly proven Forsaken to run down the overdue dispatches across the countryside.",al={"why do patience","why fetch reports"},it="why_me"},{q="Who is Gordo and why does he have a report?",a="Gordo is an abomination who wanders west near Stillwater Pond. Zygand expected a status report from him just like any other field sentry, though upon receiving it, the Executor quickly realizes that expecting written intelligence from an abomination was an embarrassing lapse in judgment.",al={"gordo","why does an abomination have a report","who is gordo"},sp=1,it="identity"},{q="Where is Deathguard Dillinger located?",a="Deathguard Dillinger is posted just west of Brill, standing watch near the local graveyard to monitor lingering Scourge activity and newly risen corpses.",al={"dillinger location","find dillinger","where is dillinger"},it="target",gp=true},{q="Where is Deathguard Kristof stationed?",a="Deathguard Kristof is stationed at the waystation along the road to the east of Brill, guarding the approaches against intruders and monitoring road traffic.",al={"kristof location","find kristof","where is kristof"},it="target",gp=true},{q="What happens when Gordo's report is turned in?",a="Executor Zygand concedes that expecting an abomination to produce a coherent written report was a foolish mistake. He quietly accepts the outcome and asks that the embarrassing oversight be kept strictly between you and him.",al={"gordo report outcome","gordo writing"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does Executor Zygand want these reports collected?",a="Executor Zygand is tasked with upholding the Dark Lady's will and maintaining strict oversight of Brill's defenses. When sentinels and scouts fail to submit their status dispatches on schedule, it compromises the Forsaken's readiness and risks incurring Sylvanas's displeasure. Zygand demands swift compliance to maintain ironclad discipline.",al={"zygand motive","why does zygand want reports","why patience quest"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is Deathguard Dillinger's Report for?",a="Dillinger's Report conveys the current security situation around the Brill cemetery just west of town. Stationed near the graveyard to watch for threats and disturbances, Deathguard Dillinger is expected to keep Executor Zygand informed about local unrest and perimeter stability.",al={"dillinger's report","what is dillingers report","dillinger report purpose"},it="item",add=true},{q="What is Deathguard Kristof's Report for?",a="Kristof's Report details patrol activity and roadway conditions around the eastern waystation. Deathguard Kristof watches the passage leading east through Tirisfal Glades, and his dispatch provides Executor Zygand with critical intelligence on traffic and approaching dangers.",al={"kristof's report","what is kristofs report","kristof report purpose"},it="item",add=true},{q="What is Gordo's Report for?",a="Gordo's Report is supposed to offer an accounting of the area around Stillwater Pond to the west, where the abomination lumbers on patrol. Though Executor Zygand expects written reconnaissance, the simple-minded brute struggles with the concept of military dispatches.",al={"gordo's report","what is gordos report","gordo report purpose"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For newly liberated Forsaken, carrying out Zygand's orders reinforces the harsh realities of survival in Lordaeron: complete devotion to the Dark Lady and zero tolerance for failure.",["faction:Horde"]="The quest offers visiting Horde allies a glimpse into the grim, highly disciplined command structure that Sylvanas Windrunner's officers enforce throughout Tirisfal."},kw={"brill","dark lady","deathguard","dillinger","gordo","kristof","patience","stillwater pond","tirisfal glades","zygand"},rel={"npc:executor-zygand","npc:deathguard-dillinger","subzone:stillwater-pond","zone:tirisfal","quest:938"},m={start="Executor Zygand",level="6",faction="Horde",id=99141,ri={"Dillinger's Report","Gordo's Report","Kristof's Report"}},src={"Patience (quest)"},fv=true}
E["quest:99142"] = {t="quest",n="Tomb Weed",z="tirisfal",s="Tomb Weed is an alchemical quest assigned by Junior Apothecary Holland in Tirisfal Glades. Holland has been modifying wild doom weed and replanting it at the haunted Balnir Farmstead, cultivating a potent new specimen that unexpectedly attracts the local Scourge.",h="Tomb Weed is an alchemical quest assigned by Junior Apothecary Holland in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Holland's Cultivation",b="Junior Apothecary Holland is not content simply harvesting native herbs for the Royal Apothecary Society; he harbors grand ambitions of his own. By gathering doom weed and subjecting it to minor alchemical alterations, he replanted the strains across the soil of the Balnir Farmstead to cultivate an evolved specimen he christened tomb weed. Holland views this hybrid flora as the centerpiece of his work and a herald of his personal destiny.",sp=0},{t="Unintended Attraction",b="While Holland anticipated alchemical potency, the transplanting of tomb weed into the Balnir Farmstead yielded an unforeseen side effect. The wandering Scourge remnants infesting the blighted fields have shown an uncanny, pervasive attraction to the burgeoning flora. Holland relies on passing travelers to push past these encroaching undead and harvest the matured crop so he can continue his experiments.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is tomb weed?",a="Tomb weed is an alchemically modified strain of doom weed cultivated by Junior Apothecary Holland. He takes native doom weed, alters it with chemical reagents, and plants it within the blighted soil of the Balnir Farmstead.",al={"what is tomb weed","tomb weed lore","tomb weed plant"}},{q="Who is Junior Apothecary Holland?",a="Holland is a member of the Royal Apothecary Society operating in Tirisfal Glades. Ambitious and often exasperated by assistants like the abomination Gordo, Holland conducts private horticultural and alchemical experiments aimed at advancing his own stature.",al={"who is holland","junior apothecary holland","apothecary holland"}},{q="Where is tomb weed grown?",a="Holland plants his modified crops at the Balnir Farmstead, an abandoned estate situated east in Tirisfal Glades that has fallen into blight and ruin.",al={"where to find tomb weed","tomb weed location","balnir farmstead weed"}},{q="Why are the Scourge attracted to tomb weed?",a="Holland has observed that the mindless Scourge haunting the Balnir Farmstead seem instinctively drawn to the modified plant, though he doesn't know why; he simply finds it curious.",al={"why scourge want tomb weed","scourge drawn to tomb weed","scourge reaction"}},{q="How does tomb weed differ from normal doom weed?",a="Doom weed is a naturally occurring plant found near the mass gravesite north of the Brill graveyard. Tomb weed is created when doom weed undergoes artificial alchemical alterations and selective replanting in farmstead soil under Holland's direction.",al={"doom weed vs tomb weed","difference doom weed tomb weed"}},{q="Who is Gordo mentioned by Holland?",a="Gordo is a wandering abomination created by the Forsaken who roams the roads of Tirisfal Glades, often assigned simple tasks by Holland that prove difficult for his sluggish mind.",al={"who is gordo","gordo run in","gordo tirisfal"}}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Junior Apothecary Holland's research demonstrates the persistent drive among Forsaken apothecaries to reshape Tirisfal's blighted flora into bespoke alchemical tools.",["profession:Herbalism"]="Tomb weed illustrates how artificial alchemical manipulation can fundamentally change the properties and behaviors of blighted herbs like doom weed."},kw={"balnir farmstead","doom weed","gordo","junior apothecary holland","royal apothecary society","tirisfal glades","tomb weed"},rel={"quest:5482","subzone:balnir-farmstead","quest:1882","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},m={level="11",id=99142},src={"In-game quest text: Tomb Weed"},fv=true}
E["quest:99156"] = {t="quest",n="Rear Guard Patrol",z="tirisfal",s="Rear Guard Patrol is a critical defense assignment given by Deathguard Linnea in Tirisfal Glades. Rather than fighting off mere wandering ghouls, patrollers are tasked with hunting down Riptear, an abomination roaming near Venomweb Vale that should never have made it past the Bulwark.",h="Rear Guard Patrol is a critical defense assignment given by Deathguard Linnea in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="The Breach at the Border",b="The Bulwark stands as the sole fortified barrier separating Tirisfal Glades from the horrors of the Western Plaguelands. The strategy of the Forsaken military hinges upon a strictly one-front war. If mindless undead slip behind the lines, the supply routes feeding the front can be strangled in an instant. While stray spirits or ghouls occasionally bypass the barricades, reports of an abomination roaming the glades signal a severe failure of containment.",sp=0},{t="The Threat of Riptear",b="The rogue monstrosity known as Riptear was sighted lumbering close to Venomweb Vale, behind the Bulwark's forward redoubts. Abominations are massive engines of slaughter, stitched together from corpses and dark necromancy. They do not wander through fortified chokepoints unseen, leaving Deathguard Linnea deeply unsettled by how the creature crossed the perimeter. Linnea demands Riptear's heart as absolute confirmation of its destruction.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who offers the Rear Guard Patrol quest?",a="The patrol order is issued by Deathguard Linnea, a Forsaken Deathguard in Tirisfal Glades who reports to Executor Zygand.",al={"who gives rear guard patrol","rear guard patrol questgiver","who gives this quest"}},{q="What is Riptear?",a="Riptear is a rogue abomination discovered roaming the eastern glades near Venomweb Vale, behind the forward defensive lines.",al={"who is riptear","what is riptear","riptear lore"}},{q="Why is an abomination near Venomweb Vale so alarming?",a="Abominations are massive constructs that require dark necromancy and heavy labor to manufacture. Slipping unnoticed through the heavily fortified Bulwark should be impossible, implying an alarming breach or an undetected path behind Forsaken lines.",al={"why is riptear a big deal","why is an abomination dangerous here","bulwark breach"}},{q="Where is the beast usually found?",a="Sightings place Riptear roaming the woods and clearings of eastern Tirisfal Glades, in the vicinity of Venomweb Vale.",al={"where is riptear","riptear location","where to find the abomination"}},{q="What proof is required to finish the patrol?",a="Deathguard Linnea requires Riptear's Heart to verify that the massive monstrosity has been permanently put down.",al={"what do i bring linnea","quest turn in item","proof of riptear death"}},{q="Who oversees the defense of the Bulwark?",a="The quest doesn't name the Bulwark's commander. Deathguard Linnea answers to her superior, Executor Zygand in Brill, who receives her commendations for patrols that keep the Scourge at bay.",al={"who is in charge of bulwark","linneas superior","executor zygand"}}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Securing Tirisfal's eastern border guarantees that the supply lines feeding the Bulwark and the Undercity remain uncompromised by rogue horrors.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken cannot afford to wage war on two fronts; any Scourge construct wandering behind their defensive barricades is an existential danger to their kingdom."},kw={"abomination","bulwark","deathguard linnea","rear guard patrol","riptear","tirisfal glades","venomweb vale"},rel={"npc:deathguard-linnea","subzone:bulwark","subzone:venomweb-vale","npc:executor-zygand","topic:scourge","zone:tirisfal","subzone:balnir-farmstead","quest:1882"},m={level="13",id=99156},src={"In-game quest text: Rear Guard Patrol","Rear Guard Patrol"},fv=true}
E["subzone:agamand-family-crypt"] = {t="subzone",n="Agamand Family Crypt",z="tirisfal",s="Tucked into the hills just north of Agamand Mills, the Agamand Family Crypt is the ancient resting ground of one of Tirisfal's wealthiest farming dynasties. Now claimed by the Scourge under Captain Dargol, its tombs have been ripped open, and the slumbering ancestors raised as mindless terrors.",h="Tucked into the hills just north of Agamand Mills, the Agamand Family Crypt is the ancient resting ground of...",sec={{t="Restless Ancestors",b="Before the Third War, the Agamand family held expansive estates across northern Tirisfal Glades, entombing their forebears within this hillside mausoleum. When the Plague brought ruin to the kingdom, the crypt fell into the clutches of Captain Dargol, an agent of the Scourge. Rather than letting the wealthy lineage rest in peace, Dargol employed dark necromancy to awaken the entombed skeletons and vengeful apparitions, transforming a sacred burial ground into an aggressive Scourge foothold right on the edge of Forsaken territory.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Agamand Family Crypt located?",a="The crypt is carved into the hillside just north of Agamand Mills in the northern reaches of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"agamand crypt location","where is the crypt","how to find agamand crypt"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who controls the crypt right now?",a="The tomb is held by Captain Dargol, a servant of the Scourge who orchestrates the dark magic within and commands the raised dead.",al={"who is in agamand crypt","crypt boss","captain dargol"},it="in_charge"},{q="What will I encounter inside the crypt?",a="The crypt is filled with awakened skeletons and restless spirits of the Agamand ancestors. Driven by dark magic, these vengeful undead assault anyone who steps into the tombs.",al={"crypt enemies","what is inside agamand crypt","agamand crypt mobs"},it="threat"},{q="Are the undead inside the crypt allied with the Forsaken?",a="No. The creatures inside are thralls of the Scourge answering to Captain Dargol, making them hostile to the Forsaken and living travelers alike.",al={"are they forsaken","agamand crypt friendly","why attack undead"},it="side"},{q="What is the Agamand Family Crypt?",a="The Agamand Family Crypt is the ancestral burial vault of the Agamand lineage, situated just north of their expansive mills in Tirisfal Glades. Once a quiet sanctuary meant to grant eternal rest to the noble family, it has fallen into darkness following the Scourge's conquest of Lordaeron.",al={"what is agamand family crypt","agamand crypt info","what is this crypt"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Agamand Family Crypt?",a="When the Scourge swept across northern Lordaeron, the Agamand estate was shattered and its burial grounds defiled. A servant of the Lich King named Captain Dargol claimed the crypt, using necromancy to awaken the corpses and ancestral spirits of the Agamand line into mindless, vengeful servitude.",al={"agamand crypt history","what happened to agamand crypt","history of agamand crypt"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Agamand Family Crypt dangerous?",a="Yes, it is exceptionally perilous. The dusty halls crawl with raised undead and vengeful specters twisted by necromantic magic. Controlled by Captain Dargol, these spirits and walking corpses violently assault anyone foolish enough to breach the tomb unbidden.",al={"is agamand crypt dangerous","agamand crypt danger","how dangerous is agamand crypt"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Agamand Family Crypt?",a="It bears the name of the Agamands, a prominent human family of Lordaeron who owned and operated the nearby Agamand Mills. The vault was constructed to house the remains of multiple generations of their ancestors before the fall of the kingdom.",al={"why called agamand crypt","agamand family crypt name origin","who are the agamands"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, Captain Dargol's occupation of the crypt is an intolerable Scourge presence in Tirisfal, mocking the dead and threatening the Undercity's northern frontier."},kw={"agamand family crypt","agamand mills","captain dargol","crypt","mausoleum","scourge","skeletons","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:agamand-mills","zone:tirisfal","topic:scourge"},src={"Agamand Family Crypt"}}
E["subzone:agamand-mills"] = {t="subzone",n="Agamand Mills",z="tirisfal",s="Agamand Mills is a sprawling farm and windmill complex nestled in the northern heights of Tirisfal Glades. Once the proud estate of the wealthy Agamand dynasty, it has fallen into ruin and stands as the largest Scourge stronghold in the region.",h="Agamand Mills is a sprawling farm and windmill complex nestled in the northern heights of Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A House Divided",b="Centuries ago, wealthy noble immigrants from Strom established the Agamand estate, building a vast milling network across northern Tirisfal. When the Third War broke out and the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, the patriarch Gregor Agamand refused to flee, fortifying the compound and convincing his retainers and laborers to make their stand. Their provisions and stone walls might have held, but their youngest son, Devlin Agamand, betrayed his blood. Devlin slaughtered the estate watchmen and let the walking dead into the sleeping quarters. Crippled from within, the remaining defenders were wiped out, and the family joined the mindless ranks of the undead.",sp=0},{t="A Bastion of Rot",b="Today, the Agamand Mills are the most entrenched bastion of the Scourge in Tirisfal Glades, posing an immediate threat to the Forsaken realm. The rotting windmills now churn above infested fields of wights, zombies, and skeletal horrors. Beneath the estate, Captain Dargol commands the garrison from the deep Agamand Family Crypt, practicing necromancy to reanimate generations of Agamand ancestors. From Brill, Magistrate Sevren and the Deathguards wage an active campaign to clear the mills and purge the Scourge presence before it can march upon the Undercity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is Agamand Mills overrun with Scourge?",a="When the Scourge ravaged Lordaeron, the Agamand family fortified their estate and attempted to hold out against the undead horde. However, the youngest son, Devlin Agamand, betrayed his family and opened the gates to the Scourge, leading to the slaughter and reanimation of everyone within.",al={"what happened to agamand mills","why are there undead at the mills","agamand mills lore"},it="ruin"},{q="Who controls the mills right now?",a="The estate serves as a regional forward outpost for the Scourge, led by Captain Dargol. Dargol operates deep within the fortified Agamand Family Crypt, where he uses dark magic to raise ancestral corpses into new soldiers.",al={"who leads agamand mills","scourge leader tirisfal","who is in charge at the mills"},it="in_charge"},{q="Why do the Forsaken care about this place?",a="The mills represent the largest concentration of mindless Scourge forces remaining in Tirisfal Glades. If left unchecked, Captain Dargol's force threatens Brill and could choke off roads leading south to the Undercity.",al={"why brill attacks agamand","forsaken interest in agamand mills"},it="conflict"},{q="Who was the Agamand family?",a="They were an ancient, wealthy human noble house descended from Stromgarde immigrants who purchased rich farmlands in Tirisfal over a millennium ago. Today, its reanimated scions—including Gregor, Nissa, Thurman, and Devlin—wander the grounds as mindless thralls.",al={"who are the agamands","agamand lineage","gregor agamand"},it="people"},{q="What is Agamand Mills?",a="Agamand Mills is a sprawling network of windmills and farmlands situated in the northern highlands of Tirisfal Glades. Before the plague swept through Lordaeron, it was a prosperous agricultural estate owned by the wealthy Agamand noble family. Today, it has fallen into decay and serves as the largest Scourge stronghold remaining in the region.",al={"agamand mills lore","what was agamand mills","what is this place"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Agamand Mills?",a="When the Scourge swept into Tirisfal, Gregor Agamand chose to fortify the mills and convince his family and workers to make a stand. Their defense was sturdy and well stocked until their youngest son, Devlin, betrayed them to the enemy. Devlin slew the sentries and let a strike force of undead into the encampment, slaughtering a third of the defenders in their sleep. Overwhelmed by subsequent assaults, the survivors succumbed to the plague and rose as mindless minions of the Lich King.",al={"fall of agamand mills","agamand mills history","how did agamand mills fall"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Agamand Mills dangerous?",a="It is exceptionally dangerous. The estate crawls with reanimated farmhands, skeletal guards, and the risen members of the Agamand family themselves. Deep in the family crypt, the Scourge commander Captain Dargol wields dark necromancy to awaken long-dead ancestors, turning the mills into an armed outpost aimed directly at the Forsaken in Tirisfal.",al={"is agamand mills safe","how dangerous is agamand mills","threat at agamand mills"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Agamand Mills?",a="The estate takes its name from the Agamands, a prominent human noble lineage that traced its arrival back to wealthy immigrants from Strom. They bought up the surrounding land centuries ago and built an influential milling empire that bore their family name until their tragic demise.",al={"why is it called agamand","agamand mills name origin","who named agamand mills"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaiming Agamand Mills is not merely tactical—it is personal vengeance against the Scourge and a settling of scores by former estate servants like Coleman Farthing."},kw={"agamand","agamand family crypt","agamand mills","captain dargol","devlin agamand","gregor agamand","scourge","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:agamand-family-crypt","quest:426","quest:408","quest:354","subzone:brill","npc:magistrate-sevren","npc:coleman-farthing","topic:scourge"},src={"Agamand Mills"}}
E["subzone:balnir-farmstead"] = {t="subzone",n="Balnir Farmstead",z="tirisfal",s="The Balnir Farmstead is a derelict estate in eastern Tirisfal Glades, northeast of the Ruins of Lordaeron. Once renowned for raising the famed white horses favored by Lordaeron's royal family, the lands now lie ruined and infested by wandering Scourge. Today, the estate stands as a somber monument to Prince Arthas Menethil's early life and the site where he claimed his undead steed.",h="The Balnir Farmstead is a derelict estate in eastern Tirisfal Glades, northeast of the Ruins of Lordaeron.",sec={{t="A Legacy of Royal Steeds",b="For generations, the Balnir family bred the legendary white stallions of Lordaeron from their homestead in the rolling hills of Tirisfal. As a boy, Prince Arthas Menethil frequently visited the farm to escape royal court life, befriending the young Jarim Balnir. It was here that Arthas assisted in the foaling of the colt he named Invincible. Years later, after a tragic riding accident claimed the steed's life, Invincible was laid to rest in the soil of the farmstead upon Arthas's personal request.",sp=0},{t="Desolation and Scourge Infiltration",b="When Prince Arthas returned from Northrend and sacked Capital City, the Scourge wiped out the Balnir family without mercy. Newly fallen as a death knight, Arthas marched to the farmstead to exhume and resurrect Invincible with Frostmourne, leaving behind an open, ransacked grave. In the wake of the Third War, the ruins serve as a foothold for mindless Scourge slipping past the Bulwark. Forsaken deathguards watch the farm warily, while Undercity magi harvest surviving snapdragons from the garden to test how deeply plague corruption has crept into the soil.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Balnir Farmstead significant to Prince Arthas?",a="Arthas spent much of his youth sneaking away to the farmstead to play with the breeder's son. He was present for the birth of his beloved horse, Invincible, and later buried the beast here after a fatal fall. Following his descent into darkness, Arthas returned to exhume and raise Invincible into undeath.",al={"arthas farm","invincible birthplace","balnir arthas connection"},it="fame"},{q="Who lived at the farmstead before the plague?",a="The farm was managed by Jorum Balnir, his wife Vara, and their son Jarim. They were master horse breeders who raised prized white horses for Lordaeron's nobility. The entire family was slaughtered when Arthas's Scourge swept through Tirisfal during the Third War.",al={"who owned balnir farm","balnir family","jorum balnir"},it="residents"},{q="Why are the Forsaken monitoring the farm now?",a="Mindless Scourge often slip past the Bulwark from the Plaguelands and gather at the farmstead, threatening the eastern approaches to Tirisfal. Additionally, Undercity scholars study the resilient snapdragons still growing in the gardens to measure the spread of plague contamination.",al={"why forsaken care about balnir","rear guard patrol lore","balnir snapdragons"},it="threat"},{q="What is the open grave on the property?",a="The disturbed plot marks the burial place of Invincible, Prince Arthas's childhood mount. After murdering his father, the fallen prince unearthed the horse and reanimated it as his monstrous skeletal charger, leaving the grave uncovered.",al={"balnir open grave","empty grave tirisfal","invincible grave"},it="place"},{q="What is Balnir Farmstead?",a="Balnir Farmstead is a derelict estate in eastern Tirisfal Glades, just northeast of the Ruins of Lordaeron. Before the kingdom fell, it was a peaceful rural homestead famous for breeding the noble white horses favoured by Lordaeron's royalty. Today, it lies broken and haunted, overgrown with wild snapdragons and overrun by wandering undead.",al={"what is balnir farmstead","balnir farm","about balnir farmstead"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Balnir Farmstead?",a="When Capital City fell during the Third War, the Scourge descended upon the farmstead and slaughtered the entire Balnir family. Shortly after murdering King Terenas, the fallen prince Arthas returned to the grounds wielding Frostmourne. He tore open the grave of his beloved steed Invincible, raising the beast into undeath and leaving behind the shattered grave you see today.",al={"balnir farmstead history","what happened here","history of balnir farmstead"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is Balnir Farmstead ruined?",a="The farmstead was devastated during the Scourging of Lordaeron when the undead overran eastern Tirisfal. The estate's owners, the Balnirs, were murdered during the onslaught, leaving the buildings to rot and the grounds to be claimed by roaming spirits and encroaching horrors from the nearby Plaguelands.",al={"why is balnir ruined","why is balnir farm abandoned","what destroyed balnir farmstead"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Balnir Farmstead dangerous?",a="Yes. It serves as a haven for restless undead and wandering spirits that slip past the Bulwark from the Western Plaguelands. Bleeding horrors and vengeful ghosts stalk the overgrown fields and ruined outbuildings, making it a constant menace to travelers along the eastern road.",al={"is balnir farm dangerous","balnir farmstead danger","threats at balnir farm"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Balnir Farmstead?",a="The land takes its name from the Balnir family, human horse breeders who tended the estate for generations. They were renowned throughout Lordaeron for raising exceptional white horses, supplying prized steeds to nobles and royalty alike until the family was wiped out during the Third War.",al={"why is it named balnir","balnir farmstead name origin","who are the balnirs"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the farmstead is a bitter reminder of the prince who doomed Lordaeron, as well as a nagging security breach where Scourge from the Plaguelands slip behind the Bulwark.",["class:Mage"]="Forsaken magi are dispatched to these blighted grounds to gather surviving Balnir snapdragons, studying them to detect the lingering magical taint of the Scourge."},kw={"arthas","balnir","farmstead","horses","invincible","jorum","snapdragon","tirisfal"},rel={"topic:arthas-menethil","zone:tirisfal","zone:ruinsoflordaeron","quest:1882","quest:356","subzone:bulwark","topic:scourge","topic:forsaken"},src={"Balnir Farmstead"}}
E["subzone:bandarion-keep"] = {t="subzone",n="Bandarion Keep",z="tirisfal",s="Bandarion Keep is a fortified redoubt associated with Tyr's Watch, nestled in the western reaches of Tirisfal Glades near the Whispering Forest. Named in honor of Garek Bandarion, the outpost serves as an isolated bulwark amidst lingering threats across the woodland frontier. Its defenders contend with everything from hostile coastal murlocs to roving zealots and unstable undead.",h="Bandarion Keep is a fortified redoubt associated with Tyr's Watch, nestled in the western reaches of Tirisfal...",sec={{t="A Bastion on the Western Border",b="Situated near the secluded Whispering Forest in western Tirisfal Glades, Bandarion Keep stands as a solitary sentinel for the Forsaken. Named after Garek Bandarion, this stronghold is closely tied to Tyr's Watch. Quartermasters like Jorin Croge work diligently to keep the garrison supplied despite its remote and challenging position far from the bustling courtyards of the Undercity.",sp=0},{t="Surrounded by Threats",b="Holding the keep is no quiet assignment. The outpost faces constant danger from Vile Fin murloc raids along nearby waters, probing skirmishes by the Scarlet Crusade, and stray, mindless Scourge thralls shambling through the gloom. Worse still are internal perils: certain paladins struggling to adjust to their unlife as Forsaken have succumbed to madness, turning their weapons against their own kindred.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Bandarion Keep located?",a="Bandarion Keep lies in the far western wilderness of Tirisfal Glades, positioned near the enigmatic Whispering Forest.",al={"bandarion keep location","where is bandarion keep","how to find bandarion keep"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who was Bandarion Keep named after?",a="The stronghold is named in honor of Garek Bandarion, a notable figure associated with the order stationed there.",al={"who is bandarion","garek bandarion","bandarion keep name origin"},it="name"},{q="What is Tyr's Watch?",a="Tyr's Watch refers to the order of Forsaken paladins and guardians operating in the region, an organization deeply entwined with the foundation and defense of Bandarion Keep.",al={"tyrs watch","what is tyrs watch"}},{q="What dangers threaten the keep?",a="The keep's defenders regularly fend off Vile Fin murlocs, skirmish with Scarlet Crusade zealots, dispatch wandering mindless Scourge, and put down unstable Forsaken paladins who lose their minds and attack their own allies.",al={"who attacks bandarion keep","threats to bandarion keep","bandarion enemies"},it="danger"},{q="What is Bandarion Keep?",a="Bandarion Keep is a stronghold held by Tyr's Watch, situated near the mysterious Whispering Forest in western Tirisfal Glades. It serves as an outpost in a quiet but perilous corner of the region, housing Forsaken who maintain their vigil far from the heart of the Undercity.",al={"what is bandarion keep","bandarion keep info","about bandarion keep"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who lives in or runs Bandarion Keep?",a="The keep is garrisoned by members of Tyr's Watch. Among those tending to the bastion is Jorin Croge, who oversees the supply efforts and ensures the garrison remains properly provisioned amid the surrounding threats.",al={"who runs bandarion keep","bandarion keep residents","who is at bandarion keep"},it="residents",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For holy warriors facing the cold reality of undeath, Bandarion Keep represents both a refuge and a warning: some paladins who cannot reconcile the Light with their unlife have broken and turned their weapons against their Forsaken peers.",["race:Undead"]="Standing far on Tirisfal's western rim, this keep proves that the Forsaken must stay vigilant not only against the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade, but also against inner turmoil within their own ranks."},kw={"bandarion","bandarion keep","garek bandarion","jorin croge","tirisfal","tyr's watch","whispering forest"},rel={"subzone:whispering-forest","topic:forsaken","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:scourge","topic:murloc","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Bandarion Keep"}}
E["subzone:brightwater-lake"] = {t="subzone",n="Brightwater Lake",z="tirisfal",s="Brightwater Lake is the largest body of water in Tirisfal Glades, stretching across the mist-shrouded lowlands northeast of Brill. Once a tranquil retreat for the kingdom of Lordaeron, its waters now lie cold and dark, surrounding a central isle where an outcast necromancer conducts his forbidden research.",h="Brightwater Lake is the largest body of water in Tirisfal Glades, stretching across the mist-shrouded...",sec={{t="Waters of the North",b="Spanning the northern plains between the town of Brill and the Scarlet Watch Post, Brightwater Lake is a quiet and eerie landmark in the heart of Tirisfal. Thousands of years ago, its misty banks bore witness to ancient conflicts, including early clashes against the dreadlord Kathra'natir. In the peaceful centuries that followed under the Kingdom of Lordaeron, the lake served as a scenic country haven. Today, like the rest of the glades, its pristine character is long gone. The Plaguelands lie not far to the east, and the murky, chilled waters reflect only grey skies, withered trees, and the constant threat of roving murlocs and hostile undead.",sp=0},{t="The Hermit's Refuge",b="Rising from the center of Brightwater Lake is an island known as Gunther's Retreat. Here dwells Gunther Arcanus, a powerful necromancer who broke free of the Lich King's grasp by his own formidable willpower. Suspicious of the Dark Lady and the newly established Forsaken in the Undercity, Gunther chose seclusion over servitude, raising his own skeletal defenders to guard the island perimeter. His stubborn independence draws keen interest from Forsaken magistrates, who are eager to either recruit his formidable talents or ensure his autonomous existence poses no threat to Sylvanas's realm.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is Brightwater Lake located?",a="Brightwater Lake sits in northeastern Tirisfal Glades, just northeast of the Forsaken hub of Brill. It lies directly south of the jagged North Coast and west of the Scarlet Watch Post that guards the mountain passes toward the Western Plaguelands.",al={"brightwater lake location","where is brightwater lake","find brightwater lake"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who lives on the island in the middle of the lake?",a="The island, known as Gunther's Retreat, is inhabited by Gunther Arcanus. He is a rogue necromancer who managed to shatter the Lich King's mental enslavement entirely on his own. Rather than joining the Forsaken in the Undercity, he remains secluded in his camp with his personal skeletal minions.",al={"who is on the island","gunther arcanus","brightwater island npc"},it="residents"},{q="What threats are found around the waters?",a="Travelers along the shorelines frequently encounter aggressive vile fins and murlocs scavenging the banks. Approaching the central island brings danger from the reanimated skeletons and servants sworn to protect Gunther Arcanus from intruders.",al={"lake monsters","brightwater enemies","dangers at brightwater lake"},it="danger"},{q="Why do the guards in Brill talk about a summer camp here?",a="Town deathguards in Brill dryly joke that the Forsaken attempted to run a children's summer camp along the shores of Brightwater Lake, but were forced to shut it down due to masked psychos terrorizing the grounds—a morbid local quip mirroring human campfire tales.",al={"summer camp rumor","brill guards lake joke","masked psycho camp"}},{q="What is Brightwater Lake?",a="Brightwater Lake is the largest body of water in Tirisfal Glades, stretching northeast of Brill and bordered to the east by the hills leading toward the Scarlet Watch Post. In the center of its dark waters lies a prominent island known as Gunther's Retreat, along with smaller outcroppings that dot the expanse.",al={"what is brightwater lake","brightwater lake lore","tell me about brightwater lake"},it="place",add=true},{q="What is the history of Brightwater Lake?",a="Long before the plague ruined Lordaeron, this region witnessed ancient struggles. Over two thousand years ago, the dreadlord Kathra'natir corrupted the surrounding lands, prompting Alodi, the very first Guardian of Tirisfal, to confront and battle the demon directly on one of the lake's small islands.",al={"brightwater lake history","what happened at brightwater lake","brightwater lake lore past"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, securing Brightwater Lake is vital for defending Brill's eastern approaches and assessing whether the autonomous necromancer Gunther Arcanus will become an asset or an enemy.",["class:Mage"]="The lake holds lingering arcana from ancient battles fought in Tirisfal, as well as the unique, unguided mastery displayed by the renegade wizard Gunther Arcanus."},kw={"brightwater lake","brill","gunther arcanus","gunther's retreat","necromancer","scarlet watch post","tirisfal glades","undead"},rel={"subzone:gunther-s-retreat","zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","subzone:scarlet-watch-post","subzone:north-coast","topic:forsaken","topic:lordaeron","subzone:crystal-lake"},src={"Brightwater Lake"}}
E["subzone:brill"] = {t="subzone",n="Brill",z="tirisfal",s="Brill is the primary Forsaken settlement in the heart of Tirisfal Glades, situated just north of the Ruins of Lordaeron. Once a prosperous human town, it now serves as the administrative and military hub for newly awakened undead stepping beyond the confines of Deathknell.",h="Brill is the primary Forsaken settlement in the heart of Tirisfal Glades, situated just north of the Ruins of...",sec={{t="From Plague Ground to Bastion",b="Before the Third War, Brill was a lively village under the sway of regional nobles such as the Barov and Agamand families. That prosperity ended when Kel'Thuzad chose the area for the Cult of the Damned's earliest experiments with the Plague of Undeath. The devastating blight swept through the population so quickly that local militia were driven out, leaving a sprawling mass graveyard in its wake.\n\nNow rebuilt by the Forsaken under the leadership of Magistrate Sevren, the town retains its weathered Lordaeron architecture beneath an atmosphere of grim purpose. Brill functions as the regional command center, coordinating defenses against Scarlet Crusade incursions, roaming Scourge remnants, and native beasts warped by necromantic decay.",sp=0},{t="Crossroads of Northern Lordaeron",b="Beyond local defense, Brill is an essential crossroads linking Tirisfal Glades to the wider Horde. Overhead, the goblin-operated zeppelin tower provides transit south toward Durotar and deep into Stranglethorn Vale. Within the settlement, Gallows' End Tavern serves not only as a dark shelter for travelers, but also as a makeshift laboratory where captured Scarlet zealots are interrogated and subjected to apothecarial testing.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who rules Brill?",a="Magistrate Sevren oversees the civil administration and local defenses of Brill on behalf of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken.",al={"brill leader","who is in charge of brill","magistrate of brill"},it="in_charge"},{q="Why is there such a massive graveyard outside Brill?",a="Kel'Thuzad conducted some of his very first live experiments with the Plague of Undeath in Brill. The contagion wiped out the local human population with horrifying speed, necessitating a mass graveyard to inter the victims before they rose as Scourge.",al={"brill graveyard","cemetery near brill","why so many graves"},it="history"},{q="What is Gallows' End Tavern used for?",a="Aside from lodging weary travelers, the tavern's cellar serves as a base for apothecaries conducting chemical and toxicological experiments on captured Scarlet Crusade zealots.",al={"gallows end","brill inn","what is happening in the brill tavern"},it="place"},{q="Where does the zeppelin tower outside Brill travel to?",a="The zeppelin tower operated by goblin crews connects Lordaeron directly to Kalimdor outside Orgrimmar, as well as south to Grom'gol Base Camp in Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"brill zeppelin destinations","where do blimps go brill","brill flight paths"},it="place"},{q="What is Brill?",a="Brill is the central settlement of Tirisfal Glades, located just north of the Ruins of Lordaeron. Once a sleepy human hamlet, it now serves as a key military hub and outpost for the Forsaken. Fresh recruits journeying from Deathknell report here to aid Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's forces in securing the surrounding glades against encroaching threats.",al={"what is brill","brill overview","brill town"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened to Brill in the past?",a="During the Third War, Brill was among the first villages stricken by the Plague of Undeath when the necromancer Kel'Thuzad began his dark trials in Tirisfal Glades. Its defenders were overwhelmed, forcing survivors to flee before Prince Arthas investigated the region. After the Scourge tore through the kingdom, the Forsaken reclaimed the ruined settlement, rebuilding it into their primary fortified hub north of the capital.",al={"history of brill","what happened at brill","brill lore"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Brill dangerous?",a="Within its borders, Brill is heavily defended by vigilant Deathguards and Magistrate Sevren's watch. Outside the town, however, danger presses from all directions. Mindless Scourge prowl the countryside, vicious Rot Hide gnolls scour nearby waterways, and fanatical Scarlet Crusaders wage relentless war across the glades, seeking the utter destruction of everyone living within the settlement.",al={"is brill safe","threats to brill","is brill dangerous"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken venturing out from Deathknell, Brill is the first glimpse of civilization and the starting ground for proving loyalty to the Dark Lady.",["faction:Horde"]="The zeppelin dock outside town serves as the northern Horde's primary link between Lordaeron and allied strongholds in Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"brill","forsaken","gallows end","lordaeron","magistrate sevren","tirisfal glades","zeppelin"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill-town-hall","subzone:gallows-end-tavern","npc:magistrate-sevren","subzone:deathknell","topic:forsaken","topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:grom-gol-base-camp"},src={"Brill"}}
E["subzone:brill-town-hall"] = {t="subzone",n="Brill Town Hall",z="tirisfal",s="Brill Town Hall is the administrative heart of the settlement of Brill in central Tirisfal Glades. Standing as the most prominent structure in town, it serves as the civic headquarters from which the Forsaken govern their local holdings and coordinate the defense of the glades.",h="Brill Town Hall is the administrative heart of the settlement of Brill in central Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Civic Seat of the Forsaken",b="While much of Lordaeron lies shattered and ruined, Brill Town Hall remains standing as a functioning civic center under undead rule. Inside, Magistrate Sevren directs town affairs from his podium, handing out vital commissions and directives to Forsaken adventurers. Aside from the Defiler's Den in the Arathi Highlands, it is one of the only recognized town halls in the known world actively run by the Forsaken, serving as a bleak parody and functional continuation of old Lordaeron's municipal bureaucracy.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Brill Town Hall used for?",a="It serves as the local seat of government for the settlement of Brill. Forsaken leadership, particularly Magistrate Sevren, uses the building to coordinate local defense, issue decrees, and assign critical tasks to travelers and newly awakened undead.",al={"brill town hall purpose","what is this building","why visit brill town hall"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is in charge here?",a="Magistrate Sevren presides over the town hall from his podium, managing municipal affairs on behalf of the Forsaken and directing operations throughout central Tirisfal Glades.",al={"who runs brill town hall","brill town hall leader","who is the magistrate"},it="in_charge"},{q="Why is this town hall notable compared to others?",a="Town halls are typically relics of living human kingdoms. Brill Town Hall is notable for being one of the only active civic halls maintained and operated by the Forsaken, mirroring only places like the Defiler's Den in Arathi.",al={"unique forsaken town hall","are there other forsaken town halls"},it="fame"},{q="What is Brill Town Hall?",a="Brill Town Hall stands as the most prominent structure in the settlement of Brill in central Tirisfal Glades. It serves as the civic and administrative heart of the town, where Forsaken leadership organizes local affairs and coordinates efforts across the region.",al={"what is brill town hall","brill town hall place","brill hall"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is Brill Town Hall dangerous?",a="Not if you are an ally of the Forsaken and the Horde. Brill Town Hall is firmly under Forsaken control, functioning safely as their local seat of governance, though any intruders hostile to the Dark Lady will find no mercy within its walls.",al={"is brill town hall dangerous","brill town hall safe","danger in brill town hall"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken venturing out from Deathknell, the town hall represents the first taste of structured civic governance under Queen Sylvanas's realm, where local defense and reclamation efforts are formally organized.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies of the Horde arriving in Tirisfal can visit the town hall to understand how the Forsaken maintain civil authority and martial order in their darkened homelands."},kw={"brill","forsaken","lordaeron","magistrate sevren","sevren","tirisfal glades","town hall"},rel={"subzone:brill","npc:magistrate-sevren","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},src={"Brill Town Hall"}}
E["subzone:cold-hearth-manor"] = {t="subzone",n="Cold Hearth Manor",z="tirisfal",s="Cold Hearth Manor is a blighted farmstead located just west of Brill along the main road of Tirisfal Glades. Once a bustling, hospitable estate known for bountiful crops, the outbreak of the Plague of Undeath wiped out its residents, leaving the surrounding fields choked with mindless Scourge. Today, the Forsaken have secured the main farmhouse as an outpost and waystation for travelers.",h="Cold Hearth Manor is a blighted farmstead located just west of Brill along the main road of Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Chilled Hearth",b="Before the Third War, this homestead bore a reputation for warmth and hospitality, supporting rich harvests in the heart of Lordaeron. When the Plague swept through the kingdom, the entire family and their laborers succumbed, rising again as husks bound to the Scourge. While feral undead still stumble through the ruined fields, Forsaken forces managed to push the mindless dead out of the main farmhouse, reclaiming the structure to serve as a trading post and foothold along the road toward the eastern glades.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to the people who lived here?",a="The farmers and workers who tended the estate were completely wiped out when the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron. Rather than finding peace in death, they were raised as mindless zombies that still haunt the surrounding fields.",al={"who lived at cold hearth","manor backstory","what happened to the family"},it="ruin"},{q="Why is it called Cold Hearth Manor?",a="The estate originally had a reputation for its warm atmosphere and welcoming fires. The grim moniker was likely adopted by the Forsaken as an ironic nod to its former life, marking how cold, dead, and blighted the farm has become.",al={"cold hearth name meaning","why cold hearth"},it="name"},{q="Who controls the manor right now?",a="The Forsaken have taken control of the primary farmhouse, using it as a safe stop for travelers and traders along the road from Brill. However, the open fields and barns surrounding the manor remain infested with hostile Scourge.",al={"who owns cold hearth manor","is cold hearth friendly"},it="residents"},{q="Why don't the Forsaken clear out the fields?",a="The Forsaken in Tirisfal Glades are stretched thin, fighting off mindless Scourge remnants while warding off Scarlet Crusade crusaders. Securing the farmhouse and main road provides immediate utility, leaving the scattered dead in the fields as a nuisance rather than an immediate threat to Brill.",al={"why are there zombies in the fields","why scourge here"}},{q="What is Cold Hearth Manor?",a="Cold Hearth Manor is a farmstead located in central Tirisfal Glades along the main road. Once celebrated across Lordaeron for its rich crops and inviting atmosphere, the estate fell during the Third War when the Plague of Undeath swept the countryside. Today, Forsaken merchants maintain a fragile roadside presence at the farmhouse, trading with travelers while keeping a watchful eye on the blighted lands around them.",al={"what is cold hearth manor","cold hearth manor overview","cold hearth manor lore"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Cold Hearth Manor?",a="During the Third War, the Plague of Undeath devastated the farmstead, killing everyone who lived and worked its soil. Mindless Scourge zombies rose from the fallen to roam the overgrown fields. Following the fall of Lordaeron, the Forsaken reclaimed the farmhouse, turning the grim remnant of human agricultural life into an outpost along the Tirisfal thoroughfare.",al={"cold hearth manor history","what happened here","cold hearth manor story"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Cold Hearth Manor dangerous?",a="The farmhouse itself provides a measure of safety alongside Forsaken merchants, but the surrounding fields are perilous. Mindless Scourge zombies still infest the barren grounds, attacking anyone who strays from the road. Travelers must stay on guard, as the Scourge presence remains an ever-present menace right outside the manor doors.",al={"cold hearth manor danger","is it safe here","cold hearth manor threats"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, reclaiming places like Cold Hearth Manor is a bitter reminder of their lost mortal lives, converting former human sanctuaries into grim defensive outposts against both the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade.",["faction:Horde"]="The manor offers a secure vantage along the road between Brill and the eastern reaches of Tirisfal, providing a safe waypoint for travelers navigating the plagued countryside."},kw={"brill","cold hearth manor","farmstead","forsaken","scourge","tirisfal glades","zombies"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath"},src={"Cold Hearth Manor"}}
E["subzone:crusader-outpost"] = {t="subzone",n="Crusader Outpost",z="tirisfal",s="Crusader Outpost is a fortified watchtower perched atop the rugged hills of eastern Tirisfal Glades. Manned by zealous members of the Scarlet Crusade, it guards the approaches leading toward their stronghold at the Scarlet Monastery. From this height, Crusaders observe travel along the road and stand ready to repel any Forsaken incursion.",h="Crusader Outpost is a fortified watchtower perched atop the rugged hills of eastern Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Eyes Over Tirisfal",b="Built into the bluffs overlooking the eastern road, the Crusader Outpost serves as a primary early-warning station for the Scarlet Crusade. From their elevated tower, sentries watch over movement between Brill and the eastern reaches of Lordaeron, ensuring that wandering undead or scouting parties are spotted long before reaching the Monastery gates. The tower also grants the garrison tactical flanking capability against any force seeking to march up the valley toward their holy bastion.",sp=0},{t="A Thorn in the Dark Lady's Side",b="Under the command of Scarlet officers like Captain Vachon, the garrison stationed here maintains unwavering vigilance. They see the surrounding woods not as sovereign territory of the Forsaken, but as unhallowed soil stolen from humanity by monstrosities. For travelers loyal to the Undercity, the outpost represents an ever-present ambush point where crossbow bolts and cleansing steel await anyone straying too close to the bluffs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Crusader Outpost?",a="It is a fortified scouting tower held by the Scarlet Crusade in eastern Tirisfal Glades. It monitors the road passing below and protects the southern approach to the Scarlet Monastery.",al={"what is this tower","crusader outpost tirisfal","scarlet watchtower"},it="place"},{q="Who controls this outpost?",a="The outpost is manned by zealots of the Scarlet Crusade under the command of Captain Vachon. They view all undead as abominations to be eradicated on sight.",al={"who owns crusader outpost","who lives here","scarlet crusade outpost"},it="people"},{q="Why is the tower situated in these hills?",a="Its elevated position offers clear sightlines over the main eastern thoroughfare of Tirisfal Glades. This allows Crusaders to detect Forsaken troop movements early and flank any forces attempting an assault on the Scarlet Monastery.",al={"why build here","outpost location","tactical purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Will the guards here attack me?",a="Yes. The Scarlet Crusade considers all outsiders—particularly the Forsaken and their Horde allies—corrupt enemies who must be purged in holy fire.",al={"is crusader outpost hostile","are they aggressive"},it="danger"},{q="Who is in charge of Crusader Outpost?",a="The outpost is commanded by Captain Vachon, a Scarlet Crusade officer stationed here to oversee reconnaissance and hold the line against intruders trespassing on Crusade territory.",al={"who leads crusader outpost","crusader outpost captain","crusader outpost commander"},it="in_charge",add=true},{q="Why is it called Crusader Outpost?",a="The outpost takes its name directly from the Scarlet Crusade, the fanatical human order that built and maintains it as an early warning post and defensive flank for the nearby Scarlet Monastery.",al={"why is it called crusader outpost","crusader outpost name origin","why crusader outpost"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This watchtower sits right on the edge of Forsaken territory, serving as a bitter reminder that zealous living crusaders still hold sovereign pockets within the glades.",["faction:Horde"]="Navigating the eastern trails of Tirisfal requires caution, as Scarlet sentries atop this hill will swiftly attack anyone traveling toward the Monastery."},kw={"captain vachon","crusader outpost","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","scout tower","tirisfal glades","watchtower"},rel={"topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:scarlet","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Crusader Outpost"}}
E["subzone:crusader-s-outpost"] = {t="subzone",n="Crusader's Outpost",z="tirisfal",s="Crusader's Outpost is a fortified scout tower manned by the Scarlet Crusade in western Tirisfal Glades, standing along the Scarlet Palisade. Situated adjacent to the Solliden Farmstead, it serves as the Crusade's westernmost position in all of Lordaeron. Its perilous distance from the main Scarlet bastions leaves its garrison isolated in the heart of hostile Forsaken territory.",h="Crusader's Outpost is a fortified scout tower manned by the Scarlet Crusade in western Tirisfal Glades...",sec={{t="A Lonely Watchtower",b="Perched inside the Scarlet Palisade just west of Deathknell, this stone tower represents the farthest reach of the Scarlet Crusade's power in Lordaeron. The garrison stationed here works in tandem with the surviving human laborers of the nearby Solliden Farmstead, attempting to hold back the encroaching undead. From the top of the tower, lookouts keep watch over the surrounding pumpkin fields and forest paths, vigilant against both roaming Scourge fiends and patrols from the Undercity.",sp=0},{t="Isolated Behind Enemy Lines",b="Holding the western frontier carries extreme risk. Cut off from their primary strongholds in the Scarlet Monastery and far to the east in Tyr's Hand, the crusaders at this outpost cannot expect swift reinforcements should an attack breach the palisade. Any siege by the Forsaken forces massing across Tirisfal means either holding the tower on their own or facing total annihilation before help can ever arrive.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade all the way out here in western Tirisfal?",a="The tower serves as an early warning post and defensive fortification along the Scarlet Palisade. It protects the living human farmers at Solliden Farmstead and watches the western approaches against both feral Scourge and Forsaken advance scouts.",al={"why are scarlet here","scarlet crusaders tirisfal west","crusader outpost purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Can reinforcements reach this outpost if it gets attacked?",a="Not easily. The outpost is the westernmost Scarlet holding in Lordaeron. Their primary bastions lie far to the east at the Scarlet Monastery and Tyr's Hand, leaving this garrison entirely cut off if Forsaken forces surround them.",al={"can scarlet reinforce","outpost help","how far is help"}},{q="What is the relationship between the tower and Solliden Farmstead?",a="The crusaders have allied with the remaining human farmers at Solliden Farmstead. The soldiers provide protection against the walking dead in exchange for sustenance and a loyal staging ground on the fringes of Lordaeron.",al={"solliden connection","why are crusaders at farm","crusaders and farmers"},it="allies"},{q="Are there other towers like this in the region?",a="Yes. This tower is one of four similar Crusader's Outposts established by the Scarlet Crusade across Tirisfal Glades to survey and patrol the surrounding lands.",al={"other crusader outposts","more scarlet towers"}},{q="What is Crusader's Outpost?",a="Crusader's Outpost is a fortified scout tower enclosed within the Scarlet Palisade in western Tirisfal Glades. Standing as the westernmost holding of the Scarlet Crusade in Lordaeron, it serves as a frontline watchpost against the surrounding Forsaken and Scourge threats, keeping vigil over the region alongside the nearby allied farmers of Solliden Farmstead.",al={"what is crusaders outpost","crusader outpost tirisfal","scarlet outpost western tirisfal"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who controls Crusader's Outpost?",a="The tower is manned and operated by zealous members of the Scarlet Crusade. Far from their strongholds to the east, these sentries and crusaders maintain an isolated garrison, working in close cooperation with the sympathetic human farmers at Solliden Farmstead to protect their small pocket of territory in Tirisfal.",al={"who lives at crusaders outpost","crusaders outpost residents","who runs crusaders outpost"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is Crusader's Outpost dangerous?",a="Yes, exceedingly so if you are an outsider or undead. The Scarlet Crusade views nearly everyone beyond their ranks as tainted or corrupted, and they attack intruders on sight. Isolated deep within hostile lands, the crusaders here remain on hair-trigger alert, making any approach perilous.",al={"is crusaders outpost dangerous","crusader outpost danger","are crusaders hostile here"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Crusader's Outpost?",a="It takes its name directly from the Scarlet Crusade, the religious military order that constructed and occupies the scout tower. It serves as one of several such outposts erected across Tirisfal Glades to secure their zealot crusade against the Scourge and Forsaken.",al={"why is it named crusaders outpost","crusaders outpost name origin"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the newly risen Forsaken emerging from Deathknell, this nearby tower and the Scarlet zealots guarding it represent an immediate existential threat right on their border.",["faction:Horde"]="The outpost serves as a bitter reminder of the Scarlet Crusade's fanatical refusal to yield Tirisfal to the Forsaken and their Horde allies."},kw={"crusader's outpost","scarlet crusade","scarlet palisade","scout tower","solliden farmstead","tirisfal glades","tyr's hand"},rel={"subzone:solliden-farmstead","zone:tirisfal","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:forsaken","subzone:tyr-s-hand","topic:lordaeron","topic:scourge"},src={"Crusader's Outpost"}}
E["subzone:deathknell"] = {t="subzone",n="Deathknell",z="tirisfal",s="Deathknell is a fortified village nestled in a secluded valley in southwestern Tirisfal Glades. It serves as the primary awakening grounds for newly freed undead breaking free from the Lich King's grasp to join the Forsaken. Besieged by mindless Scourge remnants, local zealots, and aggressive wildlife, the settlement is a harsh proving ground for those claiming a place in Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom.",h="Deathknell is a fortified village nestled in a secluded valley in southwestern Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Cradle of the Forsaken",b="Tucked away behind jagged hills just north of the Silverpine border, Deathknell is where newly awakened undead first open their eyes to their bleak reality. Unlike most settlements in Tirisfal Glades that preserved their human names from the fallen Kingdom of Lordaeron, Deathknell was explicitly renamed by the undead who claimed it. The town itself is cleaved in two: the Forsaken cling stubbornly to the southern grounds around the old church, while the northern half—including the village forge—remains overrun by mindless Scourge zombies and feral ghouls. Elite Deathguards keep the narrow gorge out of the valley securely sealed against intruders, allowing local caretakers to induct new arrivals into the Banshee Queen's service.",sp=0},{t="A Valley Under Siege",b="Survival in Deathknell is precarious. To the northwest, Night Web's Hollow—a gold mine vital for the Forsaken's fledgling economy—has been lost to monstrous spiders. To the southeast, an aggressive encampment of the Scarlet Crusade maintains constant watch, trading couriers with their strongholds to the east and probing the valley's defenses. Meanwhile, the village caretakers must continually fight off the mindless undead wandering the northern ruins while gathering scrap metal, tools, and supplies to equip newly arisen souls for the long road ahead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the town renamed Deathknell?",a="While most settlements throughout Tirisfal Glades retained their human Lordaeron names, Deathknell is a notable exception. The Forsaken chose the moniker intentionally upon taking root in the valley, marking the site as both the death of their old human existence and the grim bell sounding their rebirth into undeath.",al={"deathknell name origin","why is it called deathknell","old name of deathknell"},it="name"},{q="Why is the northern half of the town overrun by undead?",a="Not every corpse raised in the wake of the Third War broke free with their minds intact. The northern village square and forge are infested by the 'Mindless Ones'—feral zombies, ghouls, and skeletons still driven by the residual, lingering will of the Scourge. Forsaken defenders regularly clear them out to salvage equipment and defend the church.",al={"why are zombies in deathknell","scourge in deathknell","mindless ones deathknell"},it="threat"},{q="What is the Scarlet Crusade doing near Deathknell?",a="A detachment of the Scarlet Crusade maintains an encampment in the hills southeast of the village. Fanatically dedicated to destroying all undead indiscriminately, they monitor the pass, ambush newly awakened Forsaken, and exchange scouting intelligence with Scarlet strongholds elsewhere in Lordaeron.",al={"scarlet camp deathknell","crusaders outside deathknell","scarlet crusade tirisfal valley"},it="threat"},{q="Why can't the Forsaken use the local gold mine?",a="Night Web's Hollow, the gold mine situated in the cliffs northwest of the village, is completely overrun by aggressive spiders. Securing natural resources like gold is critical for the Forsaken war effort, but until the infestation is cleared, the settlement cannot exploit the vein.",al={"deathknell mine","night webs hollow","spider mine tirisfal"},it="purpose"},{q="What is Deathknell?",a="Deathknell is a small Forsaken settlement tucked into a sheltered valley in southwestern Tirisfal Glades, just north of the Silverpine border. It serves as the awakening ground for newly freed undead, where the freshly risen are received, assessed, and trained. The village is split in two: while the Forsaken hold the southern grounds near the church, the northern half and its old blacksmith forge remain overrun by mindless undead.",al={"what is this place","deathknell village","about deathknell"},it="place",add=true,nx={6,7}},{q="Was it a normal village once?",a="Yes. Before the Third War ravaged the land, Deathknell was a living human settlement within the Kingdom of Lordaeron. When the Plague of Undeath swept across the countryside, its people succumbed and were raised into the Scourge's thrall. Unlike other towns in Tirisfal that retained their original human names, this village was later renamed Deathknell by the Forsaken.",al={"deathknell past","was deathknell human","old deathknell"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who lived here before?",a="The original inhabitants were ordinary human subjects of the Kingdom of Lordaeron, including farmers, smiths, and townsfolk. When the kingdom collapsed under the Scourge, many were slaughtered or corrupted by the plague, buried in the graveyard and crypts on the hillside, or turned into mindless thralls who still wander the northern ruins.",al={"who lived here","original inhabitants deathknell","former villagers"},it="people",add=true},{q="Why is Deathknell ruined or abandoned?",a="The town fell during the Third War when the Plague of Undeath consumed Lordaeron. The Forsaken have since reclaimed only the southern portion around the church. The northern buildings, including the forge, remain abandoned and infested by hostile undead remnants of the Scourge whom the defenders have yet to fully purge.",al={"why is deathknell destroyed","why is deathknell broken","ruins of deathknell"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Deathknell dangerous?",a="Very dangerous beyond the southern perimeter. Mindless undead roam the northern half of the town, while giant Night Web spiders infest the nearby gold mine to the northwest. To make matters worse, a camp of fanatical Scarlet Crusaders sits just to the southeast, launching periodic raids against the village.",al={"is deathknell safe","danger deathknell","threats in deathknell"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why am I undead? What happened to me?",a="You died during the fall of Lordaeron, victim to the horrific Plague of Undeath unleashed by the Lich King. Your body was raised into the Scourge army and forced to serve the master's iron will. When the Lich King's grip weakened after the Third War, your mind broke free, leaving you with free will in a dead body.",al={"why am i undead","how did i die","what happened to me"},it="arrival",add=true,nx={11,12}},{q="Was I alive before this?",a="You were. You lived as a mortal resident of Lordaeron before the Third War brought ruin to the realm. You had a life, a home, and mortal ties before the plague claimed you and unholy magic remade your corpse.",al={"was i alive","was i human","my past life"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="Do I remember my old life?",a="Many undead retain fragmented, agonizing memories of their former mortal lives and the horrors they were forced to commit while under the Lich King's thrall. For most Forsaken, those memories are a source of lingering torment and quiet vengeance.",al={"do i remember","past memories undead","forsaken memory"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="Who are the Forsaken?",a="The Forsaken are intelligent, free-willed undead who broke away from the Lich King's control following the Third War. Rallied and led by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, they carved out a home in the ruins of Lordaeron and established their capital in the Undercity.",al={"who are forsaken","forsaken faction","what is the forsaken"},it="other",add=true,nx={14,15}},{q="How are we different from the other undead?",a="The vast majority of undead on Azeroth belong to the Scourge, chained completely to the mental domination of the Lich King. You and the other Forsaken broke those chains and regained your sentience, autonomy, and free will.",al={"difference between forsaken and scourge","are we scourge","free willed undead"},it="other",add=true},{q="Why did we join the Horde?",a="Surrounded by hostile living kingdoms and the relentless Scourge, the Forsaken faced total destruction alone. They entered an alliance of convenience with the Horde across the sea, securing necessary military backing and mutual survival in exchange for a northern foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why join horde","forsaken horde alliance","why horde"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Who is the Dark Lady everyone mentions?",a="She is Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen and sovereign ruler of the Forsaken. Formerly the Ranger-General of Silvermoon, she was slain and raised into banshee form by Prince Arthas during the Scourge invasion of Quel'Thalas. She later broke free of the Lich King's will, gathered the renegade undead, and reclaimed Lordaeron's catacombs.",al={"dark lady","who is dark lady","banshee queen"},it="leader",add=true},{q="What are the Mindless Zombies? Weren't they Forsaken too?",a="They were never Forsaken. When the plague raised the dead of Lordaeron, many lost all capacity for reason and higher thought, reduced to feral husks driven entirely by basic predatory instincts. Because they lack minds to break free or make choices, they remain hostile Scourge minions.",al={"mindless zombies","who are the mindless zombies","zombies in deathknell"},it="threat",add=true,nx={18}},{q="Could that have happened to me?",a="Yes. Countless corpses raised by the plague suffered total mental degradation, never recovering their sanity or awareness. It was pure fortune or iron will that preserved your intellect and self-command while so many others were lost to mindless savagery.",al={"could i be mindless","why am i not mindless","mindless zombie risk"},it="threat",add=true},{q="Who is the Scarlet Crusade and why are they attacking us?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical order born from the remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand and Lordaeron's surviving humans. They are consumed by zealous hatred for all undead, refusing to distinguish between the bloodthirsty Scourge and the free-willed Forsaken, and seek total eradication of undeath across Lordaeron.",al={"who is scarlet crusade","why scarlet crusade attacks","scarlet crusade"},it="threat",add=true,nx={20}},{q="Were they once part of the Silver Hand?",a="Yes. When the Knights of the Silver Hand shattered during the Third War after the death of Uther the Lightbringer and the fall of the capital, surviving paladins and priests banded together to fight the undead, eventually twisting into the zealous Crusade you see today.",al={"scarlet crusade silver hand","were scarlets paladins","silver hand origin"},it="history",add=true},{q="What are the Rattlecage Skeletons and why are they hostile to us?",a="Rattlecage Skeletons are Scourge automatons raised through necromancy from the bones of Lordaeron's dead. Lacking independent will or living flesh, they are bound directly to the lingering necromantic compulsion of the Lich King to slaughter any living or renegade undead intruders.",al={"rattlecage skeletons","why rattlecage attack","hostile skeletons"},it="threat",add=true,nx={22}},{q="Aren't they undead like me?",a="They are undead in body, but not in spirit. You possess your own soul, intellect, and free will. They are empty marrow animated by Scourge necromancy, following basic orders to destroy anything outside their master's fold.",al={"are rattlecage skeletons undead","aren't skeletons like us","skeletons undead"},it="threat",add=true},{q="What are the Night Web spiders and where did they come from?",a="The Night Web spiders are venomous arachnids that infested the gold mine and surrounding hollow northwest of Deathknell. As Tirisfal withered under the plague, native vermin mutated and grew aggressive, taking over the valley and driving the Forsaken away from their vital gold deposits.",al={"night web spiders","where did spiders come from","night web mine spiders"},it="threat",add=true},{q="What's next after Deathknell? What's in Brill?",a="Leaving Deathknell's valley leads into central Tirisfal Glades toward Brill. Brill is a larger Forsaken town that serves as the administrative hub for the region, complete with a town hall, the Gallows' End Tavern, flight masters, and trainers. From there, the road opens directly to the Undercity.",al={"after deathknell","what is in brill","where to go after deathknell"},it="next_step",add=true,nx={25}},{q="Where is Undercity from here?",a="Undercity lies to the east of Deathknell. To reach it, you take the northern road out of the valley, pass through Brill, and continue south beneath the ruined palace of Capital City.",al={"where is undercity","how to get to undercity","direction to undercity"},it="next_step",add=true},{q="How did I end up in Deathknell?",a="You died during the Scourge's destruction of Lordaeron and were buried in the crypts of the Shadow Grave above the village. When the Lich King's iron grip on the undead weakened, your mind snapped back to awareness. You pulled yourself free from your tomb and wandered down the slope into Deathknell, where the Forsaken gather freshly awakened corpses to see if they possess the will to serve the Dark Lady.",al={"how did i get here","why am i here","where did i come from"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Deathknell?",a="You are proving your usefulness to the Forsaken and securing this valley. Newly awakened undead must demonstrate they have not lost their minds to the mindless Scourge lurking around the village. The local leaders need you to put down rogue zombies, clear the spider infestation in the nearby gold mine, and push back Scarlet Crusade zealots seeking to purge the undead from Tirisfal Glades before you can be sent onward to Brill.",al={"what do i do here","what is my goal here","what is my mission"},it="purpose",add=true},{q="Why does a new Undead start here, in Deathknell?",a="Deathknell serves as the Forsaken's recovery grounds for newly reawakened dead. The surrounding hills and crypts, particularly the Shadow Grave, hold thousands of Lordaeron's casualties from the Third War. As the Lich King's mental domination falters, corpses continuously rise here with their free will intact. Deathguard sentries stationed throughout the valley intercept the newly risen, giving survivors shelter and guidance before madness or hostile crusaders destroy them.",al={"why do undead start here","why do i start in deathknell","why deathknell for undead"},it="arrival",rc="Undead",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every Forsaken adventurer begins here, rising from the cold earth of the Shadow Grave to find purpose and shelter under Lady Sylvanas after the horror of the Plague.",["faction:Horde"]="Though isolated and dark, this valley is the gateway to the Forsaken kingdom, supplying fresh soldiers to bolster the Horde's foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms."},kw={"deathknell","forsaken starting area","mindless ones","night web's hollow","shadow grave","sylvanas","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:shadow-grave","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","subzone:night-web-s-hollow","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:silverpine"},src={"Deathknell"}}
E["subzone:faol-s-rest"] = {t="subzone",n="Faol's Rest",z="tirisfal",s="Faol's Rest is a quiet, neglected gravesite situated in eastern Tirisfal Glades, just southwest of the Scarlet Monastery. It was erected as the tomb of Archbishop Alonsus Faol, the revered spiritual patriarch who founded the Knights of the Silver Hand. Today, untended beneath the blighted canopy, it serves as a release graveyard for those who perish near the monastery.",h="Faol's Rest is a quiet, neglected gravesite situated in eastern Tirisfal Glades, just southwest of the...",sec={{t="Tomb of the Archbishop",b="Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, this secluded hollow was a place of solemn pilgrimage. It holds the stone tomb of Archbishop Alonsus Faol, who led the Church of the Holy Light through the dark days of the Second War and instituted the order of paladins to protect humanity. Faol passed away peacefully prior to the arrival of the Scourge, spared the mortal agony of seeing his kingdom fall. In those brighter years, mourners regularly journeyed up the road from Capital City to leave offerings and pay respects to the beloved patriarch.",sp=0},{t="Desolation in the Shadow of the Monastery",b="When the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron, Tirisfal withered, and the pilgrims vanished. Today, the monument sits abandoned and cracked in the shadows beneath the Scarlet Monastery's high bluffs. The wild brush has reclaimed the pathways, and the tomb itself lies still amid the damp mist. For adventurers striving against the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade or the lingering terrors of Tirisfal, this forgotten resting place now serves primarily as a quiet threshold where fallen spirits briefly regroup before returning to the fray.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Alonsus Faol?",a="Alonsus Faol was the Archbishop of the Church of the Holy Light during the Second War. Revered for his wisdom and compassion, he organized relief for refugees and founded the Knights of the Silver Hand, transforming holy priests and pious knights into humanity's first paladins, including Uther the Lightbringer and Turalyon.",al={"who is faol","alonsus faol lore","who is buried here"},it="identity"},{q="Why is this monument located near the Scarlet Monastery?",a="The grounds near the Scarlet Monastery were historically significant religious holdings of the Church of the Holy Light in northern Lordaeron. Faol was laid to rest in this tranquil corner of Tirisfal Glades before the kingdom fell, long before the scarlet-clad zealots fortified the nearby monastery.",al={"why near monastery","scarlet monastery connection","location meaning"},it="why_here"},{q="Is anyone maintaining the tomb today?",a="No. Since the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge, the site has been utterly neglected. The Scarlet Crusade rarely descends into the hollow to tend it, and the Forsaken have little interest in honoring the shrines of the old church, leaving the stone markers weathered and overgrown.",al={"who tends faol's rest","is it abandoned","tomb upkeep"},it="today"},{q="What practical purpose does Faol's Rest serve for travelers?",a="Faol's Rest functions as the local spirit graveyard for the surrounding wilderness and the Scarlet Monastery. Adventurers who perish attempting to breach the monastery or traversing eastern Tirisfal find their spirits returning to the Archbishop's quiet monument.",al={"graveyard run","what is this place in game","monastery graveyard"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="What is Faol's Rest?",a="Faol's Rest is a secluded, solemn burial ground nestled in the hills of eastern Tirisfal Glades, just southwest of the Scarlet Monastery. Built to honor Archbishop Alonsus Faol, one of the most revered figures of the Holy Light and the founder of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the site once drew faithful pilgrims from across Lordaeron before the kingdom fell.",al={"what is faols rest","faols rest lore","faol rest"},it="place",add=true,nx={6}},{q="What happened at Faol's Rest?",a="Archbishop Alonsus Faol passed away before the Third War and was laid to rest here amidst grand mourning. Pilgrims traveled from all corners of the kingdom to pay their respects at his tomb. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, however, the kingdom collapsed into ruin and the dead rose, leaving the sacred hillside desecrated, abandoned, and untended.",al={"faols rest history","what happened to faols rest","history of faols rest"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is Faol's Rest ruined and abandoned?",a="When the Plague of Undeath ravaged Lordaeron, the kingdom that once venerated the Archbishop was destroyed. With the Scarlet Crusade locking themselves inside their nearby fortress and fighting an endless war against the undead, no one remains to care for the memorial, leaving the tomb weathered, overgrown, and largely forgotten by the outside world.",al={"why is faols rest abandoned","why is faols rest ruined","who tends faols rest"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Faol's Rest dangerous?",a="While the immediate clearing around the tomb is quiet and solemn, the surrounding slopes are perilous. Zealots of the Scarlet Crusade patrol the nearby monastery approaches, attacking any outsiders on sight, while feral beasts and roaming Scourge wander the mist-shrouded hills of eastern Tirisfal Glades.",al={"is faols rest dangerous","is faols rest safe","faols rest danger"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is this place called Faol's Rest?",a="The site was named in honor of Archbishop Alonsus Faol, who was originally interred here after his passing. Regarded as a saintly patriarch by the Church of the Holy Light, the memorial grounds were dedicated to give the beloved leader his final, peaceful rest.",al={"why is it called faols rest","who is faols rest named after","faols rest name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Every paladin owes their calling to Archbishop Faol, who first gathered Uther and his peers to unite the martial arts with the Holy Light. Standing before his untended tomb is a sobering reminder of the order's beginnings.",["class:Priest"]="As the supreme leader of the Church of the Holy Light through the Second War, Faol was the shepherd of all priesthoods in Lordaeron; seeing his monument ruined reflects the church's tragic ruin in the north."},kw={"alonsus faol","church of the holy light","faol's rest","graveyard","scarlet monastery","silver hand","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","zone:scarlet","topic:lordaeron","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge","topic:forsaken"},src={"Faol's Rest"}}
E["subzone:gallows-end-tavern"] = {t="subzone",n="Gallows' End Tavern",z="tirisfal",s="Gallows' End Tavern is the principal inn of Brill and the heart of Forsaken hospitality in Tirisfal Glades. Beyond offering shelter and spirits to weary travelers, it serves as an essential hub for class mentors, trade instructors, and covert interrogations.",h="Gallows' End Tavern is the principal inn of Brill and the heart of Forsaken hospitality in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Gathering Place for the Dead",b="Standing prominently along the road in Brill, Gallows' End Tavern serves as the primary gathering place for newly awakened Forsaken leaving Deathknell and journeying south toward the Undercity. Despite their undead condition, Forsaken still frequent its halls for fellowship, trade, and refuge. The inn houses various class instructors—guiding young warriors, priests, mages, rogues, and warlocks—as well as vendors and medical practitioners.",sp=0},{t="Secrets in the Cellar",b="Beneath the creaking floorboards and tavern laughter lies a far grimmer purpose. Down in the cellars, the Forsaken keep prisoners of war bound and guarded away from public view. Among them are a captured Scarlet Zealot and a Dwarven Mountaineer, held beneath the tavern for interrogation and experimentation as the Forsaken fight to secure their borders against both zealous crusaders and Alliance scouts.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Gallows' End Tavern located?",a="It is located in the center of Brill, the main Forsaken settlement just north of the Undercity in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"gallows end location","where is gallows end tavern","brill inn location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the undead need an inn or tavern?",a="While the Forsaken do not require sleep or mortal sustenance to survive, taverns like Gallows' End maintain social bonds and cultural habits from their past lives. It also acts as a secure civic center for training, commerce, and official business.",al={"why do undead drink","why do forsaken need taverns","undead inn lore"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is imprisoned in the basement?",a="In the cellars beneath the tavern, the Forsaken hold captive enemies including a Scarlet Zealot from the hostile Scarlet Crusade and an Alliance Dwarven Mountaineer.",al={"tavern prisoners","who is in the cellar","brill tavern dungeon"},it="target"},{q="What services and trainers can be found here?",a="The tavern features instructors for warriors, priests, mages, rogues, and warlocks, along with first aid and enchanting trainers, general trade vendors, and an innkeeper.",al={"gallows end trainers","who trains in brill inn","tavern vendors"},gp=true},{q="What is the Gallows' End Tavern?",a="Gallows' End Tavern serves as the social heart and vital resting haven of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Standing amidst the blighted ruins of Lordaeron, it is the premier fully functional tavern maintained by the Forsaken. Travelers, apothecary assistants, and passing adventurers congregate beneath its crooked timber beams to rest, seek council, trade supplies, and receive training in the dark and martial arts from resident mentors.",al={"what is gallows end tavern","tell me about gallows end tavern","gallows end tavern"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Gallows' End Tavern dangerous?",a="For loyal allies of the Forsaken and the Horde, the tavern is a fortified sanctuary protected by the deathguards of Brill. However, danger lingers just beneath the floorboards. The cellars serve as a holding pen for captured enemies—namely an interrogation-bound Scarlet Zealot and an Ironforge mountaineer—reminding all who enter that the war against living zealots and intruders is waged on their very doorstep.",al={"is gallows end dangerous","danger in gallows end tavern","is gallows end safe"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="As the first major inn encountered outside Deathknell, Gallows' End Tavern serves as the cultural and functional crossroads for newly awakened Forsaken entering the wider world.",["faction:Alliance"]="Beneath the taproom floors, the Forsaken keep captured Alliance scouts and Scarlet Crusade zealots detained for intelligence and interrogation."},kw={"brill","forsaken","gallows end","gallows' end tavern","innkeeper","scarlet zealot","tirisfal glades","undead"},rel={"subzone:brill","zone:tirisfal","npc:captured-scarlet-zealot","topic:forsaken","quest:365","npc:lars","topic:horde"},src={"Gallows' End Tavern"}}
E["subzone:garren-s-haunt"] = {t="subzone",n="Garren's Haunt",z="tirisfal",s="Garren's Haunt is an abandoned farmstead nestled in northern Tirisfal Glades. Once a prosperous human homestead, the farm fell to ruin when the Scourge wiped out the Garren family, leaving the overgrown grounds overrun by feral Rot Hide gnolls.",h="Garren's Haunt is an abandoned farmstead nestled in northern Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Blighted Homestead",b="Before the Third War, this farmstead supported a family and produced bountiful harvests under the Kingdom of Lordaeron. When the Plague of Undeath swept across the countryside, the Scourge descended upon the farm and slaughtered Garren and his kin. Today, the fields lie gray and withered, the farmhouse and barn decaying under the perpetual gloom of Tirisfal.",sp=0},{t="The Rot Hide Squatters",b="Rather than standard Scourge patrols, Garren's Haunt has been claimed by the Rot Hide gnolls—a pack of undead-tainted scavengers led by the vicious Maggot Eye. These scavengers aggressively defend their reclaimed territory, picking clean whatever remains of the farm and savaging any travelers or Forsaken patrols passing along northern Tirisfal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Garren?",a="Garren was a human farmer who lived here with his family prior to the Third War. They worked the surrounding fields until the Scourge overran Lordaeron, butchering Garren and his entire household during the spread of the plague.",al={"garren lore","who lived here","garren story"},it="identity"},{q="What creatures occupy the farm now?",a="The farm is primarily infested with Rot Hide gnolls led by Maggot Eye. Additionally, a vicious darkhound known as Plague can often be found lurking within the ruins of the barn.",al={"monsters at garrens haunt","what lives here","who controls the farm"},it="residents"},{q="Why is it called Garren's Haunt?",a="It bears the name of its murdered owner, Garren, with 'Haunt' denoting the grim, abandoned state of the land after the family was slain and their homestead was left to rot under the pall of Tirisfal.",al={"why called haunt","garren name origin"},it="name"},{q="Why haven't the Forsaken reclaimed it?",a="The Forsaken are stretched thin fortifying the Undercity and battling threats like the Scarlet Crusade and active Scourge remnants. Dealing with scavengers like the Rot Hide gnolls at outlying farms is largely left to passing mercenaries and adventurers.",al={"why forsaken ignore it","forsaken garrens haunt"},it="today"},{q="What is Garren's Haunt?",a="Garren's Haunt is an expansive, derelict farmstead situated in northern Tirisfal Glades. Before the fall of Lordaeron, it was a productive homestead featuring a large farmhouse, a barn, several silos, and wide fields. Today, blighted by the Plague of Undeath, the crops have withered into gray dust and the farm stands as a grim reminder of the kingdom's agrarian past.",al={"what is garrens haunt","garren's haunt farm","where is garrens haunt"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Garren's Haunt?",a="During the Scourging of Lordaeron, the Scourge swept across Tirisfal Glades and laid waste to the countryside. Garren and his entire family were brutally murdered when the undead overran their homestead. The blighted earth choked the life from the fields, leaving the land ruined and allowing carrion-seeking monsters to claim the empty structures.",al={"garrens haunt history","what happened here","how was garrens haunt destroyed"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is Garren's Haunt ruined and abandoned?",a="The farmstead was devastated when the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron, killing the resident family and leaving the buildings rotting under a corrupted sky. With its human owners slaughtered and the surrounding soil tainted beyond natural cultivation, no living farmers remain to tend the land, leaving the ruined structures to decay in the mist.",al={"why is garrens haunt ruined","why was garrens haunt abandoned","who ruined garrens haunt"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Garren's Haunt dangerous?",a="Yes, it is extremely dangerous for unwary travelers. The Rot Hide gnolls and their vicious leader, Maggot Eye, prowl the blight-choked fields and violently assault anyone who approaches. In addition to the savage gnolls, a monstrous darkhound known as Plague lurks within the shadows of the abandoned barn.",al={"is garrens haunt safe","dangers at garrens haunt","how dangerous is garrens haunt"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The ruined farm stands as a stark reminder of the slaughter that birthed the Forsaken, and clearing out the filthy Rot Hide gnolls helps secure northern Tirisfal for the Dark Lady."},kw={"darkhound","farmstead","garren's haunt","maggot eye","plague","rot hide","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","topic:gnoll","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:lordaeron","quest:358"},src={"Garren's Haunt"}}
E["subzone:gunther-s-retreat"] = {t="subzone",n="Gunther's Retreat",z="tirisfal",s="Gunther's Retreat is an isolated island sanctuary set in Brightwater Lake in central Tirisfal Glades. It serves as the refuge of Gunther Arcanus, a renegade necromancer who broke free from the Scourge through raw willpower. Distrustful of all outsiders, the recluse surrounds his campsite with loyal undead minions to keep intruders away.",h="Gunther's Retreat is an isolated island sanctuary set in Brightwater Lake in central Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Recluse in Brightwater Lake",b="Situated amid the misty waters of Brightwater Lake, Gunther's Retreat is an unassuming island encampment easily mistaken for another pocket of the Scourge. Its master, Gunther Arcanus, achieved what few mortal sorcerers could: when raised in undeath, his arcane mastery allowed him to tear away his soul from the Lich King's iron grip without any outside help. Mistrusting everyone, Gunther assumed the rest of the undead wandering Lordaeron were still puppets of the Scourge. He retired to this lonely isle to practice his craft in peace, posting zombie sentinels to tear apart anyone who crosses the water.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who lives at Gunther's Retreat?",a="The island is inhabited by Gunther Arcanus, a powerful necromancer who broke free of the Lich King's control under his own power. He resides there alongside a retinue of undead thralls who guard his camp.",al={"gunther island","who is on the island","gunther's retreat master"},it="residents"},{q="Why are the undead on the island hostile?",a="Gunther Arcanus believes that every other undead being in Tirisfal is still enslaved by the Scourge. Because he values his independence above all else, his minions are commanded to attack anyone who approaches the island on sight.",al={"why does gunther attack","why hostile","is gunther scourge"},it="hostile"},{q="Is Gunther aligned with the Forsaken?",a="Initially, no. While technically a free-willed undead like the Forsaken, Gunther isolates himself entirely. The authorities in the Undercity seek to prove to him that they too are free of the Lich King's grasp and recruit his talents to their cause.",al={"is gunther in the horde","gunther forsaken","gunther undercity"},it="side"},{q="What is the small islet southwest of the retreat?",a="Just off the southwestern edge of Gunther's Retreat lies a tiny, unnamed islet known as Lillith's Dinner Table, a curious and eerie landmark resting quietly in the waters of Brightwater Lake.",al={"lillith dinner table","small island near gunther","table island"},it="place"},{q="What is Gunther's Retreat?",a="Gunther's Retreat is an isolated island nestled in Brightwater Lake in central Tirisfal Glades. Sheltered by surrounding waters, the island serves as the secluded sanctuary of Gunther Arcanus, a powerful free-willed lich. Cut off from the rest of Lordaeron, the grounds are patrolled by shambling undead servants guarding their reclusive master.",al={"what is gunthers retreat","gunthers retreat lore","brightwater lake island"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is Gunther's Retreat dangerous?",a="Yes, it is very dangerous to approach unprepared. Gunther Arcanus mistakenly believes all other undead in Lordaeron are mindless thralls of the Scourge. To protect his sanctuary, he commands his zombie minions to violently attack any who set foot on the island shores.",al={"is gunthers retreat dangerous","gunther retreat safe","danger gunther retreat"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Gunther's Retreat?",a="The island takes its name from Gunther Arcanus, the free-willed lich who made Brightwater Lake his private sanctuary. After breaking away and retaining his independence in death, Gunther retreated here to escape the reach of the Scourge and seclude himself from the wider world.",al={"why called gunthers retreat","gunthers retreat name origin","who named gunthers retreat"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Gunther Arcanus mirrors the Forsaken's own tragedy: free from the Lich King, yet suspicious and guarded against a world he assumes remains in thrall to the Scourge.",["class:Mage"]="Arcanists will appreciate Gunther's rare feat; his magical will was formidable enough to sever the Lich King's mental grip entirely unassisted."},kw={"brightwater lake","gunther arcanus","gunther's retreat","lich","lillith's dinner table","tirisfal glades","undead"},rel={"npc:gunther-arcanus","subzone:brightwater-lake","quest:357","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","topic:scourge"},src={"Gunther's Retreat"}}
E["subzone:night-web-s-hollow"] = {t="subzone",n="Night Web's Hollow",z="tirisfal",s="Night Web's Hollow is a sunken vale and abandoned gold mine located just northwest of Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. Heavily overrun with arachnids, it serves as a testing ground for freshly awakened Forsaken sent to cull the vermin and prove their combat readiness.",h="Night Web's Hollow is a sunken vale and abandoned gold mine located just northwest of Deathknell in Tirisfal...",sec={{t="The Infested Mine",b="Tucked away in the northern hills of Deathknell, Night Web's Hollow was once an ordinary human gold mine before the plague swept through Lordaeron. In the aftermath of the kingdom's fall, the shafts were entirely abandoned to nature. Today, thick strands of webbing shroud the rock walls and suffocating shadows conceal a thriving colony of aggressive night web spiders. Freshly raised Forsaken are routinely dispatched into the hollow and its subterranean tunnels to cull the hatchlings, slay their voracious matriarch, and harden their resolve before moving onward into Tirisfal Glades.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Night Web's Hollow?",a="It is an abandoned gold mine and small ravine northwest of Deathknell. Overgrown with webs and swarming with aggressive spiders, it is one of the earliest proving grounds for new Forsaken recruits.",al={"night webs hollow","spider mine deathknell","spider cave tirisfal"},it="place"},{q="Why are the Forsaken clearing out the spiders?",a="The encroaching spiders pose an immediate hazard on the perimeter of Deathknell. Executors in town use the infestation as a trial to ensure newly risen undead have regained control over their motor skills and can handle basic combat.",al={"why kill spiders","deathknell spider quest"},it="purpose"},{q="What lives deep inside the mine shafts?",a="While younger spiders skitter outside in the vale, full-grown Night Web Spiders prowl the mine tunnels. In the deepest, darkest chamber sits the Night Web Matriarch, the broodmother of the entire infestation.",al={"spider boss mine","night web matriarch location"},it="threat"},{q="Was this always a spider den?",a="No. Before the fall of Lordaeron, it was a working gold mine used by the local human populace. After the Scourge wiped out the region, the shafts fell into disrepair and the spiders moved into the dark, sheltered tunnels.",al={"who dug the mine","deathknell mine history"},it="history"},{q="Who lives in Night Web's Hollow?",a="No civilized folk dwell here. The hollow and its old mine tunnels are overrun entirely by aggressive Night Web Spiders. Outside, younger arachnids skitter through the shaded vale, while fully grown spiders and their massive matriarch nest deep within the dark shafts.",al={"who lives in night web hollow","night webs hollow inhabitants","who is in night webs hollow"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Why is the mine in Night Web's Hollow abandoned?",a="Like much of Tirisfal Glades, the old gold mine fell into ruin during the Third War when the Scourge plague ravaged the kingdom of Lordaeron. With its miners dead or scattered, the shafts were left deserted, allowing the local spiders to claim the hollow and weave their nests throughout the depths.",al={"why is night webs hollow abandoned","why was night web mine abandoned","what happened to the gold mine"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Night Web's Hollow dangerous?",a="It poses a genuine threat to newly awakened Forsaken. While lone young spiders along the path are manageable, the mine interior teems with venomous adult arachnids, culminating in their vicious matriarch waiting in the deepest recesses.",al={"is night webs hollow dangerous","how dangerous is night webs hollow","night webs hollow danger"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Night Web's Hollow?",a="The valley takes its name directly from the Night Web Spiders that blanket the hollow and its abandoned mine in thick, shadowy webs. Their sprawling nests choke the valley, giving the desolate ravine its menacing title.",al={"why is it called night webs hollow","night webs hollow name origin","how did night webs hollow get its name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the freshly risen, the hollow is one of your very first trials. Slaying the arachnids proves you possess the focus and willpower required to serve the Dark Lady.",["profession:Mining"]="Although the shafts were originally excavated for gold, mineral operations here have long since ceased due to the lethal arachnid infestation."},kw={"deathknell","mine","night web matriarch","night web's hollow","spiders","tirisfal glades"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken"},src={"Night Web's Hollow"}}
E["subzone:nightmare-vale"] = {t="subzone",n="Nightmare Vale",z="tirisfal",s="Nightmare Vale is a shadowed hollow nestled in southwestern Tirisfal Glades, just west of the Scarlet Watchtower. Once a lush forest haven, its trees have withered under the blight, and vicious predators now roam among the twisted trunks.",h="Nightmare Vale is a shadowed hollow nestled in southwestern Tirisfal Glades, just west of the Scarlet...",sec={{t="A Decayed Wilderness",b="Before the Third War, this valley was a verdant part of the Tirisfal countryside, filled with songbirds and standard woodland game. In the wake of the Scourge's plague, the vegetation has blackened and rotted away. Ravenous darkhounds and massive vampire bats have devoured the native wildlife, turning the hollow into a grim haven for scavengers and beasts of prey.",sp=0},{t="Stalkers of the Hollow",b="Lurking in the shadows of Nightmare Vale is Ressan the Needler, an unusually large, pale vampire bat notorious among local travelers. Wedged uncomfortably between the haunted wilderness and the zealous sentries at the nearby Scarlet Watchtower, the vale is an unforgiving expanse where unwary adventurers quickly become prey.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Nightmare Vale located?",a="Nightmare Vale lies in the southwestern reaches of Tirisfal Glades, tucked just west of the Scarlet Watchtower.",al={"nightmare vale location","where is nightmare vale","find nightmare vale"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is the valley called Nightmare Vale?",a="Once a peaceful, vibrant forest, the valley succumbed completely to the plague that devastated Lordaeron. Its withered trees and aggressive predators—hungry darkhounds and blood-drinking bats—make it a waking nightmare for anyone passing through.",al={"why nightmare vale","nightmare vale lore"},it="name"},{q="What creatures roam Nightmare Vale?",a="The vale is overrun by savage darkhounds and giant vampire bats that have driven out or consumed the native forest animals. The rare white vampire bat, Ressan the Needler, also prowls this hollow.",al={"nightmare vale mobs","monsters in nightmare vale","beasts here"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Ressan the Needler?",a="Ressan the Needler is a rare, stark-white vampire bat known to stalk the darkened skies and withered canopy of Nightmare Vale.",al={"ressan","ressan the needler","rare bat tirisfal"},it="identity"},{q="What is Nightmare Vale?",a="Nightmare Vale is a wide, blighted valley nestled in southwestern Tirisfal Glades, just west of the Scarlet Watchtower. Once a lush forest teeming with vibrant woodland creatures, the vale has fallen completely into decay. The trees have withered away, and the once-peaceful glade has become a desolate hunting ground prowled by ravenous darkhounds and monstrous vampire bats.",al={"what is nightmare vale","nightmare vale lore","tell me about nightmare vale"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened to Nightmare Vale?",a="Like much of Tirisfal Glades, Nightmare Vale succumbed to the devastating plague and corruption that swept through Lordaeron. The verdant canopy withered and died under the blight, and the native wildlife vanished, supplanted or consumed by corrupted predators. Today, it stands as a bleak reminder of the kingdom's fall.",al={"nightmare vale history","how did nightmare vale fall","what happened here"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Nightmare Vale dangerous?",a="Yes, Nightmare Vale poses a serious threat to solitary travelers. The valley is infested with ravenous darkhounds and enormous vampire bats that hunt anything straying through the dead woods. In addition, the notorious and vicious white bat, Ressan the Needler, is known to stalk these shadows.",al={"how dangerous is nightmare vale","is it safe here","threats in nightmare vale"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The twisted wildlife of Nightmare Vale serves as a reminder of the plague's lingering footprint across Tirisfal, posing an immediate threat to Forsaken travelers venturing beyond Brill.",["class:Hunter"]="The vale is home to vicious darkhounds and vampire bats, including the rare white bat Ressan the Needler, making it a noteworthy proving ground for hunters seeking fearsome beasts."},kw={"darkhounds","nightmare vale","ressan the needler","scarlet watchtower","tirisfal glades","vampire bats"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:scarlet-watchtower"},src={"Nightmare Vale"}}
E["subzone:north-coast"] = {t="subzone",n="North Coast",z="tirisfal",s="The North Coast is a bleak, fog-shrouded stretch of shoreline marking the northern edge of Tirisfal Glades along the Great Sea. Tainted by the fall of Lordaeron, the cold beaches are littered with rotting marine life and aggressively defended by tribes of hostile murlocs.",h="The North Coast is a bleak, fog-shrouded stretch of shoreline marking the northern edge of Tirisfal Glades...",sec={{t="A Blighted Shoreline",b="Before the Third War, these northern waters and stony beaches were part of the peaceful northern boundary of Lordaeron. When the Plague of Undeath swept across the continent, its corrupting blight seeped down the bluffs and poisoned the coast. The waters turned chill and murky, washing up the carcasses of giant sea creatures that now rot upon the sand amid the perennial coastal fog.",sp=0},{t="The Vile Fin Shore",b="With the human kingdom shattered and the Forsaken focusing their attention around Brill and the Capital City, coastal murlocs claimed the desolate sands. The local Vile Fin tribe maintains huts along the surf, utilizing nearby shallows as spawning grounds. Swarms of ravenous murlocs, including notable figures like Deeb and numerous growing tadpoles, violently guard the tideline against any surface-dwellers.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What creatures inhabit the North Coast?",a="The beach is primarily occupied by aggressive Vile Fin murlocs, their tadpoles, and scavengers picking at the rotting sea life that washes ashore from the Great Sea.",al={"north coast mobs","what lives on north coast","monsters north coast"},it="residents"},{q="Who is Deeb?",a="Deeb is an individual murloc who can be found lingering along the North Coast among the huts and shallows of the shore.",al={"deeb","murloc deeb","who is deeb"},it="people"},{q="Why is the coast covered in rotting carcasses?",a="The Plague of Undeath that devastated Lordaeron seeped into the coastal ecology, poisoning the surrounding shallows and causing dead marine beasts to wash ashore to decay on the blighted sand.",al={"dead sea monsters","beached whales tirisfal","why dead creatures"},it="ruin"},{q="Is the North Coast contested by any major factions?",a="Neither the Forsaken nor any remnants of the Alliance actively garrison the North Coast. It remains a wild, untamed fringe left largely to murlocs and sea vermin.",al={"who owns north coast","forsaken presence north coast"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the North Coast?",a="The North Coast is a desolate strip of shore along the northern boundary of Tirisfal Glades, bordering the Great Sea. Tainted by the Plague of Undeath that ravaged Lordaeron, its chill waters wash ashore decaying sea creatures, and crude huts belonging to hostile coastal murlocs dot the sands.",al={"what is north coast","north coast overview","about north coast"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened to the North Coast?",a="Once a peaceful shoreline of northern Lordaeron, the coast fell victim to the Plague of Undeath alongside the rest of the kingdom. The blight poisoned the waters and marine life, leaving the beaches strewn with dead sea beasts while savage murloc tribes moved in to claim the blighted sands.",al={"north coast history","what happened here","north coast lore"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the North Coast dangerous?",a="Yes. The beach is overrun by vicious murlocs, including the local Vile Fin tribe and their kin, who fiercely defend their crude settlements and nearby spawning grounds. Between these aggressive amphibious packs and the lingering chill of the plague, unwary travelers will quickly find themselves swarmed and cut down.",al={"is north coast dangerous","threats north coast","north coast hazards"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="While Forsaken patrol routes cling to the road through Brill, clearing the North Coast of encroaching murlocs ensures the northern flank of Tirisfal Glades remains relatively secure."},kw={"beach","deeb","great sea","murloc","north coast","tirisfal glades","vile fin"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","topic:murloc","subzone:brill","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:lordaeron"},src={"North Coast"}}
E["subzone:scarlet-monastery"] = {t="subzone",n="Scarlet Monastery",z="tirisfal",s="Perched in the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery was once a peaceful center of scholarship and worship for the Church of the Holy Light. In the wake of Lordaeron's fall, it has become a heavily fortified redoubt for the fanatical Scarlet Crusade. Anyone approaching without red colors is treated as an infected carrier of the plague and executed on sight.",h="Perched in the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery was once a peaceful center of...",sec={{t="From Sanctuary to Stronghold",b="Before the Third War, this complex was known simply as the Old Monastery. Under the stewardship of Lordaeron's priesthood and the patronage of noble houses, it served as a vital center for theological study, sacred archives, and paladin training. When the Kingdom of Lordaeron fell to the Scourge, surviving remnants of the Silver Hand reclaimed the site as a forward bastion to strike against the undead occupying Capital City. As the defenders devolved into the zealous Scarlet Crusade, they fortified the cloister, turning its peaceful libraries, gardens, and cathedral into an armed citadel.",sp=0},{t="A Bastion of Fanaticism",b="Divided into four distinct wings—a defiled graveyard, an expansive library, an armory, and a grand cathedral—the Monastery now houses a massive garrison commanded by Scarlet Commander Mograine and High Inquisitor Whitemane. The Crusaders here view all outsiders, whether living or undead, Alliance or Horde, as plague-bearers. While the Forsaken seek to eliminate the garrison threatening their very doorstep, Alliance leadership increasingly views the Crusade's violent extremism as a dangerous perversion of the Light.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Monastery here?",a="It was originally built during the reign of Lordaeron as a peaceful sanctuary for study, housing sacred scriptures and training paladins. Surviving crusaders seized and fortified it after the kingdom fell to serve as a military staging ground against the undead in Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what is scarlet monastery","why did they build monastery in tirisfal","monastery purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does the Scarlet Crusade attack Alliance members?",a="The Crusade's zealotry has spiraled into complete paranoia. They believe anyone who has traveled outside their secured compounds could be a carrier of the Scourge's plague of undeath, and they refuse to take chances with outsiders regardless of race or allegiance.",al={"why are scarlet hostile to alliance","are scarlet crusade alliance","why do crusaders attack humans"},it="hostile"},{q="Who leads the forces within the Monastery?",a="The garrison is held by Scarlet Commander Mograine, who directs military operations from within the cathedral, and High Inquisitor Whitemane, a zealous priestess renowned for her devotion and resurrection powers.",al={"who is the boss of scarlet monastery","monastery leaders","who runs the scarlet crusade here"},it="leader"},{q="What are the four wings of the complex?",a="The complex is split into four distinct areas: the Graveyard, overrun by restless dead and torture chambers; the Library, preserving historical archives and tomes; the Armory, where the Crusade manufactures weapons and armor; and the Cathedral, the spiritual heart and commander's seat.",al={"monastery sections","what wings are in scarlet monastery","monastery layout"}},{q="What is the Scarlet Monastery?",a="Tucked into the northeastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Monastery was once a peaceful cathedral and center of learning for Lordaeron's priesthood. Following the Third War and the devastation of the kingdom, the Scarlet Crusade seized the ancient sanctuary and transformed it into a heavily fortified stronghold. Its halls, once dedicated to enlightenment and the training of holy paladins, now echo with fanatical zealotry.",al={"what is scarlet monastery","scarlet monastery lore","tell me about scarlet monastery"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Scarlet Monastery?",a="Originally known as the Old Monastery under the Church of the Holy Light, the complex served Archbishop Alonsus Faol and trained clerics and paladins, including a young Alexandros Mograine. When the Scourge overran Lordaeron, surviving Knights of the Silver Hand reclaimed the site as a redoubt. After the order fractured, the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade took complete control, purging the grounds of lingering undead and repurposing the peaceful holy seat into an armed fortress.",al={"history of scarlet monastery","what happened here","monastery past"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Scarlet Monastery dangerous?",a="It is exceptionally dangerous. The complex is garrisoned by hundreds of heavily armed, zealous crusaders who view any outsider as a potential carrier of the plague of undeath. Whether you belong to the Horde or the Alliance, their standing orders are merciless execution. Even beyond their martial soldiers, the inner sanctum is protected by high-ranking inquisitors whose holy powers can resurrect the fallen back into combat.",al={"is scarlet monastery dangerous","how dangerous is the monastery","is it safe to enter"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Scarlet Monastery?",a="Before the Third War, the site was known simply as the Old Monastery or the Monastery of Lordaeron. When the remnants of the Silver Hand fractured and the extremist Scarlet Crusade reclaimed the complex from the Scourge and fortified it as their regional headquarters, they renamed it in honor of their crimson order.",al={"why is it called scarlet monastery","scarlet monastery name origin","where does the name come from"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The Monastery sits right on the Undercity's doorstep, making its heavily armed crusaders an active, existential threat to Forsaken territory.",["class:Paladin"]="The complex once trained paladins under the Silver Hand, making its corruption into an intolerant bastion of fanaticism a bitter sight for faithful holy warriors."},kw={"armory","cathedral","graveyard","library","mograine","old monastery","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","tirisfal glades","whitemane"},rel={"zone:scarlet","subzone:scarlet-monastery-cathedral","subzone:scarlet-monastery-armory","subzone:scarlet-monastery-library","subzone:scarlet-monastery-graveyard","npc:scarlet-commander-mograine","topic:scarlet-crusade","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Scarlet Monastery"}}
E["subzone:scarlet-watch-post"] = {t="subzone",n="Scarlet Watch Post",z="tirisfal",s="The Scarlet Watch Post is an isolated watchtower stationed in the rocky hills of northeastern Tirisfal Glades, just north of the Scarlet Monastery. Maintained by a vigilant garrison of Crusaders, its sole purpose is to safeguard the monastery's vulnerable northern flank against flanking assaults.",h="The Scarlet Watch Post is an isolated watchtower stationed in the rocky hills of northeastern Tirisfal...",sec={{t="Guarding the Northern Approach",b="Perched in the rugged heights behind the Scarlet Monastery, the Scarlet Watch Post serves as a critical early-warning bastion for the zealots below. While the southern road up to the monastery gate receives heavy traffic and fortifications, the rugged cliffs along Tirisfal's northern coastline provide a tempting back route for infiltrators. To eliminate this blind spot, the Scarlet Crusade maintains this fortified stone tower—one of several Crusader outposts scattered across Tirisfal Glades. Under the stern command of Captain Melrache, a mixed force of Neophytes, Vanguards, and devout Friars keeps ceaseless vigil over the cliffs, ensuring neither the Scourge nor the Forsaken can surprise the order from behind.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Scarlet Watch Post?",a="The outpost protects the Scarlet Monastery from being flanked. While the monastery's main entrance faces south toward the Tirisfal roads, its rear slopes toward the northern wilderness, leaving it vulnerable to coordinated assaults. The watch post ensures sentries spot and intercept any intruders attempting a northern bypass.",al={"why is scarlet watch post here","scarlet watch post purpose","crusaders run role"},it="purpose"},{q="Who commands the Scarlet Watch Post?",a="Captain Melrache oversees the garrison stationed at the tower. He commands a defensive retinue including personal bodyguards, front-line Vanguards, aspiring Neophytes, and spellcasting Friars.",al={"scarlet watch post leader","who leads scarlet watch post","captain melrache"},it="in_charge"},{q="Is the Scarlet Watch Post related to the other towers in Tirisfal?",a="Yes. It is one of four distinct Crusader outposts ringing the northern and western reaches of Tirisfal Glades. These stone towers create a defensive perimeter to monitor Scourge activity and contain the Forsaken expansion from the Undercity.",al={"tirisfal towers","crusader outposts tirisfal","other scarlet towers"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Scarlet Watch Post?",a="The Scarlet Watch Post is an isolated watchtower stationed in the rugged hills of northeastern Tirisfal Glades, just north of the Scarlet Monastery. It serves as a fortified scout outpost built to guard the vulnerable northern approach to the Crusade's mighty bastion.",al={"what is scarlet watch post","scarlet watch post lore","what is this tower"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Scarlet Watch Post dangerous?",a="Yes, exceedingly so for any outsider. The post is garrisoned by zealous Scarlet crusaders, including friars, neophytes, and heavily armed vanguards and bodyguards sworn to butcher any intruder, living or undead, on sight.",al={"is scarlet watch post dangerous","scarlet watch post threat","watch post danger"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken, the Scarlet Crusade is an existential threat lingering right on the Undercity's doorstep; purging isolated towers like this disrupts the zealots' communication network and secures northern Tirisfal.",["faction:Horde"]="The Crusaders view all outsiders—living or dead—as tainted carriers of undeath, making this watch post strictly hostile ground to any Horde travelers braving the hills."},kw={"captain melrache","crusader outpost","crusader's run","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","scarlet watch post","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","zone:scarlet","subzone:crusader-s-outpost","topic:scarlet-crusade"},src={"Scarlet Watch Post"}}
E["subzone:scarlet-watchtower"] = {t="subzone",n="Scarlet Watchtower",z="tirisfal",s="The Scarlet Watchtower is a ruined scout outpost nestled in the southern hills of Tirisfal Glades. Held by the zealous Scarlet Crusade, it provides a direct vantage over aerial traffic entering and leaving the Undercity. Its close proximity to the Forsaken capital's rear sewer entrance makes it a constant thorn in the side of the local undead defenders.",h="The Scarlet Watchtower is a ruined scout outpost nestled in the southern hills of Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Thorn at the Gates",b="Perched above the southern ridge of Tirisfal Glades, the Scarlet Watchtower serves as an audacious observation post for the Scarlet Crusade. Standing mere paces away from the sewer back entrance of the Undercity, the ruined tower allows crusader scouts to monitor incoming and outgoing flight routes and surface movements into Queen Sylvanas's capital. Commanded by Captain Perrine, the zealots stationed here dare to gaze directly into the heart of Forsaken territory, seeking any opportunity to gather intelligence or strike at the undead they consider abominations.",sp=0},{t="Network of Zealotry",b="The tower is not an isolated redoubt, but one of four Crusader's Outposts established across Tirisfal Glades. Together, these vantage points form an early-warning web and scouting perimeter for Scarlet forces operating against Lordaeron's new masters. Its position so close to the ruined kingdom's seat of power makes it an intolerable insult to the Royal Apothecary Society and Forsaken commanders, who regularly direct emerging champions to thin the outpost's ranks and silence Captain Perrine.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade so close to the Undercity?",a="The Scarlet Crusade established the watchtower to monitor flight routes and scouting movement coming in and out of the Forsaken capital. Its high vantage point near the southern hills allows them to keep an eye on undead surface operations and the nearby sewer exit.",al={"why scarlet tower near undercity","scarlet watchtower purpose","why are scarlets here"},it="purpose"},{q="Who commands the Scarlet Watchtower?",a="The outpost is commanded by Captain Perrine, a Scarlet Crusade officer who directs local scouts and maintains surveillance over the approaches to the Undercity.",al={"scarlet watchtower boss","tower commander","captain perrine"},it="leader"},{q="How does this tower fit into the wider Scarlet presence in Tirisfal?",a="This tower is one of four Crusader's Outposts strategically scattered across Tirisfal Glades. Together, they create a scouting network designed to harass Forsaken patrols and keep the Scarlet Crusade informed of undead maneuvers.",al={"other scarlet towers","crusader outposts tirisfal","scarlet network"},it="ties"},{q="What is the tower's connection to the Sewers of the Undercity?",a="The tower sits directly in the southern hills near the drainage canal leading into the Undercity Sewers. This backdoor passage is an important alternative route into the capital, making the presence of hostile crusaders right above it an active security hazard for the Forsaken.",al={"tower near sewers","undercity back door tower","sewer entrance scarlet"},it="threat"},{q="What is the Scarlet Watchtower?",a="The Scarlet Watchtower is a ruined scout post perched in southern Tirisfal Glades, standing right near the back entrance to the Undercity's sewers. It is one of four Crusader's Outposts established across the glades, held by fanatical Scarlet Crusade troops to monitor flights and travel arriving at and departing from the Forsaken capital.",al={"what is scarlet watchtower","scarlet watchtower tirisfal","about scarlet watchtower"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Scarlet Watchtower dangerous?",a="Yes, it is very dangerous to anyone outside the Scarlet Crusade, particularly members of the Horde and the Forsaken. The zealots stationed here are heavily armed, utterly hostile, and actively looking to eradicate what they see as the undead scourge creeping through Lordaeron.",al={"is scarlet watchtower dangerous","scarlet watchtower threat","is it safe near scarlet watchtower"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The existence of an enemy observation tower practically peering into your capital's drainage exits is an unacceptable indignity that local Forsaken authorities are eager to eliminate.",["faction:Horde"]="Travelers and scouts arriving on bat-back to the Undercity risk coming under observation from the zealous sharpshooters stationed on the heights of this tower."},kw={"captain perrine","crusader's outpost","scarlet crusade","scarlet watchtower","sewers","tirisfal glades","undercity"},rel={"topic:scarlet-crusade","subzone:crusader-s-outpost","zone:undercity","subzone:sewers","zone:tirisfal","topic:forsaken","quest:94"},src={"Scarlet Watchtower"}}
E["subzone:shadow-grave"] = {t="subzone",n="Shadow Grave",z="tirisfal",s="Shadow Grave is a stone crypt perched atop the sloping burial grounds above Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades. It serves as the physical awakening point where newly liberated Forsaken shake off the stupor of death and the Lich King's faded control.",h="Shadow Grave is a stone crypt perched atop the sloping burial grounds above Deathknell in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="Cradle of the Forsaken",b="Tucked into the rocky foothills behind Deathknell Graves, the Shadow Grave is a simple stone mausoleum with an extraordinary purpose. Inside its damp vaults, corpses raised into the mindless servitude of the Scourge gradually awaken to free will. Under the watchful eye of caretakers like Undertaker Mordo, these freshly liberated undead stand up from their stone slabs and take their first shuddering steps into a world that despises them, setting out down the footpath toward Deathknell to pledge themselves to the Banshee Queen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Shadow Grave used for?",a="It is the crypt where newly awakened Forsaken rise into consciousness. Instead of remaining mindless Scourge thralls in the dirt, these resurrected individuals regain their free will inside the tomb and begin their second life serving Sylvanas Windrunner.",al={"shadow grave purpose","what is shadow grave","shadow grave crypt"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Shadow Grave located?",a="Shadow Grave is situated at the top of the hill above the Deathknell Graves, backed against the southern mountains of Tirisfal Glades. A dirt trail winds downhill from its entrance toward the settlement of Deathknell.",al={"where is shadow grave","shadow grave location","shadow grave map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who oversees the newly awakened here?",a="Undertaker Mordo attends the newly awakened within the tomb, assessing whether those rising from the slabs have recovered their minds and are fit to serve the Forsaken cause.",al={"who is in shadow grave","shadow grave npcs","undertaker mordo location"},it="residents"},{q="What is the Shadow Grave?",a="The Shadow Grave is a secluded stone crypt situated high on a hillside among the Deathknell Graves in western Tirisfal Glades. Connected to the settlement of Deathknell by a winding downward path, this solemn tomb serves as the quiet threshold where newly awakened Forsaken first claw their way back into consciousness, sheltered from the threats that roam the surrounding glades.",al={"what is shadow grave","shadow grave lore","tell me about shadow grave"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Shadow Grave dangerous?",a="The crypt itself remains relatively safe, functioning under the vigilant care of the Forsaken attendants stationed here to assist the newly awakened. However, just beyond its stone doorway lies the perils of Tirisfal Glades, where mindlessly wandering Scourge undead, scavengers, and the ever-watchful agents of the Scarlet Crusade threaten anyone who strays too far unprepared.",al={"is shadow grave dangerous","shadow grave safe","are there enemies in shadow grave"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every Forsaken adventurer begins their journey on the cold stone slabs of this crypt, opening their eyes to the grim reality of unlife and free will."},kw={"awakening","crypt","deathknell","shadow grave","starting tomb","tirisfal glades","undertaker mordo"},rel={"subzone:deathknell","zone:tirisfal"},src={"Shadow Grave"}}
E["subzone:solliden-farmstead"] = {t="subzone",n="Solliden Farmstead",z="tirisfal",s="Solliden Farmstead is an active human pumpkin farm nestled in western Tirisfal Glades, just north of Deathknell. It represents a rare anomaly in northern Lordaeron: living native farmers who survived the Plague of Undeath and cling fiercely to their soil under the shadow of the Scarlet Crusade.",h="Solliden Farmstead is an active human pumpkin farm nestled in western Tirisfal Glades, just north of...",sec={{t="A Defiant Remnant",b="While most of Lordaeron fell to ruin and rot, the fields of Solliden Farmstead still produce healthy crops. The settlement traces its lineage back centuries to when immigrant noble families from Strom acquired pioneer plots across Tirisfal. Today, these surviving human farmers live under the watchful shield of the Scarlet Crusade patrolling from a nearby tower. Traumatized and insular, they defend their homestead with unyielding hostility, striking at any outsiders who draw near—Horde and Alliance alike.",sp=0},{t="Eyes in the Undercity",b="To the Forsaken just emerging from Deathknell, Solliden is both a threat and a resource. The Royal Apothecary Society eyes the farm's pristine pumpkins as prime organic testing material for brewing their New Plague. Meanwhile, Forsaken officers like Deathguard Simmer send fledgling recruits to raid the fields, steal those pumpkins for Apothecary Johaan in Brill, and teach the living a lesson.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are there living humans farming in Tirisfal Glades?",a="The Solliden farmers are native Lordaeron humans who miraculously escaped infection during the Third War. Shielded by the Scarlet Crusade patrolling from a nearby tower, they stubbornly continue cultivating the land rather than fleeing their ancestral home.",al={"why are they alive","who are these farmers","living humans in tirisfal"},it="residents"},{q="Why are the Solliden farmers hostile to Alliance players?",a="Living through the fall of Lordaeron has left the farmers paranoid, bitter, and completely untrusting of anyone outside their immediate circle. Protected by the fanatical Scarlet Crusade, they view all trespassers as potential threats or infected scavengers, attacking Alliance and Horde travelers indiscriminately.",al={"why do farmers attack alliance","are they alliance","why red to alliance"},it="hostile"},{q="Why do the Forsaken want pumpkins from Solliden Farmstead?",a="Apothecaries serving the Royal Apothecary Society require fresh, uncorrupted organic matter to test and refine their New Plague. The plump, untainted pumpkins growing in Solliden's soil serve as ideal living hosts for developing toxic strains.",al={"why steal pumpkins","fields of grief pumpkin quest","royal apothecary society pumpkins"},it="item"},{q="Who is Deathguard Simmer, and why does he target the farm?",a="Deathguard Simmer is a Forsaken officer stationed near Deathknell. He sends fledgling Forsaken to raid the farm, steal ten of its pumpkins for Apothecary Johaan in Brill, and teach the humans and their Scarlet Crusade protectors a lesson.",al={"why kill the farmers","deathguard simmer quest","reaping the reapers"},it="threat"},{q="What is Solliden Farmstead?",a="Solliden Farmstead is an active pumpkin farm located in western Tirisfal Glades, tucked just north of Deathknell. Remarkably, it stands as one of the very few places in northern Lordaeron where living native humans managed to survive the Plague of Undeath entirely uncorrupted, clinging fiercely to their soil under the shadow of the nearby Scarlet Crusade.",al={"what is solliden farmstead","solliden farm","tell me about solliden farmstead"},it="place",add=true},{q="What is the history of Solliden Farmstead?",a="The land was first settled thousands of years ago during the expansion of the ancient empire of Arathor. Roughly twelve hundred years before the opening of the Dark Portal, wealthy noble families migrating from Strom purchased large swathes of established farmland across Tirisfal Glades, securing plots including what became the Solliden Farmstead. Against all odds, it remained an active agricultural estate right through the Third War.",al={"solliden farm history","who founded solliden farm","what happened at solliden farmstead"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Solliden Farmstead dangerous?",a="Yes, if you get too close. The surviving farmers have grown deeply paranoid and aggressive from surviving surrounded by horrors. Rather than welcoming outsiders, they will attack both Horde and Alliance travelers on sight. Furthermore, the farm sits in close proximity to the Scarlet Crusade's defenses at the Scarlet Palisade, making the surrounding area treacherous.",al={"is solliden farm dangerous","are solliden farmers dangerous","solliden farmstead threat"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the newly risen Forsaken, Solliden Farmstead is an immediate lesson in realpolitik: living Lordaeronians who despise what you have become, serving as both raw material for Apothecary toxins and a potential southern beachhead.",["faction:Alliance"]="Seeing living humans tilling Lordaeron's soil brings a glimmer of hope, but the bitter reality is that these isolated survivors view Stormwind's citizens with the same xenophobic hatred they hold for the undead."},kw={"deathguard simmer","deathknell","fields of grief","pumpkins","scarlet palisade","solliden","solliden farmstead","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:deathknell","quest:365","npc:deathguard-simmer","topic:royal-apothecary-society","topic:scarlet-crusade"},src={"Solliden Farmstead"}}
E["subzone:stillwater-pond"] = {t="subzone",n="Stillwater Pond",z="tirisfal",s="Stillwater Pond is a quiet, stagnant body of water resting just west of Brill in Tirisfal Glades. Once a beloved picnic and swimming spot for the living children of Lordaeron, it has decayed into a murky pool inhabited by skeletal fish and prowled by dangerous scavengers.",h="Stillwater Pond is a quiet, stagnant body of water resting just west of Brill in Tirisfal Glades.",sec={{t="A Ruined Respite",b="Before the Third War and the ravages of the Plague of Undeath, Stillwater Pond served as a tranquil retreat for the folk of Tirisfal Glades. Families from nearby hamlets gathered on its grassy banks for afternoon picnics, and children too young to brave the treacherous depths of Brightwater Lake learned to swim in its shallows. Today, the idyllic waters have turned lifeless and gray. The living have long vanished, leaving only skeletal fish darting beneath the surface and wandering undead drifting through the surrounding woods.",sp=0},{t="Prowlers in the Mist",b="Though quiet compared to the besieged roads and contested farms of Tirisfal, Stillwater Pond holds its own perils. The murky waters provide water only to the blighted beasts of the glades, most notably a ferocious darkhound named Bayne who stalks the shoreline. Travelers cutting through the western meadows on their way toward Brill must tread carefully, as predatory beasts and aimless zombies frequently linger along the water's edge.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What was Stillwater Pond like before the Plague?",a="In the days of human Lordaeron, Stillwater Pond was a scenic and popular spot for local picnics. Because it was shallow and calmer than the expansive Brightwater Lake, families frequently brought young children here to swim safely.",al={"stillwater pond history","what was stillwater pond used for","tirisfal swimming spot"},it="history"},{q="Is there anything living in the water now?",a="Normal aquatic life did not survive the blighting of Tirisfal. The only things swimming beneath the gray surface are the animated, skeletal remains of fish left behind by the Scourge's corruption.",al={"stillwater pond fish","what lives in stillwater pond","stillwater creatures"}},{q="What dangerous creatures wander near the pond?",a="Apart from mindless undead wandering the glades, the most notorious threat along the shore is Bayne, an unusually powerful and aggressive darkhound who feeds on unwary travelers.",al={"stillwater pond monsters","who is bayne","darkhound near brill"},it="danger"},{q="Where is Stillwater Pond located?",a="Stillwater Pond sits in central Tirisfal Glades, directly west of the town of Brill and southwest of the larger Brightwater Lake.",al={"where is stillwater pond","stillwater pond location","how to find stillwater pond"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Stillwater Pond?",a="Stillwater Pond is a quiet, shadowed lake lying just west of the town of Brill. Once a cherished leisure spot for the living families of Lordaeron, it now rests cold, gray, and still, mirroring the grim desolation that blankets the rest of Tirisfal Glades.",al={"what is stillwater pond","stillwater pond lore","tell me about stillwater pond"},it="place",add=true},{q="Why is Stillwater Pond ruined and abandoned?",a="The pond fell to ruin alongside the rest of the kingdom during the Third War, when the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath across Tirisfal. The blighted corruptions that swept through Lordaeron snuffed out mortal life around its banks, turning pleasant picnic grounds into a desolate wilderness stalked by wild beasts and the wandering dead.",al={"why is stillwater pond abandoned","what ruined stillwater pond","why is stillwater pond dead"},it="ruin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For Forsaken who retain memories of life in Lordaeron, Stillwater Pond stands as a grim reminder of lost childhood days spent picnicking and swimming along its once-clear banks.",["class:Hunter"]="The blighted wildlife around the pond, including feral darkhounds like Bayne, makes the area a hazardous hunting ground for scouts patrolling the outskirts of Brill."},kw={"bayne","brightwater lake","brill","darkhound","skeletal fish","stillwater pond","tirisfal glades"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:brill","subzone:brightwater-lake","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:forsaken"},src={"Stillwater Pond"}}
E["subzone:undercity"] = {t="subzone",n="Undercity",z="tirisfal",s="Carved beneath the shattered palace of Capital City, the Undercity serves as the sprawling subterranean stronghold of the Forsaken. Originally a tangle of royal crypts, dungeons, and sewers expanded by the Scourge, it was seized by Sylvanas Windrunner and forged into a heavily fortified capital for her free-willed undead.",h="Carved beneath the shattered palace of Capital City, the Undercity serves as the sprawling subterranean...",sec={{t="From Royal Crypts to Scourge Bastion",b="Before the Third War, the subterranean passages beneath Capital City were known as the Undercity—a vast network of sewers, royal catacombs, and dungeons. It was here that the Alliance once imprisoned the captured warchief Orgrim Doomhammer, and where the poorest citizens scraped a living in the dark. During the fall of Lordaeron, Prince Arthas Menethil ordered his Scourge to begin expanding these depths into a subterranean seat of power. When the Lich King summoned Arthas away to Northrend, the death knight left the construction unfinished.",sp=0},{t="The Forsaken Capital",b="Amid the civil war in the Plaguelands, Sylvanas Windrunner rallied those undead whose minds had broken free from the Lich King's grip. After overthrowing the dreadlords and driving out rival forces, the newly christened Forsaken claimed the subterranean complex. They completed the twisted maze of catacombs the Scourge had started, fortifying it into an impenetrable stronghold beneath the silent ruins of their former kingdom. Today, the Dark Lady rules her people from within its poisoned halls.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Was Undercity always here before the undead took over?",a="Yes, though in a much smaller form. Under the Kingdom of Lordaeron, the area beneath the palace held ancient royal crypts, dungeons, and the city's sewer system, where paupers dwelled and high-profile prisoners—including the captured Orc warchief Orgrim Doomhammer—were kept.",al={"undercity origins","was undercity always there","history of undercity"},it="origin"},{q="Who actually built the modern Undercity?",a="The initial expansion was begun by Prince Arthas Menethil, who intended to make it the Scourge's seat of power in Lordaeron. When Arthas was recalled to Northrend, Sylvanas Windrunner and her Forsaken captured the unfinished tunnels and spent years excavating and fortifying them into the fortress city seen today.",al={"who made undercity","who built the undercity","undercity construction"},it="origin"},{q="Why live underground instead of rebuilding the city above?",a="The surface ruins of Capital City are exposed to attacks from the Scarlet Crusade, Scourge remnants, and the Alliance. The subterranean catacombs are naturally defensible, hidden from view, and well suited to the needs of the undead, who do not require daylight, warmth, or open air.",al={"why live underground","why not rebuild lordaeron","why undercity"},it="why_here"},{q="Who rules the Undercity?",a="The city is ruled by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen and leader of the Forsaken, from the royal quarters deep inside the complex.",al={"who is the leader of undercity","undercity leader","who rules undercity"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Undercity?",a="The Undercity is the subterranean capital of the Forsaken, sprawling directly beneath the shattered ruins of Lordaeron's Capital City. Originally composed of ancient royal crypts, dungeons, and sewers, the vast complex was expanded into a labyrinthine fortress. Today it serves as the heart of Queen Sylvanas Windrunner's realm, functioning as a fortified haven, political center, and research bastion for the free-willed undead of the Horde.",al={"what is undercity","undercity overview","undercity lore"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Undercity?",a="Following the fall of Lordaeron, Prince Arthas began carving out a stronghold beneath the capital to serve the Scourge. When the Lich King's power weakened and Arthas fled to Northrend, Sylvanas Windrunner led a rebellion of liberated undead. During the Plaguelands civil war, her forces defeated rival dreadlords and Alliance remnants to seize the subterranean network, claiming it as the bastion of the newly founded Forsaken.",al={"history of undercity","undercity history","what happened here"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who lives in the Undercity?",a="The city is populated primarily by the Forsaken—undead who tore themselves free from the Lich King's iron grip. Within its districts, you will encounter the Royal Apothecary Society brewing deadly concoctions, shadowy rogues, dark spellcasters, and deathguards watching the perimeter. Visiting emissaries and warriors from the Orc, Tauren, and Darkspear Troll nations of the Horde also walk the corridors, though rarely comfortably.",al={"who lives in undercity","undercity residents","undercity population"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is the Undercity dangerous?",a="It is exceedingly perilous to outsiders and unwary visitors. For Alliance trespassers, death is swift and assured at the hands of the vigilant deathguards. Even for sworn Horde allies, the city poses hazards: vast open shafts, vats of toxic alchemical sludge, brooding necromancers, and scheming apothecaries conduct experiments where a single misstep can prove fatal.",al={"is undercity dangerous","how dangerous is undercity","undercity dangers"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Undercity?",a="The name predates the Forsaken by many years. Long before the kingdom fell, the labyrinth of royal crypts, dungeons, and sewers carved deep beneath Capital City was colloquially called the Undercity. Though meant for prisoners and the dead, the poorest citizens of Lordaeron squatted in its damp passageways, giving the subterranean warren a name that outlived the living city above.",al={"why undercity name","undercity name origin","why is it called undercity"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Undercity is the beating heart of Forsaken civilization—a sanctuary built from the ruins and crypts of your former lives where you are sheltered from the Scourge and the living alike.",["faction:Horde"]="The subterranean city stands as the Horde's primary military bastion in the Eastern Kingdoms, though its dim, apothecary-tainted passages test the comfort of visitors from Kalimdor."},kw={"arthas","capital city","catacombs","crypts","doomhammer","forsaken","lordaeron","sylvanas","undercity"},rel={"zone:undercity","zone:ruinsoflordaeron","zone:tirisfal","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:forsaken","topic:arthas-menethil","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","subzone:sewers"},src={"Undercity"}}
E["subzone:venomweb-vale"] = {t="subzone",n="Venomweb Vale",z="tirisfal",s="Venomweb Vale is a shadowed valley in eastern Tirisfal Glades, sitting just south of the Scarlet Monastery. Once a lush woodland teeming with game, the vale has devolved into a blighted nest overrun by aggressive, venomous arachnids. Unknown to most who pass through, it also holds an ancient legacy as the sacred meeting place of the Council of Tirisfal.",h="Venomweb Vale is a shadowed valley in eastern Tirisfal Glades, sitting just south of the Scarlet Monastery.",sec={{t="A Blighted Refuge",b="Before the plague consumed Lordaeron, this valley was a peaceful retreat of lush grass and verdant trees, favored by local hunters tracking deer and rabbits. Today, rot and creeping gloom dominate the landscape. Giant, venomous spiders swarm among the trees and hollows, led by the monstrous arachnid Sri'skulk. The vale's northern edge borders the territory of the Scarlet Crusade, where darkhounds prowl near the fortified Scarlet Encampment under Lieutenant Sanders.",sp=0},{t="Legacy of the Council",b="Long before spiders infested the hollows, the vale served as the clandestine gathering ground for the ancient Council of Tirisfal. It was here that the very first Guardian of Tirisfal was empowered to battle demonic incursions. In a desperate and misguided attempt to secure their sanctuary against intruders, one council member bred and unleashed the deadly spiders that plague the basin today. An odd boulder of fool's gold—the result of a centuries-old transmutation mishap by the apprentice Falric—still marks the secluded spot where Guardian Aegwynn once convened with the Council.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are there so many spiders in Venomweb Vale?",a="The spiders are not simply wild beasts or products of the Scourge plague. Centuries ago, a member of the secret Council of Tirisfal magically created and unleashed them in a mad attempt to defend their secluded meeting grounds from trespassers. They multiplied and overtook the valley.",al={"venomweb spiders origin","why so many spiders","who made the spiders"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Council of Tirisfal's connection to this valley?",a="Venomweb Vale served as the traditional secret assembly site for the Council of Tirisfal. Within this quiet valley, elven and human magi gathered to bestow their collective might upon the realm's defenders, including empowering the very first Guardian of Tirisfal.",al={"council of tirisfal vale","guardian meeting place","council history venomweb"},it="ties"},{q="Who rules the arachnids in the vale?",a="The spider brood is led by a massive, deadly arachnid known as Sri'skulk, who stalks the shadowed hollows beneath the valley's blighted canopy.",al={"spider boss venomweb","sri'skulk","spider leader tirisfal"},it="leader"},{q="What is the story behind the fool's gold rock in the vale?",a="Centuries ago, an apprentice named Falric accidentally transformed a boulder into fool's gold during his studies. The strange landmark became the exact meeting point where the famed Guardian Aegwynn would gather with the Council of Tirisfal.",al={"fools gold rock","aegwynn meeting rock","falric gold rock"}},{q="What is Venomweb Vale?",a="Venomweb Vale is a wide, blighted valley in eastern Tirisfal Glades, sitting just south of the Scarlet Monastery. Once a lush woodland teeming with deer and rabbits, it has decayed into a murky, poisoned hollow overrun by aggressive arachnids and prowling beasts, flanked nearby by vigilant Scarlet Crusade patrols.",al={"what is venomweb vale","venomweb vale info","tell me about venomweb vale"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Venomweb Vale in the past?",a="Long before the plague tainted Lordaeron, this valley served as the secret gathering ground for the ancient Council of Tirisfal. It was here that the councilors performed the ritual to empower the very first Guardian of Tirisfal. Centering their meetings in this secluded glade, they shaped the secret magical defense of Azeroth for generations.",al={"venomweb vale history","what happened at venomweb vale","history of venomweb vale"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who lives in Venomweb Vale?",a="No civilized folk dwell in the heart of the vale. It is dominated entirely by poisonous spiders under the broodmother Sri'skulk, alongside wandering darkhounds. Toward the northern edge of the valley, the Scarlet Crusade maintains an armed outpost known as the Scarlet Encampment under Lieutenant Sanders.",al={"who lives in venomweb vale","venomweb vale inhabitants","who controls venomweb vale"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Why is Venomweb Vale ruined?",a="The vale's decline began when a paranoid member of the Council of Tirisfal bred monstrous spiders in an attempt to protect their secret sanctum. Following the fall of Lordaeron, the Scourge's blighted corruption swept the glades, leaving the trees withered, the grass dead, and the spider brood unchecked to poison the land.",al={"why is venomweb vale ruined","what ruined venomweb vale","venomweb vale corruption"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is Venomweb Vale dangerous?",a="Yes, it is exceptionally perilous for unwary travelers. Lethal, venomous spiders nest throughout the valley's blighted trees, ambushing prey under the shadow of Sri'skulk. Furthermore, aggressive darkhounds roam the northern perimeter, where zealots of the Scarlet Crusade violently attack anyone approaching their camp.",al={"is venomweb vale dangerous","venomweb vale dangers","threats in venomweb vale"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Venomweb Vale?",a="The valley earned its grim moniker from the dense, toxic webs spun across the trees by the massive spiders that infest the hollow. Once an unnamed woodland haven, travelers and locals began calling it Venomweb Vale after the deadly arachnids claimed the entire basin.",al={"why is it called venomweb vale","venomweb vale name meaning","how did venomweb vale get its name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Mage"]="For practitioners of the arcane, the vale is hallowed ground: it was the historic meeting site of the Council of Tirisfal, where Guardians of old—including Aegwynn—were chosen and empowered.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken travelers traveling toward the Scarlet Monastery must navigate both the lethal toxins of Sri'skulk's brood and the watchful patrols of Lieutenant Sanders's Crusaders to the north."},kw={"aegwynn","council of tirisfal","falric","lieutenant sanders","scarlet encampment","sri'skulk","tirisfal glades","venomweb vale"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","zone:scarlet","topic:scarlet-crusade"},src={"Venomweb Vale"}}
E["subzone:whispering-forest"] = {t="subzone",n="Whispering Forest",z="tirisfal",s="The Whispering Forest is a secluded, mountainous woodland nestled behind the steep cliffs west of Deathknell. Largely cut off from the rest of Tirisfal Glades, it harbors uncanny wildlife, a mysterious fairy circle, and deep-seated corruption sleeping far beneath the soil.",h="The Whispering Forest is a secluded, mountainous woodland nestled behind the steep cliffs west of Deathknell.",sec={{t="The Hidden Glen",b="Tucked away behind Northface Rock and Bandarion Keep, the Whispering Forest feels entirely alienated from the surrounding plague-touched kingdom. While it contains afflicted deer and roaming predators, it also features a tranquil lake and a strange ring of giant mushrooms, glowing with an uncanny energy beneath a hovering cloud of mist. The ghostly Knight of Mourning wanders the fairy circle, bound there by oath, amid a natural magic that seems to defy the Scourge-ruined land.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow Beneath the Woods",b="Despite its outward wonder, the forest is tainted by an ancient dread. Centuries ago, the high elves briefly attempted to settle in Tirisfal Glades before being driven away when the latent evil sleeping deep underground began driving them mad. Around the local lake, Vile Fin murlocs and the undead threshadon Blightsculler prowl the waters, while the ruined settlement of Shadowvale lies blighted by necromantic remnants. Even mad wanderers across Lordaeron mutter fearful warnings of the consuming darkness resting beneath Tirisfal's roots.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Whispering Forest located?",a="The forest lies in the mountains of western Tirisfal Glades, positioned just west of Deathknell and south of Tyr's Fall. It is walled off by Northface Rock, the steep ridge upon which Bandarion Keep rests.",al={"whispering forest location","where is whispering forest","how to get to whispering forest"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens at the giant mushroom circle?",a="The ring of giant mushrooms glows with a strange energy beneath a hovering cloud of mist. The ghostly Knight of Mourning wanders the circle, bound there by an oath, but what the ring's magic truly is remains unknown.",al={"mushroom circle tirisfal","faerie dragon ring","fairy ring ritual"}},{q="Who watches over the forest?",a="The overlooking heights of Bandarion Keep are held by Tyr's Watch, an order of Forsaken paladins. Near the fairy circle itself, the spectral Knight of Mourning is bound by oath to wander the woods.",al={"who guards whispering forest","bandarion keep whispering forest"},it="in_charge"},{q="Why did the high elves abandon this region long ago?",a="When the high elves originally landed on Lordaeron's shores, they attempted to build a settlement in Tirisfal. Within a few years, an unsettling presence buried deep underground began eroding their sanity, compelling them to abandon the glades and travel north.",al={"high elves tirisfal","why elves left tirisfal","madness beneath tirisfal"},it="history"},{q="What is Shadowvale?",a="Shadowvale is a ruined village situated southwest of the forest's lake. It is occupied by the Tarnished alongside mindless undead afflicted by a necromantic crystal fragment dating back to clashes involving the Undercity.",al={"shadowvale tirisfal","ruined village whispering forest"}},{q="What is the Whispering Forest?",a="The Whispering Forest is a secluded, mist-shrouded woodland tucked beyond the steep western mountains of Tirisfal Glades. Separated from the rest of the region by the heights of Northface Rock, it is an eerie, untamed wilderness harboring diseased wildlife, mysterious fairy rings, forgotten ruins, and secluded mountain lakes cut off from ordinary travel.",al={"what is whispering forest","whispering forest lore","tell me about whispering forest"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Whispering Forest dangerous?",a="Yes, deeply so. Beyond its sickly and diseased wildlife, the bluffs and waters are infested with displaced Vile Fin murlocs and the monstrous undead threshadon Blightsculler. Further inland, mindless undead haunt the ruins of Shadowvale around a fallen necromantic crystal fragment, while hostile Tarnished cultists lurk amidst the blighted woods.",al={"whispering forest danger","is whispering forest safe","threats in whispering forest"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The glowing faerie ring stands out as one of the few pulses of natural magic remaining within blighted Lordaeron.",["race:Undead"]="Nestled just past Deathknell and overseen by Tyr's Watch at Bandarion Keep, the secluded woodland hides forgotten dangers that pose a silent threat right on the Undercity's doorstep."},kw={"bandarion keep","blightsculler","deathknell","faerie ring","shadowvale","tirisfal","whispering forest"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:bandarion-keep","subzone:deathknell","topic:lordaeron","topic:forsaken","npc:nat-pagle","topic:murloc"},src={"Whispering Forest"}}
E["subzone:whispering-shore"] = {t="subzone",n="Whispering Shore",z="tirisfal",s="The Whispering Shore is a somber stretch of coastline in western Tirisfal Glades, resting just north of the Solliden Farmstead. Once a tranquil and bountiful retreat for Lordaeron's fishermen, its cold waters have since been overrun by a violent tribe of murlocs.",h="The Whispering Shore is a somber stretch of coastline in western Tirisfal Glades, resting just north of the...",sec={{t="A Ruined Haven",b="Before the Third War swept across northern Lordaeron, the Whispering Shore was renowned among locals as a favored fishing haven, celebrated for its calm waters and plentiful catches. Today, the chilling spray of the Great Sea crashes against an empty coast stripped of its peaceful past. The waters have grown cold and dark, devoid of their former bounty, and any unfortunate traveler wandering down from the hills risks being ambushed by territorial coastal dwellers.",sp=0},{t="The Murloc Menace",b="The beach is now firmly claimed by a band of hostile murlocs who ruthlessly attack anyone approaching the surf. Led by a fierce chieftain named Muad, these aquatic scavengers guard the shallows and tidal flats aggressively, posing a constant danger to nearby roads and the nearby Solliden Farmstead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Whispering Shore located?",a="The Whispering Shore lies along the western coast of Tirisfal Glades, directly north of the Solliden Farmstead.",al={"whispering shore location","where is whispering shore","whispering shore map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What used to be at the Whispering Shore before the Scourge?",a="In the days of the Kingdom of Lordaeron, the shore was a beloved and tranquil spot for local fishermen drawn to its rich coastal waters.",al={"whispering shore history","what was whispering shore used for","whispering shore past"},it="history"},{q="Who controls the Whispering Shore now?",a="A tribe of aggressive murlocs has taken over the coastline. They are led by a particularly vicious murloc chieftain named Muad, attacking anyone who strays near the water.",al={"who lives on whispering shore","whispering shore enemies","whispering shore mobs"},it="residents"},{q="Can you still fish peacefully at the Whispering Shore?",a="The coastline is no longer the peaceful haven it once was. Its waters are cold and dark, having lost much of their former bounty, and hostile murlocs aggressively guard the shoreline.",al={"fishing at whispering shore","whispering shore fish"},gp=true},{q="What is the Whispering Shore?",a="The Whispering Shore is a cold, dark stretch of coastline along the western reaches of Tirisfal Glades, situated just north of the Solliden Farmstead. Once known as a quiet and bountiful retreat for local fishermen, the blighted coast has since fallen to ruin and chaos, leaving behind little of the peaceful waters that common folk once enjoyed.",al={"whispering shore lore","what is whispering shore","tell me about whispering shore"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Whispering Shore dangerous?",a="Yes, it is extremely dangerous for unwary travelers. The coast is currently overrun by a hostile band of ravenous murlocs led by the vicious Muad. These creatures aggressively attack anyone who wanders too close to the surf, making the shore uninhabitable for living settlers and a hazard to anyone traveling along western Tirisfal.",al={"is whispering shore safe","whispering shore threats","whispering shore danger"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why was the Whispering Shore abandoned?",a="Following the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge and the corruption of Tirisfal Glades, the once-bountiful fishing waters turned cold, dark, and barren. Without peaceful settlers to maintain the coast, predatory murlocs moved in and claimed the shoreline, driving away anyone who might have hoped to return.",al={"why is whispering shore ruined","what ruined whispering shore","why was whispering shore deserted"},it="ruin",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="For the Forsaken securing western Tirisfal Glades, the murloc infestation at Whispering Shore poses an unruly threat right along the perimeter of their holdings."},kw={"coast","fishing","muad","murloc","solliden farmstead","tirisfal glades","whispering shore"},rel={"zone:tirisfal","subzone:solliden-farmstead","topic:murloc"},src={"Whispering Shore"}}
E["zone:tirisfal"] = {t="zone",n="Tirisfal Glades",z="tirisfal",s="Tirisfal Glades is the haunting northern territory of Lordaeron, now serving as the heartland of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken. Beneath blighted skies and rotting boughs lie fallen manors, dormant crypts, and the vast underground capital of the Undercity. Surrounded by mindless Scourge and zealous Scarlet crusaders, it is a perilous realm where free-willed undead carve out their survival.",h="Tirisfal Glades is the haunting northern territory of Lordaeron, now serving as the heartland of Lady...",sec={{t="A Land in Twilight",b="Once the fertile cradle of humanity, Tirisfal Glades has withered beneath the curse of the Lich King's plague. The sun rarely penetrates the murky gloom, casting an eternal sickly pale over dying woods, neglected pumpkin patches, and cold coastal hills. While remnants of old farmsteads like the Agamand Mills and Balnir Farmstead remain, their former masters have largely been swept into mass graves or risen as restless dead. Despite the devastation, a dark resilience permeates the countryside, anchored by towns like Brill and the cloistered sanctuary of Deathknell, where freshly awakened Forsaken learn to endure their second life.",sp=0},{t="A War on Two Fronts",b="Though Lady Sylvanas claims dominion over the region from the subterranean Ruins of Lordaeron, the Forsaken remain embattled on all sides. Residual Scourge thralls still infest hollows, fields, and dilapidated crypts, entirely subservient to the frozen throne of Northrend. To the east, the fanatical Scarlet Crusade has made northern Tirisfal its primary bastion, operating out of the Scarlet Monastery. Zealously devoted to the extermination of every undead creature, living and unliving alike, their outposts and patrols wage constant guerrilla warfare against Forsaken scouts and civilian travelers.",sp=0},{t="Echoes of the Ancient Past",b="Long before human kingdoms crowned their first monarchs, the Glades bore an ancient legacy. In the ancient tongue of the vrykul, 'Tirisfal' translates to 'Tyr's Fall,' christened after the heroic titan keeper Tyr fought to the death against colossal cosmic horrors. In subsequent eras, high elven exiles briefly tried to settle these woods before shadowy energies deep beneath the earth began driving them mad, prompting their migration north. In later centuries, the secret mortal cabal known as the Council of Tirisfal chose these shadowed groves to empower solitary Guardians, shielding Azeroth from demonic intrusion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the sky green and the land rotting?",a="During the Third War, Prince Arthas and the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath across the Kingdom of Lordaeron. The magical blight poisoned not only the populace, but the soil, trees, and water itself, leaving the glades in an eternal state of eerie, sickly decay.",al={"why is tirisfal blighted","what happened to tirisfal","why is tirisfal dying"},it="ruin"},{q="What is the difference between the Forsaken and the Scourge here?",a="The Forsaken are undead who broke free from the Lich King's telepathic domination after the Battle of Mount Hyjal, choosing to follow Lady Sylvanas Windrunner with their own free will. The Scourge roaming the farmsteads and ruins remain mindless slaves bound to the Lich King's commands.",al={"forsaken vs scourge","why are zombies attacking undead","are all undead evil"},it="conflict"},{q="Who are the red-armored soldiers attacking travelers?",a="They belong to the Scarlet Crusade, an uncompromising splinter order of former Silver Hand paladins and survivors. Convinced all undead are irredeemable abominations that must be purified by sword and fire, they execute both mindless thralls and free-willed Forsaken with fanatical hatred.",al={"scarlet crusade","red humans","monastery humans"},it="threat"},{q="Where does the name Tirisfal come from?",a="The region is named in honor of the titan keeper Tyr. According to legend, Tyr fell in battle against eldritch horrors in this glade. In the ancient vrykul tongue, 'Tyr's Fall' evolved into the name 'Tirisfal,' and the keeper's silver hand became an emblem woven deeply into early human mythology.",al={"tirisfal meaning","tyrs fall","origin of tirisfal"},it="name"},{q="Where do newly awakened Forsaken begin their journey?",a="Newly awakened Forsaken emerge from the crypts of the Shadow Grave inside Deathknell, a cloistered town nestled in southwestern Tirisfal. There, administrators and executors evaluate whether the risen dead possess the sanity and loyalty required to serve the Dark Lady.",al={"undead starting zone","where is deathknell","forsaken starting area"},it="arrival"},{q="How do travelers leave Tirisfal for other continents?",a="A large goblin-operated zeppelin tower sits on the hillside south of Brill, right outside the Ruins of Lordaeron. These airships provide direct, regular transit across the seas to Orgrimmar in Kalimdor and Grom'gol Base Camp in Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"tirisfal zeppelins","how to get to orgrimmar","how to get to stranglethorn"},it="place"},{q="What is Tirisfal Glades?",a="Tirisfal Glades is the heartland of the Forsaken on the northern coast of Lordaeron. Once the peaceful, fertile core of a proud human kingdom, it was ravaged during the Third War by the Scourge's Plague of Undeath. Today, blighted woods and perpetual green gloom blanket the hills, framed by the ruins of Lordaeron's capital and surrounded by hostile zealots, feral beasts, and lingering undead.",al={"what is tirisfal","tell me about tirisfal glades","tirisfal zone lore"},it="place",add=true,nx={8}},{q="Was this always Undead land?",a="No. For thousands of years, Tirisfal was the cradle of human civilization and the thriving heart of the Kingdom of Lordaeron. Peaceful farmsteads, prosperous mills, and small hamlets flourished under King Terenas Menethil. That era ended violently during the Third War when Prince Arthas and the Cult of the Damned swept through with the plague, turning the inhabitants into undead slaves before Sylvanas broke them free.",al={"was tirisfal always undead","who lived here before","tirisfal before plague"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who lives in or runs Tirisfal Glades?",a="The Forsaken claim Tirisfal Glades under the rule of Queen Sylvanas Windrunner from the Undercity. However, their hold is constantly contested. The fanatical Scarlet Crusade commands fortifications and their great Monastery in the northeast, while mindless Scourge prowl derelict farms. On the eastern border at the Bulwark, the Forsaken coordinate alongside the Argent Dawn to keep the horrors of the Plaguelands contained.",al={"who rules tirisfal","who controls tirisfal glades","tirisfal factions"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is Tirisfal Glades dangerous?",a="Extremely dangerous. Beyond the walls of Forsaken outposts, you face mindless Scourge remnants, savage gnolls, giant arachnids, and vicious wildlife tainted by the plague. Worse still are the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, who execute any undead on sight. To the far east lies the Western Plaguelands, where monsters far deadlier than anything in Tirisfal will tear apart the unwary.",al={"how dangerous is tirisfal","tirisfal threats","is it safe here"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Who's in charge in Brill?",a="Magistrate Sevren oversees the civic administration of Brill, managing the town's rebuilding and daily survival. Military authority and tactical operations, particularly the campaign against the nearby Scarlet Crusade, are commanded by Executor Zygand.",al={"brill leader","who leads brill","in charge of brill"},it="in_charge",add=true,nx={12,13}},{q="Who does Magistrate Sevren answer to?",a="Sevren serves the Dark Lady, Queen Sylvanas Windrunner. As magistrate of Brill, he represents the Forsaken crown's civilian authority in the region, operating in direct concert with royal executors and the Royal Apothecary Society based in the Undercity.",al={"sevren boss","who does sevren report to","brill leadership chain"},it="in_charge",add=true},{q="Where is the Banshee Queen?",a="Lady Sylvanas Windrunner resides in the Royal Quarter of the Undercity, deep beneath the ruins of Capital City just south of Brill. From her throne room, she directs the Forsaken in their war against the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"where is sylvanas","find sylvanas","banshee queen location"},it="leader",add=true},{q="What is Brill?",a="Brill is the primary Forsaken town in central Tirisfal Glades, situated along the crossroads north of Capital City. Once a living settlement under the Barov estate, it was rebuilt after the Third War to provide newly awakened undead a place of rest, trade, and organization before venturing further into the world.",al={"about brill","what kind of town is brill","brill lore"},it="place",add=true,nx={15,16}},{q="Were living people here before?",a="Yes. Brill was once a quiet human community within the Kingdom of Lordaeron. It became ground zero for early plague experiments orchestrated by Kel'Thuzad, resulting in mass casualties and massive communal graves before Prince Arthas ever arrived to investigate.",al={"did humans live in brill","brill living history","who lived in brill"},it="history",add=true},{q="What's the Gallows' End Tavern?",a="The Gallows' End Tavern is Brill's central gathering hall and inn. It offers shelter, foul libations, and respite from the grim glades outside. In its darker corners, the Forsaken keep captured enemies—such as Scarlet zealots—for sinister Apothecary concoction trials.",al={"gallows end tavern","brill inn","what is gallows end"},it="place",add=true},{q="What's the deal with the Agamand Mills?",a="Agamand Mills was once the wealthiest and most productive farming estate in Tirisfal Glades, famous for flour and grain. When the plague struck, the entire complex was overrun and slaughter ensued. Today, it stands as a rotting, windmill-dotted ruin choked with lingering Scourge undead.",al={"agamand mills lore","what happened to agamand mills","agamand mills"},it="place",add=true,nx={18,19}},{q="Who were the Agamands?",a="The Agamands were a noble, wealthy human dynasty who developed the sprawling mills and held property throughout Tirisfal, including Brill. During the Third War, their fortune could not save them; the family fell to the Scourge and their reanimated patriarchs continue to haunt the estate.",al={"agamand family","who lived at agamand mills","the agamands"},it="people",add=true},{q="Why are they hostile to us if they're undead too?",a="The undead haunting the Agamand Mills never regained their free will. Unlike the Forsaken, who shook off the Lich King's mental grip, these creatures remain enslaved as mindless Scourge thralls, compelled to destroy any living or rebellious dead who trespass on their domain.",al={"why do agamands attack us","why are agamand undead hostile","why fight other undead"},it="threat",add=true},{q="Where does all this lead after Tirisfal?",a="Most Forsaken journey south through the mountain pass into Silverpine Forest to assist in securing the southern frontier and battling Dalaran agents and Rot Hide gnolls. Alternatively, zeppelins south of Brill provide passage to Orgrimmar or Grom'gol Base Camp across the sea.",al={"where to go after tirisfal","next zone after tirisfal","after tirisfal glades"},it="next_step",add=true,nx={21,22}},{q="What's Undercity?",a="The Undercity is the sprawling subterranean capital of the Forsaken, built within the ancient sewers, crypts, and catacombs beneath the shattered Ruins of Lordaeron. It serves as Queen Sylvanas's fortress and the command center for Forsaken operations across Azeroth.",al={"what is the undercity","undercity lore","about undercity"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is the Silverpine border next?",a="Yes. Following the road south out of Tirisfal leads directly into Silverpine Forest. It is the natural continuation of your service to the Forsaken, where you confront the growing threat along the southern borders of Lordaeron.",al={"silverpine next","do i go to silverpine next","silverpine border"},it="next_step",add=true},{q="How did I end up in Tirisfal Glades?",a="If you are newly risen Forsaken, you died during the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge and were buried here. With the Lich King's grip broken, your free will returned, and you clawed your way out of the grave in the secluded valley of Deathknell. If you belong to the other Horde nations, you likely arrived through the zeppelin towers situated between Brill and the Undercity, which connect this blighted realm directly to Orgrimmar and Grom'gol Base Camp.",al={"how did i get here","how did i get to tirisfal glades","how did i arrive here"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Tirisfal Glades?",a="Your goal is to help the Forsaken secure their tenuous hold over Lordaeron's blighted heartlands. Moving out from the crypts of Deathknell toward the town of Brill, you must purge the mindless Scourge haunting abandoned farms, eliminate fanatical zealots of the Scarlet Crusade encroaching from the north, and cull plagued beasts roaming the rotting woods. Doing so proves your worth to Lady Sylvanas and prepares you to serve her growing kingdom beneath the ruins of Capital City.",al={"what should i do here","why am i in tirisfal","what is my goal here"},it="purpose",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This is your ancestral homeland twice over: the kingdom you built in life, and the precarious sanctuary you must defend in undeath under Lady Sylvanas.",["faction:Horde"]="Tirisfal is the Horde's vital bastion in the northern Eastern Kingdoms, anchoring diplomatic ties with the Forsaken while warding off human extremists and the Scourge.",["faction:Alliance"]="For the surviving nations of the Alliance, Tirisfal is a tragic monument to Lordaeron's fall, filled with the desecrated tombs of former brothers and sisters in faith.",["class:Priest"]="Historic holy grounds like Faol's Rest—the tomb of Archbishop Alonsus Faol, founder of the Silver Hand—lie here, warped by death and contested between zealots and Shadow priests."},kw={"agamand","balnir","brill","deathknell","forsaken","glades","lordaeron","scarlet crusade","sylvanas","tirisfal","tyr","undercity"},rel={"zone:undercity","zone:ruinsoflordaeron","zone:scarlet","zone:silverpine","zone:westernplaguelands","subzone:brill","subzone:deathknell","subzone:bulwark"},m={levels="1-10"},src={"Tirisfal Glades (Classic)","Tirisfal Glades"}}
